window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"mozart-requiem":["About fifty-five minutes of choral setting of the Latin mass for the dead, left unfinished at Mozart's death and completed by another hand. The scoring is dark by design: no flutes or oboes, two basset horns and bassoons in the treble, trombones doubling the chorus, trumpets and drums for the day of wrath. The music runs from the whispered chromatic entries of the opening to the shouting Dies irae, the trombone solo of the Tuba mirum, and the eight bars of Lacrimosa that Mozart lived to write.","Commissioned anonymously in July 1791 through an intermediary by Count Franz von Walsegg, an amateur who intended to pass it off as his own memorial for his young wife. Mozart worked on it between La clemenza di Tito, Die Zauberflote and a final illness, and died on 5 December with much of it as skeleton score. Constanze, needing the fee, had it finished secretly, first briefly by Joseph Eybler and then by Franz Xaver Sussmayr.","Listen closer: the fugue subject of the Kyrie is taken almost note for note from a chorus in Handel's Messiah, music Mozart had recently rescored for Baron van Swieten. The Introit is the only movement fully written out by Mozart; from there he left vocal parts and a bass line with occasional instrumental cues. The Lacrimosa stops at bar eight. A sketch for an Amen fugue, found in the 1960s, shows he planned to close the sequence contrapuntally.","The completion is the permanent argument. Sussmayr's version is the one everyone knows and is demonstrably weaker in the movements he invented; a dozen modern alternatives by Beyer, Maunder, Levin, Druce and others restore the Amen fugue or rewrite the orchestration, and none has displaced him. The romantic legend, that Mozart believed he was writing his own requiem after a visit from a grey messenger, comes from later retellings and should be treated as biography, not fact.","Listen to the opening Introit first, knowing it is entirely his, then to the Lacrimosa and stop after eight bars to hear where the pen fell. Compare a Sussmayr recording with a Robert Levin or Richard Maunder completion on the Amen and Sanctus. Period-instrument performances bring out the basset horns, which give the whole work its bitter colour."],"mozart-mass-c-minor":["An hour of music, unfinished: the Kyrie and Gloria are complete, the Credo breaks off partway, and the Agnus Dei was never written. The chorus splits into eight parts for the great fugal movements, which look back deliberately to Handel and Bach. Two soprano soloists carry the most elaborate writing; the Et incarnatus est is a slow aria accompanied by flute, oboe and bassoon in a concertante trio, more operatic than liturgical, and among the most demanding soprano solos Mozart wrote.","Begun in Vienna in 1782, apparently as a private vow connected to his marriage to Constanze against his father's wishes, and taken unfinished to Salzburg in 1783. Constanze sang one of the soprano parts at a performance in St Peter's Abbey on 26 October 1783, probably with gaps filled from earlier masses. Baron van Swieten's Sunday sessions had just introduced Mozart to Bach and Handel manuscripts, and the influence is audible on every page.","Listen closer: the double-chorus Qui tollis is built over a relentless descending bass line of a kind Bach used for grief, and the two choirs answer each other in overlapping cries rather than blocks. Mozart recycled the finished movements two years later into an Italian cantata, Davidde penitente, adding new words and two fresh arias, which is why the same music exists in two guises. The eight-part writing is the densest counterpoint he ever set for voices.","Because it is a torso, editors must decide what to perform: some use only the completed movements, others graft in music from earlier masses or from Davidde penitente, and completions by H. C. Robbins Landon, Richard Maunder and Ulrich Leisinger differ substantially. Nobody knows why Mozart abandoned it. Its reputation has grown as the twentieth century became less troubled by its stylistic mixture of Baroque fugue and opera-house aria.","Start with the Et incarnatus est, an aria of extraordinary length and difficulty, then the Qui tollis for the eight-part chorus. Compare the fugues with Bach's B minor Mass to hear what Mozart had just discovered. Choose a recording that states its edition; a performance of the completed movements alone is more honest than a patched whole."],"mozart-gran-partita":["Seven movements, close to fifty minutes, for thirteen players: twelve wind instruments and a double bass holding the floor. It is far longer and more ambitious than any dinner-table serenade needed to be. The third movement, an Adagio, sets a slow throbbing accompaniment in the lower instruments while oboe, clarinet and basset horn take turns with a single long melodic line passed hand to hand. There are two minuets, each with two trios, a set of variations and a bustling finale.","The dating is uncertain; the manuscript is undated and scholars place composition between 1781 and 1784, in Mozart's first Vienna years. Wind bands, Harmoniemusik, were standard for aristocratic entertainment, and Joseph II kept one at court, but they normally played arrangements of opera hits in six or eight parts. Mozart wrote for a much larger and stranger group including basset horns, and produced a work that will not fit in the background.","Listen closer: the basset horn is the reason the piece sounds unlike anything else, a low clarinet with a soft, slightly hollow tone that Mozart used almost nowhere except here, in Masonic music and in the Requiem. The double bass, not a cello, anchors the texture, so the bottom sounds an octave lower than in most chamber music. In the Adagio the melody never belongs to one player for more than a few bars.","A newspaper notice by the playwright Johann Friedrich Schink describes a Vienna benefit concert on 23 March 1784 for the clarinettist Anton Stadler at which four movements of a great wind piece were played, and this is generally taken as the first documented performance. The 1984 film Amadeus made the Adagio famous by putting a description of it in Salieri's mouth. Scholars still dispute whether the work was conceived complete or assembled from separate pieces.","Go straight to the third movement, the Adagio, and follow the melody as it moves from oboe to clarinet to basset horn while the accompaniment pulses underneath. Then listen to the whole thing as background and notice that it refuses to stay in the background. Period instruments make the difference here, particularly real basset horns rather than substitutes."],"mozart-clarinet-quintet":["Four movements, a little over half an hour, for clarinet with two violins, viola and cello. The strings open alone with a rising phrase and the clarinet answers with a rippling arpeggio, a pattern of question and decoration that recurs throughout. The Larghetto is a slow song for the clarinet over muted strings. The minuet has two trios, one for strings alone and one a peasant dance led by the clarinet, and the finale is a set of variations with one slow, shadowed variation in the minor.","Entered in Mozart's catalogue on 29 September 1789 and first performed in Vienna that December at a benefit for the Tonkunstler-Societat. It was written for Anton Stadler, a fellow Freemason and orchestral clarinettist whose playing Mozart admired above all others and to whom he also lent money he could not spare. Mozart's own finances were desperate, Constanze was ill and taking cures at Baden, and Vienna's war with the Turks had emptied the concert calendar.","Listen closer: Mozart wrote it for Stadler's basset clarinet, an extended instrument reaching four semitones below the ordinary range, and the original score is lost. What is published was adapted after his death to fit standard instruments, which flattens several passages where the line was meant to plunge lower. Reconstructions restoring the basset notes now exist. In the finale's variations, notice the one that slows and darkens, the only place the piece admits trouble.","Universally admired and rarely argued about, except over the instrument. Since the 1950s scholars including Alan Hacker and Colin Lawson have reconstructed the basset clarinet version, and a growing number of players use it, which alters roughly a hundred notes. Nineteenth-century sentimentality about the work as a farewell has faded now that its date, two years before Mozart's death and in a productive period, is better known.","Start with the Larghetto, four minutes of clarinet over muted strings, which is the piece most people remember. Then listen to the finale for the sudden minor-key variation. Seek out a basset clarinet recording and follow the low notes; hearing the line drop where the standard version cannot go explains what was lost."],"mozart-string-quintet-4-g-minor":["String quartet plus a second viola, which thickens the middle of the texture and lets the music sit lower and darker. Around thirty-five minutes in four movements. The first movement's theme sags downward from the start and never finds a firm footing. The minuet is broken by harsh off-beat chords. The slow movement puts mutes on all five players. Then, instead of a fast finale, comes a long G minor Adagio introduction that seems to be dying, before a cheerful G major rondo arrives from nowhere.","Dated 16 May 1787 in Vienna, two weeks before Leopold Mozart's death in Salzburg; his father had been seriously ill for months and Mozart knew it. He wrote it alongside the C major quintet as a pair, advertising them by subscription in the newspapers, an attempt to raise money directly from the public that failed and had to be postponed for lack of subscribers. Don Giovanni was commissioned that same year.","Listen closer: the two violas are not a filler. Mozart repeatedly splits the ensemble into two three-part groups that overlap, so a phrase can be handed between two miniature trios sharing the first viola. The finale's transition is the strangest thing in his chamber music: a slow, sinking Adagio that leads to a sunny dance with no bridge and no explanation, and the debate over whether it is consolation or evasion has never ended.","Nineteenth-century listeners heard grief for the composer's father and treated the major-key finale as an embarrassment or a failure of nerve; Tchaikovsky reportedly wept at the slow movement. Modern writers point out the quintet was finished before Leopold died and warn against reading biography into the notes. What survives is the formal problem: whether the ending answers the work or refuses to.","Listen to the last movement complete, slow introduction and rondo together, then decide whether the happy ending convinces you; that judgment is the piece's real subject. Afterwards go back to the muted slow movement. Compare it with the C major quintet written the same month, which solves the same problems in the opposite mood."],"mozart-string-quartet-19-dissonance":["Half an hour in four movements, the last of six quartets dedicated to Haydn. It is famous for its first twenty-two bars: the cello starts alone on a repeated low note, the viola enters against it, then the second violin and finally the first violin, each arriving on a pitch that grinds against what is already sounding, so the music has no key at all until the Allegro suddenly lands in bright C major. The rest is airy, contrapuntal and untroubled by comparison.","Completed on 14 January 1785 in Vienna. The six quartets took Mozart nearly three years, unusually slow for him, and he wrote in the published dedication that they were the fruit of long and laborious effort. The model was Haydn's Opus 33, published in 1781, which had reinvented the quartet as four equal conversationalists. Haydn came to Mozart's apartment to play through several of them with the composer and his father.","Listen closer: the introduction is not random dissonance but a strict sequence in which each voice is correct on its own terms and only the overlap is painful, which is why it took nineteenth-century editors so long to find anything to correct. The cello's opening repeated note is the quiet pulse that everything else collides with. Later in the quartet the same falling chromatic shapes reappear, now harmless, in the middle of cheerful music.","Publishers and theorists assumed there were printing errors; Giuseppe Sarti attacked the passage in print, and the Belgian scholar Fetis published a corrected version in the nineteenth century that removed the clashes. Leopold Mozart recorded Haydn telling him at a play-through in February 1785 that his son was the greatest composer known to him, in person or by name, the most quoted sentence in Mozart biography.","Listen to the first minute, then the next minute, and notice the physical relief when C major arrives; the introduction only works if you let it be uncomfortable. Then compare it with the opening of Haydn's Emperor Quartet to hear two different ideas of how a quartet should begin. A quartet playing on gut strings makes the clashes rougher and more convincing."],"mozart-sinfonia-concertante-k364":["Three movements, about half an hour, for two soloists who are exact equals: violin and viola, entering together on the same held note and then diverging. The orchestra's opening tutti is long and grand, with divided violas thickening the string sound. The C minor Andante is the emotional centre, the two soloists trading a grieving phrase in close imitation. The finale is a fast rondo in which the pair chase each other through increasingly playful exchanges.","Written in Salzburg, probably in the second half of 1779, shortly after Mozart returned from the disastrous Paris journey on which his mother died and he found no employment. He had heard the sinfonia concertante genre flourishing in Paris and Mannheim, where a work for two or more soloists with orchestra was the height of fashion. Back in Salzburg he was a resentful court organist; no performance in his lifetime is documented.","Listen closer: the viola part is written in D major, a semitone lower than it sounds, because Mozart instructed the player to tune all four strings up a semitone. Tighter strings ring brighter and louder, so the viola can hold its own against the violin instead of being buried, a piece of practical acoustics few composers bothered with. Most modern violists ignore the instruction and simply play in E-flat, which loses the edge Mozart engineered.","It has long been regarded as the finest thing Mozart wrote before leaving Salzburg, and as the work in which the viola first becomes a solo instrument of equal dignity. The scordatura tuning is the live performance-practice argument: period specialists retune, most modern players do not, and recordings differ audibly in how well the viola projects. A related double concerto attributed to Mozart, K. 320e, survives only as a fragment.","Start with the C minor slow movement, where the two soloists finish each other's sentences. Then listen for the very first entry of the pair in the opening movement, arriving together on one note before separating. If you can find a recording where the violist retunes, compare it with a standard one and hear the viola stop apologising."],"mozart-piano-sonata-8-a-minor":["Twenty minutes, three movements, and one of only two piano sonatas Mozart wrote in a minor key. The opening movement is driven by a hammering repeated chord in the left hand under a rigid dotted melody, and the development turns violent, with crashing chords and hand-crossing far beyond the polite keyboard writing of the day. The F major slow movement begins as consolation and then breaks apart in the middle. The finale is a fast, whispering rondo that never raises its voice or finds the major key.","Written in Paris in the summer of 1778, during the trip on which Mozart, twenty-two, failed to find a post and his mother Anna Maria fell ill and died in their rented rooms on 3 July. He wrote home in stages, first preparing his father for the news. Parisian taste ran to elegant keyboard music for amateurs; this sonata is unplayable as background music and was not what the market wanted.","Listen closer: the left hand's repeated chords in the first movement are marked to be played with a specific insistent weight, and the effect is closer to a drum than to accompaniment. In the slow movement's middle section the texture suddenly fragments into short gasping phrases separated by rests, the writing breaking down. The finale is marked to be played quietly almost throughout, so the aggression is contained rather than released, and it ends without consolation.","The temptation to hear his mother's death in it is old and strong, and equally old is the objection that the dating is imprecise and the minor-key style was a recognised Parisian manner. Both are probably true. Beethoven knew the sonata and its opening left-hand drumming reappears in his own early minor-key writing. Performers argue about the first movement's tempo, which many take too fast for the harmony to register.","Play the first thirty seconds and listen to the left hand rather than the tune; the relentless repeated chords are what makes it frightening. Then go to the middle of the slow movement, where the music comes apart. Compare a fortepiano recording, where the hammering is drier and harsher, with a modern grand, which makes it sound more romantic than it is."],"beethoven-symphony-no-1":["Four movements for a Haydn-sized orchestra, about twenty-six minutes. It opens with a joke: a soft chord that belongs to the wrong key, then a second one, so that C major is only reached after the ear has been led away from it. An Andante cantabile follows, then a movement labelled Menuetto that runs far too fast for anyone to dance to, and a finale whose introduction assembles a scale one note at a time before the tune can start.","Beethoven was twenty-nine, famous in Vienna as a pianist and as a writer of trios, quartets and sonatas, but with no symphony to his name. He put this one at the centre of his first benefit concert at the Burgtheater on 2 April 1800, on a programme that also held Mozart and Haydn. Haydn's London symphonies were barely five years old and set the mark he had to hit; the dedication went to Baron Gottfried van Swieten.","Listen for how much work the oboes, clarinets and bassoons are given. Early reviewers grumbled that it sounded like a wind band with strings attached, and in the third movement the winds do hold a chord while the violins scurry beneath. The finale's slow introduction is a joke about scales themselves, adding one note per attempt until the melody is finally allowed to run.","The Leipzig Allgemeine musikalische Zeitung praised the work while complaining of too much wind writing and too much hurry. For a century afterwards it was heard as the last Haydn symphony rather than the first Beethoven one, and it is still often used as a curtain-raiser. Period-instrument performances since the 1980s have given it back its bite: hard timpani sticks, fast minuet tempos, audible wit.","Start at bar one and notice that for fifteen seconds you cannot tell what key you are in. Then go to the third movement and time it against a real Haydn minuet: this is a scherzo wearing a minuet's coat. A band of forty players suits it better than a full modern orchestra."],"beethoven-symphony-no-2":["About thirty-four minutes in four movements, opening with the longest slow introduction anyone had yet written for a symphony: a loud unison, then a slow spread of harmony that keeps refusing to settle. The second movement is a broad Larghetto in A major with tunes handed round the winds. The third is the first movement in any of his symphonies actually headed Scherzo, and the finale begins with a rude upward flick that sounds like a hiccup.","Written mostly in the summer of 1802 at Heiligenstadt, the village outside Vienna where in October he drafted the unsent letter to his brothers describing his deafness and his thoughts of suicide. Nothing of that shows in the music, which is the sunniest thing he had written. The premiere came on 5 April 1803 at the Theater an der Wien in a long self-promoting concert. The dedication went to Prince Karl Lichnowsky.","The Larghetto meant enough to him that he later arranged the whole symphony for piano trio himself, an unusual act of recycling. Listen also to how the finale keeps threatening to end and then starting again: the coda is nearly as long as the exposition, a habit that would soon swallow whole movements.","A Viennese critic notoriously described the finale as a crude monster, a wounded dragon thrashing about and refusing to die. Nineteenth-century audiences preferred it to the Eroica for decades. Modern commentary tends to hear it against the Heiligenstadt document, which risks turning cheerfulness into defiance; performers increasingly just let it be fast and funny.","Begin with the Larghetto, then jump straight to the funeral march of the Eroica written two years later and hear how far he travelled. In the finale, listen for the opening flick coming back in the wrong places. A lean, quick reading suits it better than a grand one."],"beethoven-symphony-no-3-eroica":["Roughly fifty minutes, about twice the length of a Haydn symphony, in four movements. Two hammered chords launch a first movement whose main theme is little more than an arpeggio, worked for a quarter of an hour. Then a funeral march in C minor with a fugue at its centre and a coda where the tune breaks into fragments. Then a fast scherzo with three horns whooping in the trio, and a finale of variations that begins with the bass line alone before the melody appears.","Composed 1803 to 1804, when Beethoven had accepted that his hearing would not return and had decided, in Czerny's report, to take a new path. Ferdinand Ries described his teacher tearing up the title page bearing Bonaparte's name on hearing that Napoleon had made himself Emperor. The published score of 1806 instead says the symphony was written to celebrate the memory of a great man, and it is dedicated to Prince Lobkowitz, in whose palace it was first tried out.","The finale's theme is not new. The bass line and tune come from his own ballet The Creatures of Prometheus and the piano variations Op. 35, which means the whole symphony is built to arrive at music about a fire-bringer. Four bars before the recapitulation of the first movement a horn steals in with the theme against the wrong harmony; Ries thought the player had come in early and was shouted at for saying so.","Early reviewers split three ways: some called it the greatest instrumental work yet written, some wanted heavy cuts, one suggested it would be better if it stopped after fifteen minutes. The Napoleon story rests on Ries and has been polished by retelling, though the scratched-out page survives. Arguments now concentrate on the metronome marks and on whether the funeral march mourns anyone in particular.","Give the funeral march its own hearing first, then start the symphony from the beginning so the march arrives where it belongs. Listen for the grinding dissonant chord at the crisis of the first movement, and for the early horn entry. A period band makes the three horns sound like a hunting party rather than a wall."],"beethoven-symphony-no-4":["Thirty-three minutes in four movements. The opening is a shock after the Eroica: a held note in the dark, then a slow feeling-about through remote harmony for two minutes before the Allegro bursts out. The Adagio runs on a ticking accompaniment figure that never lets up, even under the singing violin line. The third movement plays its trio twice, and the finale is a perpetual-motion piece with a famously exposed bassoon.","Written quickly in 1806, largely while Beethoven was staying in Silesia with Count Franz von Oppersdorff, who maintained a private orchestra and paid for the piece. Beethoven set aside the half-finished Fifth Symphony to write it. First heard at a private concert at Prince Lobkowitz's Vienna palace in March 1807, alongside the Coriolan overture and the Fourth Piano Concerto.","The third movement's five-part scheme, scherzo and trio alternating three times and twice, was a new shape that he reused in the Sixth and Seventh. In the first movement, listen for the moment near the recapitulation where the timpani alone hold the pulse while the harmony gropes back home: he does almost nothing for twenty seconds and the tension is enormous.","Schumann called it a slender Greek maiden between two Norse giants, a line that has kept the symphony in the shade ever since. Weber mocked the wandering introduction. Berlioz, by contrast, wrote at length about the Adagio. Conductors argue over whether to take the finale at the marked speed, which pushes the bassoonist to the edge.","Start with the introduction and try to guess where it is going; the answer is much brighter than the sound suggests. Then take the finale and follow the bassoon. Compare it with the Eighth, its cheerful twin, rather than with the symphonies on either side."],"beethoven-symphony-no-6-pastoral":["Forty minutes in five titled movements, the last three running without a break: arrival in the country, scene by the brook, a peasants' merrymaking, a thunderstorm, and a shepherds' song of thanksgiving. The music is built from repetition rather than argument, the same short figures turning over for pages at a time. The storm brings in piccolo, timpani and two trombones for the only time, then clears into a horn call and a rising hymn.","Finished in 1808 and premiered on 22 December that year at the Theater an der Wien, in a four-hour concert in an unheated hall that also gave the Fifth Symphony, the Fourth Piano Concerto and the Choral Fantasy their first performances. Beethoven walked in the countryside around Heiligenstadt daily and filled sketchbooks outdoors. Pastoral symphonies with storms already existed; what was new was building one on this scale.","Beethoven wrote in a sketchbook that the work is more an expression of feeling than painting, and yet he labelled the birds in the score at the end of the brook scene: nightingale for flute, quail for oboe, cuckoo for clarinet. In the peasants' dance the second oboe comes in on the wrong beat and the bassoonist can manage only three notes, a portrait of a village band that Beethoven had probably heard.","Nineteenth-century critics used the symphony to argue about programme music, with Berlioz and Liszt treating it as permission and Hanslick citing Beethoven's own caution as a limit. The bird calls embarrassed some Victorians. Recent scholarship reads the piece against the cult of nature in German writing of the 1790s rather than as simple illustration.","Start with the brook scene and let it run long past the point where you expect a change; the stillness is the subject. Then hear the storm break in from the peasants' dance without a pause. A period orchestra makes the trombones in the storm genuinely frightening."],"beethoven-symphony-no-7":["Thirty-eight minutes in four movements, each one driven by a single rhythmic cell repeated until it becomes hypnotic. The longest slow introduction he wrote leads to a skipping dotted figure that never stops. The second movement, in A minor, is a slow tread over the same short-long pattern, gathering weight as instruments join. The scherzo is very fast with a broad trio played twice, and the finale is a stamping dance that ends with the basses pounding a two-note figure under the whole orchestra.","Sketched in 1811 at the Bohemian spa town of Teplitz, where Beethoven was taking a cure, and finished in the spring of 1812. The premiere on 8 December 1813 in the university hall in Vienna was a charity concert for soldiers wounded at Hanau, sharing the bill with the battle piece Wellington's Victory. Salieri, Spohr, Hummel and Meyerbeer all took part in the orchestra. It was the greatest public success of Beethoven's life.","Follow one rhythm at a time and the design opens up: the whole first movement after the introduction uses essentially one dotted pattern, and the Allegretto keeps the same tread going even when the melody moves elsewhere. The trio of the scherzo is built on a slow, hymn-like tune that may come from an Austrian pilgrims' song, held by the winds while the violins hover on one long note.","The Allegretto was encored at the premiere and was for a century detached and played on its own. Weber is supposed to have said the piece proved Beethoven was ripe for the madhouse, an anecdote with no reliable source. Wagner's description of the symphony as the apotheosis of the dance has shaped performance ever since; the current argument is over the very fast metronome marks.","Start with the Allegretto, then go back and hear how the introduction prepares the whole work by repeating a single rising scale. Play the finale loud. Compare a large modern orchestra with a period band, where the hard drums and narrow-bore trumpets make the last movement sound dangerous."],"beethoven-symphony-no-8":["Twenty-six minutes and the shortest of the nine, with no slow movement at all. The first movement begins without preamble, in mid-conversation. The second is a brisk Allegretto scherzando over ticking wind chords. The third goes backwards in history to a real minuet, with horns and clarinet in the trio. The finale is enormous by comparison with everything before it, a fast rondo repeatedly derailed by one loud stray note that finally forces the music into a distant key.","Written in the summer and autumn of 1812 in Linz and the Bohemian spas, the same months as the unsent letter to the woman known only as the Immortal Beloved. Premiered on 27 February 1814 in Vienna on a programme with the Seventh, which the audience preferred. Beethoven, asked why the newer symphony had made less impression, is reported to have said that it was because the Eighth is so much better.","The loud intruding note in the finale is the key to the movement: it turns up in the first minute as an unexplained interruption, and near the end the whole orchestra follows it into a foreign key before hauling itself back. The coda then runs on for minutes, insisting on the home chord long past the joke. The ticking second movement has often been tied to Maelzel's metronome, but the canon that supports the story is probably a later forgery.","Nineteenth-century writers treated it as a lightweight beside its neighbours and it was played less often than any of the odd-numbered symphonies. Twentieth-century conductors from Toscanini onward made a case for its density: more notes per minute, and stranger harmony, than the Seventh. The minuet's tempo divides performers between courtly and brisk.","Listen to the finale first, and count the appearances of the intrusive loud note; the piece is a detective story about it. Then hear the second movement and try beating time against the winds. It rewards a quick, light orchestra and suffers under a heavy one."],"beethoven-symphony-no-9":["About seventy minutes in four movements, the last with four soloists and chorus. It opens on bare open fifths, a sound with no key in it, out of which the main theme crashes down. The scherzo is driven by a timpani solo tuned to break the pattern. The Adagio alternates two slow themes in ever more decorated variations. The finale reviews and rejects the themes of the earlier movements in instrumental recitative, finds a simple tune in the low strings, and builds it into the setting of Schiller's ode.","The Philharmonic Society of London commissioned a symphony in 1817; Beethoven delivered in 1824, by then wholly deaf. The premiere on 7 May 1824 at the Kärntnertortheater was conducted by Michael Umlauf with Beethoven beside him indicating tempos, which the players were told to ignore. The contralto Caroline Unger turned him round to see the applause he could not hear. The score is dedicated to King Friedrich Wilhelm III of Prussia.","Before any voice enters, the cellos and basses play recitative: instrumental speech, complete with the phrasing of a singer, arguing with the orchestra. Beethoven drafted words to go under those notes, then removed them and gave the sense to the baritone instead. He had wanted to set Schiller since the 1790s, and the tune of the earlier Choral Fantasy is a first sketch of the same idea.","Verdi thought the first three movements superb and the finale badly written for voices, an objection singers still make about the soprano line. Wagner made the symphony the founding text of his own project. The performance history is a history of tempo: the metronome marks were long dismissed as impossible and are now widely used. The finale's melody became the anthem of the European Union in 1972.","Start with the Adagio, then the moment in the finale where the cellos and basses first give out the tune quietly, unharmonised, and it grows one instrument at a time. Hear one big modern chorus and one small period-instrument reading; the difference in the scherzo and in the Turkish march episode is startling."],"beethoven-piano-concerto-no-1":["Three movements, about thirty-seven minutes, with the largest orchestra of his early concertos: clarinets, trumpets and drums all present. The first movement marches out of a quiet military figure and runs a long time before the soloist arrives. The Largo moves to the distant key of A-flat and gives its long melody to the clarinet, with the piano decorating around it. The finale is a rondo whose tune keeps landing on the wrong beat, ending with a false stop and a quick exit.","Composed in 1795 and revised for a concert tour to Prague and Berlin in 1798, then published in 1801 after the earlier B-flat concerto, which is why the numbering is misleading. Beethoven was building a career as a travelling virtuoso, and a concerto was a calling card that he kept largely in his head, publishing only when he no longer needed to protect it from rivals.","The Largo is a wind serenade in disguise; the strings are muted for long stretches and the clarinet leads. Beethoven left three separate cadenzas for the first movement, written at different times. The last of them is far more violent than anything in the concerto around it, and pianists who play it produce a strange collision between the young composer and the older one.","Contemporaries admired the piece and Beethoven's own playing of it more than they admired the printed text. It has always stood in the shade of the last three concertos, and modern criticism tends to hear it as Mozart plus muscle. Cadenza choice is the live question: play the wild late one, or one of the smaller two.","Go to the finale and listen for the way the theme keeps starting a beat too early. Then take the Largo and follow the clarinet rather than the piano. Compare it with a Mozart C major concerto, then note how much longer Beethoven makes you wait for the soloist."],"beethoven-piano-concerto-no-2":["Twenty-nine minutes in three movements, scored for the smallest orchestra of the five: one flute, oboes, bassoons, horns and strings, with no clarinets, trumpets or drums. The first movement is neat and busy, the piano writing full of scales and broken chords. The Adagio is the surprise, a slow song that ends with the piano murmuring a hushed recitative over held strings. The finale is a rondo whose theme begins on the wrong beat and never quite recovers its footing.","Begun in Bonn in the late 1780s and rewritten repeatedly in Vienna, it is the earliest of the five, whatever the numbering says. Beethoven played it at his Vienna public debut on 29 March 1795, reportedly finishing the finale in the last two days with copyists waiting in the next room. When he sold it to the publisher Hoffmeister in 1801 he priced it low and said plainly that he did not count it among his best works.","The cadenza printed in modern editions was written around 1809, roughly fifteen years after the concerto, and belongs to a different composer's world: it breaks into stern two-part counterpoint and wanders through keys the movement never visits. Pianists who play it are gluing an old man's argument onto a young man's showpiece, and most do it anyway.","The concerto has never been more than an occasional visitor to concert programmes, usually described as Mozartian apprentice work. Recordings of the complete cycle have kept it alive, and period-instrument performances have made a stronger case for it: on a light Viennese piano the textures stop sounding thin and start sounding transparent.","Start with the Adagio and wait for the quiet recitative at the end. Then the finale, for its limping first bar. Hear it on a fortepiano if you can, next to a Mozart concerto in the same key, and the family resemblance becomes the point rather than the problem."],"beethoven-piano-concerto-no-3":["Thirty-five minutes in three movements. Strings alone announce the C minor theme in bare octaves, and the piano finally enters with three rising scales, as if forcing the door. The Largo moves to E major, about as far from C minor as tonal music goes, and opens with the piano alone playing a slow hymn. The finale is a rondo on a jabbing theme that turns to C major near the end, speeds up, and finishes with timpani and horns in the major.","Sketched from around 1800 and finished only just in time for the premiere on 5 April 1803 at the Theater an der Wien, a marathon benefit concert that also introduced the Second Symphony and the oratorio Christ on the Mount of Olives. Ignaz von Seyfried, turning pages, found the solo part largely blank, with a few marks Beethoven could read and he could not. The dedication went to Prince Louis Ferdinand of Prussia, himself a fine pianist.","The model is Mozart's C minor concerto K. 491, which Beethoven admired openly, and the resemblance is deliberate down to the key and the bare unison opening. The join between the E major Largo and the C minor finale is made by a single held note in the winds, a bridge so thin that it makes the huge harmonic distance audible in one second.","The premiere was under-rehearsed and reviews were mixed; the concerto's reputation grew steadily through the century as the first of his works in the C minor manner to become popular. Debate now centres on whether the darkness is genuine or inherited from Mozart, and on which cadenza to use: Beethoven's own is long and stormy.","Wait for the piano's first entry, three plain scales, and notice how much more force they carry than any tune would. Then listen to the Largo's opening as if it were a slow movement from a sonata. Compare it directly with Mozart's C minor concerto."],"beethoven-piano-concerto-no-4":["Thirty-four minutes in three movements. The soloist begins alone, five bars of quiet chords, and the orchestra answers in a distant key, as though it had not been listening. The Andante con moto is a dialogue in which the strings, playing gruff unison octaves, are answered by soft piano phrases until they fall silent altogether. The finale follows without a break and brings in trumpets and drums for the first time in the piece, a rondo that sounds like a rustic dance in slightly the wrong key.","Written in 1805 and 1806, the same period as the Fourth Symphony and the Razumovsky quartets. First played privately at Prince Lobkowitz's palace in March 1807, then publicly at the freezing four-hour concert of 22 December 1808, where Beethoven himself was the soloist for the last time in one of his own concertos. Dedicated to Archduke Rudolph, his pupil, patron and by then his most reliable protector.","The opening chords have exactly the rhythm of the Fifth Symphony's four-note motif, being written at the same time, but here it is quiet and consoling instead of threatening. In the slow movement the piano is instructed to play with the sustaining pedal down through the last bars, so that the strings' hostility dissolves into blur. The whole concerto avoids loud entries; the soloist's power is in restraint.","The concerto disappeared after Beethoven's death and was brought back by Mendelssohn in Leipzig in 1836. The reading of the slow movement as Orpheus taming the Furies goes back to nineteenth-century writers, not to Beethoven, and scholars now treat it as a helpful fiction. Arguments continue over the two surviving cadenzas and over how quietly the opening can be taken.","Listen to the first thirty seconds twice: a concerto that starts with the soloist alone was new, and the orchestra's reply in the wrong key is the joke. Then take the slow movement as pure theatre, following the strings and the piano as two characters. Try a fortepiano recording for the sheer quietness."],"beethoven-piano-concerto-no-5-emperor":["Forty minutes, the longest and loudest of the five. Three orchestral chords open the work, each answered by a sweep of piano runs, so that the cadenza comes first and the theme second. The Adagio un poco mosso moves to the remote key of B major and is a slow hymn with the piano weaving decoration around muted strings. It does not end: a horn holds a note, the piano feels its way down a semitone, and the rondo bursts out with the same tune it has been hinting at.","Composed in 1809 while the French army besieged and then occupied Vienna. Beethoven spent part of the bombardment in his brother's cellar with pillows over his head to protect what hearing he had left; Archduke Rudolph, the dedicatee, had fled with the court. Too deaf to perform it, Beethoven left the premiere to Friedrich Schneider in Leipzig on 28 November 1811 and to Carl Czerny in Vienna the following February.","At the head of the score Beethoven writes that no cadenza is to be improvised and that the player must go straight on. The flourishes of the opening are the cadenza, moved to the front, and the tradition of the soloist showing off at the end is abolished. Listen also to the transition into the finale: the whole gear-change is one falling semitone, played twice, quietly.","The nickname is not Beethoven's and probably comes from the London publisher Cramer; in German the work is simply the fifth concerto. Nineteenth-century pianists made it a vehicle for grandeur, and the twentieth century argued back with lighter, faster readings. Whether the piece is imperial or a defiance of empire is a debate that says more about the tellers than about the score.","Start with the join between the second and third movements, about a minute of music, and play it three times; it is one of the great pieces of musical carpentry. Then hear the opening. A big modern orchestra suits the outer movements; a period band makes the Adagio far more fragile."],"beethoven-violin-concerto":["Forty-three minutes in three movements, unusually spacious for its date. It begins with five soft strokes on the timpani, and those four repeated notes turn out to govern the whole first movement, appearing in the woodwind, under the soloist, and eventually in a foreign key where they sound like an intruder. The Larghetto is a set of variations over muted strings in which the soloist mostly decorates rather than leads. A short cadenza links into a hunting rondo in six-eight.","Written at speed in late 1806 for Franz Clement, leader of the orchestra at the Theater an der Wien, who gave the premiere on 23 December that year and reportedly played parts of it at sight. Clement's programme also included a solo item played on one string with the violin upside down, which suggests the atmosphere of the evening. The score is dedicated to Beethoven's boyhood friend Stephan von Breuning.","Beethoven left no cadenza for the violin, but he did arrange the concerto for piano, and for that version he wrote a first-movement cadenza with the timpani joining in. Violinists including Wolfgang Schneiderhan have translated it back, which produces the odd and convincing spectacle of the soloist duetting with the drums that opened the piece.","The premiere passed without much impression and the work lay unplayed for decades. Its revival dates from 1844, when the twelve-year-old Joseph Joachim performed it in London under Mendelssohn; Joachim's cadenza became standard, with Kreisler's as the popular alternative. Modern debate is about whether to use the composer's own timpani cadenza and about the very fast tempo Beethoven marked for the finale.","Count the timpani strokes in the first bars, then listen for those four notes returning through the movement, including one moment where they arrive in the wrong key and stop everything. Compare Joachim's cadenza with the piano-version one on drums. Then hear the Brahms concerto, written in the same key with this piece firmly in view."],"beethoven-triple-concerto":["Thirty-six minutes for three soloists and orchestra. The cello leads almost every entry, usually high on its top string, so the trio speaks from the top down rather than the bottom up. The first movement is very long, with the same broad theme handed round all three players and then the orchestra. The Largo in A-flat is short, a single sustained song for the cello, and it runs straight into a Rondo alla polacca, a polonaise with a swagger and a fast coda.","Written in 1803 and 1804 alongside the Eroica, published in 1807 with a dedication to Prince Lobkowitz. Archduke Rudolph, then a teenager and Beethoven's pupil, may have been the intended pianist, which would explain why the piano part is the least demanding of the three. The concertante concerto for multiple soloists was already going out of fashion, and Beethoven never wrote another.","Notice how the piano is kept out of the hardest writing while the cello gets the heroics and the highest register: the balance of difficulty is the opposite of what a piano composer would normally arrange. The polonaise finale is a nod to the Polish dances fashionable in aristocratic Vienna, and Beethoven puts the tune in the cello again, where it has to be played almost at the edge of the fingerboard.","Long dismissed as diffuse and unequal, the concerto has been rescued by star trios on record rather than by critics; the famous 1969 recording with Oistrakh, Rostropovich and Richter made it repertoire. The doubts concern the first movement's length and whether the soloists ever truly interact or merely take turns.","Start at the Largo and stay with it into the polonaise, about eight minutes in all; that stretch is the best of the piece. Follow the cello throughout rather than the piano. Compare a starry modern trio with a period-instrument group, where the gut-strung cello sits more easily in its high register."],"beethoven-fidelio":["Two acts, a little over two hours, sung in German with spoken dialogue between the numbers. Leonore disguises herself as a boy called Fidelio to enter the prison where her husband Florestan is being starved to death for political reasons. The first act mixes domestic comedy with a canon quartet in which four people sing the same melody about entirely different feelings, and ends with the prisoners let into the light. The second act is set in the dungeon and turns on an offstage trumpet call.","The libretto came from a French rescue play by Jean-Nicolas Bouilly already set by two other composers. The premiere on 20 November 1805 fell days after Napoleon's army occupied Vienna, and the audience was largely French officers. Beethoven cut and rewrote in 1806 and again in 1814, when Georg Friedrich Treitschke reworked the text; along the way he composed four different overtures, three of them called Leonore.","The trumpet call heard offstage in the dungeon is the same signal that appears in the Leonore overtures, which is why those overtures give the whole plot away before the curtain rises. In the 1814 revision Leonore's great aria was moved and its horn writing kept, so that three horns and a bassoon spend the introduction promising a rescue that the words have not yet mentioned.","The opera has always been called flawed and always kept its hold: singers complain about the writing, dramaturgs about the join between comedy and tragedy. The 1805 version is now performed and recorded under the title Leonore. Mahler's practice of inserting the Leonore No. 3 overture before the final scene is still argued over, since it repeats music the audience has just lived through.","Start with the quartet in the first act, four voices entering one by one on the same tune. Then the prisoners' chorus, then the moment the trumpet sounds. Hear one 1814 recording and one of the 1805 Leonore to see how much of the drama is in the cuts."],"beethoven-missa-solemnis":["Eighty minutes, five movements, four soloists who sing almost always as a group rather than in arias, and a chorus pushed to the top of its range for long stretches. The Gloria and the Credo each end in a huge fugue. After the Sanctus, an orchestral interlude leads to the Benedictus, where a solo violin descends from the top of its register and stays there, floating above the voices. The Agnus Dei is interrupted twice by military trumpets and drums.","Begun in 1819 for the installation of Archduke Rudolph as Archbishop of Olmütz in March 1820, and finished three years after the ceremony it was meant to adorn. Beethoven studied Palestrina, Handel and treatises on the church modes while writing it, and afterwards tried to sell manuscript copies by subscription to the courts of Europe. The first complete performance took place in St Petersburg on 7 April 1824.","Over the opening Kyrie Beethoven wrote that the music came from the heart and may it go again to the heart. The Agnus Dei carries a heading asking for peace within and without, and the trumpets and drums that break in are his own commentary on a text that says nothing about war. In the Credo he sets the words about the life of the world to come as a fugue that starts slowly and then doubles in speed, as though impatient.","The Mass is too long and too loud for any ordinary liturgy, and churches have mostly left it to the concert hall, which raises the awkward question of what it is for. Twentieth-century writers found it harder to grasp than the late quartets, and Adorno wrote a well-known essay on its strangeness. Choral directors still argue about the punishing tessitura and about how far to reduce forces.","Start with the Benedictus and follow the solo violin, not the singers. Then hear the Agnus Dei whole, so the intrusion of the drums lands as a shock. A chorus of forty with period instruments makes the counterpoint audible where a large modern choir turns it into weather."],"beethoven-diabelli-variations":["Thirty-three variations on a short, plain waltz, running about fifty-five minutes. The theme is repetitive by design, and Beethoven attacks it from every angle: a stamping march for the first variation, canons, a piece that is nothing but scales, a comic imitation of a piano lesson, three slow minor-key variations near the end that stop the momentum altogether, a full fugue, and a closing minuet that gives up the waltz for something older and quieter.","The Viennese publisher Anton Diabelli sent his waltz to fifty composers, asking each for one variation to fill a patriotic album; Schubert, Czerny and the eleven-year-old Liszt all obliged. Beethoven refused, then wrote a set of his own, working at it between 1819 and 1823 alongside the Missa solemnis and the last piano sonatas. The dedication went to Antonie Brentano.","Variation twenty-two quotes Leporello's complaint about working day and night from the opening of Don Giovanni, a joke aimed straight at the drudgery of Diabelli's tune. Listen too for how little of the theme survives: by the late variations Beethoven is working from the bare harmonic skeleton and a single turn of phrase, and the closing minuet answers the waltz by refusing to be one.","Hans von Bülow called the set a microcosm of Beethoven's art, and it has been treated ever since as the summit of variation writing, alongside Bach's Goldberg set. Schindler's story that Beethoven dismissed Diabelli's theme as a cobbler's patch comes from an unreliable witness. Pianists disagree about whether the set is one continuous span or thirty-three separate pieces.","Play the theme by itself first, twice, so you know how ordinary it is. Then jump to the three slow minor variations near the end and the fugue that follows them. Compare the closing minuet with the returning aria of the Goldberg Variations."],"beethoven-les-adieux-sonata":["Seventeen minutes in three movements with titles: The Farewell, Absence, The Return. It opens with three slow descending notes over which Beethoven wrote the syllables of Lebewohl, a horn call in the piano's middle register. The middle movement is short and unsettled, drifting through keys without cadencing properly, and runs straight into a finale that bursts in fast, the two hands tumbling over each other, then slows at the very end to remember the farewell.","In May 1809 the French bombarded Vienna and Archduke Rudolph, Beethoven's pupil and patron, left the city with the imperial family; he returned in January 1810. Beethoven dated the movements accordingly and dedicated the sonata to him. It is the only one of his thirty-two piano sonatas with a programme he supplied himself, and the events behind it are documented rather than invented by later writers.","In the coda of the first movement the two hands play the three-note farewell out of step with each other, so that the call and its echo overlap and grind against the harmony: two horns receding at different speeds. Beethoven complained to his publisher that the French title was wrong, since Les Adieux is said to an assembly while Lebewohl is said warmly to one person.","Nineteenth-century pianists loved the programme; twentieth-century ones sometimes resisted it, arguing that the sonata works as pure structure and that the titles limit it. The publisher's decision to print the French title against the composer's wishes is why the piece is still known by a name he disliked. Debate now concerns the tempo of the finale, which is very fast as marked.","Listen to the last minute of the first movement for the overlapping horn calls, then hear the join between the second and third movements without a break. Compare it with the Emperor Concerto, written in the same months, in the same key and out of the same siege."],"beethoven-hammerklavier-sonata":["Forty-five minutes, far longer than any piano sonata written before it. Four movements: an opening Allegro launched by a leap up from the bass into two hammered chords; a two-minute scherzo that parodies the opening; an Adagio sostenuto in F-sharp minor lasting nearly twenty minutes, slow enough to feel like a separate work; and a finale that begins with an aimless slow search through unrelated keys before settling into a three-voice fugue with a trill built into its subject.","Written in 1817 and 1818, years of very little finished music, when Beethoven was almost totally deaf, in poor health, and consumed by the lawsuit over guardianship of his nephew Karl. New pianos with a wider range and heavier action had arrived from London and Vienna, and he wrote for their limits. He gave it a defiantly German title, Great Sonata for the Hammerklavier, and dedicated it to Archduke Rudolph.","After sending the manuscript to his publisher Beethoven wrote again to add two notes at the start of the Adagio, a small rising interval that changes how the whole movement is heard, and pianists still discuss what those two notes do. The fugue is headed with an instruction that it is partly free, partly worked out, and it puts the theme upside down, backwards and in long notes before it is finished.","For decades it was considered unplayable; Liszt's Paris performance in 1836 was a landmark, and Schnabel's recording in 1935 established it in the repertoire. The metronome mark for the first movement is so fast that most pianists ignore it, and the resulting argument, between those who take Beethoven at his word and those who do not, is the sonata's permanent controversy.","Start with the Adagio alone and give it the twenty minutes; it is the longest slow movement he wrote for piano. Then go to the fugue and follow the first entry of the theme, with its trill, before the texture thickens. Compare a fast, driven recording with a broad one."],"beethoven-piano-sonata-op-109":["Twenty minutes in three movements of very unequal weight. The first is short and starts in mid-flow, a rippling figure that keeps being interrupted by slow, freely shaped passages, as though two pieces were sharing a movement. The second is a brief, furious Prestissimo in E minor. The third is a hymn-like theme marked to be played with the most intimate feeling, followed by six variations that grow steadily further from it, and then the theme returns exactly as it began.","Written in 1820 while the Missa solemnis was occupying most of his time, and published by Schlesinger in Berlin as the first of the last three sonatas. The dedication went to Maximiliane Brentano, the daughter of Antonie and Franz Brentano, who had known Beethoven since she was a small child; he had written her a short trio movement years before.","Nothing in the first movement is announced: it begins as if already under way and ends before it has settled. In the variations, the fifth is a strict piece of fugal writing and the sixth builds a low held note under trills stacked higher and higher until the sound becomes a haze with no clear melody in it. Out of that haze the theme comes back unchanged, and the sonata's whole point is that it is unchanged.","Nineteenth-century critics found the late sonatas formless; the variation finale was the exception they praised. Modern writers treat the sonata as the moment when Beethoven's late manner settles, with the argument centring on whether the first two movements are a substantial pair or an extended introduction to the finale.","Go straight to the last movement, hear the theme, then wait for its return twenty minutes of variation later and notice what has changed in you rather than in the notes. Then start again from the beginning. Compare pianists on the trills near the end: some blur them deliberately, some keep every note."],"beethoven-piano-sonata-op-110":["Twenty minutes in three movements that run almost without a break. The first is a singing Moderato marked to be played with much feeling, its theme spun out over gentle broken chords. The second is a short, blunt fast movement built on two Viennese street songs. The third is the strange one: a recitative, then a slow lament over pulsing chords, then a calm three-voice fugue, then the lament again marked exhausted, then the fugue upside down, gathering speed until it ends in a blaze of broken chords.","Written in 1821, when Beethoven was ill with jaundice and working simultaneously on the Missa solemnis; it is the middle sonata of the last three, published in Paris and Berlin. His domestic life was dominated by the guardianship of his nephew, and his output had shrunk to a handful of works a year. He signed the manuscript with the date on Christmas Day.","The tunes in the fast second movement are folk songs, one of them a comic ditty about a cat having kittens, and Beethoven puts this street humour immediately before the lament. When the lament returns it is written with the chords broken into gasps and the instruction that the singer is losing strength; the inverted fugue that answers it starts slower, then doubles and doubles again in speed as if being revived.","The sonata was once thought the most approachable of the last three and is now recognised as the most theatrical: an aria, a collapse and a recovery staged on a keyboard. Debate concerns how literally to take the exhaustion markings, and whether the ending is triumph or simply the machinery of the fugue reaching its end.","Listen to the third movement whole, without stopping, about eleven minutes. Follow the lament, the fugue, then the lament's return and the fugue turned upside down. Then compare it with the Arioso-like slow music in Bach's passions, which Beethoven certainly knew."],"beethoven-piano-sonata-op-111":["Twenty-seven minutes in two movements and nothing more. A stern slow introduction with a jagged falling leap leads to a compressed C minor Allegro that behaves like a fugue in a hurry and ends abruptly. Then an Arietta in C major, a plain tune in slow triple time, followed by variations that cut the beat into ever smaller parts until the music seems to swing, then thin out into long trills high in the instrument, over which fragments of the theme drift back.","Completed in 1822, his last piano sonata, written between the Missa solemnis and the Diabelli Variations and dedicated to Archduke Rudolph. Beethoven had been ill and was largely cut off from society by deafness. When his publisher queried the two-movement design and asked whether a finale had gone missing in the post, the question was passed on and, according to Schindler, Beethoven answered that he had not had time to write one.","The third variation's off-beat, jerking rhythm has been described as anticipating jazz for over a century; what it actually is, is the beat halved and halved again until the ear can no longer hold the original pulse. Once the trills arrive the pulse dissolves completely, and the return of the theme in the last page is played over a shimmer rather than an accompaniment.","Thomas Mann built a chapter of Doctor Faustus around a lecture on why there is no third movement, which fixed the sonata in the general imagination as a farewell to the form. Musicologists point out that two-movement sonatas were not unheard of. Pianists divide over the swing of the third variation: play it straight or lean into the syncopation.","Take the first movement's storm, then let the Arietta begin without a gap; the contrast is the piece. Follow the variations by counting how many notes fill one beat each time round. Compare two very different recordings, one fast and dry, one slow and pedalled."],"beethoven-string-quartet-op-59-no-1-razumovsky":["Forty minutes, symphonic in scale, in four movements. The cello begins the melody low down over a pulsing accompaniment and the music runs for a long time before it reaches a proper cadence. The second movement is a scherzo whose first sound is a single note tapped repeatedly on the cello, a rhythm rather than a tune. Third comes a long F minor Adagio marked mournful, and it leads without a break into a finale built on a Russian folk song.","Commissioned in 1806 by Count Andreas Razumovsky, the Russian ambassador in Vienna, a competent amateur violinist who kept the Schuppanzigh Quartet on his household staff and played second violin with them. Beethoven wove Russian tunes taken from a published folk-song collection into two of the three quartets as a courtesy to his patron. He had not written a quartet since the six of Op. 18, six years earlier.","The Russian theme in the finale is marked as such in the score and is by origin a slow song, which Beethoven forces to run at speed; near the end he slows it back to something like its proper tempo and its real character surfaces for a few bars. Listen also for the opening movement's repeat, which he cancelled, so the exposition never comes round again as the ear expects.","The players at the first readings reportedly thought Beethoven was joking; the violinist Radicati is said to have told him so and to have been told in reply that the music was not for him but for a later age. Reviewers found the set long and difficult. It is now the standard dividing line between the Classical quartet and everything after.","Listen to the first two minutes and notice that the music never lands; that suspension is the innovation. Then take the slow movement and stay with it into the finale, which arrives out of a single violin line. A group with real weight in the cello suits it best."],"beethoven-string-quartet-op-95-serioso":["The shortest of his quartets, twenty-one minutes for four movements, and the most compressed music he wrote. It begins with a snarling unison figure, and within seconds the argument has been stated and cut off. The second movement starts with a descending cello scale and grows a gentle fugato; the third is a fast, grim movement he marked serioso; and the finale, after a slow introduction, races to a coda that suddenly turns bright and fast in F major and vanishes.","Written in the autumn of 1810 in a matter of weeks, between the Egmont music and the Archduke Trio, and not published until 1816. The dedication went to Nikolaus Zmeskall, a court official, amateur cellist and one of Beethoven's oldest Viennese friends. Beethoven wrote of the piece that it was made for a small circle of connoisseurs and was never to be played in public.","The quartet keeps stopping. Whole transitions that any other composer would compose out are replaced by a silence and a jump to a new key, and the first movement is over in about four minutes. The finale's coda has divided listeners for two centuries: the sudden fast major-key music can be heard as mockery, as relief, or as a joke about the convention that a piece must end well.","Nineteenth-century audiences found it forbidding and it was played far less than the Razumovsky set. Twentieth-century critics reclassified it as the first of the late quartets in everything but date, pointing to the compression, the silences and the refusal to explain. Whether the closing bars are ironic remains genuinely unsettled.","It lasts twenty minutes, so hear it in one sitting, then play the last ninety seconds twice and decide for yourself what that ending means. Compare it with the Fifth Symphony's move from minor to major, which does the same thing over four times the span."],"beethoven-string-quartet-op-127":["Thirty-seven minutes in four movements. Six slow, solemn chords open the work like a door being pushed back, then a flowing melody begins; the chords return twice more, in different keys, and are never developed. The slow movement is a long set of variations in A-flat on a theme shared between first violin and cello. The scherzo is built from a dotted figure passed round all four players, and the finale ends by floating into a high, hushed coda in a new rhythm.","The first of three quartets commissioned by Prince Nikolai Galitzin of St Petersburg, an amateur cellist who wrote to Beethoven in 1822 asking for quartets and largely failed to pay for them. Beethoven wrote it in 1824 and 1825, immediately after finishing the Ninth Symphony, and it opened the extraordinary run of five late quartets written in the last two and a half years of his life.","The opening chords work as memory rather than material: each time they return the music simply notes them and moves on. In the slow movement the theme is not stated plainly at all; it emerges from a dialogue between violin and cello, so the first variation arrives before the listener is sure the theme has finished. One later variation moves to a remote key and slows almost to a standstill.","The premiere by the Schuppanzigh Quartet in March 1825 went badly, under-rehearsed and bewildering; the violinist Joseph Böhm re-studied the work and repeated it soon after to real success, and Beethoven attended the rehearsals, watching the bows since he could not hear. The late quartets were called incomprehensible for a century and are now the summit of the chamber repertoire.","Start with the slow movement and let the theme find itself. Then go back for the opening chords and count how often they come back. Choose a quartet with a warm, blended sound rather than a brilliant one; this piece rewards patience over projection."],"beethoven-string-quartet-op-130":["Six movements instead of four, about forty-five minutes. A slow introduction keeps interrupting the fast music of the first movement, so the two tempos argue throughout. Then a two-minute Presto that is gone before it registers, a gently limping third movement, and a German dance. Fifth comes the Cavatina, an aria for the first violin marked at one point oppressed, where the melody breaks into short gasps out of step with the accompaniment. The finale exists in two versions.","The third of the Galitzin quartets, written in 1825 and early 1826 and first played by the Schuppanzigh Quartet on 21 March 1826. Its original finale was the enormous fugue now known as the Grosse Fuge, which the players struggled with and the audience did not follow. The publisher Artaria persuaded Beethoven to detach it and write a new ending; he completed the replacement in November 1826 and died the following March.","Beethoven told a friend that nothing he had written had ever moved him as the Cavatina did, and that he wept composing it. The oppressed passage puts the first violin deliberately out of alignment with the other three, so the line seems to be catching its breath. The little German dance that precedes it is in a different key from anything around it and sounds like a memory of a village band.","Everything about the quartet is contested: six movements or five, which finale, whether the light replacement ending betrays the work or completes it. Most groups now play the new finale in concert and record the fugue separately, though some restore the original order. Nineteenth-century critics called the whole thing the product of a deaf man's confusion.","Start with the Cavatina, five minutes, and listen for the moment the violin loses its footing. Then hear both finales one after the other and decide. A recording that offers the fugue as an appendix lets you make the comparison directly."],"beethoven-string-quartet-op-132":["Five movements, about forty-five minutes, arranged symmetrically around an enormous slow centre. A four-note figure creeps in on the cello before the first movement proper begins. The second is a stiff, courtly dance. The third is a hymn in an old church mode, its slow phrases alternating with faster, brighter sections. Then a short march, broken off by a violin recitative that leads straight into a driving finale in six-eight, its tune haunted and restless until it turns to the major at the very end.","Beethoven fell seriously ill in the spring of 1825 with an intestinal complaint, stopped work for a month and recovered. He headed the third movement with a note calling it a holy song of thanksgiving from a convalescent to the divinity, in the Lydian mode, and marked the faster passages as feeling new strength. It was the second of the Galitzin quartets to be written, though numbered fifteenth, and Schuppanzigh's players gave the first performance in 1825.","The mode Beethoven uses has no sharpened fourth degree, which removes the usual pull towards a home chord, so the hymn seems to hover without ever needing to arrive. The four-note cell that opens the quartet also opens Op. 130 and the Grosse Fuge: three works from the same months share a single idea. The finale's main theme was originally sketched as a possible instrumental finale for the Ninth Symphony.","Nineteenth-century writers found the third movement unaccountable and the finale weak; both judgments have reversed. Modern reception owes much to writers outside music, from Aldous Huxley, who has a character play it in Point Counter Point, to T. S. Eliot, who took the late quartets as a model for Four Quartets. Performers argue over how slowly the hymn can be taken before it stops moving.","Hear the third movement on its own, about eighteen minutes, and notice the two kinds of music alternating: the hymn in long chords and the quicker music marked as new strength. Then listen to the whole quartet so the finale's restlessness has something to push against."],"beethoven-grosse-fuge":["Sixteen minutes for four string players, and among the most violent music of the nineteenth century. A short overture states the theme four times in different disguises, slow and fast, smooth and jagged. Then a huge double fugue in which the theme leaps about while a second, hammering line saws against it in cross-rhythms. A quiet slow section in a distant key follows, then a bouncing fugue in six-eight, then fragments of everything heard before, and an abrupt close.","Written in 1825 as the finale of the B-flat quartet Op. 130. The Schuppanzigh Quartet found it barely playable and the first audience found it unintelligible, so the publisher Artaria asked for a replacement, which Beethoven supplied. The fugue was issued separately in 1827 as Op. 133 with a dedication to Archduke Rudolph, and Beethoven also made an arrangement of it for piano duet, published as Op. 134.","At the head of the score Beethoven writes that the fugue is partly free and partly worked out, which is both a description and a warning. The opening presents one idea in four costumes, and everything that follows is that idea seen from another angle. Its first interval is the same four-note cell that opens Op. 130 and the A minor quartet, so three works of the same months grow from one seed.","An 1826 Vienna review called it incomprehensible, like Chinese. It was ignored for a century, then adopted by twentieth-century composers as a founding document: Stravinsky described it as a piece that will be contemporary forever. Conductors from Furtwängler to Bernstein have played it with string orchestra, which purists reject as smoothing away the strain that is the point.","Play the first forty seconds twice before going on, so the four disguises of the theme are in your ear. Then let the whole thing run without trying to follow the counterpoint. Afterwards, hear the light replacement finale of Op. 130 and consider what the quartet lost and gained."],"beethoven-archduke-trio":["Forty minutes in four movements, the broadest piano trio anyone had written. The piano alone announces a wide, calm opening theme and the strings take it up. The scherzo begins with the cello walking up a scale, and its trio starts with a creeping chromatic line before turning into a waltz. Third is a set of variations on a hymn-like theme in D major, and these run without a break into a rondo finale that suddenly speeds up at the end and dances out.","Written in about three weeks in March 1811 and dedicated to Archduke Rudolph, Beethoven's pupil, patron and the recipient of more dedications than anyone else. The first public performance came on 11 April 1814 with Beethoven at the piano, Schuppanzigh on violin and Joseph Linke on cello. It was one of his last appearances as a pianist; Spohr, present at a rehearsal, described the playing as rough and the loud passages as a mess of noise.","Listen to how the variations are laid out: the theme is given by the piano with the strings answering, and each variation hands the material down through the group, with one placing the cello deep in its bass register while the piano decorates far above. The link into the finale is deliberately awkward, the new theme arriving in a key that has to be corrected within a few bars.","The trio has never lacked admirers and has one of the most storied recorded histories in chamber music, from the 1928 Cortot, Thibaud and Casals version onwards. Discussion concerns balance more than interpretation: modern pianos can easily bury the strings in a work conceived for a lighter instrument, which is one argument for period-instrument performances.","Start with the slow variations and stay through the join into the finale, which arrives without warning. Then hear the opening theme again and notice how little Beethoven does to it. Compare a classic modern trio with a fortepiano recording to hear how the balance changes."],"schubert-die-schone-mullerin":["Twenty songs to poems by Wilhelm Müller, running about an hour without a break in the story. A young miller follows a brook downstream, takes work at a mill, loves the miller's daughter, loses her to a hunter in a green coat, and gives himself to the water. The piano is the brook: rippling figures that barely stop from the opening walking song in B flat to the closing lullaby in E major, which the water itself sings over the drowned boy.","Schubert was twenty-six and had just fallen seriously ill; he spent part of 1823 in Vienna's general hospital, and the disease diagnosed that year shadowed the five years he had left. He found Müller's poems in a friend's rooms, dropped the poet's mocking prologue and epilogue, and set twenty of the remaining verses. Songs with piano were the staple entertainment of middle-class Viennese evenings, sold in cheap instalments for players of modest skill.","The brook is a character, not scenery. In the last two songs it answers the boy directly, and Schubert marks the change by taking the rippling right hand away and replacing it with slow rocking chords. Follow the colour green as well: hope first, then the hunter's coat, then something the boy cannot look at, the same word turning cold as the music stays sweet.","Published in five instalments from 1824, the songs sold steadily but were long treated as agreeable domestic fare; the baritone Julius Stockhausen's complete public performances from the 1850s established the cycle as a concert work. Argument now runs on voice type, since Schubert's friend Vogl sang it but baritones have owned it on record, and on whether Müller's irony survives Schubert's straight face.","Start with the walking song that opens it, then jump to the boy's dialogue with the brook near the end, then the final lullaby. Keep a translation beside you; the words carry the plot and the music assumes you know it. Compare a light tenor such as Fritz Wunderlich with a baritone such as Fischer-Dieskau: the same story told by a boy or remembered by a man."],"schubert-winterreise":["Twenty-four songs, about seventy minutes, again to Müller. A man leaves a town at night after a love affair has ended and walks into the snow; there is no plot after that, only what he passes and imagines: a weathervane, a frozen river, a linden tree, a crow that follows him, a marsh light, a dream of spring, and at the end a barefoot hurdy-gurdy player turning his crank for nobody. The piano keeps a tread going almost throughout.","Schubert found twelve of the poems in an almanac early in 1827 and set them as a complete work; when Müller's full twenty-four appeared he had to add the rest as a second part rather than reshuffle the order. He was in his last year, correcting the proofs of the second half while dying in 1828. Vienna wanted cheerful songs, and friends remembered being unsettled when he sang these through to them.","The hurdy-gurdy at the end is two bare notes held under a tune that keeps stopping: the accompanist becomes a beggar's instrument, and the singer's last words ask to go with him. Elsewhere listen for the posthorn in the song about the mail coach, a bright fanfare that means no letter has come, and for how often the walking rhythm continues under music that has stopped going anywhere.","Haslinger published the two halves in 1828 and early critics found them monotonous and morbid; the twentieth century made the cycle the summit of the repertoire and a favourite of staged and filmed adaptations. Live arguments remain: Schubert wrote for a high voice and most singers transpose down, period fortepianos change the balance completely, and nobody agrees whether the traveller is dying, unhinged, or simply walking.","Begin with the linden tree and the dream of spring, the two songs that remember warmth, then hear the whole thing in one sitting, late, with the text. Notice how rarely the piano lets the pulse stop. Compare Fischer-Dieskau with Hans Hotter for weight, a tenor such as Peter Pears with Britten for lightness, or a fortepiano recording for the sound Schubert's friends heard."],"schubert-schwanengesang":["Fourteen songs of the last year, about fifty minutes: seven to poems by Ludwig Rellstab, six by Heinrich Heine, one by Johann Gabriel Seidl. It is a collection, not a journey, and the two halves sound like different composers. The Rellstab group holds the famous serenade and a love song under rustling leaves; the Heine group is stark, built from bare octaves, held chords and long silences, and contains the blackest music Schubert wrote.","Schubert composed these in the summer and autumn of 1828 and died in November at thirty-one. His brother Ferdinand sold the manuscripts to the Viennese publisher Tobias Haslinger, who issued them the next year under a title of his own devising, added the Seidl song at the end to finish on a smile, and thereby invented a work Schubert never assembled. The Rellstab poems had perhaps reached him from Beethoven's papers.","Heine's six poems come from one section of his Die Heimkehr, and Schubert reordered them; put them back in the poet's sequence and a narrative appears that Haslinger's edition hides. Notice too how the Heine settings avoid melody: the voice often circles three or four notes while the piano supplies the shock, the opposite of the singable Rellstab songs printed alongside them.","Because neither the title nor the order is Schubert's, performers treat the set as negotiable: some sing only the Heine songs, some restore Heine's sequence, many drop the cheerful pigeon-post finale. Critics from Brahms onward heard the Heine group as pointing decades ahead, and the collection's split personality still divides opinion between an accidental anthology and a deliberate late statement.","Take the Heine songs first, especially the doppelgänger and the seaside one, then hear the serenade afterwards and feel the gap. Read Heine's poems in order before listening. Recordings differ enormously in what they include, so check the track list; Fischer-Dieskau, Hotter and Matthias Goerne all make different arguments about what the set is."],"schubert-der-doppelganger":["Four minutes for voice and piano, and almost nothing happens in it. The piano states four slow bare chords and then repeats them, nearly unchanged, under the entire song, while the voice half-speaks a line that circles three or four notes. A man stands at night before the house where his lost love lived and sees someone else standing there wringing his hands: himself. One loud phrase at that recognition, then everything sinks back down.","Heine's poem comes from Die Heimkehr, published in 1826, and its flat, bitter tone was a new thing in German verse; Vienna's censors kept a nervous eye on him. Schubert set it in the last months of his life, in a city where the fashionable song was still a strophic tune with a rocking accompaniment. He gave the poem no title of its own; the familiar one was supplied by the publisher.","The repeating four-chord pattern is a Baroque device, the kind of ground bass used for laments a century before Schubert, dressed here in modern harmony. Its notes barely move; the whole song is built from a figure a beginner could play. At the climax one note is bent upward, and that single alteration carries the entire shock. The final chord arrives without the cadence the ear has been waiting for.","Nineteenth-century singers largely avoided it, and Haslinger buried it inside a mixed collection. Later composers did not: Liszt transcribed it, and the song is routinely cited as a step toward the harmonic freedom of Wagner and the songs of Wolf. Recent scholarship reads it alongside Heine's irony and asks whether Schubert took the poem at face value or heard the joke in it.","Play it twice: once following the words, once watching only the piano's four chords and counting how many times they come round. Then hear the serenade from the same collection immediately after, to feel how far apart these two Schuberts stand. Hotter, Fischer-Dieskau and Goerne take it at very different speeds; the slower it goes, the more it stops sounding like a song at all."],"schubert-der-tod-und-das-madchen-song":["Two and a half minutes containing a whole scene. The piano begins alone with a slow, low chorale in D minor, like a procession heard from indoors. The girl enters in short, breathless, rising phrases, begging to be passed by. Death answers on almost a single repeated note, quiet and unhurried, offering sleep rather than punishment. The chorale returns to close, but in the major, which is either comfort or the coldest possible irony.","Matthias Claudius wrote the poem in 1775, in a tradition of dance-of-death woodcuts in which a skeleton leads away people of every age and rank. Schubert set it in February 1817, at twenty, when he was teaching in his father's schoolroom in the Vienna suburbs and writing songs at a rate of several a week. It circulated in manuscript among his circle before publication in 1821.","Death barely sings: his line hovers around one note, the shape of Latin chant rather than of a tune, so that the girl's frantic melody and his monotone belong to different musical worlds. Note also that Schubert kept the piano's opening chorale for himself. Seven years later it became the theme of the variations in his D minor quartet, which took the song's name and left the singer behind.","The song was long treated as a curiosity beside the quartet that borrowed it, and only in the twentieth century did recital programmes restore its priority. Performance argument concerns the two voices: whether one singer should darken and slow for Death, or keep a single tone and let Schubert's writing do the work. Editors also debate the tempo, since the surviving markings are unusually slow.","Listen for the moment the piano stops and Death begins, and count how few notes he uses. Then play the slow movement of the D minor quartet straight afterwards to hear the same chorale stripped of words. Baritones and mezzos both claim the song; a low voice makes the dialogue eerier, a high one makes the girl's terror sharper."],"schubert-die-forelle":["Two minutes of pure delight. The piano flicks a little six-note figure that darts and turns like a fish in clear water, and the voice sings two verses of the same cheerful tune above it. In the third verse the angler muddies the stream and lands his catch; the music darkens, the darting figure vanishes, and the watcher is left indignant on the bank. Then the fish figure returns, unrepentant, to finish.","The poem is by Christian Friedrich Daniel Schubart, a writer and musician imprisoned for a decade by the Duke of Württemberg for insolence, who meant the trout's fate as a lesson about the powerful. Schubert set it in 1817 and rewrote it at least four times, adjusting the piano introduction; one copy he sent to a friend carries an apology for an inkblot made when he fell asleep over it.","Schubert dropped Schubart's moralising final stanza, which warns young women about men with rods and lines, so the song reads as a fishing scene rather than a fable. The famous leaping introduction is not in the earliest version either: he added it later, which means the piano figure everyone knows as the trout was an afterthought about a poem he had already set.","The song's fame owes as much to the piano quintet of 1819, where it became the theme of a variation movement, as to recitals; some critics grumble that the quintet has swallowed the original. Debate about which of the versions to sing continues, since Schubert left several and none is obviously final, and singers differ on whether to point the third verse or let it darken by itself.","Listen once for the piano alone, tracking the fish figure until it disappears at the catch. Then hear the fourth movement of the Trout Quintet and notice how long Schubert withholds that same rippling figure before letting it out again. Elly Ameling and Fischer-Dieskau are contrasting classics; a fortepiano makes the darting figure sound sharper and less plush."],"schubert-an-die-musik":["Two verses to the same music, under three minutes. The piano's left hand walks in steady even notes from the first bar and never really stops, while the right hand supplies quiet chords; over this the voice sings one long arching phrase that rises, hangs, and comes down. The words thank music itself for carrying the singer out of a grey hour into a better world. After the second verse the piano answers alone.","The poem is by Franz von Schober, the well-connected, unreliable friend in whose lodgings Schubert lived at various points and who supplied him with texts, contacts and trouble. Schubert set it in March 1817, in the middle of the most productive songwriting years of his life, for the private evenings his circle called Schubertiads, where he accompanied and Vogl sang, in flats with a piano and thirty guests.","The walking bass is the whole design: it is the same steady tread under both verses, and because the song is strictly strophic every difference between them must come from the singer. Listen to the piano's closing bars, where the instrument repeats the voice's arching phrase by itself, as if the art being thanked were replying, and hear how the harmony leans away from home just before it settles.","The song became the standard encore and memorial piece of German-speaking musical life, sung at funerals, farewells and the closing of concert halls, which has made it hard to hear freshly and easy to over-sweeten. Critics have argued that its sincerity is exactly what makes it fragile: the barest performance works, the affectionate one collapses. Its strophic plainness also made it a favourite teaching example.","Listen twice through and compare what the singer does with the identical music of the two verses; that is the whole performance. Keep an eye on the left hand's walk. Then hear it in an orchestral arrangement to feel what is lost. Elisabeth Schwarzkopf, Fischer-Dieskau and Janet Baker all recorded it; the best versions do almost nothing."],"schubert-der-hirt-auf-dem-felsen":["Eleven minutes in three linked sections for an unusual trio. A shepherd sings from a height and the clarinet answers him like an echo across the valley, the two lines chasing and overlapping over a simple piano accompaniment. The middle section turns to the minor for loneliness and distance from the beloved. Then spring arrives and the piece takes off into a fast, high, brilliant finish in which voice and clarinet run together.","Written in October 1828, within weeks of Schubert's death, for the soprano Anna Milder-Hauptmann, who had been Beethoven's first Leonore and had asked him for something showy that would let her display a wide range and long lines. The obbligato aria with a solo wind instrument was a Viennese operatic tradition; Schubert transplanted it to the recital room. Milder sang it in 1830 and it was published the same year.","The text is stitched together from more than one poet: most of it is Wilhelm Müller, the poet of both song cycles, but the closing spring stanzas come from elsewhere, probably from Helmina von Chézy, who wrote the play Rosamunde. The clarinet's echo effects are literal, repeating the singer's phrase a beat later and slightly transformed, so that the mountain answers with the shepherd's own words.","Its length and difficulty kept it out of ordinary recitals for a long time, and it lived mainly as a display piece for high sopranos with a friendly clarinettist. Recent performances have taken it more seriously as Schubert's last completed vocal work and as a summary of the Müller settings. Debate concerns whether the extrovert finale sits oddly with everything else, or is precisely the point.","Follow the echo game in the first section, watching which phrases the clarinet copies exactly and which it alters. Then wait for the switch into the spring music and notice how the accompaniment changes gear. Compare a bright coloratura account with a warmer lyric one, and listen for whether the clarinettist plays as an equal partner or as an accompanist; the best recordings sound like a conversation."],"schubert-du-bist-die-ruh":["Four minutes that hardly move. The piano lays down an unhurried, even accompaniment and keeps it going almost unchanged; the voice enters low and quiet and stays quiet, addressing the beloved as peace itself, as a place to come home to. Twice near the end the singer climbs to a high note and holds it at the softest possible dynamic, the same phrase repeated a step higher, and then the music simply lets go and subsides.","Friedrich Rückert, an orientalist and poet who supplied texts to composers for a century afterwards, published the verses among his love poems; Schubert set four Rückert poems in 1823, the year of his hospital stay and of Die schöne Müllerin. This was the moment when the Viennese song market was expanding fast, and quiet, short, technically modest pieces sold better than dramatic ballads.","The harmony barely leaves home, and there is almost no incident in the accompaniment; every effect depends on that repeated rising phrase, which Schubert marks to be sung as softly as possible at exactly its highest and most exposed point. It is one of the hardest quiet notes in the repertoire, and a singer who leans on it destroys the song. The second attempt goes higher still.","Long a favourite of teachers and audition panels precisely because it hides nothing, the song has attracted a mild critical suspicion of being too easy on the ear. Performers divide over tempo: taken slowly it becomes an act of stillness, taken at a walking pace it stays a love song. Rückert's popularity with later composers, Mahler included, has also coloured how the poem is read.","Wait for the two high notes and judge the singer entirely on them. Before that, listen to how little the piano changes, and how much difference a single altered chord makes when it comes. Set it beside Nacht und Träume for a pair of studies in near-motionless music. Schwarzkopf, Ameling and Barbara Bonney offer very different degrees of stillness."],"schubert-nacht-und-traume":["Under four minutes, marked very slow and very soft throughout. The piano murmurs a continuous cushion of quiet chords with no melody of its own; the voice enters low, moves in long unhurried steps, and never rises to anything that could be called a climax. Night comes down, dreams float in, and the singer asks them to come back when day drives them away. The song ends more quietly than it began.","The poem is by Matthäus von Collin, a Viennese professor and dramatist who was a cousin of one of Schubert's closest friends and who gave him several texts. The song was written around 1822 or 1823 and published in 1825. Its world is the Biedermeier interior: shuttered windows, a candle, a piano in a small room, and music made for a dozen listeners rather than a hall.","Everything happens in the harmony under an almost immobile vocal line. At the words welcoming the sweet dreams the music slides to a remote key and back again by moving one note at a time, the same sleight of hand Schubert used in the late sonatas. The breath required by the long phrases is famously punishing, which is the point: the singer must sound as if breathing were not an issue.","Nineteenth-century critics said little about a song that refuses to do anything; the twentieth century made it a touchstone. Samuel Beckett borrowed both the title and the music for a wordless television play in 1982, which brought the song a second audience entirely. Performers argue about tempo, since Schubert's marking is slower than most singers can sustain, and about whether to break the longest phrases.","Listen in a quiet room at low volume and notice how the piano's chords change colour while the voice stays put. Try to hear where the harmony leaves home and where it returns. Pair it with Du bist die Ruh, then with Beckett's short film if you can find it. Slow, steady singers such as Hotter, Schwarzkopf or Goerne suit it better than expressive ones."],"schubert-symphony-5":["Four movements in under half an hour, scored for the smallest orchestra Schubert ever used: one flute, no clarinets, no trumpets, no drums. It opens with four bars of descending strings, a curtain rather than an introduction, and then a tune that skips. The slow movement in E flat sings; the minuet is a hard-driven piece in G minor; the finale runs lightly and quickly, more like chamber music enlarged than a symphonic conclusion.","Written in the autumn of 1816, when Schubert was nineteen, teaching in his father's school and desperate to get out of it. It was intended for the amateur orchestra that had grown out of a family string quartet and met in the house of Otto Hatwig, a Vienna violinist; the modest scoring probably reflects the players available rather than any aesthetic programme. Vienna's professional concert life was closed to a nineteen-year-old schoolmaster.","The G minor minuet leans hard on the minuet of Mozart's G minor symphony, in the same key and with the same off-balance stresses, and the trio then answers it with a country tune, exactly Mozart's own contrast. Schubert had written in his diary that year about Mozart's music as a consolation. The absence of trumpets and drums also means the tuttis have no floor, giving the whole symphony its lightness.","The symphony waited until 1841 for a public performance and 1885 for publication, by which time Schubert's reputation rested on the songs and the two late symphonies. It has since become the most played of the early six, though critics regularly file it as charming apprentice work in Mozart's shadow. Period-instrument performances have made the case that its transparency is a design, not a limitation.","Start with the finale for sheer speed, then go back to the opening four bars and hear how little ceremony there is. Listen for the missing weight: no trumpets or timpani anywhere. Compare a big modern orchestra with a chamber band such as the Orchestra of the Age of Enlightenment, then play Mozart's G minor symphony and hear where the minuet came from."],"schubert-symphony-9-great":["Nearly an hour in four movements. A pair of horns state a bare tune alone before the orchestra has agreed what key it is in; that call returns, transformed, to crown the first movement. The second is a walking march in A minor with a plaintive oboe tune and a devastating stop near its centre. The scherzo is a full-length symphonic movement in its own right, and the finale drives forward on hammered repeated notes for a quarter of an hour.","Composed in 1825 and 1826, mostly after a walking summer in upper Austria. Schubert offered it to the Vienna Gesellschaft der Musikfreunde, whose orchestra found it too long and too hard and shelved it; he never heard a note of it played. Schumann visited Ferdinand Schubert in Vienna on New Year's Day 1839, found the manuscript among the family papers, and sent it to Mendelssohn, who conducted the premiere in Leipzig that March.","The finale's second theme is built on four repeated notes that fall in the shape of the Ode to Joy, a nod to Beethoven, who had died while Schubert was writing. Listen too for the trombones, which Schubert uses to sing rather than to make noise, quietly announcing themes in the first movement, an idea orchestras had not previously credited the instrument with.","Schumann's review coined the phrase heavenly length, meant as praise. Orchestras disagreed: the Paris players laughed at the finale in rehearsal in 1842, London's Philharmonic refused it in 1844, and cuts were routine into the twentieth century. Dating has moved too, from the 1828 once read off the autograph to the 1825 to 1826 now accepted, which dissolves the old mystery of a lost Gastein symphony.","Listen for the horn call at the very start and then again when it comes back in the brass at full strength. In the second movement wait for the sudden silence after the climax, one of the longest and most unnerving in the repertoire. Compare a big-orchestra reading such as Furtwängler or Solti with a period band; the faster, lighter ones make the finale's repeated notes dance instead of hammer."],"schubert-rosamunde":["Incidental music for a play, usually heard as a suite of about forty minutes: an overture, three entr'actes to cover scene changes, two ballet numbers, a shepherds' chorus with wind band, a romance for low female voice and a chorus of spirits. The best-known number is the third entr'acte in B flat, a long unhurried tune passed round the strings, and the second ballet piece, a lilting dance that most listeners recognise instantly.","Helmina von Chézy's romantic drama about a shipwrecked princess of Cyprus opened at the Theater an der Wien in December 1823 and closed after two nights; the spoken text is largely lost. Schubert had a few days to write the music and reused an existing overture. He was twenty-six, recovering from serious illness, and still trying to make his name in the theatre, which he never managed to do.","The overture played in concerts is not the one heard at those two performances: it belongs to Schubert's earlier fairy-tale opera Die Zauberharfe and acquired the Rosamunde name by publishing accident. And the great B flat entr'acte tune had two further lives, as the theme of the slow movement of his A minor quartet and as the subject of the variations in the second set of impromptus.","The play was ridiculed and the score survived it: parts of the music were rediscovered in a Vienna cupboard in 1867 by George Grove and Arthur Sullivan on a hunting trip for Schubert manuscripts. Critics have often treated the suite as pleasant filler, while performers point out how much of the late chamber music it seeded. Which numbers belong in a concert suite is still decided ad hoc.","Start with the B flat entr'acte, then the ballet music, then go straight to the A minor quartet's slow movement and hear the same tune moved indoors. Note how thin and simple the theatre scoring is compared with the symphonies, since it had to be learnt fast. Any of the standard suite recordings will do; look for one that includes the choruses."],"schubert-string-quintet-c":["Four movements, about fifty minutes, for a string quartet with a second cello instead of the usual second viola. The first movement opens with one held chord that swells and dies before anything moves, and its second theme is a duet for the two cellos. The Adagio in E major holds a slow, barely moving web of sound for minutes on end, then erupts in the middle. A hunting scherzo with a strange slow trio, and a finale with a limping Hungarian tilt.","Written in the late summer of 1828, weeks before Schubert died at thirty-one in his brother's flat in the Vienna suburbs. He mentioned it in a letter to a publisher, who was not interested. There is no record of a performance in his lifetime; the first documented one came in 1850 and publication followed in 1853, a quarter-century after his death, when the whole late repertoire was being excavated.","The second cello is the whole idea. Mozart and Beethoven added a second viola to the quartet, thickening the middle; Schubert added depth instead, freeing the first cello to sing as a tenor voice above a real bass. In the Adagio, note that the outer sections and the violent central storm use the same accompanying pattern, plucked and pulsing, so the eruption is the calm turned inside out.","Unknown for decades, the quintet is now routinely named as the summit of chamber music, and its Adagio appears in surveys of listeners' desert-island choices. Scholars have questioned the tidy story that Schubert never heard it, since his circle read new works privately. Performance arguments concern the first-movement repeat, the Adagio's tempo, and how much portamento a nineteenth-century sound requires.","Give the slow movement your full attention first, timing how long the middle section takes to arrive and how completely it changes the same material. Then go back to the opening chord and hear how Schubert refuses to start. Compare the Casals or Stern recordings with a modern quartet plus guest cellist, and try a gut-string account for a leaner, stranger sound."],"schubert-death-and-the-maiden-quartet":["Four movements, forty minutes, all four in minor keys, which almost no quartet before it had risked. The first movement is built from a hammered triplet figure announced in the opening bar and never really abandoned. The second is a set of variations on the piano chorale from Schubert's 1817 song of the same name. Then a snapping, off-balance scherzo, and a finale that gallops in a fast six-beat dance for eight or nine minutes without letting up.","Composed in March 1824, in a wretched year: Schubert wrote to a friend that he felt himself the most unhappy creature in the world, that his health would never be right and that each night he hoped not to wake. He was also, that spring, writing the Octet and the A minor quartet. This one was read privately in 1826, rejected by a publisher, and printed only in 1831, three years after his death.","The song's vocal line never appears. What Schubert varies is the piano's chorale, the music of Death's approach rather than either character's words, so the quartet is haunted by an accompaniment. Notice too that the variations end in the major, the one moment of consolation in the whole work, and that the finale immediately takes it away with a dance that is usually described as a tarantella.","An early private run-through reportedly went badly enough that Schubert put the piece aside. Its later fame was such that Mahler arranged it for full string orchestra, a version still played and still argued about. Modern debate centres on the finale's tempo, which is nearly unplayable at the marking, and on how far the biographical reading, illness and premonition, should govern performance.","Hear the 1817 song first, then the second movement, so you know what is being varied and can catch what is missing. In the finale, count how rarely the rhythm stops. Compare a hard-driven modern quartet with a warmer older one such as the Busch; then try Mahler's string-orchestra version to hear what mass does to the argument."],"schubert-trout-quintet":["Five movements, about forty minutes, for an odd and delightful line-up: the usual second violin is replaced by a double bass, which anchors the bottom and lets the pianist play in bright octaves high up instead of supplying the bass. The fourth movement is six variations on Schubert's own song about a trout; the rest is holiday music, wide-open tunes, a scherzo that stamps, and a finale with a Hungarian swing to it.","Written in the summer of 1819 during a walking holiday in Steyr in upper Austria with the singer Johann Michael Vogl, in country Schubert loved. The commission came from Sylvester Paumgartner, a wealthy mine manager and keen amateur cellist, who asked for variations on the trout song and for the same unusual instrumentation as a popular quintet by Hummel. The piece was published in 1829, after Schubert's death.","Because the double bass covers the low register, the piano writes almost no left-hand bass at all: listen for how often both of the pianist's hands are above middle C, which gives the whole quintet its glitter. In the variations, Schubert withholds the song's famous rippling piano figure through five of them, and only in the last does the fish reappear, exactly as in the song.","The quintet has always been popular and has often been patronised for it, filed as sunny holiday music by critics looking for depth. Its instrumentation remained a curiosity with almost no successors. Performance questions are practical: balancing a modern concert grand against a single string of each kind is hard, and many groups now prefer a fortepiano, which lets the strings breathe.","Start with the variations and follow the trout tune from instrument to instrument, waiting for the piano's darting figure to return. Then listen to the whole for the double bass, audible in a way it never is in an orchestra. The Curzon and Amadeus recording is a classic; a fortepiano version, such as those by period groups, changes the balance completely and is worth hearing next."],"schubert-piano-trio-2":["Four movements and three quarters of an hour. The opening is a unison call from all three players. The slow movement is the famous one: a walking march in C minor, the cello singing a bare tune over the piano's tread, cold and processional. A canonic scherzo follows, and then a finale of extraordinary length that interrupts itself twice to bring back the march theme from the slow movement, unchanged, as if remembering it.","Written in November 1827, the year Beethoven died and Schubert walked in his funeral procession. It was played at the only public concert devoted to Schubert's own music, on 26 March 1828 in Vienna, eight months before his death, and was one of the very few large works published in his lifetime, by Probst in Leipzig. His letters about it are unusually businesslike, chasing proofs and worrying about the opus number.","The march tune probably came from a Swedish song, Se solen sjunker, which Schubert heard sung in Vienna in 1827; a friend recorded his fascination with it. Note also that he cut roughly a hundred bars from the enormous finale for publication, and that most modern performances restore them, so two quite different endings circulate under the same catalogue number.","Schumann reviewed the trio and heard it as the active, dramatic counterpart to the B flat trio's sunniness, and it has kept that reputation. The slow movement's second life began when Kubrick used it throughout Barry Lyndon in 1975, which made it one of the most recognised pieces of Schubert outside the concert hall. The cut-or-uncut finale remains the main performing decision.","Go to the slow movement first and listen to the piano's tread, which never varies, under the cello. Then hear the finale and wait for the march's two returns; that is the point of the whole work. Compare Beaux Arts Trio brilliance with a fortepiano group, and check the timing to see whether the recording takes the cut or the full text."],"schubert-piano-trio-1":["Four movements, a little under forty minutes, and the friendliest large piece of Schubert's last years. It opens with a broad tune stated by violin and cello together over rolling piano. The slow movement hands the cello a long singing melody above a rocking accompaniment, then gives it to the violin. The scherzo is light and quick, with a waltzing trio, and the rondo finale limps along on a dotted rhythm that keeps tripping over itself.","Almost certainly written in 1827, in the same burst as the E flat trio, though no dated autograph survives to prove which came first. Schubert was thirty, at the height of his powers and still without a secure income, living between friends' lodgings in Vienna. The trio was not published until 1836, eight years after his death, by Diabelli, who was steadily bringing out the manuscripts the family and friends still held.","The dating is genuinely open: scholars have placed the trio before and after its E flat companion, and the case rests on paper types and handwriting rather than documents. In the finale, listen for the main tune returning in a different metre near the end, the same melody re-barred so that it dances rather than limps; it is the sort of trick Schubert usually saved for variations.","Schumann's 1836 notice is the one everybody quotes: one glance at the trio and the troubles of human existence disappear and the world is fresh and bright again. That reading, of the B flat as the sunny trio and the E flat as the dark one, has stuck for nearly two centuries and arguably flattens both. Performance debate is mostly about repeats and about balancing a modern piano against gut strings.","Start with the slow movement and follow the cello tune until the violin takes it over. Then listen to the finale for the moment the limping theme straightens out. Play the E flat trio's funeral march immediately afterwards for the contrast Schumann heard. The Beaux Arts, Istomin-Stern-Rose and period-instrument recordings offer three quite different weights of sound."],"schubert-string-quartet-13-rosamunde":["Four movements, about thirty-five minutes. The first begins with the second violin murmuring and the cello and viola marking a soft pulse before the first violin enters with a sighing tune, so the accompaniment exists before the melody does. The slow movement takes the long B flat entr'acte tune from the Rosamunde theatre music. Then a minuet that quotes one of Schubert's own songs, and a finale that turns to the major with a Hungarian lilt but never sounds carefree.","Written early in 1824 and the only string quartet Schubert saw published, as Op. 29, with a dedication to Ignaz Schuppanzigh, whose quartet had premiered most of Beethoven's and who played this one in Vienna in March 1824. Schubert had just told a friend he intended to work his way toward the grand symphony through quartets. He was ill, broke and, on the evidence of his letters, wretched.","The minuet quotes his own setting of Schiller's Die Götter Griechenlands at the words asking where the beautiful world has gone, so the movement carries a hidden question mark for anyone who knows the song. And the opening murmur, which sounds like scene-setting, turns out to be the true subject: it keeps going under everything and is what the movement is finally left with.","Reviewers in 1824 praised its melancholy and its finish, and a story that Schuppanzigh advised Schubert to stick to songs is probably later invention. For a century it stood in the shadow of the D minor and G major quartets as the gentle one; performances since the 1970s have found more strain in it. Its many self-quotations now attract as much comment as the music itself.","Listen to the first fifteen seconds twice, before the tune arrives, so that you hear the accompaniment as the real material. Then compare the slow movement with the Rosamunde entr'acte in its orchestral dress. Quartets such as the Alban Berg, Takács and Quatuor Mosaïques take it at very different temperatures; the gut-string versions make the murmuring sound like breathing."],"schubert-octet":["Six movements and a full hour for a mixed band of three wind and five strings, the sound of an outdoor serenade brought indoors. A slow introduction leads to a bounding allegro; then a long clarinet-led slow movement, a scherzo with hunting horns, a set of variations on a tune from one of Schubert's own stage works, a minuet, and a finale that begins with a dark tremolo in the low strings before shaking it off and racing to the end.","Commissioned in early 1824 by Count Ferdinand Troyer, chief steward to Beethoven's patron Archduke Rudolph and a capable amateur clarinettist, who asked explicitly for something like Beethoven's Septet. Schubert wrote it in a few weeks in February and March, alongside the D minor quartet, and it was first played privately at Troyer's. A public performance followed in 1827; publication waited until 1853.","The debt to Beethoven's Septet is structural, not vague: same six-movement plan, same slow introduction, same variation movement and minuet in the same places, with one extra violin added. Against that, the finale's opening is pure Schubert, a menacing tremolo that has nothing to do with serenade music and that returns near the end, briefly, before being dismissed.","Troyer's guests got what they asked for, and the Octet has been treated ever since as the most companionable of Schubert's large works, which has kept it slightly below the quartets and quintet in critical esteem. Recent writing takes the finale's dark introduction more seriously as a crack in the entertainment. Ensembles argue about the number of repeats, since taking them all pushes the piece past an hour.","Listen to the variations movement first for the tune passing round the eight players, then to the finale's opening tremolo and its return. Notice how often the clarinet leads, since it was written for the man paying. The classic Vienna Octet recordings are warm and unhurried; a period-instrument group makes the wind sound rougher and the balance more like an outdoor band."],"schubert-arpeggione-sonata":["Three movements, about twenty-five minutes, written for an instrument nobody plays any more: a six-string bowed guitar with frets, held like a cello. The first movement gives the soloist a long melancholy tune over a rocking piano; the short slow movement is a single lyrical span leading straight into the finale, a rondo with a lilting main theme. The melodic line sits unusually high and leaps unusually wide, and that is the giveaway.","The arpeggione was built in Vienna in 1823 by the guitar-maker Johann Georg Stauffer and had disappeared within about a decade. Schubert wrote the sonata in November 1824, probably for Vincenz Schuster, the instrument's chief advocate, who published a tutor for it. There is no record of a public performance, and the sonata was not printed until 1871, by which time cellists had to adopt it or lose it.","The passages that feel awkward and stretched on a cello lie easily under the fingers of a fretted six-string instrument tuned like a guitar, which is why the piece has a reputation for being ungrateful. Hearing it on a modern reconstruction reveals a lighter, more guitar-like tone, closer to the piano's weight, and explains why Schubert wrote so high and so wide without worrying.","Written for a dead end, the sonata survives entirely by adoption: cellists, violists, flautists, double bassists and guitarists all play it, and each transcription argues about where to put the line. Critics have called it lightweight beside the 1824 quartets, and defenders reply that its modesty is deliberate, a display piece for an amateur novelty. A handful of arpeggione recordings now exist for comparison.","Hear a cello version first, for the familiar warmth, then a viola one, which sits closer to the original range, then, if you can find it, a period arpeggione. Follow the first movement's opening tune and note how far it leaps. Rostropovich and Britten made the classic cello recording; it is worth setting beside a lighter, quicker modern reading."],"schubert-piano-sonata-d958":["Four movements, about half an hour, the most openly dramatic of the last three sonatas. It opens with a stark rising figure played in bare octaves, a challenge rather than a theme. The slow movement in A flat starts as a hymn and is repeatedly derailed by lurches into distant keys. The minuet keeps stopping in the middle of its phrases, as if losing the thread, and the finale is a galloping six-beat ride that runs for ten minutes.","One of three sonatas written in a single burst in September 1828, two months before Schubert died. He offered them to the publisher Probst in Leipzig, who wanted songs instead; they appeared in 1839 from Diabelli with a dedication to Schumann that Schubert had nothing to do with. Vienna's piano culture was dominated by variation sets and dances, and long serious sonatas were not what sold.","The opening figure is close to the theme of Beethoven's 32 Variations in C minor, a piece every Viennese pianist knew, and Beethoven had died eighteen months earlier; the whole sonata argues with him in his own key. The finale is a tarantella, the dance once believed to cure a spider's bite by exhaustion, and it behaves accordingly, refusing to stop until it has run itself into the ground.","The three last sonatas were dismissed for a century as diffuse and unpianistic, and Schumann himself thought them under-composed. Artur Schnabel's advocacy in the 1930s, and later Richter, Kempff and Brendel, reversed that judgment completely. The C minor is still the least played of the three, and critics divide over whether its Beethovenian manner is homage, competition, or a mask.","Start with the finale for the sheer stamina of it, then go back and listen to the minuet for the places where the music simply stops mid-sentence. Play Beethoven's 32 Variations before the first movement to hear the model. Kempff is warm, Brendel argumentative, Richter slow and severe; the differences here are larger than in almost any other repertoire."],"schubert-piano-sonata-d959":["Four movements, close to forty minutes. It opens with grand marching chords and then dissolves them into a quiet falling figure. The second movement is the strange one: a bare, limping walking song in F sharp minor that suddenly tears itself apart in the middle, wandering through unrelated keys with hammered chords and near-silence, before returning as though nothing had happened. A light scherzo follows, then a long, singing rondo finale.","Written in September 1828 alongside its two companions, while Schubert was living in his brother Ferdinand's damp new flat in the Vienna suburbs and taking counterpoint lessons, at thirty-one, to fill gaps he thought he had. He was already unwell; he died on 19 November. The three sonatas were sketched in continuous drafts first, then written out fair, and the sketches survive to show how much he changed.","The finale's flowing main theme is borrowed from Schubert's own A minor sonata of 1817, where it served as a slow movement; he transplanted it, sped it up and built a twelve-minute rondo on it. Listen also to the very last page, which brings back the marching chords of the opening movement, so the sonata closes by answering its own first bar.","The central breakdown of the slow movement has attracted more interpretation than almost anything else in Schubert: hearings range from madness and hallucination to the effect of illness, and most scholars now resist the biographical reading while admitting nothing prepares the listener for it. Like its companions the sonata was neglected until the twentieth century, and it is now the most performed of the three.","Go straight to the slow movement and wait for the point where the walking song loses control; time how long the disturbance lasts. Then listen to the finale and, if you can, to the 1817 sonata it borrows from. Brendel, Uchida, Lupu and Schiff differ most in how violent they let the middle section become; a fortepiano makes it sound genuinely broken."],"schubert-piano-sonata-d960":["Four movements, about forty minutes, and the calmest music Schubert wrote. The first movement's broad opening tune is interrupted within a few bars by a low trill from the bottom of the keyboard, a rumble from somewhere under the floor that keeps returning to redirect the argument. The slow movement rocks between two chords in C sharp minor over a wide, empty gap in the middle of the keyboard. A weightless scherzo, then a finale that keeps starting on the wrong note.","The last of the three September 1828 sonatas, finished on 26 September, seven weeks before Schubert's death. No publisher wanted them; Diabelli printed them in 1839. Schubert's own instrument was a Viennese fortepiano with a lighter action, shorter sustain and clearly different registers, so the low trill and the empty middle of the slow movement were sharper effects than a modern concert grand makes them.","If the first-movement repeat is taken, nine extra bars appear that exist nowhere else in the piece, and they contain the low trill's loudest and most disruptive moment. Omit the repeat and that music is simply never heard. Listen also to the finale's opening: it starts on a note outside the key, and Schubert spends the whole movement pretending that was intentional.","The repeat is the sharpest argument in the piano literature: Richter took it and stretched the movement past twenty minutes, others cut it as padding, and the nine unique bars are the reason it matters. Long dismissed as shapeless, the sonata is now routinely called Schubert's greatest work for the instrument, and its slow tempo, silences and sudden loud interruptions read as modern rather than unfinished.","Listen for the low trill in the first minute, then track every return; it is the hinge of the movement. Check whether your recording takes the repeat, and hear one that does and one that does not. Richter is famously slow, Brendel and Uchida more flowing, Andreas Staier and other fortepianists reveal how much the registers differ on the piano Schubert knew."],"schubert-wanderer-fantasy":["Twenty-two minutes in four sections played without a break: a hammering fast opening, a set of slow variations, a scherzo, and a fugal finale that returns to the opening material. Everything grows from one short rhythm, long-short-short, heard in the very first bar and audible in every section afterwards. The writing is thick, loud and technically brutal, with octaves and leaps that ask for more hands than a pianist has.","Written in November 1822 for Emanuel von Liebenberg, a wealthy amateur who had studied with Hummel and could pay for a dedication, in the same weeks that Schubert abandoned the Unfinished Symphony. He is reported to have broken down while playing the finale and said that the devil could play it. It was published quickly, in 1823, being exactly the kind of showpiece Viennese publishers wanted.","The slow section is a set of variations on Schubert's own 1816 song Der Wanderer, specifically the phrase where the wanderer says that happiness is always where he is not; the rhythm of those words is the cell from which all four sections are built. Once you have heard the song, the fast outer music stops sounding like display and starts sounding like a man being chased by a sentence.","Contemporary reviewers found it brilliant and hard; later critics have argued about whether the fugue's stiffness betrays Schubert's discomfort with counterpoint. Its afterlife is more important than its reception: Liszt arranged it for piano and orchestra in 1851 and took its design, four movements fused into one continuous piece on a single motif, as a template for his own B minor sonata and beyond.","Hear the song Der Wanderer first, just for the phrase about happiness, then listen for that rhythm in each of the four sections. Try Liszt's piano-and-orchestra version afterwards to hear what he thought the piece wanted to be. Pollini, Brendel and Richter make it sound like three different works; a fortepiano makes the thick textures far clearer."],"schubert-impromptus-d899":["Four separate pieces of six to eight minutes each. The first, in C minor, opens with a single loud unaccompanied note and builds a march that keeps changing its mind about whether it is grim or consoling. The second is a stream of running triplets in E flat with a stamping, angry middle section. The third floats a long singing melody in G flat over a rippling accompaniment, and the fourth cascades downward in showers of notes before finding a warm hymn underneath.","Written in 1827, between the two song cycles, when Schubert had finally acquired a publisher's interest in short piano pieces. Haslinger printed the first two in 1827 under the title impromptu, which was a marketing word of the moment, probably supplied by the publisher rather than the composer. The last two waited until 1857. Vienna's expanding class of amateur pianists wanted pieces of exactly this length and difficulty.","The third impromptu was first printed in G major with the note values halved, to spare amateurs the six flats and the unfamiliar look of Schubert's original; the true text was restored only much later. Notice too that the first impromptu keeps the same marching rhythm under every transformation, so a piece that sounds like a series of moods is really one idea, relit.","Schumann argued that the four make a disguised sonata, and the case has been debated ever since on the evidence of their keys and the first piece's shape. Critics have sometimes treated the set as salon music, and pianists have answered by playing them complete rather than as encores. Editions still differ on the third, and on how much of Schubert's pedalling to follow.","Play the third first for its long melody, then the first for its argument, and only then hear all four in order to test Schumann's sonata theory. Listen for how rarely the accompaniment patterns change within each piece. Radu Lupu is the classic warm account, Brendel the analytic one, and a fortepiano makes the cascading fourth sound like water rather than glitter."],"schubert-impromptus-d935":["The second set, longer and more ambitious than the first. The opening F minor piece runs to eleven minutes and has a long, becalmed central episode that sounds like a different work. The second, in A flat, is a slow processional with a rocking trio. The third is a set of five variations in B flat on the entr'acte tune from Rosamunde, and the last is a fast, hard-driven F minor dance with a Hungarian accent and a violent ending.","Written in December 1827, the year of Beethoven's death and of Winterreise, and offered to publishers who were not interested in anything long. They appeared in 1839, eleven years after Schubert died, from Diabelli, with a dedication to Liszt that the publisher added to help sales. By then Liszt was the most famous pianist in Europe and Schubert a name being rediscovered.","The B flat variations use the same Rosamunde tune Schubert had already put into the A minor quartet, so one throwaway theatre melody serves three separate works. Schumann's suspicion that this set is a broken-up sonata is stronger here than for the first: the keys line up, the opening piece has a full sonata shape, and the finale behaves exactly like a sonata finale.","Long treated as the weaker of the two sets because it is less immediately charming, the D. 935 group has gained ground with pianists who value the scale of the first and last pieces. The sonata question remains open and probably unanswerable. Editorial debate concerns Diabelli's tempo markings, which are not Schubert's, and the extent of his corrections to the text.","Begin with the variations, since you may already know the tune from Rosamunde, then take the first piece whole and listen for the moment its middle section stops moving. Finish with the F minor finale for the shock of the ending. Compare Lupu's warmth with Schnabel or Brendel, and hear at least one fortepiano recording for the drier sound."],"schubert-moments-musicaux":["Six short pieces, two to six minutes each, gathered into a set. The third, in F minor, is the famous one: a scampering tune over a hopping bass, over in under two minutes. The first is a wary little march, the second a slow rocking piece that darkens twice, the fourth a running study in perpetual motion, the fifth a stamping dance. The sixth, in A flat, is a slow processional that keeps sliding into unrelated keys and back.","Composed over several years and assembled by the Viennese publisher Leidesdorf in 1828 under a French title, since French words sold sheet music. Two of the pieces had already appeared separately in fashionable almanacs, the annual gift books that carried poems, engravings and a page or two of music for the parlour. Schubert wrote them for players of moderate ability, which is why so many pianists meet him here first.","The F minor piece was published on its own as a Russian air, a marketing label with no connection to Russia. The sixth is the one to watch: its middle section moves through remote keys by shifting a single note at a time, the same procedure Schubert used in the last sonatas, so a modest domestic piece contains the harmonic method of his final year.","Because they are short and playable, the moments musicaux were long filed as domestic miniatures, and the third became a teaching and encore staple detached from its companions. Twentieth-century writers, Alfred Brendel among them, made the case for the sixth as one of Schubert's profoundest pages. The set's compilation by a publisher means its order carries no authority.","Play the third for the pleasure of it, then go straight to the sixth and listen for the middle section where the harmony starts drifting; the distance between the two is the point of the set. Compare a modern piano with a fortepiano, which makes the short pieces sound sharper and less cushioned. Schnabel, Lupu and Brendel are the reference recordings."],"schubert-mass-6-e-flat":["A full setting of the Latin ordinary lasting close to an hour, in which the chorus does nearly all the work and the soloists step forward only briefly. The Sanctus opens with three hammered chords that slide into unrelated keys, three shocks in twenty seconds. The Credo's centre is a slow, hushed setting of the incarnation for two tenors and soprano over pulsing strings, and two large fugues close the Gloria and the Credo.","Written in the summer of 1828, at the same time as the String Quintet and the last sonatas, probably for the parish church of Holy Trinity in the Alsergrund district, where Schubert's brother Ferdinand was choirmaster. Schubert did not live to hear it: the first performance took place there in the autumn of 1829, a year after his death, conducted by Ferdinand. He had written masses since he was seventeen.","In all six of his masses Schubert cuts the same words from the Credo, the clause professing one holy catholic and apostolic church. The omission is consistent enough to be deliberate, and arguments about what it means, doubt, anticlericalism, or simply an inherited local text, have run for a century. Listen also to the Sanctus, where three consecutive chords stand in keys with almost nothing in common.","The mass sat unpublished until 1865 and outside the repertoire far longer, partly because its choral writing is punishingly high and long. It is now the most performed of Schubert's sacred works, and scholarship treats it alongside the quintet as late Schubert rather than as church routine. Debate continues over the missing Credo clause and over whether to restore it in performance.","Start with the Sanctus for the three opening chords, then the incarnation section of the Credo for its stillness, then a Gloria fugue for the sheer strain on the choir. Compare it with Beethoven's Missa solemnis, written a few years earlier in the same city. Look for a recording with a real chorus rather than a small consort; the writing needs numbers."],"schubert-mass-5-a-flat":["A setting of the ordinary lasting about fifty minutes, gentler and more lyrical than its successor. The Kyrie is soft throughout, with the chorus entering quietly over a rocking accompaniment rather than in supplication. The Gloria breaks into brilliance and ends with a large fugue; the Credo is built over a repeating pulsing figure that continues almost unbroken; the Benedictus gives the soloists their most operatic writing, and the Agnus Dei ends in unusual calm.","Schubert began it in 1819 and worked on it until 1822, unusually long for a composer who normally wrote a mass in weeks. He hoped it might secure him a court appointment and submitted it toward one, without success; he never held a salaried musical post. He revised the closing fugue of the Gloria in 1826, which means the score exists in two forms and editors must choose.","The same Credo clause about the one holy catholic church is missing here as in all his masses. Listen for the setting of the incarnation, where Schubert leaves the home key entirely for a remote one and the orchestra thins to almost nothing, a device he would push much further six years later. The long gestation also shows: the fugues are worked far harder than in the early masses.","Overshadowed by the E flat mass and by the early, more singable G major setting, this one was published in 1875 and remains the least often heard of the mature masses. Critics have praised its lyricism and complained that it is short on liturgical weight, which may be exactly what Schubert intended. The two versions of the Gloria fugue give conductors a genuine choice.","Listen to the Kyrie first for its unexpected quietness, then to the Credo's incarnation section for the moment the music moves somewhere else entirely. Follow with the E flat mass to hear the same ideas taken further. Recordings are fewer than for the other masses; look for one that states which version of the Gloria fugue it uses."],"chopin-etudes-op-10":["Twelve pieces, each built on a single technical problem and each a complete piece of music: continuous right-hand arpeggios spanning a tenth in the opening C major number, a lament threaded through busy inner voices in the E major third, black keys only in the fifth, left-hand storms under a held melody in the last. About half an hour when played complete. Nothing here is a mere exercise; the difficulty is always the expressive idea, and the hand is stretched and twisted into shapes no earlier composer required.","Chopin drafted most of them between the ages of nineteen and twenty-two, in Warsaw and then in Vienna and Paris, and published the set in 1833 with a dedication to Franz Liszt, who was already playing them. The piano had lately acquired a wider range, a heavier action and a double-escapement mechanism allowing fast repetition. Collections of studies were a publisher's staple, sold to amateurs by the thousand; Chopin turned a teaching genre into concert repertoire almost single-handed.","Listen for how often the melody is buried inside the hand rather than laid on top of it. In the E major study the tune sits under a thicket of moving parts; in the sixth, in E flat minor, the singing line is passed between fingers already occupied with the accompaniment. Chopin's own fingerings, printed in the first editions, deliberately break the old rule against putting the thumb on black keys.","Early German critics found the harmonies rough and the demands unreasonable; Ludwig Rellstab notoriously suggested that anyone attempting them should keep a surgeon nearby. Within a generation the same pieces were the entrance requirement for a concert career, and every later collection of studies has measured itself against them. Modern argument turns on tempo, on whether the fast numbers should sound effortful or weightless, and on which of the three simultaneous first editions carries Chopin's last thoughts.","Hear them as a set rather than a lucky dip: take the first three in order and notice how a study in the widest possible stretch, then one in awkward finger-crossing, then a slow song cover three worlds in nine minutes. Compare Maurizio Pollini's cool precision with Alfred Cortot's freer, more spoken account, which is inexact and often more interesting."],"chopin-etudes-op-25":["Twelve more studies, published four years after the first set and generally warmer in tone. They open with a shimmer of broken chords under a melody picked out by the weakest finger, and close with a C minor piece in which both hands sweep the keyboard in vast arcs. In between: parallel thirds at speed, a study devoted to the left hand's leaping octaves, and the A minor eleventh, where a bare four-bar tune is swallowed by a gale of chromatic figuration.","Chopin assembled the set in Paris in the mid-1830s, at the height of his teaching practice; the dedication went to Marie d'Agoult, the countess who had left her husband for Liszt. He was by then earning most of his income from lessons at twenty francs an hour and playing almost exclusively in private salons rather than public halls. The pieces distil what he told pupils about touch, hand position and the impossibility of forcing tone.","Several of these are studies in the ear rather than the fingers. The first is marked to be played with the lightness of an Aeolian harp: the tune consists of notes the pianist must lean on inside a ripple that never stops. In the seventh the left hand plays a long cello-like song while the right accompanies, so the usual roles of the hands are exactly reversed and the pianist must think upside down.","Schumann heard Chopin play the first before publication and described it as a poem rather than a study, which is where the Aeolian harp nickname comes from. Nineteenth-century pianists cut and reordered the set freely; performing all twelve in sequence is a twentieth-century habit. Argument continues over the last two, whose familiar titles were added by publishers rather than the composer, and over how literally to take the printed metronome marks.","Start with the seventh, the slow C sharp minor duet, so you meet Chopin the melodist before Chopin the athlete; then the third and the ninth, both under two minutes and both funny. Save the last three for one sitting. Look for a recording with real dynamic range, since the eleventh only works if the pianist lets the tune almost vanish."],"chopin-piano-concerto-no-1":["Three movements, about forty minutes. A long orchestral introduction lays out both themes before the piano enters and takes them over, and from then on the orchestra rarely does more than hold a cushion of chords under the soloist. The slow movement, marked Romanze and in E major, is a nocturne with muted strings; the finale is a rondo in the rhythm of the krakowiak, a Polish dance in a bouncing two beats, launched at the end by a horn call.","Chopin was twenty and about to leave Warsaw for good. He gave the premiere on 11 October 1830 at the National Theatre; three weeks later he crossed the border, and the November uprising against Russian rule broke out while he was on the road. He never saw Poland again. This concerto was written after the F minor but published first, which is why it carries the lower number.","The dedication is to Friedrich Kalkbrenner, the Paris virtuoso with whom Chopin briefly considered studying on arrival. Listen to the join into the slow movement: the strings sink by step into E major and the piano begins almost alone, as if the hall had emptied. The orchestral parts have been rewritten by many hands since, Karl Klindworth and Carl Tausig among them, so what you hear is often not quite Chopin's scoring.","The orchestration has been the standing complaint for nearly two centuries: thin, doubling the piano, harmonically slow. Defenders point out that Chopin was writing for the concerto-with-accompaniment convention of Hummel and Field rather than Beethoven's symphonic model, and that a light band under the soloist is a virtue in a small room. Chamber-sized accompaniments and period instruments have gained ground on record.","The Romanze is the way in; four minutes of it will tell you whether the rest is for you. Then go back and listen to the piano's first entry after the long orchestral opening, which is designed as an arrival. Compare a big modern orchestra with a period band and an 1830s Pleyel, on which the famous balance problem largely disappears."],"chopin-piano-concerto-no-2":["Written first and published second. The opening movement is dark and restless, with the piano decorating its themes in long chains of ornament that sound improvised on the spot. The Larghetto in A flat is the heart of the work: an aria interrupted by a recitative, over which the strings shudder while the piano declaims in free rhythm. The finale is a mazurka rondo, and near the close the strings tap their strings with the wood of the bow while a horn signals the end.","Chopin composed it in Warsaw at nineteen and played the premiere on 17 March 1830 at the National Theatre, the first of his own public concerts there. He wrote to his friend Tytus Woyciechowski that he had found his ideal, someone he had not dared speak to in six months; she was Konstancja Gładkowska, a singing student at the conservatory. The Italian opera performed in Warsaw, Rossini above all, is audible in every ornamented line.","Of the slow movement Chopin told Tytus that it was not meant to be loud, more a romance, calm and melancholy, giving the impression of gazing tenderly at a place that calls up a thousand dear memories. Listen for the passage where the accompaniment begins to tremble and the piano speaks in broken phrases with no steady pulse: opera recitative moved to the keyboard, and it halts the movement for nearly a minute.","Both concertos were long treated as apprentice work with awkward scoring, and Chopin never wrote another. Reassessment has come partly from performers using pianos of 1830, whose lighter tone sits inside the orchestra instead of fighting it, and partly from scholars pointing to the Warsaw concert tradition Chopin was actually writing for. The finale's Polish dance rhythm, once heard as local colour, is now taken as the point.","Go straight to the Larghetto and wait for the moment the strings start to shiver and the soloist begins to talk rather than sing. Then hear the finale for the sideways lurch of the mazurka, where the weight falls on the second or third beat. Krystian Zimerman's recording, in which he directs the orchestra himself, and any version on an instrument of the period make very different cases."],"chopin-preludes-op-28":["Twenty-four pieces, one in each major and minor key, arranged in a circle of rising fifths with each minor key following its major. They run from a dozen bars to a four-minute funeral procession, about forty minutes in all. Nothing here prepares anything: each is a single idea stated and dropped. Played complete they behave as one large work, the shortest numbers acting as connective tissue between the weightier ones, so the silences between them matter as much as the notes.","Chopin worked at them for several years and finished the set in the winter of 1838–39, in a disused Carthusian monastery at Valldemossa on Majorca, where he had gone with George Sand and her children for his health. It rained for weeks, he coughed blood, the villagers shunned the household as consumptive, and the piano he had ordered from Paris sat in customs. He sold the set to the publisher Camille Pleyel for two thousand francs.","The design is a system Bach would have recognised, but Chopin orders his keys by rising fifths rather than rising semitones, so each piece lands a step further round the circle than the last. Hear the very short numbers as hinges: the sixteen-bar A major is one mazurka phrase said twice, and its brevity is exactly what makes the storm that follows land.","Schumann called them sketches, ruins and eagle's feathers, admiring and slightly baffled; Liszt defended them as finished poems. The Majorca legend, above all George Sand's story about the fifteenth prelude and dripping water, has been doubted ever since, and Chopin disliked descriptive readings of his music. Scholars now agree most of the pieces predate the island and that only the copying and final ordering happened there.","Hear all twenty-four in order at least once; the sequence is the work. For a single door in, take the fourth, in E minor, whose left hand simply sinks by half-steps for two minutes; then the twentieth, thirteen bars of chords that both Rachmaninoff and Busoni later used as a theme. Martha Argerich for speed, Grigory Sokolov for weight."],"chopin-ballade-no-2":["Eight minutes built from two things that cannot be reconciled: a gentle siciliana in F major, rocking in a slow lilt, and a furious Presto con fuoco in A minor that erupts without warning. They alternate, the quiet music returning shorter and less certain each time, until the storm wins and the piece ends in A minor, a key it did not begin in. The last page is a fragment of the opening tune, alone and stripped of its harmony.","Chopin worked on it around the Majorca winter and revised it afterwards, dedicating the published score to Robert Schumann, who had dedicated Kreisleriana to Chopin the year before. Schumann remembered hearing an earlier version that ended peacefully and reported that the composer had since altered it. The ballade as a keyboard title had no precedent; Chopin took the word from narrative poetry and invented the genre with four pieces.","The two ideas are less separate than they sound. The storm's opening figure is the siciliana's rising step compressed and hammered, and when the calm music returns for the third time it has absorbed the storm's harmony and can no longer settle. Ending in a key other than the one it started in is the crux: no sonata of the period would have permitted it, and here the story requires it.","Schumann's report that the ending was changed after he heard it is the earliest evidence of any Chopin work in revision and has fuelled arguments about how far the finished piece answers him. Nineteenth-century writers tied all four ballades to the poems of Adam Mickiewicz, on the authority of a remark Schumann attributed to Chopin; most scholars now treat the connection as loose inspiration rather than plot.","Listen twice: once for the shock of the first eruption, once for how badly the quiet music is damaged by it. Then compare the First Ballade, which builds towards its catastrophe instead of being ambushed by one. On record, Krystian Zimerman's complete set and Arturo Benedetti Michelangeli's account differ sharply over how brutal the Presto should be."],"chopin-ballade-no-3":["The sunniest of the four. It opens with a two-part conversation, a question and a soft answer, then settles into a swinging figure in a rocking six-beat pattern that carries most of the piece. The middle turns dark, a repeated note tolling in the bass under quiet chords, and builds over several minutes into a full-throated return of the melody in octaves. About eight minutes, and the only ballade to end in triumph rather than collapse.","Written in Paris and at Nohant, George Sand's country house in Berry, where Chopin spent most summers from 1839 and did much of his best work. He was thirty-one, at the height of his earning power as a teacher to the aristocracy and giving perhaps one public concert a year. The dedication is to a pupil, Pauline de Noailles. Publishers in Paris, Leipzig and London issued it simultaneously to protect the copyright in each country.","The whole piece grows from the falling-then-rising shape of the first two bars. It returns as the lilting main theme, then as the murmuring accompaniment of the dark central section, then thundering in octaves at the climax, each time in a different rhythm. Note also that the great return is of the second theme rather than the first, which slips back only in the final bars, almost as an afterthought.","Contemporaries liked this one best of the four; Schumann noticed at once that it differed in form and character from its predecessors, and it became a salon favourite in a way the F minor never did. Later critics have sometimes held its charm against it. Performance argument concerns the central build, which can be paced as one slow crescendo of several minutes or broken into episodes, and the rhythmic freedom Chopin's pupils described in his own playing.","Follow one shape through the whole piece: the small rise and fall of the opening two bars. Once you hear it inside the accompaniment of the dark middle section you will hear it everywhere. Then notice how long Chopin makes you wait for the big return. Rubinstein for singing line, Zimerman for architecture; the same eight minutes feel quite different."],"chopin-ballade-no-4":["Twelve minutes, the longest and most tightly argued of the four. A short introduction that seems to be feeling its way gives out to a waltz-like tune in F minor which returns five times, never twice the same: once in strict canon, once buried under filigree, once whispered as a chorale. Five soft chords stop everything near the end, and then a coda of pure fury runs the piece into the ground in under a minute.","Composed at Nohant in the summer of 1842 and dedicated to Baroness Charlotte de Rothschild, whose family salon had opened Paris society to Chopin a decade earlier. He was thirty-two, increasingly ill and working slowly, filling sketchbooks with rejected versions. Around it he wrote the A flat Polonaise and the Scherzo in E, and the three were published together in the autumn of 1843, an unusually strong run for a composer who published little.","The variations on the main tune are the piece's real subject, and one of them is a strict canon: the left hand repeats the melody a bar behind the right, a piece of Bach-derived craft hidden inside a Romantic reverie. The five quiet chords before the coda function like a door closing. They belong to no theme, occur once, and every pianist has a theory about how long the silence after them should last.","Long the connoisseur's choice among the ballades and now, for many pianists, Chopin's finest single piece. Analysts point to a fusion of variation, rondo and sonata thinking with no earlier model, and to the way the coda answers material from ten minutes before. The main practical dispute is textual: the sources disagree over notes in the closing pages, and modern editions still print different readings of the most exposed bars.","Count the returns of the main tune; there are five, and hearing them as one melody redressed each time turns twelve minutes into a story. Wait for the five soft chords near the end — you will know them when they come — and hear how the coda answers. Compare a fiery reading such as Martha Argerich's with the slower, darker one Krystian Zimerman recorded."],"chopin-piano-sonata-no-2":["Four movements, about twenty-three minutes. A short hesitant opening gives way to a driving first movement; a scherzo in E flat minor batters away before opening into a slow trio. Then the funeral march, written two years before the rest, with a central section of extreme simplicity. The finale lasts eighty seconds: both hands in bare unison, quiet from start to finish, rushing in triplets with no chords and no clear tune, ending on a single abrupt loud note.","Written mostly at Nohant in the summer of 1839, after the Majorca disaster, while Chopin slowly recovered. The funeral march dates from 1837 and had already circulated. Sonatas were unfashionable in the Paris salons where he made his living; reviewers expected a virtuoso to publish variations and fantasies, not four-movement abstract works, and building one around an existing funeral march struck contemporaries as doubly perverse.","The march's famous tune is not the movement's heart; the trio in D flat, marked to be played simply, is a lullaby placed inside a burial. Some hear the march's tread still moving inside the finale's rushing unison, played too fast to see. Nothing in that last movement rises above a whisper until the final chord, and no note in it is ever harmonised.","Schumann said Chopin had bound together four of his maddest children under one roof, and complained that the finale sounded more like mockery than music. Others heard the same page as wind over graves, a phrase that has stuck to it ever since. Twentieth-century analysts have defended the work's unity, and the funeral march became public property, played at state funerals from Chopin's own onwards.","Do not start with the funeral march, which you already know; start with the finale, eighty seconds long, and let it baffle you. Then take the sonata from the beginning and hear the whole thing as a run-up to that vanishing. Seek a performance that observes the first-movement repeat, which changes the proportions, and try an 1840s Pleyel, on which the finale is stranger still."],"chopin-piano-sonata-no-3":["Four movements, about twenty-seven minutes, and the most spacious thing Chopin wrote. The first movement has a second theme of unusual breadth in D major; the scherzo is a two-minute flicker in E flat with a becalmed middle; the Largo is a wide slow movement in B major with a long central stretch of soft repeated figures. The finale is a rondo in a galloping rhythm, launched by loud chords, that gathers speed to a blazing B major close.","Composed at Nohant in the summer of 1844, months after the death of Chopin's father, and dedicated to a pupil, the Countess Émilie de Perthuis. He was thirty-four and had five years to live; his life with George Sand was starting to fray. He had been studying Bach closely, keeping the Well-Tempered Clavier on the piano, and the counterpoint of the outer movements shows it plainly.","The first movement never brings back its opening theme after the development. The recapitulation begins with the second subject instead, a shortcut that removes several minutes of expected music and that nineteenth-century critics read as a mistake. In the Largo, listen for the long passage where the melody stops altogether and the hands simply murmur in a slow rocking pattern for over two minutes — the closest Chopin came to writing music about nothing happening.","It has always been the more admired of the two mature sonatas among musicians and the less popular with audiences, having no funeral march to sell it. Nineteenth-century writers repeated the charge that Chopin could not build large forms and cited the truncated recapitulation as proof of incompetence rather than design. That reading has been reversed, and the sonata is now standard competition repertoire.","Begin with the Largo, whose opening summons and slow tune show the mature Chopin at full stretch, then the finale for sheer momentum. On a second hearing follow the first movement and notice that the tune it opened with never returns where you expect. Compare Murray Perahia's singing line with Maurizio Pollini's tighter, cooler reading."],"chopin-fantaisie-f-minor":["Thirteen minutes in one span. It begins with a slow march, grave and faintly military, that never returns; out of it grows a fast section with three linked themes that rise by thirds through a chain of keys. Halfway through, everything stops for a hushed chorale in B major, marked slow and sustained, as though a door had opened onto another room. The piece ends in A flat major, not the F minor in which it began.","Written at Nohant in the autumn of 1841, Chopin's most productive year, alongside the Third Ballade and the F sharp minor Polonaise. He was living with George Sand, financially secure, and had largely stopped performing in public. The title fantaisie freed him from a sonata's obligations while allowing something on a sonata's scale, and publishers could sell a fantasy to amateurs who would never buy an abstract four-movement work.","The three fast themes are one idea in different clothes, and each entry begins a third higher than the last, so the music climbs without your noticing. The B major chorale is recalled once more, briefly, just before the end and in another key — the only moment the two halves of the piece touch. Listen too for the triplet figure that runs underneath almost everything after the march.","Liszt singled it out in his book on Chopin. The household legend that the march depicts a quarrel and reconciliation among Sand's guests has never had documentary support, and Chopin left no programme of any kind. Critics have argued for a century about what form this is — sonata, ballade, arch, or none — and the answer usually reveals more about the analyst than the piece. The A flat ending divides opinion between triumph and evasion.","Listen for the opening march, then deliberately wait for it to come back; it will not, and noticing that is the piece. The hushed chorale about eight minutes in is the emotional centre. Compare a big rhetorical reading such as Vladimir Horowitz's with a more classical one such as Maria João Pires's: the march can sound like a funeral or a procession."],"chopin-berceuse":["Five minutes over an almost unchanging left hand: two chords rocking back and forth, repeated for nearly the entire piece. Above them the right hand plays a simple lullaby and then sixteen variations on it, each adding more notes — trills, chains of thirds, runs so fine they blur into a wash — until the melody is a haze of decoration. Then the ornament thins away and the cradle rocks to a halt on the softest chord in the piece.","Composed in 1844 and published the following year, originally under the title Variantes, which describes it better than the one it kept. Chopin was writing for a market of amateur pianists who bought sheet music by the thousand, and he could sell a five-minute piece for as much as a sonata. Childhood and the nursery were newly fashionable subjects in French salons; Schumann's Kinderszenen had appeared six years earlier.","The rocking bass barely changes across the whole piece, and the single place where the harmony does slip is more startling than any of the fireworks above it. The variations are also a study in how far a melody can be buried before it disappears: by the twelfth the tune exists mainly in your memory of the first, which is why the piece grows on repeated hearing rather than on first contact.","Nineteenth-century listeners took it for a charming trifle; twentieth-century pianists made it a test of control, since everything depends on evenness at a whisper. Debussy and Ravel both knew it well, and the technique of a fixed bass beneath an increasingly ornate surface passes directly into French piano writing. Modern critics group it with the Barcarolle among the late works whose harmony is most adventurous under the calmest surface.","Play it twice in a row. The first time follow the melody as it vanishes under ornament; the second time listen only to the left hand and hear how little it does. Then compare the Barcarolle, written two years later on a similar rocking pulse. Look for a pianist who keeps the level low throughout rather than building to a climax the piece does not contain."],"chopin-barcarolle":["Nine minutes in the swaying twelve-beat lilt of a Venetian boat song. After four introductory bars the left hand sets up a rocking accompaniment and the right sings in thirds and sixths, two voices moving together like a duet. A slower section in A major grows quieter and more inward; then the barcarolle returns in full sunlight, the melody now in massive chords, before a coda of trills and one last soft rise into the air.","One of Chopin's final large works, written in Paris and at Nohant across 1845 and 1846 as his health collapsed and his life with George Sand came apart; they separated the following year. Barcarolles were a familiar salon item — Mendelssohn had published gondola songs a decade earlier — and Chopin had never been to Venice. It was among the pieces he played at his last Paris concert in February 1848.","Listen to the two-voice writing: almost everywhere the melody is doubled a third or a sixth below, so the piece is a love duet for two singers who never separate. Charles Hallé, present at that final concert, reported that Chopin played the loud closing passage as a whisper, being too weak to do otherwise, and found it more moving that way. The long trill in the coda is the piece exhaling.","Later French composers took it as the summit of Chopin's late writing, and Fauré's barcarolles descend from it directly. Argument centres on the ending — whether to obey the printed loud markings or follow Hallé's account of the composer's own dying quietness — and on how much rhythmic freedom the gondolier's rhythm can absorb before the boat stops rocking altogether.","Find the pulse first: a slow swing underneath a decorated surface, the boat and the water. Then listen for the moment the middle section stops moving and the music turns inward. Compare the Berceuse, built on the same principle of a fixed rocking bass. Arthur Rubinstein and Dinu Lipatti recorded very different accounts, one warm, one lucid."],"chopin-polonaise-fantaisie":["Thirteen minutes that begin nowhere: rolled chords and rising arpeggios drift through unrelated keys for a full minute before a polonaise rhythm assembles itself out of the mist. The dance keeps dissolving back into reverie; a slow, remote section in B major sings a hymn-like tune; and only in the last two minutes does the polonaise arrive whole, in blazing octaves, as though it had been trying to be born throughout.","Chopin's last large work, finished in 1846 and dedicated to a Madame Veyret, otherwise unknown. He was ill, quarrelling with George Sand's children, and living through the collapse of the household at Nohant. He would leave for England and Scotland in 1848 and die a year after returning. The polonaise, a stately Polish processional dance, had been his patriotic emblem for twenty years; here it can barely walk.","The opening is a study in avoiding a key: the arpeggios outline chords that could resolve almost anywhere, and the music takes over a minute to admit it is in A flat. Notice how the polonaise rhythm starts and stops three or four times before managing a complete phrase. The B major episode is the same rhythm slowed to a crawl and stripped of its accent, so the dance survives as a memory of itself.","Liszt disliked it, finding its picture of a dying nation painful rather than moving, and nineteenth-century pianists mostly avoided it. Its reputation rose in the twentieth century, when the formal freedom and unstable harmony began to look forward rather than backward, and it is now often named as Chopin's most advanced single piece. Debate continues over whether it is a polonaise interrupted by a fantasy or a fantasy that fails to become a polonaise.","Give the first ninety seconds your full attention and try to guess the key; the disorientation is deliberate. Then wait for the polonaise's proper arrival near the end and notice how long you have been waiting for it. Compare the Heroic Polonaise, which does in eight bars what this piece cannot manage in eleven minutes. Try Krystian Zimerman or Sviatoslav Richter."],"chopin-cello-sonata":["Four movements, close to half an hour, and Chopin's only mature chamber work. The first movement is long and densely argued, the piano carrying much of the material while the cello sings across it; a short scherzo in D minor follows, then a slow movement of barely four minutes that is a single sustained duet, and a finale in a driving triple rhythm. The cello writing sits mostly in its tenor register, where the instrument sounds most like a voice.","Written for Auguste Franchomme, the cellist who had been Chopin's closest musical friend in Paris for fifteen years and who advised on the string writing. Chopin laboured over it as over nothing else, filling pages with rejected drafts while his health failed and his life with George Sand ended. The two men played the last three movements at his farewell Paris concert on 16 February 1848; he judged the opening movement too long for an audience.","The piano part is harder than the cello's, and Chopin, who never wrote an idle accompaniment, gives the keyboard the argument while the cello supplies the song. Listen to the slow movement: about thirty bars, a single melody handed between the instruments over a rocking accompaniment, and it stops before it has developed. The outer movements are unusually contrapuntal, his Bach study surfacing under the Romantic surface.","Reviewers of the 1848 concert barely mentioned it, and the sonata was long dismissed as the tired work of a dying man; the first movement in particular was called shapeless. Cellists kept it alive because there is so little else of quality from the 1840s. Recent scholarship, working through the heavy sketch material, has reframed its density as deliberate, and complete performances including the first movement are now standard.","Start with the Largo, four minutes of pure duet, then the scherzo for its abrupt good humour. Save the first movement for later; it repays a second hearing far more than a first. Listen for the balance — this needs a pianist who plays as a partner, not an accompanist. Rostropovich with Argerich, and Fournier with Kempff, take opposite views of how romantic it should sound."],"liszt-annees-pelerinage-suisse":["Nine pieces, about fifty minutes, following a traveller through Switzerland. A mountain chapel opens the book with horn calls; a lake is painted by an unbroken shimmer of soft repeated figures; a spring bubbles in high glittering runs; a storm hammers in octaves. At the centre stands Vallée d'Obermann, fourteen minutes of brooding self-examination that grows from a sinking bass line to a tremolo climax and then subsides. The set ends with the bells of Geneva.","Liszt eloped to Switzerland with Marie d'Agoult in 1835 and wrote first versions there; he revised them thoroughly in Weimar in the late 1840s, after abandoning the touring life for a post as court music director, and published the book in 1855. Each piece carries an epigraph from Byron, Schiller or Senancour. Alpine tourism was a new middle-class industry, and this is the first great travel diary written for the piano.","Vallée d'Obermann is built from one descending scale fragment, which returns as the storm, as a recitative and finally as a hymn in C major — the same handful of notes carrying four meanings. In Au bord d'une source the water is made of two hands playing slightly different patterns that never quite line up. Liszt cut most of the display writing from the 1830s versions; the later texts are simpler and considerably stranger.","The earlier Album d'un voyageur versions were criticised as showy travelogue, and the revisions answer the charge directly. For most of a century the book was played in fragments, and only Au bord d'une source held a place in recitals. Complete recordings since the 1950s, Lazar Berman's above all, made the case for the set as one journey, and Vallée d'Obermann is now treated as a central Liszt work.","Begin with Au bord d'une source, three minutes of falling water, then Vallée d'Obermann, which needs your whole attention and rewards it. Read the epigraphs printed in the score, since Liszt meant them as part of the music. Then compare Debussy's water pieces sixty years later, which begin from exactly this sound."],"liszt-annees-pelerinage-italie":["Seven pieces responding to Italian art and poetry: a Raphael painting of the Virgin's marriage, Michelangelo's brooding statue of a seated thinker, a song attributed to the painter Salvator Rosa, three sonnets of Petrarch that began life as songs for tenor, and finally Après une lecture du Dante, a seventeen-minute one-movement sonata opening with a jagged falling interval in bare octaves and working through hell to a shattering close. Fifty minutes complete.","Liszt and Marie d'Agoult moved on from Switzerland to Italy in 1837, spending time in Bellagio, Milan, Venice and Rome, and he drafted most of this music then; the revisions came around 1849 in Weimar and publication in 1858. He was reading Dante with d'Agoult and had absorbed the idea, current among French Romantics, that the arts could be translated into one another. The Petrarch settings exist both as songs and as piano pieces.","Après une lecture du Dante is built on the tritone, the interval medieval theorists called the devil in music, hammered out in octaves at the start; Liszt then turns the same interval into the gentle chorale that stands for Paradise, theological arithmetic hidden inside the fireworks. In Sposalizio the tune is picked out in the middle of the texture with figuration above and below — exactly the effect Thalberg had made famous.","Reviewers of the day complained that piano music had no business illustrating paintings, and translating poetry into notes was mocked as literary pretension. The Dante Sonata, once dismissed as vulgar, is now the most played piece in the book, and analysts treat its single-movement design, with everything grown from a few intervals, as the workshop for the B minor Sonata. The three sonnets are still argued over as songs versus piano pieces.","Take the Petrarch Sonnet No. 104 first — six minutes, and the most direct melody Liszt wrote — then the Dante Sonata, which needs volume and no interruptions. Read the sonnet text alongside the music. Compare a large-scale reading such as Lazar Berman's with a leaner one; the Dante can be played as a horror film or as an argument."],"liszt-jeux-d-eaux-villa-d-este":["Eight minutes of falling water. The piece is made almost entirely of high, fast, quiet figuration — trills, tremolos and cascading arpeggios in the top two octaves — over slow-moving harmony, with only fragments of melody surfacing through it. Twice the water gathers itself into a full-voiced hymn, and at the second of these Liszt writes a line of Latin scripture into the score. It ends with a last spray of notes and one plain soft chord.","Liszt spent his last years shuttling between Rome, Weimar and Budapest, lodging for long stretches at the Villa d'Este at Tivoli, whose Renaissance gardens are terraces of fountains. He had taken minor orders in 1865 and was widely known as the Abbé Liszt. The piece belongs to the third book of the Années, published in 1883 and made up largely of elegies for dead friends; it is the only bright thing in the collection.","The Latin sentence in the score comes from John's gospel — the water Christ gives becomes a spring welling up to eternal life — so the fountains are a religious image rather than a picture postcard. Musically it anticipates nearly everything the French would do twenty years later: harmony used as colour, whole passages that do not move, melody reduced to a glint on a moving surface.","Ignored for decades along with the rest of late Liszt, it was rediscovered when Debussy's and Ravel's water pieces sent listeners hunting for ancestors. Debussy's admiration for the old man's last music is documented, and Ravel's Jeux d'eau of 1901 starts from the same sound. The piece is now a fixture, and the third book, once dismissed as the work of a spent composer, is treated as his most radical.","Listen once for the texture alone and do not chase a tune; there barely is one. Then listen again for the two moments when the water becomes a hymn. Play Ravel's Jeux d'eau straight afterwards and the family resemblance is unmistakable. Seek a recording with a bright, clear top register: this music dies on a muddy piano."],"liszt-transcendental-etudes":["Twelve studies, over an hour complete, and among the hardest music written for the instrument. Ten carry titles: a one-minute Preludio that simply sweeps the keyboard; Paysage, a quiet pastoral; Mazeppa, a galloping ride in which the melody is played by the thumbs while both hands leap around them; Feux follets, marsh-lights flickering in double notes; Wilde Jagd, a wild hunt; Ricordanza, a faded love letter; and Chasse-neige, a snowstorm made of tremolos.","Liszt published a set of twelve exercises at fifteen, rewrote them in 1837 into a version so difficult that Schumann guessed only ten or twelve pianists alive could play it, and revised them a third time in Weimar, thinning the textures and adding the titles. The 1852 print is dedicated to Carl Czerny, his teacher. By then he had given up touring and was building a career as conductor and champion of other composers.","The 1852 revision is mostly subtraction. Liszt removed handfuls of notes, widened the spacing and made the pieces playable without making them any easier to play well; setting the 1837 Grandes études beside them shows a virtuoso editing his own excess. Chasse-neige, the last, is one long crescendo of tremolos with a tune buried inside that is progressively covered, like snow falling on a landscape until nothing is left.","Opinion split from the start between awe and disgust: Clara Schumann and the Leipzig circle heard empty display where Liszt's pupils heard poetry. The pieces were long played singly and hardly ever complete, since few pianists could sustain an hour of them. Recordings by Claudio Arrau, Lazar Berman and Boris Berezovsky made the full cycle familiar, and argument has shifted to how the descending chain of flat keys binds the set together.","Start with Ricordanza, the gentlest, then Feux follets, which is quiet and impossibly quick, then Mazeppa for the noise. Keep Chasse-neige for last. If you can find one of the specialist recordings of the 1837 version, compare it with the familiar text: hearing what Liszt cut tells you what he thought mattered."],"liszt-reminiscences-don-juan":["Seventeen minutes on three numbers from Mozart's Don Giovanni. It opens with the stone guest's music, hammered out in tremolos and rising scales, then turns to the seduction duet Là ci darem la mano, set as variations of increasing brilliance, and finishes with the Champagne Aria as a whirling toccata. The statue's music returns to darken the ending. Two hands are made to sound like a whole opera house, chorus and orchestra included.","Liszt wrote it during the touring years, when he crossed Europe giving what he was the first to call recitals: solo concerts played from memory, with the piano turned so that the audience saw his profile. Opera paraphrases were the backbone of those programmes. Most listeners knew the tunes and few had heard the operas, there were no recordings, and a pianist who could conjure an opera at the keyboard was performing a public service as well as a stunt.","The choice of numbers is an argument about the opera. Liszt frames the seduction duet with the statue at both ends, so Don Giovanni's charm is heard inside his damnation rather than before it; Mozart's ordering is the reverse. Listen for the way the grinding tremolo of the stone guest keeps threatening to break into the love music, so the variations are shadowed long before the reckoning arrives.","Paraphrases were the most despised genre of the century among serious critics and the most popular with audiences; the Leipzig party treated them as vandalism of the classics. Busoni disagreed, ranking this one at the summit of writing for the instrument. Its rehabilitation came with the twentieth-century revival of Liszt as a composer rather than a showman, and pianists now programme it as a serious work rather than an encore.","Hear Mozart's Là ci darem la mano first, four minutes, so you know what is being taken apart. Then follow how Liszt keeps the tune audible under increasingly dense decoration. Recordings by Jorge Bolet and Marc-André Hamelin strike very different balances between menace and glitter; Bolet is grander, Hamelin faster and cleaner."],"liszt-faust-symphony":["Three long movements, each a portrait rather than a narrative: Faust, restless and questioning; Gretchen, an oboe melody over gentle strings with the delicacy of chamber music; and Mephistopheles, who has no music of his own and instead twists Faust's themes into sneering parodies. About seventy-five minutes. Liszt later added a quiet choral ending for tenor and men's voices on Goethe's closing lines, arriving after all the noise like a door opening onto silence.","Liszt had known Goethe's Faust since Berlioz introduced him to it in Paris in the 1830s, and the symphony is dedicated to Berlioz. He wrote it in Weimar in 1854, during the years when he conducted the court orchestra, championed Wagner's operas and gathered around him the composers later attacked as the New German School. He conducted the premiere on 5 September 1857 in Weimar, at the unveiling of a monument to Goethe and Schiller.","The opening bars run through all twelve notes, stacked as four augmented triads, a deliberately keyless gesture written half a century before atonality had a name. The Mephistopheles movement is the structural joke: every theme in it is a deformation of a Faust theme, with one exception. Gretchen's music the devil cannot touch, and it survives his movement intact to end the symphony.","The programme symphony was the front line of a culture war; Hanslick and the Brahms party rejected the whole idea of portraits in sound, while Wagner admired it and its transformed themes fed straight into the Ring. Conductors still choose between the purely orchestral ending Liszt wrote first and the choral one he added, and the sheer length keeps it off many programmes even now.","Skip to the Gretchen movement, ten minutes of oboe and strings and easily detachable, and let its main tune settle in your ear. Then hear the Mephistopheles movement and listen for that same tune being mocked, and for the one moment it returns unharmed. Bernstein's expansive recording and a leaner modern account can differ by nearly twenty minutes."],"alkan-concerto-solo-piano":["Three of the twelve studies in Alkan's Op. 39 form a concerto with no orchestra: one pianist plays both parts. The first movement alone runs close to half an hour, longer than most complete concertos, opening with a full orchestral exposition rendered in hammered chords before the soloist enters over it. A slow movement in F sharp minor tolls like a funeral procession; the finale is a wild dance in a limping rhythm. Fifty minutes, and physically punishing throughout.","Alkan was a Paris prodigy, a friend and neighbour of Chopin, who withdrew almost entirely from public life in the 1850s and composed for decades in near-total seclusion. Op. 39, published in 1857, is a set of twelve studies in all the minor keys which also contains a four-movement symphony for solo piano and a set of variations. He was a serious Hebrew scholar and spent years translating the Bible.","The trick is orchestration on one keyboard. Alkan writes the tutti passages thick and heavy in the middle register and the solo passages light and high, so the ear supplies an orchestra that is not there. Listen to the long development of the first movement, where he keeps both roles going at once, accompaniment in one hand and argument in the other, for minutes at a stretch without letting the seam show.","Almost unplayed for a century. Busoni ranked Alkan with Liszt, Chopin, Schumann and Brahms among the great writers for the instrument after Beethoven, but the difficulty and the composer's obscurity kept the music out of the repertoire until Raymond Lewenthal and Ronald Smith took it up in the 1960s. The old story that Alkan died crushed by a falling bookcase while reaching for the Talmud is almost certainly legend.","Do not attempt all fifty minutes cold. Start with the slow movement, seven or eight minutes of dark procession, then take the first movement in two sittings. Listen for the moment the imaginary orchestra hands over to the imaginary soloist; Alkan makes the join audible on purpose. Marc-André Hamelin's recording is the standard, Ronald Smith's the more eccentric."],"thalberg-fantasy-moise":["Ten minutes on the great prayer from Rossini's opera about Moses in Egypt. After a solemn introduction the hymn appears in the middle of the keyboard, sustained and singing, while both hands throw arpeggios above and below it — the effect that made Thalberg famous and that everyone described as playing with three hands. Variation follows variation, the surrounding filigree growing denser while the tune never loses its weight, until a broad final statement.","Thalberg was Liszt's chief rival, admired for a motionless posture and an unruffled tone as different as possible from Liszt's platform manner. On 31 March 1837 the two played on the same programme at Princess Cristina Belgiojoso's Paris salon in aid of Italian refugees; the princess is said to have concluded that Thalberg was the first pianist in the world and Liszt the only one. This fantasy was his calling card for thirty years of touring.","The three-hand effect is a matter of pedalling as much as of fingers. The melody notes are struck by alternating thumbs and then held by the sustaining pedal while the hands move away to play the figuration around them, so the ear hears a continuous singing line that no finger is actually holding down. Thalberg took the principle from the harp writing of his day and built a career on it.","Contemporaries ranked him with Liszt; posterity did not, and his music vanished within a generation as the paraphrase went out of fashion. Nineteenth-century critics who disliked Liszt's showmanship held Thalberg up as the tasteful alternative, which now reads as a proxy argument about performing style rather than about composition. Recent recordings have restored a handful of the fantasies as documents of how the Romantic piano actually sounded in a salon.","Listen for the tune in the middle of the texture and try to work out which hand is playing it; you cannot, which is the point. Then hear the Rossini original, a slow prayer for chorus, and notice how little Thalberg alters it. Compare Liszt's Don Juan fantasy for the opposite approach: Thalberg preserves the melody, Liszt dismantles it."],"henselt-piano-concerto-f-minor":["Three movements, about half an hour, in the grand manner: a stormy first movement with an orchestral introduction and a soloist who barely stops playing, a Larghetto that is essentially a long nocturne with orchestral accompaniment, and a finale in a driving march rhythm. The piano writing demands unusually wide stretches, with chords spread across a tenth and arpeggios that hold outer fingers down while the inner ones keep moving.","Henselt trained in Weimar under Hummel and then in Vienna, made a few sensational concert appearances, and settled in St Petersburg in 1838 as court pianist and inspector of music in the imperial girls' institutes. Stage fright kept him off the public platform for most of the rest of his life. He taught for fifty years and effectively founded the Russian piano school; this concerto was his one large orchestral work, and other pianists carried it round Europe for him.","The stretches are the whole idea. Henselt practised holding wide intervals silently while playing between them, producing a texture in which the harmony is sustained by the hand rather than by the pedal, so the sound comes out clearer than the same notes would from another composer. Listen to the slow movement for melody lines threaded between held notes inside a single hand.","Clara Schumann took it up and kept it in circulation for decades; Schumann reviewed Henselt warmly and Liszt admired his playing. Then it vanished, too hard for the musical return, and for most of the twentieth century survived only as a name in accounts of Russian pianism. Recordings since the 1990s have revived it as a missing link between Hummel's concertos and the Russian grand manner that followed.","Start with the Larghetto, a nocturne with an orchestra behind it, which shows why Henselt was called the Chopin of the north. Then take the first movement for its sheer weight of notes. Compare Chopin's F minor concerto, written fifteen years earlier in the same key, and then Rachmaninoff, whose teachers inherited this sound directly."],"moscheles-etudes-caracteristiques":["Twelve concert studies, each given a title and a mood rather than a bare technical label: wrath, reconciliation, a bacchanal, a children's tale, moonlight on the sea, a dream, night. Each works at one difficulty — repeated notes, crossed hands, wide leaps, evenness between the hands — while sustaining a character piece around it. About forty minutes complete, and considerably more grateful under the fingers than Chopin's or Liszt's studies of the same decade.","Moscheles was born in Prague, learned his trade in Beethoven's Vienna, where he prepared the piano score of Fidelio under the composer's eye, and settled in London in 1825 as the city's leading pianist and teacher. He taught Mendelssohn and remained his friend, following him to Leipzig in 1846 to teach at the new conservatory. These studies come from the London years, when the concert study was becoming a genre in its own right.","The titles are instructions about touch rather than decoration. Moscheles was among the last great players of the older, lighter Viennese and English instruments and disliked the heavier action and thicker tone arriving with the new grands; the studies assume a piano whose sound decays quickly, which is why they can seem thin on a modern concert instrument and why the pedal markings are so sparse.","Moscheles bridged two eras and was overtaken by both. He found Chopin's harmony harsh and Liszt's playing excessive, and said so in print, though he warmed to Chopin after meeting him; his reputation as a composer sank while his teaching lineage flourished through Leipzig. The earlier Op. 70 studies survived as pedagogy, these more ambitious pieces did not, and they now appear occasionally on period instruments where they make far better sense.","Pick three: the stormy first, the gentle piece that answers it, and the children's tale. Listen for the way each keeps one technical figure running while a tune proceeds above it. If you can find a recording made on an 1830s instrument, take it. Then play Chopin's Op. 25, published the same year, to hear what a generation gap sounds like."],"liszt-dante-symphony":["Two movements and a choral coda, about fifty minutes. Inferno opens with brass and low strings intoning the words carved over Hell's gate, then storms through a chromatic whirlwind, pausing for the doomed lovers Francesca and Paolo, whose music is given to cor anglais and harp. Purgatorio is calmer, with a long fugue for strings. The work ends not with a finale but with women's voices singing a Magnificat, distant and deliberately unresolved.","Liszt planned the piece in the 1840s and composed it in Weimar in 1856, dedicating it to Wagner, who talked him out of a third movement for Paradise on the grounds that no orchestra could depict heaven. He conducted the premiere in Dresden on 7 November 1857; the orchestra was under-rehearsed and the performance failed badly. He had also hoped to accompany it with painted dioramas, which were never built.","The gate of Hell is a setting of words nobody sings: Liszt notates Dante's inscription under the brass so that the rhythm of the Italian line dictates the theme. Francesca's episode treats the poem's most famous sentence the same way, a cor anglais recitative following the syllables of verse. The soft, unresolved Magnificat at the close is his substitute for the Paradise he agreed not to write.","The Dresden fiasco left a mark, and the symphony has never caught up with the Faust. Critics have complained that Hell is more convincing than Purgatory and that the Magnificat is an evasion. Liszt supplied two endings, one loud and one quiet, and conductors still argue over which he really wanted. Recent writing takes the work more seriously as a study in depicting a text without narrating it.","Start with the Francesca episode, the still centre of the first movement, then go back for the opening: brass chanting the inscription over Hell's gate is one of the great arresting starts. Follow the cor anglais throughout. Choose a recording that places the chorus at a distance, since the closing Magnificat should sound as though it came from somewhere else entirely."],"liszt-piano-concerto-no-1":["Nineteen minutes in four linked sections played without a break, so the whole thing works as one movement with a symphony's shape inside it. A snarling orchestral figure opens; the piano answers with crashing octaves. A slow section brings a long duet for cello and piano; a scherzo follows, marked out by a triangle tapping through it; then everything returns transformed in a marching finale. The soloist is protagonist and antagonist at once.","Liszt sketched the main theme in the 1830s and worked at the piece for two decades before finishing it in Weimar. He played the premiere there on 17 February 1855 with Berlioz conducting, at a festival Liszt had organised around Berlioz's music. By then he had abandoned touring and was using the court orchestra as a laboratory for the one-movement, thematically linked forms he was developing in the symphonic poems.","Everything grows from the four notes of the opening orchestral snarl; Liszt is said to have fitted German words to them meaning that none of you understand this. The triangle in the third section is structural rather than decorative, the one instrument carrying the scherzo's pulse. Listen for the finale, which introduces almost no new material: it is the slow movement's melody speeded up into a march.","Hanslick nicknamed it the Triangle Concerto after the Vienna performance and the jibe stuck for fifty years, keeping it off German programmes while audiences elsewhere went on loving it. Modern opinion treats the four-in-one design as the important thing, a concerto compressed into a single arch, and the piece is now a standard vehicle, criticised if at all for wearing its brilliance too openly.","Nineteen minutes is short enough to take in one go, so do that first and only then pull it apart. On a second hearing track the opening four notes; they are in every section. The cello and piano duet in the slow music is the tender heart of it. Sviatoslav Richter with Kondrashin for weight, Martha Argerich for speed and edge."],"liszt-piano-concerto-no-2":["Twenty-two minutes in one continuous movement of six linked sections, beginning not with a flourish but with woodwind breathing a quiet theme that the piano takes up almost apologetically. The music passes through a stormy episode, a long cello solo accompanied by the soloist, a march that turns the opening theme into brass fanfare, and a scampering coda. There is no contest between piano and orchestra here; the soloist threads through the ensemble like a chamber player.","Drafted in 1839, at the end of the Italian years with Marie d'Agoult, and revised repeatedly until 1861 — Liszt kept rewriting it long after he had finished the First. He conducted the premiere at Weimar on 7 January 1857 with his pupil Hans von Bronsart as soloist, and dedicated the score to him. He originally called it a symphonic concerto, a label that describes the intention better than the title it ended up with.","Every section is the opening woodwind theme in disguise: as a storm, as a love song for cello, as a military march, as a final gallop. This is thematic transformation in its purest form, and it is easy to test — learn the first six notes and you will find them everywhere. Notice too how often the piano accompanies rather than leads, decorating someone else's tune for minutes at a time.","It has always lived in the shadow of the First, being less immediately brilliant and much harder to bring off; conductors find the joins between sections difficult to pace, and in a poor performance it sounds like a string of unrelated episodes. Advocates argue it is the better piece, more inventive in its scoring and more genuinely symphonic, and its standing has risen steadily since the 1960s.","Learn the first six notes on the woodwind and then listen for them: that is the entire piece. The cello solo about halfway through is the moment most listeners fall for it. Compare the First Concerto, half again as flashy and half as subtle. Richter, Zimerman and Thibaudet all take different views of how heavily to play the march."],"liszt-nuages-gris":["Three minutes and about fifty bars. The left hand tolls a slow figure low in the instrument; the right adds a bare drooping line and a tremolo; the harmony never settles and never resolves. Near the end a rising figure in the left hand crosses under sustained right-hand notes, producing clashes that belong to no key at all, and the piece simply stops on an unresolved chord. Nothing is loud, nothing is fast, nothing arrives.","Liszt was seventy, half-blind, drinking heavily and grieving a run of deaths, moving between Rome, Weimar and Budapest. He had stopped writing for the public and produced a stream of tiny, bleak pieces that no publisher wanted; this one was not printed until 1927, forty-one years after his death. He gave it a German title as well as a French one, Trübe Wolken, grey or gloomy clouds.","The piece works by refusing cadences: every phrase points somewhere and then declines to go there. The augmented chords in the last bars have no root in the ordinary sense, and the final sonority holds notes belonging to two keys at once. Liszt wrote this in the same years he was giving masterclasses in Weimar to pupils who had no idea the music existed.","Unknown for four decades and dismissed as senility when it surfaced, this and the other late miniatures were rediscovered by twentieth-century composers who found their own harmonic world already sketched in them. Commentators have called late Liszt the seed of everything from Debussy to atonality, sometimes overstating the case for a body of work that consists largely of fragments left in a drawer.","Three minutes, so listen twice. The first time notice how tired the music sounds; the second time wait for the ending and hear that it does not conclude so much as give up. Then play the Hungarian Rhapsody No. 2 straight afterwards and marvel that the same man wrote both. Look for a pianist who resists the temptation to make it beautiful."],"liszt-lugubre-gondola":["A funeral gondola crossing the Venetian lagoon. The left hand rocks in a slow barcarolle rhythm that never becomes comfortable; above it a bare line rises and falls as a lament, sometimes in octaves, sometimes as recitative with no accompaniment at all. It exists in two versions, one about five minutes and the other nearer eight, the later one starker. Neither really ends: the rocking thins out and the music fades from the room.","Liszt spent the winter of 1882–83 at the Palazzo Vendramin in Venice with his daughter Cosima and her husband Richard Wagner. He wrote afterwards that he had been seized by a premonition and set the piece down as a presentiment of Wagner's death. Wagner died in that palace on 13 February 1883, and his body was carried away by gondola along the Grand Canal, as Venetian funerals required.","The title is literal, not poetic: Venice used a black gondola to carry coffins to the cemetery island. Set it beside a conventional barcarolle and the difference is exact — Liszt keeps the rocking figure but drains it of any settled key, so the motion continues while the harmony refuses to say where the boat is going. The unaccompanied recitative near the middle is a voice with nothing at all beneath it.","The premonition story comes from Liszt himself and has been treated with reasonable suspicion, since he dated the first version before Wagner's death but produced the second version and the related Wagner elegies afterwards. Unpublished in his lifetime like the rest of the late music, it entered the repertoire only in the mid-twentieth century, when its bleakness stopped being heard as failure.","Hear it beside Chopin's Barcarolle, written thirty-six years earlier on the same rocking rhythm, and the distance the century travelled becomes audible in ten minutes. Take the second version if the recording says which it is. Then try the arrangement for cello and piano, which turns the lament into a literal voice and makes the silences longer."],"schumann-carnaval":["Twenty-one short pieces for solo piano, most of them under two minutes, running together into about half an hour. Masked figures from Italian comedy arrive and leave: a slouching Pierrot, a leaping Arlequin, a coquette, a waltzing crowd. Schumann's two invented alter egos appear as neighbours, the dreamy Eusebius drifting in soft irregular groupings, the impulsive Florestan crashing off mid-phrase. A march in three-time closes the set, sweeping the whole cast forward over a deliberately clumsy old dance tune in the bass.","Schumann was twenty-four, a piano student whose right hand had failed him, editing the journal he had founded in Leipzig to attack musical philistinism. He was engaged for a time to Ernestine von Fricken, from the Bohemian town of Asch, and the piece is subtitled scenes on four notes. Two of the portraits salute living colleagues, Chopin in a nocturne-like page and Paganini in a snapping intermezzo, turning a private carnival into a manifesto for the young Romantic party.","The four notes of Asch spell out in German note-names, and they also happen to be the only letters of Schumann's own surname that are musical. Between two of the movements sits a page called Sphinxes: three bare groups of long notes, printed without rhythm, showing the reader the cipher. Most pianists pass over it in silence, but a few play it as a whisper, and Rachmaninoff among others made it audible.","Liszt played parts of it and Schumann was disappointed with the result, feeling the pieces needed a room rather than a hall. Nineteenth-century audiences found the set fragmentary, and Clara Schumann long avoided programming it whole. Its reputation rose when pianists stopped treating the sections as encores and started shaping them as one arc. The argument now is over pacing: whether the pauses between numbers should be theatrical or almost non-existent.","Listen for the little three-note motto that opens almost every number, then track how differently it is dressed each time. Compare the two self-portraits, Eusebius and Florestan, back to back. The finale is the test of a performance: the old grandfather's dance in the bass should sound genuinely stuffy before Schumann's march tramples it. Try a recording that treats the whole set as one continuous sweep."],"schumann-kreisleriana":["Eight fantasies for solo piano, played as a single half-hour span. The odd-numbered pieces tend to be fast, chordal and unstable, the even-numbered ones slow and long-breathed, so the music keeps swinging between fever and reverie. Several movements are built in layers where the melody sits in an inner voice and the hands cross to reach it. The last piece is the strangest ending in Schumann: a light, limping dance in the right hand over dry offbeat notes below, which simply walks out of earshot.","Schumann wrote it in Leipzig in a burst during 1838, while Clara Wieck's father was doing everything he could to keep them apart and Clara herself was touring. The title comes from Johannes Kreisler, the volatile fictional Kapellmeister of E. T. A. Hoffmann's stories, a figure Schumann treated as a mirror. He dedicated the score to Chopin, who returned the compliment only formally. The piano itself had recently gained the heavier action and wider range the writing assumes.","Schumann told Clara that she and one of her own musical ideas played the leading part in the work, and small turning figures related to her music surface repeatedly. Listen for how often a movement seems to end and then adds a coda that contradicts what came before. The second piece contains two contrasting interludes, so its slow melody returns twice, each time slightly rewritten, as if remembered rather than repeated.","Early reviewers found it wilful and formless, and it was slow to enter recital programmes. Twentieth-century writers, from Charles Rosen onward, made it central to the argument that Schumann's fragments are a deliberate poetics rather than a failure of structure. Performers still disagree about the finale: some play it as a ghost, barely audible; others give it a grim edge. The published revisions of 1850, which smooth some textures, remain contested.","Start with the second piece, the long slow one, and let its melody unfold; it is the emotional centre. Then go back to the opening and hear how violent the contrast is. Notice how often the tune hides in the middle of the texture rather than on top. Recordings by pianists who take the fast movements slightly under the limit tend to keep the counterpoint audible."],"schumann-fantasie-c":["Three large movements for solo piano, about half an hour. The first begins mid-flight over a churning left hand, never settles into a home key for long, and contains a quiet inward episode marked to be played in the manner of a legend. The second is a hard-driving march with a coda of leaps that pianists dread, the hands flung in opposite directions at speed. The third abandons rhetoric entirely: a slow, spacious song that ends in stillness rather than triumph.","Schumann drafted it in 1836, at the lowest point of his separation from Clara Wieck, and offered it as a contribution to the fund for a Beethoven monument in Bonn, proposing florid movement titles he later withdrew. He revised it before publication in 1839 and dedicated it to Liszt. The printed score carries an epigraph from Friedrich Schlegel about a soft tone running through every sound of earth, audible only to the listener who listens in secret.","The first movement's long ecstatic close quotes the last song of Beethoven's cycle An die ferne Geliebte, on the words about taking back these songs. Schumann told Clara the whole first movement was a lament for her. He originally ended the finale by recalling that same Beethoven phrase and cut it before publication; the discarded page survives, and a few pianists restore it, which changes the work from an open question to a circle.","Liszt accepted the dedication but rarely performed the piece, and it made its way slowly. Nineteenth-century critics thought the finale anticlimactic; the modern view is that its refusal to end loudly is the point. Scholars have argued at length over whether the Beethoven quotation is homage, love letter or both, and over which of the two endings represents Schumann's final intention. The 1836 draft is now published alongside the familiar text.","Listen to the first movement twice: once for the surge, once waiting for the hushed passage marked to be told like a legend. Then jump to the last movement and notice that nothing in it hurries. If you know the Beethoven song cycle, keep it in your ear for the first movement's final page. Try a pianist who lets the second movement's coda sound risky rather than clean."],"schumann-kinderszenen":["Thirteen miniatures for solo piano, most about a minute long, adding up to under twenty minutes. Each has a title: strange lands and people, catch-me-if-you-can, an important event, a child falling asleep. The writing is deliberately modest, mostly within a child's hand span, with no display of any kind. The last piece steps outside the set entirely, marked as the poet speaking, and closes with a bar of free recitative that hangs unresolved before the final chords.","Schumann selected these thirteen from around thirty pieces sketched in Leipzig in 1838. He was writing constantly to Clara Wieck, and told her they were reflections of an adult looking back, not music for children to play. The market for domestic piano albums was booming as pianos entered middle-class parlours across Germany, and publishers wanted short, saleable, titled pieces. Schumann supplied the format and quietly subverted it, giving the titles only after the notes were written.","The titles were added last, and Schumann warned that they were hints rather than programmes. Several pieces share the same rising four-note shape, so the set is far more tightly argued than its nursery surface suggests. The piece about a child falling asleep never reaches a settled final chord in its own key; it slips away, and the poet's epilogue has to finish the job. The whole cycle is over before most listeners expect it.","It became one of the most widely sold piano albums of the century, which for a long time worked against it: critics filed it under domestic sentiment. Recent writing takes the set seriously as a study of memory and adult loss, and points out how many of the pieces end in the wrong key or on an unfinished gesture. Debate now centres on tempo, especially whether the famous seventh piece should flow or be drawn out.","Play the whole thing straight through; it takes less time than a symphony's slow movement. Listen for the moment in the final piece when the music stops, speaks a free phrase as if aloud, and then closes. Compare a plain, unsentimental reading with a heavily shaped one, and see which leaves the pieces more room. Horowitz and Argerich sit at opposite ends of that spectrum."],"schumann-symphonic-etudes":["A theme and a chain of studies for solo piano, about half an hour. The theme is a slow, plain funeral-march tune in the minor. What follows treats the piano as an orchestra: one study is a horn chorus, another a scampering scherzo, another a grinding double-note exercise, another a canon that keeps stepping on its own heels. The finale breaks the pattern completely, switching to the major and marching for eight minutes on a fresh, square, almost brassy tune.","The theme came from Baron von Fricken, an amateur flautist in the Bohemian town of Asch and the adoptive father of Ernestine, to whom Schumann was briefly engaged. Schumann worked on the set from 1834, published it in 1837 as studies in orchestral character, and dedicated it to the young English composer William Sterndale Bennett, then a friend in Leipzig. He was building a reputation as a critic faster than as a composer, and wanted a piece that proved technical seriousness.","The finale's marching theme is borrowed from an opera by Heinrich Marschner, on words hailing proud England: a coded salute to the English dedicatee, hidden in plain hearing for twenty minutes. Five further variations, cut before publication, appeared posthumously in 1873; most pianists now interleave two or three of them among the printed studies, so no two performances of the piece contain quite the same music.","Nineteenth-century pianists treated it as a technical showcase and often played selections. The posthumous variations complicated matters permanently: purists play the 1837 text, others argue that the extra pieces restore a lyrical counterweight the published set lacks. Cortot and later Richter made the case for the mixed version, and it is now the norm. Critics still divide over whether the long major-key finale crowns the work or overstays.","Hear the theme first, then notice how rarely it appears literally again; Schumann keeps the bass line and throws the tune away. Listen for the study that imitates a horn section and the one that sounds like plucked strings. Compare a recording that includes the posthumous variations with one that does not, and decide whether the interruptions help. The finale should sound bright, not grand."],"schumann-dichterliebe":["Sixteen songs on Heine, for one singer and piano, about half an hour. Several last under a minute. The cycle traces an affair from spring infatuation through jealousy to bitter comedy, ending with the poet packing his songs into a coffin the size of a cathedral and sinking it in the Rhine. The piano is not an accompanist here: it finishes half the singer's sentences, and after the last word it plays alone for nearly a minute, contradicting everything the voice has just said.","Schumann wrote almost nothing but songs in 1840, the year he finally married Clara Wieck after winning a court case against her father. He had spent a decade insisting that song was a lesser form. Heine's Lyrical Intermezzo, published in 1827, was the fashionable poetry of the moment, prized for its habit of undercutting its own romance in the final line. Schumann originally set twenty poems and cut four before publication in 1844.","The opening song never resolves; it hangs on an unfinished chord and leaves the next song to answer it, so the cycle begins in mid-air. The long piano postlude at the very end is not new music: it returns to material from an earlier song about a dream of weeping, transposed into a consoling major. The voice never hears it. Schumann gives the listener a tenderness the poet is denied.","Publication was delayed and reviews were thin; the cycle grew into the central German song collection only in the later nineteenth century. Debate runs on two lines: whether Schumann softens Heine's irony or sharpens it, and whether the four cut songs should be restored. Transposition is contested too, since the printed keys form a scheme. Performance practice has moved toward less crooning and more speech-like delivery, following Fischer-Dieskau's example and then reacting against it.","Follow a translation the first time; the poems are short and the jokes are in the last lines. Notice how often the piano gets the final word, and how often it disagrees with the singer. Then listen again without the text, just for the postlude. Compare a baritone and a tenor version, and if you can, a period fortepiano recording, which lets the singer speak more quietly."],"schumann-liederkreis-op-39":["Twelve songs on Eichendorff for one singer and piano, roughly twenty-five minutes. There is no story. Instead there are recurring images: forests, a hunting horn heard far off, a ruined castle, moonlight, a wanderer who does not know whether the woods are protecting him or hunting him. The most famous number, on a moonlit night, floats over slow repeated chords for four minutes without once raising its voice. The last song scatters everything in a rush of spring nightingales.","Written in Leipzig in May 1840, in the middle of Schumann's year of song, weeks before his marriage. Eichendorff was the poet of German Romantic forest mysticism, and his verse was already twenty years old and much loved. Schumann called this his most Romantic music and said the poems had a shudder in them. He rearranged the order twice and swapped the opening song in a later edition, which changes the key scheme of the whole set.","Listen for horn calls: a rising open-air figure in the piano turns up in song after song, sometimes as reassurance, sometimes as a warning that someone is out there. The moonlight song is built on a piano figure that spells a rising motif associated with Clara, and its voice part enters late every time, always a beat behind the accompaniment, as if half asleep. The cycle's outer songs share the same harmonic gesture.","It has always lived slightly in the shadow of Dichterliebe, partly because it has no plot to summarise. Critics from the 1850s onward praised its atmosphere and doubted its coherence; recent analysis has traced the motivic links that hold it together. The choice between the two published orderings is a live editorial argument. Singers dispute whether the cycle suits a woman's voice, though there is no dramatic reason it should not.","Begin with the moonlight song, then go back to the start and hear how the same hush is prepared. Keep an ear on the piano's horn figure and note where it turns sinister. The final song should sound almost too fast, breathless rather than joyful. For comparison, hear a soprano and a baritone recording; the forest sounds like a different place in each."],"schumann-frauenliebe-und-leben":["Eight songs on poems by Adelbert von Chamisso, for one singer and piano, a little over twenty minutes. A woman narrates her life in sequence: first sight of the man, disbelief that he has chosen her, the ring, the wedding preparations, pregnancy, a child at the breast, and then his death. The seventh song is the only exuberant one. The eighth is bare and stunned, and after the voice stops the piano returns, without alteration, to the music of the very first song.","Composed in Leipzig in July 1840, days after the court finally permitted Schumann's marriage to Clara Wieck. Chamisso's cycle dated from 1830 and was widely read as a model of bourgeois womanhood. Clara was already a touring virtuoso who would go on supporting the household by performing; the gap between the poems' domestic ideal and the marriage Schumann was actually entering is one reason the work is argued over. He set the poems quickly, omitting Chamisso's ninth.","The last song contains no new melodic material of consequence for the voice, which sinks into near-monotone recitation. The piano's closing return of the opening song is written out exactly as before, so the cycle is a closed loop: memory replayed, not resolved. Schumann also drops Chamisso's final poem, in which the woman addresses her granddaughter, cutting off the consolation the poet supplied and leaving the widow alone.","Adored through the nineteenth century, it became the most contested cycle in the repertoire after 1970, when feminist critics attacked the poems as a male fantasy of female self-erasure. Defenders answer that Schumann's music is more ambivalent than the verse, and that the final piano return is an act of authorial pity. Some singers now perform it with spoken framing or refuse it entirely; others argue it should be heard as a period document, sung straight.","Read the poems first and form your own view; the argument is unavoidable and worth having. In the music, listen for the flat, almost speechless delivery of the last song, then wait for the piano. Compare an older recording that plays it as pure devotion with a recent one that finds irony. Lotte Lehmann and later singers give startlingly different accounts of the same notes."],"schumann-piano-quintet":["Four movements for piano and string quartet, about half an hour. It opens with all five players hammering the same bright theme in unison, so the sound is immediately closer to a small orchestra than to a drawing-room ensemble. The second movement is a funeral march for muted strings, interrupted twice, once by consolation and once by real agitation. A scherzo of running scales follows. The finale ends by stacking its own main theme against the theme from the first movement, both at once.","Schumann devoted 1842 to chamber music with the same single-mindedness he had given to song in 1840, after a year of symphonies. He and Clara were in Leipzig; she had just returned from a tour to Copenhagen that he had not joined, and the marriage was under strain. He dedicated the quintet to her. The combination of piano with string quartet had almost no precedent as a standard grouping; Schumann effectively invented the medium as concert music.","At the first private run-through Clara was unwell, and Mendelssohn sat down and sight-read the piano part. He suggested rewriting the scherzo's second trio, and Schumann did. The finale's closing pages are a double fugue: the finale's own subject and the very first theme of the piece are played simultaneously, in a different key from the home key at first, so the arrival of E-flat at the end feels earned rather than assumed.","It was an immediate success, unusually for Schumann, and became the model for every later piano quintet, Brahms and Dvořák included. Later criticism focused on balance: the piano writing is thick, and nineteenth-century instruments were lighter than modern ones. Period-instrument groups argue that a straight-strung piano solves the problem the score is often accused of creating. The funeral march's tempo remains disputed, between processional and merely slow.","The opening ten seconds tell you what kind of piece this is; nothing is held back. Go next to the funeral march and listen for the two interruptions. In the finale, wait for the last minute and try to hear two tunes running at once. If the piano is drowning the strings, look for a period-instrument recording, or one with a lighter-toned modern pianist."],"schumann-symphony-no-1-spring":["Four movements, about half an hour. It begins with a bare fanfare for horns and trumpets alone, which the whole orchestra then takes up and speeds into the main theme, so the introduction and the allegro share the same notes at different tempos. The slow movement is a long-breathed string song ending with trombones intoning the coming scherzo's theme. The scherzo has two contrasting trios rather than one. The finale is light-footed and includes a solo flute cadenza near the close.","Schumann sketched the symphony in four days in January 1841, in the first flush of married life, having spent years being told by critics, including himself, that he could not write for orchestra. A poem by Adolf Böttger about spring driving over the valley gave him the opening rhythm. Mendelssohn conducted the premiere at the Leipzig Gewandhaus on 31 March 1841. Schumann attached seasonal titles to the movements while composing and removed them before publication.","The opening fanfare was originally written a third lower, where the natural horns and trumpets of 1841 produced muffled, stopped notes; at the first rehearsal it sounded ridiculous and Schumann transposed it up. Modern valved instruments would have played the original perfectly well, so the version we hear preserves a workaround for a problem that no longer exists. A triangle, rare in a symphony then, brightens the first movement's texture.","The premiere went well and the symphony was played widely in Schumann's lifetime. Later, conductors from Mahler onward complained that the orchestration doubles too much and re-scored it, and for most of the twentieth century the Schumann symphonies were treated as clumsy. That view has largely reversed: period orchestras with smaller string sections show that the doubling is a colour, not an error, and the original scoring is now normally used.","Listen for the opening call, then catch it again as the fast music's main theme; it is the same idea wearing running shoes. Wait for the end of the slow movement, where the trombones quietly announce what is coming next without a break. In the finale, the flute solo near the end is a moment of stillness. A period-instrument recording will make the textures far clearer."],"schumann-symphony-no-3-rhenish":["Five movements, a little over half an hour. The first surges out without introduction on a broad theme whose accents fight the bar-lines, giving the impression of a wide river with a current under it. A rustic slow dance follows, then a short, gentle intermezzo. The fourth movement is the surprise: solemn, slow, contrapuntal, scored with three trombones that have been silent until this point. The finale then bursts in as if nothing had happened, cheerful and quick.","Schumann had just moved from Dresden to Düsseldorf to take up a municipal music directorship, his first secure post, and was delighted by the Rhineland. He visited Cologne Cathedral, then still unfinished, and attended or heard about the ceremony elevating Archbishop von Geissel to cardinal. He labelled the fourth movement as being in the character of an accompaniment to a solemn ceremony, then removed the label. He conducted the premiere in Düsseldorf on 6 February 1851.","The fourth movement is written in old church style, its themes worked in strict imitation like a Renaissance motet, and the trombones' first entrance in the whole symphony arrives there, so the shift is an event of colour as much as of mood. Its main subject returns, transformed and hurried, inside the finale, where almost nobody notices it. The opening theme's displaced accents also recur, disguised, in the last movement.","Received warmly, and the fourth movement was singled out from the start. The symphony suffered later from the general verdict that Schumann orchestrated badly, and Mahler's retouched version circulated for decades. Scholars have since argued that the thick doubling produces the intended organ-like blend, particularly in the cathedral movement. There is still disagreement about whether the nickname, which Schumann did not authorise, helpfully or misleadingly ties the music to a place.","Start with the fourth movement and hear the trombones arrive; then play the first movement and notice how differently the same orchestra is used. Count the beats in the opening theme if you like puzzles, or just feel it pulling against the pulse. Compare a big modern orchestra with a period band: the cathedral movement gains enormously from lighter, drier brass."],"mendelssohn-octet":["Four movements for eight string players, about half an hour. It opens with the first violin launching a soaring theme over a shimmering tremor from the other seven, a sound closer to an orchestra than to a chamber group. The slow movement is shadowed and songlike. The scherzo is the famous one: rapid, feather-light, played almost entirely quietly, and it ends by simply evaporating. The finale is a fugal chase that gathers all eight parts in and quotes the scherzo before its final rush.","Mendelssohn was sixteen and living in his family's Berlin house, where Sunday concerts gave him a private orchestra to test ideas on. He wrote the octet for the birthday of Eduard Rietz, a violinist friend and his teacher's pupil, and headed the score with an instruction that it should be played by all instruments in a symphonic orchestral style, with the dynamics observed more strictly than usual. Nothing quite like this scoring existed; Spohr's double quartets kept the two groups separate.","According to Fanny Mendelssohn, the scherzo was inspired by the Walpurgis Night scene in Goethe's Faust, the passage about drifting cloud and mist, and the music is meant to end with everything blown away. The finale's fugue subject is answered, part way through, by a phrase from Handel's Messiah chorus about the Lord reigning for ever, sung by the cellos: a sixteen-year-old planting his flag in the older tradition he was reviving.","Long treated as a prodigy piece, admired for its precocity rather than its substance. That condescension has faded: the octet is now usually rated as the finest music written by anyone under twenty. Debate concerns forces, since the composer's own note licenses performance by a small string orchestra, which some ensembles do and purists resist. Mendelssohn revised the work around 1832, and the earlier readings occasionally resurface.","Go to the scherzo first, about four minutes of music that never rises above a murmur, and listen for the moment it disappears rather than ends. Then start from the beginning and notice how the first violin is treated as a soloist with a seven-part orchestra behind it. In the finale, listen for the scherzo tune sneaking back. One-to-a-part groups keep the counterpoint sharpest."],"mendelssohn-symphony-no-4-italian":["Four movements, under half an hour. The first begins at once with the winds pulsing in rapid repeated chords while the violins throw out a bounding tune, and the momentum barely slackens. The second movement is a slow processional over a walking bass, plain and a little archaic. The third is a graceful minuet with a trio for horns and bassoons. The finale is the shock: a whirling southern dance in the minor key, so the sunniest of symphonies ends in shadow.","Mendelssohn toured Italy in 1830 and 1831, aged twenty-one, sending home letters about Roman light, Holy Week ceremonies and street processions. He completed the symphony in Berlin for the Philharmonic Society of London, which he conducted at the premiere on 13 May 1833. He was already an international figure, shuttling between Germany and Britain, and Britain treated him as an adopted national composer. The work was published only after his death.","Mendelssohn was never satisfied and revised the last three movements substantially in 1834, withholding the piece from publication for the rest of his life; the version everybody plays is the one he rejected. The finale is a saltarello that turns into a tarantella, and its perpetual motion is generated by a figure the strings hand round without ever stopping. The slow movement's walking bass may recall a pilgrims' procession he described in a letter.","The London premiere was a success and the symphony has never left the repertoire, which makes the composer's dissatisfaction hard to explain. Twentieth-century performances of the 1834 revision have not displaced the familiar text, and most conductors find the revisions heavier. The wider critical story is the collapse and recovery of Mendelssohn's reputation: dismissed after Wagner's attack as facile, he is now read as a rigorous classicist working at speed.","Listen for the pulsing wind chords under the first bar, the engine that drives the whole first movement. Then jump to the finale and notice that a piece which began in bright A major refuses to return there. Compare the second movement with a slow Baroque procession. A period-instrument recording, with lighter strings and hard timpani sticks, restores the leanness the piece assumes."],"mendelssohn-symphony-no-3-scottish":["Four movements played without a break, about forty minutes. A dark, swaying introduction for low strings and winds returns at the end of the first movement and again, briefly, near the close of the work. The scherzo is the brightest thing in it: a clarinet sets off a skipping tune built from a gapped scale that sounds like a pipe band heard from a distance. The slow movement alternates song with a stiff funeral march. The finale ends with a broad, striding major-key hymn for the full orchestra.","Mendelssohn visited Scotland in the summer of 1829 with his friend Karl Klingemann, walked in the Highlands and stood in the roofless ruin of Holyrood Chapel, where Mary Queen of Scots had been crowned; he wrote home that he had found the beginning of a Scottish symphony there. He then left it for thirteen years, completing it in Leipzig in 1842 and conducting the premiere at the Gewandhaus on 3 March. Queen Victoria accepted the dedication.","Mendelssohn asked that the four movements be played continuously and marked the score accordingly, so the piece is really one forty-minute span with internal joints; many performances still insert pauses. He never used an actual Scottish folk tune. The scherzo's Highland flavour comes entirely from a five-note scale he built himself. Late in the finale the opening lament returns in the major, which is the moment the symphony decides how it will end.","Well received in 1842 and a British favourite ever since, partly for national reasons. Wagner's 1850 pamphlet poisoned Mendelssohn's standing in Germany for decades and this symphony was demoted with the rest. Twentieth-century advocacy, notably by Klemperer and later by period conductors, restored it. The finale's closing hymn divides opinion: some hear a genuine transformation, others a conventional bright ending pasted onto a darker piece.","Try to hear it as one continuous piece; if your recording has gaps, ignore them. Listen for the clarinet at the start of the scherzo, then for the way the opening lament keeps returning in disguise. Compare the ending with the finale of Brahms's First, which solves a similar problem differently. Recordings that take the finale briskly make the closing hymn feel less grandiose."],"mendelssohn-elijah":["An oratorio in two parts for four soloists, chorus and orchestra, over two hours. The prophet Elijah, a bass, sings first, cursing Israel with drought before any orchestral introduction has been heard. Part one builds to the contest on Mount Carmel, where the chorus, as the priests of Baal, shouts itself hoarse to a god who does not answer. Part two covers persecution, despair, the still small voice, and the ascent in a chariot of fire.","Mendelssohn had revived Bach's St Matthew Passion in 1829 and spent years planning oratorios on the Handelian model for the English choral festivals, which by the 1840s commanded choruses of several hundred amateurs. Birmingham commissioned this one. The German text was assembled from Luther's Bible with Julius Schubring; William Bartholomew made the English singing version, and the premiere was given in English at Birmingham Town Hall on 26 August 1846. Mendelssohn revised it heavily for London the next year.","The curse comes before the overture, so the audience is addressed by a character before the orchestra has spoken: an operatic stroke in a form that officially forbade the stage. The still small voice is not sung at all; it is a passage for quiet chorus after the storm has been depicted, and the music simply refuses to make it dramatic. Mendelssohn, born Jewish and baptised, chose a Hebrew prophet who confronts an idolatrous state.","The premiere was a triumph with eight encores, and for a century Elijah was second only to Messiah in British choral life, which eventually made it seem institutional rather than inspired. Shaw and later modernists mocked its comfort. Recent performances, often with smaller and faster forces, have recovered its harshness, and scholars have taken more interest in the composer's own position between Jewish descent and Lutheran faith. The 1846 and 1847 versions are both performed.","Start with the Baal chorus and hear a crowd getting louder and more desperate as its god stays silent. Then hear the quiet chorus that follows the storm in part two. If a large amateur choir is available live, hear it that way once; then compare a lean period-instrument recording, which makes the drama sound less Victorian and more like opera."],"mendelssohn-midsummer-night-s-dream-incidental-music":["Thirteen numbers written to be played around Shakespeare's spoken text, usually prefaced by the earlier concert overture. There are entr'actes, a fairies' song for two sopranos and women's chorus, and short passages of music under speech. The scherzo is a four-minute flurry for winds ending with a solo flute; the nocturne gives a solo horn one of the longest sustained melodies in the orchestral repertoire; the wedding march announces the triple marriage; and a clownish funeral march parodies the mechanicals' play.","Frederick William IV of Prussia brought Mendelssohn to Berlin in 1841 as director of music for the royal court and wanted Greek and Shakespearean drama staged with music. Mendelssohn returned to the play he had set as a seventeen-year-old in 1826, when the overture had been written for piano duet with Fanny at home. The complete production opened at the Neues Palais, Potsdam, on 14 October 1843, in Ludwig Tieck's staging with the Schlegel translation.","The four soft wind chords that open the 1826 overture return at the very end of the whole score, sixteen years of the composer's life apart, so the fairy world closes exactly as it opened. Bottom's braying, a violin figure sliding down in the overture, is worked into the later numbers. The nocturne's horn solo is written for the natural horn, whose hand-stopped notes give the melody a veiled quality modern valve horns have to imitate.","The wedding march became detached almost immediately and, after its use at a royal wedding in 1858, turned into a social ritual, which has coloured judgement of the whole score. Critics have long argued that the incidental numbers are a supplement to the perfect overture, though performances with the spoken text tell against that. Reviving the full theatrical version is now common, with actors, which restores the parts written to underlie speech.","Hear the nocturne on its own first, for the horn. Then find a recording that includes the spoken play, or at least the melodramas, and notice how much of the music is written to end exactly on a cue. Listen for the four opening chords coming back at the very end. Period horns and light strings suit the fairy music far better than a heavy modern orchestra."],"mendelssohn-songs-without-words":["Forty-eight short piano pieces issued in eight books of six, each running two to four minutes. The premise is constant: a singing melody in one hand, an accompaniment pattern in the other, exactly as if a song had lost its words. Within that frame the variety is wide. There are gondola songs over rocking left-hand figures, a hunting piece with horn calls, spinning-wheel patterns, and a handful of pieces where the melody migrates into the middle of the texture and the pianist has to make it heard through the accompaniment.","Mendelssohn began the series in 1829 and added books until his death; the last two appeared posthumously. Fanny Mendelssohn was writing pieces of the same kind at the same time, and the idea may well have been shared between them. The market was the new domestic piano, which by the 1830s stood in enormous numbers of European homes, played mostly by women. Mendelssohn supplied music that amateurs could actually manage without writing down to them.","Almost all the familiar titles are publishers' inventions. Mendelssohn named only a few, mostly the gondola songs, and argued in a letter that music is not too vague to express thoughts but too precise, since words mean different things to different people while a tune does not. Several pieces slip the melody into an inner voice or split it between the hands, so what sounds like a simple song is a technical problem in disguise.","Immensely popular in the nineteenth century and then held against the composer as evidence of parlour sentimentality, especially after Wagner's attack. Twentieth-century recitalists largely dropped them. They have returned by way of players who take them as seriously as Schubert's short pieces, and by way of scholarship on domestic music-making and on Fanny Mendelssohn's parallel output. The publishers' titles are increasingly dropped from programmes.","Do not try the whole set at once. Take one book of six and hear it as a group. Listen for where the tune sits: on top, underneath, or buried in the middle. Compare a gondola song with one of the fast hunting pieces to see how far the format stretches. Recordings on an 1840s piano, with its lighter bass, keep the melody clear without effort."],"mendelssohn-piano-trio-no-1":["Four movements for piano trio, under half an hour. The cello announces the first theme low and unaccompanied in feel, with the piano rippling beneath, and the violin answers an octave up; that long-breathed melody dominates the movement. The slow movement is one of the composer's songs without words with two string players added. The scherzo is the fastest kind of Mendelssohn fairy music, gone in three and a half minutes. The finale is restless in the minor and turns to a broad major-key chorale before the end.","Written in Frankfurt and Leipzig in 1839, when Mendelssohn was conducting the Gewandhaus orchestra and had made the city the busiest musical centre in Germany. He showed the score to Ferdinand Hiller, who told him the piano part was old-fashioned beside what Liszt and Chopin were doing. Mendelssohn, who disliked Liszt's manner, nevertheless rewrote the piano writing to be more brilliant, adding the sweeping arpeggios that now define the sound of the piece.","The rewriting is audible: the strings carry melodies conceived in one style while the piano surrounds them with figuration conceived in another, and the friction is part of the character. Listen for how rarely the piano states a main theme first; it almost always decorates. In the finale, the chorale-like tune that arrives near the end is not a quotation of any hymn, but it is scored to sound like one.","Schumann reviewed it enthusiastically, calling it the master trio of the age and its composer the Mozart of the century, a verdict that helped the piece and later hurt the composer when the comparison came to sound like a limit. It has stayed in the repertoire without interruption. Modern debate is mostly practical: how loudly a full-size concert grand should play the rewritten figuration against gut or modern strings.","The opening cello theme is the way in; hear how long Mendelssohn can spin a single melody. Then listen for the piano and try to catch it leading rather than accompanying. Compare the slow movement with any of the Songs without Words. Recordings with a lighter piano, or a period instrument, let the strings through without the players having to force."],"mendelssohn-string-quartet-no-6":["Four movements for string quartet, around twenty-five minutes, and unlike anything else its composer wrote. It opens with all four instruments shuddering in tremolo under a jagged theme that will not settle, and the movement stays agitated to the end. The scherzo is grim and off-balance rather than light, with accents falling in the wrong places. The slow movement is a bleak song in A-flat that keeps collapsing back to the minor. The finale drives to a hard, unrelieved close.","Fanny Mendelssohn Hensel died suddenly of a stroke in Berlin in May 1847. Her brother collapsed on hearing the news, and spent the summer in Interlaken in Switzerland, drawing and trying to work. He wrote this quartet there. He died himself in November, aged thirty-eight, and the quartet was published posthumously in 1850. It is the only piece in which the composure that defines his public style breaks down completely.","The tremolo that opens the piece is not decoration; it is a texture that never stops long enough for a melody to establish itself, which is why the movement sounds panicked rather than merely fast. Mendelssohn's scherzo, normally his signature of lightness, here uses the same rhythmic habits with all the charm removed, so the listener who knows the octet hears a familiar mechanism turned against itself.","Nineteenth-century critics found it uncharacteristic and tended to explain it away as an outburst rather than a work. It was rarely played. Its reputation rose sharply in the later twentieth century, when Mendelssohn was being reassessed and this quartet offered evidence against the charge of unruffled complacency. It is now often described as a requiem for Fanny, though he left no such label, and some scholars caution against reading the biography straight into the notes.","Listen to the first thirty seconds and note that you never get a clean melody with a settled accompaniment. Then play the octet's scherzo and come straight back to this one; the same fingerprints produce the opposite effect. Do not expect consolation in the slow movement. Quartets that play it with a raw, unblended tone serve it better than those that smooth it out."],"brahms-symphony-no-1":["Four movements, three quarters of an hour. It opens with the timpani pounding a slow, unvarying beat while the strings climb and the winds descend against each other, one of the most oppressive introductions ever written. Two shortish middle movements follow, neither a true scherzo, both restrained. The finale begins in gloom, then a solo horn rings out over shimmering strings, a trombone chorale answers, and the strings finally give out a broad striding theme in C major that carries the movement to a blazing close.","Brahms had been circling a symphony since the 1850s, oppressed by the expectation, loudly voiced by Schumann in print, that he would be Beethoven's heir. He drafted a first movement in 1862, shelved the work, and finished it only in 1876 at the age of forty-three, having spent the intervening years mastering the orchestra in serenades, the German Requiem and the Haydn Variations. Otto Dessoff conducted the premiere at Karlsruhe on 4 November 1876, deliberately away from the Vienna critics.","The horn tune in the finale is a melody Brahms had sent Clara Schumann on a postcard in 1868, written down from an alphorn call he heard in the Swiss mountains, with words about greeting her a thousand times from high on the mountain. The slow pounding introduction was added last, after the rest was complete. The broad C major theme resembles the Ode to Joy closely enough that Brahms snapped that any fool could see it.","Hans von Bülow's label, Beethoven's Tenth, stuck and did the work both a service and lasting damage, framing it as a sequel rather than a statement. Wagner's party heard academicism; Hanslick's heard vindication. The modern argument is about tempo and about the exposition repeat in the first movement, which many conductors once cut. Brahms's own metronome habits and the smaller Meiningen orchestra he favoured have pushed recent performances toward lighter, faster readings.","Listen to the first minute for the timpani alone, a heartbeat that never varies while everything above it strains. Then skip to the finale and wait for the horn call after the storm; that entry is the hinge of the whole symphony. Compare a big late-Romantic recording with a period-instrument one on a Meiningen-sized orchestra; the counterpoint in the finale becomes audible in the second."],"brahms-symphony-no-2":["Four movements, about forty minutes. Cellos and basses turn a three-note figure over in the first bar, horns answer with a warm call, and that tiny cell generates nearly everything that follows. The first movement is pastoral but shadowed, with trombones darkening the texture early. The slow movement is the densest music in the piece. The third is a gentle wind-led dance that repeatedly speeds up and slows down. The finale starts almost under the breath and ends with the trombones blazing out in D major.","Written in a single summer at Pörtschach on the Wörthersee in Carinthia in 1877, the year after the First Symphony finally appeared. Brahms, now fully established, was famous for slow, tormented composition; this one came easily and he joked about it, telling his publisher the score was so melancholy that it should be printed with a black border and warning friends it would make them miserable. Hans Richter conducted the Vienna premiere on 30 December 1877.","The three notes in the first bar, a step down and back up, are the seed of the main themes of all four movements; once you notice the shape you cannot stop hearing it. Brahms cut a passage from the first movement's coda where a solo horn plays over held strings. The trombones, held back in most symphonies until a climax, enter within two minutes here and darken rather than exalt.","The premiere was an immediate success, and the third movement had to be encored. Because it followed the long-laboured First, critics called it the pastoral one and the label stuck, obscuring the shadows Brahms himself pointed to. Recent writing has taken his black-border joke more seriously. Performance debate concerns the first movement's exposition repeat and how much rubato the third movement's tempo changes really invite.","Listen to the first ten seconds and memorise the little three-note turn; then hear it start every subsequent movement in disguise. Notice how early the trombones arrive and how quiet they are. The third movement is the charmer: an oboe tune that keeps being interrupted by faster versions of itself. Compare a warm, broad recording with a lean period one, which finds more grit in the finale."],"brahms-symphony-no-3":["Four movements, the shortest of the four symphonies at about thirty-five minutes. Two chords in the winds throw the strings into a plunging theme, and the harmony is unstable from the first bar, torn between major and minor. The second movement is a mild clarinet-led idyll. The third is the famous one, a cello melody in the minor over a rocking accompaniment, repeated by the horn. The finale mutters, erupts, and then, extraordinarily for a symphonic close, dies away to nothing, recalling the opening theme in the fading strings.","Composed in the summer of 1883 at Wiesbaden, where Brahms was fifty and in his most secure period, wintering in Vienna and summering in spa towns. Hans Richter conducted the Vienna premiere on 2 December 1883, where Wagner's supporters, still fighting the war of the two parties two months after Wagner's death, hissed between movements. Richter afterwards called the piece Brahms's Eroica, a comparison the composer did not encourage.","The three notes that open the work, rising and falling with a flattened middle, are usually read as a motto on the German words for free but joyful, a private answer to the violinist Joachim's free but lonely. The motto returns at the head of the finale and in the last bars. Every one of the four movements ends quietly, which no symphony of comparable ambition had done before, and the finale's dissolution is the most radical of the four.","Hanslick and the Vienna establishment received it as the finest of the symphonies; the Wagnerians hissed. The third movement was detached almost at once and became a light-music standard, and later a film cue, which has skewed the work's popular image. The critical question since has been the ending: whether a symphony that fades out abdicates or achieves something new. Conductors differ sharply on how slowly the last page may go.","Listen for the three-note motto at the very start, then again at the beginning of the finale, then in the final bars. Skip past the famous third movement on a first hearing so it does not eat the rest. Above all, wait for the last two minutes and notice that a symphony in F major ends by whispering. Try Furtwängler's approach against a modern period-band recording."],"brahms-symphony-no-4":["Four movements, about forty minutes. The violins begin at once with a falling two-note sigh, repeated in a descending chain, with no introduction and no preparation. The slow movement opens with bare horn calls in an old church mode before the strings warm it. The third movement is the only outright boisterous scherzo in the four symphonies, with a triangle. The finale is the singular one: thirty-two variations on a rising eight-bar bass, unbroken, gathering weight until the coda drives the whole orchestra to a hard minor-key end.","Written at Mürzzuschlag in the Styrian Alps over the summers of 1884 and 1885, when Brahms was in his early fifties and increasingly preoccupied with old music; he owned and studied manuscripts of Bach and Renaissance polyphony. He conducted the premiere with the small, superbly drilled Meiningen court orchestra on 25 October 1885. Friends who heard the two-piano run-through in Vienna were dismayed, and Brahms half expected the piece to fail.","The finale is a passacaglia, a Baroque form nobody had used for a symphonic movement, and its bass line is taken with one added note from the closing chorus of a Bach cantata about days of sorrow. At the twelfth variation the music slows and a solo flute plays a long, aching line that stops the machine in its tracks. The whole first movement, meanwhile, is built from chains of falling thirds hidden in almost every theme.","Bülow's Meiningen tour made converts, but Vienna was divided and Hugo Wolf's review was savage. The finale's archaism was the sticking point: was reviving a passacaglia in 1885 mastery or antiquarianism? Schoenberg's 1933 essay on Brahms as a progressive settled the argument for the twentieth century by presenting the technique as forward-looking. Debate now runs on tempo, especially whether the finale's variations should keep one pulse or breathe.","Listen for the first two notes, a falling sigh, and count how many of the movement's ideas turn out to be the same shape. Then take the finale and try to hear the eight-bar bass repeating underneath, thirty-two times; once you catch it the movement changes character. Wait for the flute solo about two-thirds through. Meiningen-sized orchestras keep the bass line audible."],"brahms-german-requiem":["Seven movements for chorus, orchestra and two soloists, about seventy minutes, arranged as an arch: the outer movements share material, the second and sixth are the two great dark spans, and the fourth is a serene centre. It opens without violins at all, the sound rising from violas and cellos on the words about those who mourn. The second movement is a slow funeral march in three-time on the text that all flesh is as grass. Two movements end in long fugues over a single held bass note.","Brahms assembled the words himself, choosing passages from Luther's German Bible and the Apocrypha rather than the Latin Mass for the Dead. His mother died in 1865 and Schumann had died in 1856; both losses are usually invoked, though he never confirmed either. Six movements were performed in Bremen Cathedral on Good Friday 1868, an enormous success that made his reputation across Germany, and the fifth movement, for soprano, was added afterwards for the complete Leipzig premiere in 1869.","There is no Day of Judgement, no plea for the dead, and the name of Christ never occurs; the cathedral's director of music objected before the premiere and asked for an insertion, which Brahms refused. The consolation is addressed entirely to the living. In the third and sixth movements the fugues are built over a bass note the double basses and organ hold for pages, so the counterpoint whirls above ground that never shifts.","The Bremen premiere was the turning point of Brahms's career, though an earlier partial performance in Vienna had failed when the timpanist played the pedal point far too loud. Nineteenth-century critics debated the omission of Christ; some heard humanism, others evasion. Later arguments concern scale: Brahms sanctioned a version with piano duet accompaniment for domestic use, and small-choir performances now compete with the massed-chorus tradition.","Start with the second movement and let the funeral march build twice before its release. Then go back to the opening and notice the missing violins, which is why the sound seems to come from below. Listen for the held bass note under the two fugues. Compare a large festival chorus with a chamber-sized period performance; the words become audible in the second."],"brahms-violin-concerto":["Three movements, about forty minutes. The first is the longest, opening with a spacious orchestral paragraph before the soloist crashes in from above with a violent flourish; the writing lies high and demands enormous stretches, and there is a full cadenza near the end. The slow movement gives the main tune not to the violinist but to a solo oboe, with the soloist entering afterwards to decorate it. The finale is a rondo in gypsy style, double-stopped and stamping, with a curious march-like coda.","Written at Pörtschach in the summer of 1878 for Joseph Joachim, the greatest violinist of the age, Brahms's friend since 1853 and the man who had introduced him to the Schumanns. Joachim advised on the solo writing throughout their correspondence, and Brahms accepted some suggestions and ignored many. Joachim gave the premiere at the Leipzig Gewandhaus on 1 January 1879 with the composer conducting, playing his own cadenza, which most violinists still use.","Brahms originally planned four movements and scrapped the middle two, telling Joachim he had substituted a feeble adagio, which is the movement now regarded as the heart of the piece. Sarasate refused to play the concerto, unwilling, he said, to stand on the platform violin in hand while the oboe played the only tune in the work. The finale's Hungarian material is a nod to Joachim, who was Hungarian-born.","Bülow quipped that Brahms had written a concerto against the violin rather than for it, and early violinists complained about the thick orchestration and awkward stretches. It nonetheless entered the core repertoire within a generation. The cadenza question is the live one: Joachim's is standard, but Busoni, Kreisler, Auer and others wrote alternatives, and a few modern players improvise. Recent scholarship has published the discarded movements' surviving traces.","Listen for the soloist's entrance, which does not present the theme but attacks around it. Then go to the slow movement and hear the oboe get the melody first; Sarasate's complaint is the best listening instruction ever given. In the finale, listen for the moment the stamping dance turns into a quiet march. Compare Joachim's cadenza with Kreisler's on different recordings."],"brahms-piano-concerto-no-1":["Three movements, close to fifty minutes, on a scale nobody had attempted in a piano concerto. The orchestra opens alone with a huge shuddering theme over a held drum roll, the horns bellowing against the key, and the soloist does not appear for several minutes and then enters quietly, with something entirely different. The slow movement is a hushed, hymn-like meditation in which the piano mostly decorates. The finale is a hard-driving rondo in the minor that only turns to the major in the last pages.","Brahms was twenty in 1853 when Schumann's article announced him to Germany as the coming man. In February 1854 Schumann threw himself into the Rhine and was confined to an asylum until his death in 1856. Brahms, living near Clara and her children in Düsseldorf, began this music as a sonata for two pianos, tried to make it a symphony, and finally turned it into a concerto, working through five years and repeated rescorings before the premiere in Hanover in January 1859.","Over the sketch of the slow movement Brahms wrote the Latin words blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord, and he later told Clara that the movement was a portrait of her. The opening theme's violent trill and its refusal to settle in the home key have often been connected with the news from the Rhine, though Brahms never said so. Traces of the abandoned two-piano version survive in the piano's blunt, doubled octaves.","The Leipzig performance five days after the Hanover premiere was a disaster: three people applauded and the rest hissed. Brahms wrote to Joachim that the failure had made an impression on him and he would go on. The concerto took thirty years to establish itself, hampered by a solo part that gives the pianist enormous labour and few opportunities to shine. It is now regarded as the more searching of his two, and the harder to bring off.","Give the first four minutes your full attention before the piano enters; that orchestral opening is where the argument is set. Then notice that the soloist's first idea is a new one, not the theme you have been waiting for. Take the slow movement quietly and slowly. Compare an old broad recording with a modern one at a brisker tempo, and see which makes the finale cohere."],"brahms-piano-concerto-no-2":["Four movements, around fifty minutes, longer than most symphonies. A solo horn calls in the first bar, the piano answers with a rippling phrase, and then the soloist alone plays a full cadenza before the orchestra has properly begun. The second movement is a stormy scherzo, unusual in a concerto. The third opens with a long solo cello melody, the pianist listening; two clarinets take it over near the end. The finale is light-footed, almost playful, with a lilting Hungarian tinge.","Sketched after a second Italian journey and completed at Pressbaum near Vienna in the summer of 1881, twenty-two years after the First Concerto. Brahms was at the height of his authority, and described the piece in a letter with heavy irony as a tiny little concerto with a tiny little wisp of a scherzo. He gave the premiere himself in Budapest on 9 November 1881 and then toured it widely, and dedicated the score to Eduard Marxsen, his first teacher in Hamburg.","The piano's opening answer is not accompaniment but the theme's continuation, so soloist and horn are two halves of one melody before the orchestra has said anything. The added second movement gives the concerto a symphony's four-movement shape, which is why the piece feels like a symphony with piano. In the slow movement the principal cellist is effectively a second soloist, and the pianist spends long stretches in an accompanying role.","Received far better than the First, and Brahms performed it repeatedly, though critics noted at once that it asks a soloist for endurance rather than display. Later pianists have argued about whether the piano part should dominate or blend, and orchestras about whether the cello solo belongs to the section principal or an invited player. The four-movement design remains the standard example of Brahms importing symphonic architecture into concerto form.","Listen to the horn and piano in the opening minute as one melody split between two players. Then go straight to the slow movement for the cello solo and hear how long the pianist waits. The finale is the relief: light, quick and much shorter than what came before. Try a recording with a great orchestral cellist, and one with a pianist who resists the temptation to force."],"brahms-haydn-variations":["A theme, eight variations and a finale, about eighteen minutes. The theme is stated by oboes and bassoons over pizzicato strings with a rustic wind-band colour, and its phrases are five bars long rather than four, which unsettles the ear from the first hearing. The variations range from a whispering scherzo to a lurching minor-key waltz, a pair of horn-and-flute nocturnes and a rushing string study. The finale is a passacaglia: a five-bar bass repeated seventeen times while the orchestra builds to a full-voiced return of the theme.","Brahms found the theme in a wind divertimento in the Vienna library of his friend Carl Ferdinand Pohl, Haydn's biographer, where it was labelled a chorale of Saint Anthony. He wrote the piece at Tutzing on the Starnberger See in the summer of 1873, in parallel versions for two pianos and for orchestra, and conducted the orchestral premiere in Vienna on 2 November 1873. It was his first purely orchestral work of full ambition, three years before the First Symphony.","The theme is almost certainly not by Haydn; scholars now doubt the attribution of the whole divertimento, and no chorale to Saint Anthony has been traced, so a set of variations on a borrowed identity is honouring a piece that may be by nobody in particular. The five-bar phrase is the real subject: Brahms keeps it in the bass of the finale, so seventeen repetitions pass before the listener notices the ground has never been in four.","An immediate and durable success, and the piece that persuaded Vienna that Brahms could handle an orchestra. The attribution question emerged in the twentieth century and has changed the title in some programmes to the Saint Anthony Variations. The two-piano original has its own advocates who argue it is the sharper piece. Performance debate concerns whether the variations should run without pause and how heavily the finale's climax may be weighted.","Count the phrases of the theme; you will run out at five, and that irregularity is the joke that powers everything. Then listen to the finale and try to follow the bass line rather than the tune above it. Compare the orchestral version with the two-piano one, which shows the counterpoint stripped bare. Period-instrument recordings restore the reedy wind-band sound of the opening."],"brahms-clarinet-quintet":["Four movements for clarinet and string quartet, close to forty minutes. The two violins turn a rocking figure that is neither major nor minor before the clarinet slides in over it, and that ambiguity colours the whole work. The slow movement is a long veiled song for muted strings whose middle section releases the clarinet into free rhapsodic writing. A short intermezzo follows. The finale is a set of five variations, and at the end the opening bars of the first movement return and the piece closes where it began.","Brahms had drawn up his will in 1890 and told friends he had finished composing. In March 1891 he visited Meiningen and heard Richard Mühlfeld, the court orchestra's principal clarinettist, who had taught himself the instrument after starting as a violinist. Brahms was captivated, called him his dear nightingale, and wrote four late works for him. The quintet was tried privately at Meiningen in November 1891 and given publicly in Berlin on 12 December with the Joachim Quartet.","The middle of the slow movement is written in the manner of Hungarian gypsy playing, with the clarinet imitating a cimbalom band's improvising leader over shivering strings, an idiom Brahms had loved since his teens. The finale's last variation slows into the tempo and material of the first movement's opening, so the variation set does not end but dissolves into a recollection, and the final cadence is the same one heard forty minutes earlier.","Received warmly and quickly recognised as the summit of the late chamber music. Nineteenth-century listeners heard autumnal resignation, a reading that has become almost automatic and that some scholars now resist, pointing to the rigour of the variation writing. Mühlfeld's playing style, with heavy vibrato and portamento by later standards, is documented well enough to have started a small performance-practice movement, though most clarinettists still play it straight.","Listen for the very first phrase in the violins, before the clarinet enters, and hold it in mind for forty minutes; you will hear it again at the very end. Go to the slow movement's middle section for the clarinet's wild improvising. Compare a recording on a modern instrument with one on a period German clarinet, whose darker low register changes the balance completely."],"brahms-klavierstucke-op-118":["Six short pieces for solo piano, a little over twenty minutes: four intermezzos, a ballade and a romance. The first is barely two minutes, a restless surge that behaves like an introduction to the second, which is the famous one, a warm A major song whose middle section turns into a canon. The ballade is the only loud, striding piece in the set. The last is the strangest: an intermezzo in a remote flat minor built on a bare, tolling three-note figure.","Brahms was sixty and had stopped writing for orchestra. In the summers of 1892 and 1893 at Bad Ischl he produced four sets of short piano pieces, of which this is the third, and sent them to Clara Schumann, to whom the set is dedicated, as she was in failing health; she wrote back about learning them despite her hands. He had known her for forty years, since the months around Robert Schumann's collapse, and she would die three years later.","The tolling figure that opens the last piece is close to the plainchant of the medieval Dies irae, the chant of the Day of Wrath used by Berlioz, Liszt and Rachmaninoff for the same purpose, and Brahms lets it end the whole set. The second piece's middle section is a strict canon, the second voice trailing the first, so the most beloved melody in his late piano music is also a piece of formal discipline hidden in plain hearing.","Clara Schumann received the pieces privately, and they entered concert life slowly, since the nineteenth century regarded them as domestic. Schoenberg's essay on Brahms as a progressive singled out the late piano writing for its economy, and pianists from Kempff onward have programmed the sets whole. The current argument is whether the six pieces form a cycle with a designed key scheme or a publisher's bundle to be excerpted freely.","Play the whole set in order once, then decide whether it hangs together. Listen for the canon in the middle of the second piece, the one everybody knows. Then go to the last piece and hear the bare falling figure that opens it, and how the set refuses to end warmly. Compare a spare, quiet reading with a fuller Romantic one; these pieces reward understatement."],"rossini-il-barbiere-di-siviglia":["Two acts, about two and three-quarter hours. Count Almaviva lays siege to Doctor Bartolo's house to win Rosina, and the barber Figaro hires himself out to every side at once. The sound is fast, dry and sharply articulated: patter lines that accelerate until the words blur, woodwind chattering beneath the voices, and the long orchestral crescendos Rossini used so often they took his name. Each act ends in an ensemble where everyone sings their confusion at once.","Rossini was twenty-three and wrote it in a few weeks for Rome's Teatro Argentina, under a contract that gave him almost no time. Setting Beaumarchais's play was provocative because Paisiello's much-loved version of 1782 still held the stage, so Rossini called his opera Almaviva and printed an apology. Paisiello's partisans disrupted the first night in February 1816; by the second performance the house had turned.","The overture is second-hand: Rossini had already used it for two earlier operas, and it contains no theme from the comedy at all. Listen also to how often the orchestra, not the singer, carries the tune while the voice patters above it in short repeated notes. That division of labour is a Rossini fingerprint, and Verdi kept it for his own comic writing seventy years later.","Its reputation has never seriously wavered, but performing practice has. For a century Rosina was sung by high sopranos with interpolated fireworks; the critical edition of 1969 restored the lower mezzo Rossini wrote and stripped away accumulated cuts and traditional cadenzas. Arguments continue over how much ornamentation a singer should improvise, since Rossini expected some but wrote out more than his contemporaries did.","Start with Figaro's entrance aria, then jump to the Act I finale to hear a whole stage go mad in strict rhythm. Notice how the tempo almost never sags. Compare Paisiello's gentler setting of the same play. Look for a recording with a genuine mezzo Rosina and a conductor who takes the ensembles quickly."],"rossini-la-cenerentola":["Two acts, roughly two and a half hours, on the Cinderella story with the supernatural removed: no fairy godmother, no glass slipper, but a philosopher-tutor, a bracelet and a stepfather instead of a stepmother. The title role is a mezzo-soprano who begins with a plain folk-like tune sung over and over and ends with a rondo of florid runs. Between them come sextets, a storm, and a first-act finale built on one bewildered ensemble.","Rossini wrote it in under a month for Rome's Teatro Valle at Christmas 1816, after the subject he had planned was refused by the censor. Papal Rome would not tolerate a stage stepmother's cruelty or a heroine changing clothes, so the librettist Jacopo Ferretti reworked Perrault into a moral comedy about goodness rewarded. The premiere in January 1817 was cool; within a year the piece had travelled across Europe.","Rossini did not write all of it. His collaborator Luca Agolini supplied several numbers, including the tutor's first aria, which Rossini later replaced with a grander one of his own. The overture, again borrowed, came from an earlier comedy. The heroine's opening song, a simple ballad about a king who married a commoner, returns transformed in the finale as her act of pardon.","For a century it was overshadowed by Il barbiere and often given in cut, transposed versions. The revival came with Teresa Berganza and Claudio Abbado in the 1970s and the critical edition of 1971, which restored Rossini's own numbers and the mezzo tessitura. Critics still divide over the tone: whether the ending is genuinely moving or a formal exercise in Rossinian mercy.","Listen for the moment the chattering ensemble freezes and everyone describes the same knot of confusion in different words. Then go to the final rondo and hear how virtuosity itself becomes the sign of forgiveness. Compare the heroine's opening ballad with its return at the end. A warm, agile mezzo matters more here than a starry tenor."],"rossini-guillaume-tell":["Four acts and roughly four hours in French, on Schiller's play about the Swiss rising against Austrian rule. The overture is a four-part tone poem: dawn on five solo cellos, a storm, a shepherd's cor anglais tune, a cavalry gallop. The opera that follows is built the same way, from long stretches of landscape music, choral tableaux and ballet rather than a chain of arias. The tenor part sits punishingly high.","Rossini had moved to Paris in 1824 to run the Theatre-Italien and then to write for the Opera. Guillaume Tell was premiered there in August 1829 under a contract promising further works; the July Revolution of 1830 swept away the government that guaranteed his pension, and after years of litigation he never wrote another opera. He was thirty-seven and lived another thirty-nine years.","The score is full of Swiss local colour Rossini researched rather than invented: the herdsmen's ranz des vaches, horn calls, the yodelling turns in the shepherds' chorus. Arnold's fourth-act aria climbs repeatedly to a high C in full voice, and the chest-voice solution the tenor Adolphe Nourrit and his successors found for it changed what audiences expected of the voice type for the rest of the century.","Everyone admired it, few could stage it: the length invited savage cuts, and Paris routinely gave a mutilated version. Told that only the second act would be performed one evening, Rossini is said to have replied that this was the whole of his opera. Italian translations dominated for a century. Modern uncut French performances have restored a work that justifies its scale.","Everyone knows the overture's gallop; hear the whole overture instead, and notice that the storm and the shepherd's tune both come back inside the opera. Then take the second-act trio in which the three Swiss cantons swear their oath. Seek a French-language recording with a tenor who does not transpose Arnold's music down."],"rossini-stabat-mater":["Ten movements for four soloists, chorus and orchestra, about an hour, setting the medieval poem on Mary at the cross. The manner is frankly operatic: the tenor's Cujus animam is a march ending on a very high note, the unaccompanied quartet Quando corpus is hushed four-part writing, and the finale is a double fugue on Amen that runs the chorus hard. The opening moves from a slow tread to a full choral outcry.","A Spanish cleric, Manuel Fernandez Varela, commissioned it in Madrid in 1831. Rossini, unwell, wrote six movements and had his friend Giovanni Tadolini supply the rest; when the manuscript was sold after Varela's death Rossini replaced the borrowed numbers with his own, and the complete version was given in Paris in January 1842. He had written no opera for over a decade and would write none again.","The revision is the tell: the newer movements sit noticeably later in style, more chromatic and less symmetrical than the numbers of 1832 beside them. Listen for the closing fugue, which Rossini built strictly by the book, as if answering critics who said he could not write counterpoint. The unaccompanied quartet has no organ at all, unusual for Italian church music of the day.","Heinrich Heine defended it; German critics attacked the theatrical tone as unfit for sacred words, and the argument over whether Italian church music of the period is devotion or entertainment has never quite closed. Wagner was contemptuous. It was nonetheless among the most performed choral works of the nineteenth century, and the tenor solo became a stand-alone display piece.","Try the unaccompanied quartet first: it is the movement that answers the charge of vulgarity. Then hear the tenor solo and decide for yourself. Compare Verdi's Requiem, which grows directly from this sound world. A recording with soloists who can float quietly as well as project will show the work at its best."],"donizetti-lucia-di-lammermoor":["Three acts, about two and a half hours, after Walter Scott. Lucia is forced by her brother to marry a man she does not love, murders him on the wedding night and appears among the guests, bloodied and out of her mind. The score's centre is that scene, a long coloratura aria in which the voice duets with a solo instrument while the harmony slips out of place. Earlier, a sextet stops the wedding in its tracks.","Donizetti wrote it for Naples in 1835, weeks after Bellini's death removed his chief rival, and produced it on the usual Italian schedule of a few weeks' composition. Scott's novels were a European craze, and a Scotland of feuds, moors and ruined towers gave Italian opera a Gothic setting it had lacked. Naples supplied him with a great soprano, Fanny Tacchinardi Persiani, and the tenor Gilbert Duprez.","Donizetti scored the mad scene's obbligato for glass harmonica, whose thin, unearthly tone was meant to sound like a voice from elsewhere; a dispute with the instrument's player forced a rewrite for flute, and the flute version became standard. The harmonica part has only been widely restored in recent decades. The traditional cadenza with the flute is not Donizetti's at all but a later addition.","The premiere was a triumph and the piece never left the repertory, but the nineteenth century treated it as a soprano vehicle and cut it accordingly, dropping the tower scene between brother and lover. Maria Callas in the 1950s insisted the role was a tragic character rather than a display of high notes, and modern performances increasingly restore the cuts, the original keys and the glass harmonica.","Do not start with the mad scene. Start with the sextet, where six people freeze mid-catastrophe, then go back and hear how the mad scene reassembles music we have already heard. Listen for the moment the orchestra drops away and only the obbligato instrument answers her. Callas, and any recording using glass harmonica, both repay attention."],"donizetti-l-elisir-d-amore":["Two acts, about two and a quarter hours, in an Italian village. Nemorino, a poor farmhand, buys a love potion that is actually cheap wine, and Adina, who owns the farm, takes most of the opera to admit she loves him. The comedy is fast, with a quack doctor's patter aria, a sergeant's swagger and a chorus of villagers, and then it stops dead for Una furtiva lagrima, a bassoon-led lament in the minor.","Donizetti was asked in early 1832 to supply Milan's Teatro della Canobbiana with a new opera at short notice after another composer defaulted; the librettist Felice Romani adapted a recent French text, and the whole thing was ready in a few weeks. Northern Italy was under Austrian rule, which explains the recruiting sergeant on stage. It became the most performed opera in Italy over the following decade.","The famous tenor aria nearly did not survive: Romani thought a mournful minor-key solo wrong at that point in a comedy and objected, and Donizetti kept it anyway. Notice its accompaniment, a solo bassoon and plucked strings, which is chamber music inside a comic opera. The quack's patter, by contrast, is written so that the words matter more than the pitches, an old buffo technique carried forward.","Audiences took to it at once and have never stopped; critical opinion long treated it as charming but slight beside Lucia. That has shifted, partly because singers from Caruso to Pavarotti made the tenor role a test of line rather than volume, and partly because scholars have shown how carefully Donizetti paces the turn from farce to feeling. Debate now concerns ornamentation and the traditional cadenzas.","Listen for the bassoon that begins the tenor's aria, and for the fact that it introduces the only sustained minor-key music in the opera. Before that, enjoy the quack doctor's sales pitch as pure verbal machinery. Compare Rossini's Barber for the comic mechanics. A light, elegant tenor suits this better than a heroic one."],"donizetti-don-pasquale":["Three acts, a little over two hours. An elderly bachelor decides to marry and disinherit his nephew; the nephew's beloved plays the shy convent girl at the fake wedding, then turns into a spendthrift tyrant who redecorates the house and slaps her husband in public. The writing is quick and light: a patter duet for baritone and bass taken at speed, a serenade with guitar-like strings, an offstage chorus of servants gossiping about the bills.","Donizetti wrote it in Paris for the Theatre-Italien in 1843, at the height of a career that had him running between Paris, Vienna and Naples. He was already showing the early symptoms of the illness that would incapacitate him within three years. The cast was extraordinary, and he tailored every number to those particular voices, which is why the ensembles fit together like clockwork.","The slap is the hinge: for a moment the orchestra stops joking, the old man says quietly that this is the end of everything, and the audience is asked to feel for a figure it has been laughing at. Donizetti also recycled the serenade's melody from an earlier score of his own. The overture is a medley assembled after the opera was finished, not a prelude conceived for it.","It was an immediate success, and its standing as the last great opera buffa was recognised almost at once. Later criticism has argued over the tone: whether the humiliation of Pasquale is affectionate or genuinely brutal, and how far a production may push it. Casting is contested too, since the title role sits between comic bass and lyric baritone and rewards a singer who can do both.","Listen for the patter duet in the third act, usually encored, and time how fast the two men can take it. Then wait for the slap and notice how the music changes colour for about thirty seconds. Compare Verdi's Falstaff, which grows out of exactly this problem. Choose a recording with real Italian comic diction."],"bellini-norma":["Two acts, about two hours forty, set among Druids in Roman-occupied Gaul. Norma, their priestess, has secretly borne two children to the Roman proconsul; when he leaves her for a younger priestess she comes close to killing the children, then confesses publicly and walks into the fire with him. Bellini's manner is long-limbed melody over sparse accompaniment, and Casta diva unfurls one enormous arch of tune above a rocking harp and flute.","Written for La Scala in 1831, Bellini's second opera of a single year, to a libretto by Felice Romani after a French tragedy staged in Paris months earlier. Bellini worked slowly by the standards of the trade, revising melodies obsessively, and built the title role for Giuditta Pasta, a soprano with an unusually wide and uneven range. Northern Italy was Austrian territory, and an opera about occupation carried a charge.","Casta diva was transposed down a step for Pasta, who refused it at first, and most sopranos still sing the lower key. Bellini is said to have written the tune out eight times before he was satisfied. Notice too how rarely the orchestra does anything but support: he strips the accompaniment so the vocal line carries all the harmonic weight, which is exactly why the opera is so hard to sing well.","The first night at La Scala went badly, and Bellini called it a fiasco; it recovered within days. Wagner, who conducted it, singled it out as proof that melody could carry tragedy. In the twentieth century it became a Callas property, and the debate ever since is whether the title role needs a dramatic soprano, an agile one, or the rare singer who is both. Cuts to the ensembles remain contested.","Begin with Casta diva but do not stop there: the duets for the two women, especially Mira, o Norma, show the style at its most concentrated. Listen for how little the orchestra does. Then hear the final scene, where the confession is set almost without ornament. Callas, Sutherland and Caballe each solve the role differently."],"bellini-la-sonnambula":["Two acts, about two and a quarter hours, in a Swiss village. Amina is found asleep in a stranger's bedroom, loses her fiance's trust, and is vindicated when the village watches her sleepwalk across a mill roof. The score is gentle and diatonic, built from long melodies over guitar-like accompaniment; the final scene pairs a slow, almost motionless lament with a bright, elaborate closing number of pure joy.","Bellini and Romani produced it for Milan's Teatro Carcano in March 1831, six months before Norma, after abandoning a projected Victor Hugo subject the Austrian censors would not have passed. Sleepwalking was a fashionable topic in the theatre and in the medical writing of the day, tangled up with mesmerism. Giuditta Pasta again sang the lead, and the tenor Giovanni Battista Rubini's high notes shaped the male part.","The tenor role rises to a written high F, sung by Rubini in a mixed head voice that no longer exists as a technique; modern tenors transpose. Notice that the whole opera contains no villain at all. The plot turns on a misunderstanding, and Bellini accordingly keeps the orchestra soft-edged throughout. The sleepwalking scene borrows its stillness from the Italian preghiera, a prayer number, without the prayer.","It was a hit and stayed one through the century, then slipped as taste turned against sweetness. The revivals of the 1950s, with Callas under Bernstein and later Sutherland, argued that its apparent simplicity conceals real technical severity. Critics still divide on the ending, which asks a singer to switch from grief to brilliance in a few bars, and on whether the tenor's original keys should be attempted.","Listen to the last ten minutes as one span: the slow lament over the withered flowers, then the sudden release of the closing number. Notice how plain the harmony is and how much the singer must supply. Compare Amina's music with Norma's to hear the same composer in two registers. Seek a soprano with an even, unforced top."],"bellini-i-puritani":["Three acts, roughly two and three-quarter hours, set during the English civil wars. Elvira loses her reason when her Cavalier bridegroom appears to abandon her, and recovers when he returns; a pardon arrives in the last minutes to prevent the executions. The writing is spacious and very high, with a famous quartet, a mad scene in two parts, and a duet for two basses that later served as a rallying song. The orchestra is richer than in Bellini's Italian scores.","Bellini's last opera, written in Paris for the Theatre-Italien and premiered in January 1835; he died that September, aged thirty-three. The libretto was by Carlo Pepoli, an exiled Italian nobleman rather than a professional man of the theatre, which Bellini complained about constantly. He had four of the greatest singers of the age at his disposal and wrote to their limits, which is why the piece is so hard to cast.","There are two versions. Bellini prepared a second for Naples with a mezzo Elvira and a different distribution of numbers; it was never staged in his lifetime and has only been performed in modern times. The bass duet Suoni la tromba was politically loaded enough to attract the censors' attention, and Italian patriots later took it up. Rubini's part demands notes above high C.","Paris received it rapturously; Rossini, then living there, had advised on the score. Later critics found the plot inert and the ending arbitrary, and for decades the opera survived mainly as a vehicle. The revivals of the 1960s with Sutherland and Pavarotti reopened it, and work on the Naples version has complicated the picture. Assembling four star voices at once remains the practical obstacle.","Start with the second-act mad scene, then the bass duet that follows it for the contrast of tenderness and bluster. Listen for the orchestra's fuller palette, with horns, harp and more woodwind detail, compared with Norma. A recording with a tenor who can manage the stratospheric writing without shouting is worth searching out."],"verdi-nabucco":["Four parts, about two and a quarter hours, on the Babylonian captivity of the Hebrews. Nebuchadnezzar declares himself a god, is struck mad, and recovers to free the exiles. The score's engine is the chorus: massed unison writing, blunt rhythms and brass, and in the third part the slow lament Va, pensiero, sung quietly and almost entirely in unison over a rocking accompaniment before it opens out. The overture stitches the main tunes together.","Verdi's third opera and his first success, at La Scala in March 1842. He was twenty-eight, had lost his wife and both children within two years, and had resolved to give up composing; the impresario Bartolomeo Merelli pressed the libretto on him and the chorus text caught his eye. Lombardy was under Austrian rule, and audiences heard exile and occupation in a biblical story. Giuseppina Strepponi sang Abigaille and later became his wife.","The legend that Va, pensiero was encored as a patriotic demonstration on the first night is not supported by the evidence; the encore that evening was probably a different chorus, and the political reading grew afterwards. Notice the writing for Abigaille, which lurches between low chest notes and high leaps. It is a part so punishing that it has ended careers, and one Verdi never wrote again in that form.","It ran for an unprecedented number of performances in its first season and made Verdi's name across Italy. Critics of the time noted the crudeness of the orchestration and the reliance on rhythmic drive, charges Verdi half-accepted when he later called these his years in the galleys. Modern scholarship has spent much effort separating the real politics of 1842 from the Risorgimento myth built around this opera later.","Listen to Va, pensiero for its first two minutes, when nearly the whole chorus sings the same line together and the effect comes from restraint rather than volume. Then take Abigaille's second-act scene to hear how ruthlessly Verdi tests a voice. Compare the choral writing with Rossini's Guillaume Tell. A large, well-drilled chorus matters more here than star soloists."],"verdi-rigoletto":["Three acts, about two hours, after Hugo. A hunchbacked court jester mocks a father whose daughter the Duke has ruined, is cursed for it, and hires an assassin against the Duke only to find his own daughter in the sack. The third-act quartet has four people singing four different things at once; the storm outside the inn is made from offstage voices humming in the wind; and the Duke's cynical song returns in the last minute as the worst possible news.","Written for Venice in 1851, when Verdi was thirty-seven and newly free of the treadmill years. The Austrian censors banned Hugo's play about a French king, so the setting moved to Mantua and the king became a duke; Verdi fought hard to keep the jester's deformity and the sack. He described the piece as a chain of duets, deliberately avoiding the standard aria-and-cabaletta pattern, and kept the tenor's tune secret until the dress rehearsal.","The curse is a musical object, not just a plot device: a bare descending figure in the brass over a drum roll that opens the prelude and returns whenever the jester remembers it. The storm's chorus sings no words at all, only closed-mouth vowels. And the Duke's song is deliberately banal. Verdi wrote a cheap tune on purpose, then made its cheapness the point when it drifts back over the dying girl.","It was a triumph in Venice and quickly the most performed opera in Italy. Nineteenth-century objections were moral rather than musical: the subject was called obscene. Modern discussion centres on the title role's balance of pathos and grotesquerie, on whether to allow the traditional interpolated high notes that Verdi did not write, and on how far a production may update a story that depends on absolute ducal power.","Go straight to the quartet and listen for four separate emotional lines running at once: seduction, mockery, grief and rage, in one piece of music. Then hear the storm, and notice that the wind is human voices. Finally, catch the moment the Duke's tune returns offstage. A baritone with weight and vulnerability matters more than a glamorous tenor."],"verdi-il-trovatore":["Four parts, about two and a quarter hours, in fifteenth-century Spain. A gypsy woman who burned the wrong baby years earlier has raised her enemy's son as her own; two brothers who do not know they are brothers love the same woman, and the opera ends with one executing the other. The score is a chain of famous numbers, among them an anvil chorus, a soldiers' chorus, a nuns' procession and a tenor's call to arms, driven by short hammering rhythms.","Premiered in Rome in January 1853, six weeks before La traviata opened in Venice; Verdi was writing the two simultaneously and they could hardly be less alike. His librettist Salvadore Cammarano died before finishing the text, and Leonardo Emanuele Bardare completed it. Verdi's own mother had died in 1851, and the central figure of a woman obsessed with fire, a mother and a lost child belongs to that period of his life.","The plot's key events all happen before the curtain rises and are narrated twice, first by a soldier and later by Azucena herself; the opera is built on two conflicting accounts of the same night. Listen for how the tale of the burning returns in her music as a fixed, obsessive rhythm. The famous Di quella pira is almost always sung with a high C that Verdi did not write.","It was a colossal popular success and became shorthand for operatic absurdity. Caruso's remark that all it needs is the four greatest singers in the world captures both the contempt and the truth. Twentieth-century criticism, and productions from Visconti onward, took Azucena seriously as the real protagonist. Arguments continue over the interpolated high C and over whether the second-act ending should be cut.","Ignore the plot on a first hearing and follow Azucena instead: her narrative in the second part, then her scene in the prison. Notice how the same rhythm keeps returning under her words. Then enjoy the anvil chorus for exactly what it is. A recording needs a genuine dramatic mezzo as much as it needs a ringing tenor."],"verdi-la-traviata":["Three acts, about two and a quarter hours. Violetta, a Parisian courtesan dying of consumption, leaves her lover at his father's request, is publicly humiliated by him, and dies in the last scene. The prelude begins with divided violins high and thin, the sound of illness, and that music returns before the final act. Between them come a drinking song, a party with gypsy and matador entertainers, and one of Verdi's great baritone-soprano confrontations.","Premiered at La Fenice in Venice in March 1853, from a play Verdi had seen in Paris the previous year, itself drawn from Dumas's novel about a real courtesan, Marie Duplessis. Verdi was living openly with Giuseppina Strepponi, unmarried, in a small provincial town that disapproved, which sharpens the scene where a father demands that a woman leave for the family's good. The management insisted on period costume rather than modern dress.","Violetta's music changes technique across the evening: brilliant coloratura in the first act, long lyrical lines in the second, and a wasted, almost speech-like manner in the third, so the role requires three different sopranos' skills in one throat. Listen also for the offstage carnival chorus that intrudes during her death, the world going on cheerfully outside the window, an effect Puccini studied closely.","The first night failed; Verdi blamed the singers, and a stout soprano playing a dying consumptive drew laughter. A year later, with a different cast, it succeeded and has never faltered since. Modern debate concerns the drastic first-act cuts once traditional, the interpolated high note at the end of Sempre libera, and whether the piece is a social critique or a sentimental one. Productions since the 1980s have pushed hard on the first reading.","Listen to the prelude and then, an hour and a half later, to the prelude to the third act: the same thin violin sound, emptied out. In between, hear the duet with Germont, where the older man's smooth melody works like pressure. Compare Puccini's Mimi. A soprano who can do both fireworks and quiet exhaustion is the whole thing."],"verdi-un-ballo-in-maschera":["Three acts, about two and a half hours. A ruler in love with his best friend's wife visits a fortune-teller, is warned that he will die by the hand of a friend, and is stabbed at a masked ball. The tone shifts constantly: a page's laughing music in fast, light rhythms against the conspirators' dark unisons, a midnight scene under the gallows, and a ballroom where the dance band plays on stage while a murder is arranged in front of it.","Verdi wrote it for Naples in 1857 and 1858 on a subject drawn from the real assassination of Gustav III of Sweden. The censors, alarmed by the attempt on Napoleon III's life in January 1858, refused to allow a king murdered on stage; Verdi withdrew the opera, was sued, and eventually staged it in Rome in 1859 with the action moved to colonial Boston. Restoring the Swedish setting is now common.","The page Oscar is a high soprano in male dress whose glittering music was modelled on French comic opera, which Verdi had absorbed in Paris. Listen for how that brightness keeps cutting across the tragedy, including at the murder itself. In the gallows scene the strings play a figure that seems to shiver; Verdi wanted the sound of wind through a gibbet rather than a character's fear.","It was well received in Rome and quickly established, though the Boston transposition was mocked from the start. Twentieth-century opinion has ranked it among Verdi's subtlest scores for its mixture of registers, comparing it with Mozart. Productions now choose freely between Sweden and Boston; scholars point out that the Swedish text was never actually set by Verdi, so a restored setting is itself an editorial construction.","Listen for Oscar: every time the page sings, the opera changes weather. Then take the second-act love duet, which is one long accelerating span, and the final scene, where the on-stage dance band keeps playing. Compare the conspirators' snickering music with Iago's, thirty years later. Any recording with a bright, agile Oscar will do the work."],"verdi-don-carlos":["Five acts in French, close to four hours uncut, after Schiller. The Spanish infante loves his stepmother; his friend Posa argues for Flemish liberty and is shot for it; the Grand Inquisitor, blind and ninety, tells King Philip that the state may kill its own son. The score's centre is a scene for two low male voices in near-darkness, with cellos and basses growling underneath. There is also a public burning of heretics, with stage bands.","Commissioned by the Paris Opera for the Exposition Universelle of 1867, in the French five-act form with an obligatory ballet. Verdi cut music before the premiere simply so that suburban audiences could catch the last trains home. He revised the work repeatedly, producing a four-act Italian version for Milan in 1884 and a five-act Italian hybrid in 1886. Which text to perform is still an open question.","Philip's monologue, alone at dawn, is preceded by a solo cello: Verdi gives the king's loneliness to an instrument before a word is sung. Some of the music cut in 1867 was rediscovered in the twentieth century in the Opera's library, including a lament over Posa's body that Verdi later reused, note for note, in the Requiem's Lacrymosa. Listen for the friendship theme returning in the last bars.","Paris found it long and insufficiently tuneful, and it took a century to be recognised as arguably Verdi's greatest score. Visconti's 1958 Covent Garden production and Julian Budden's scholarship changed the verdict. The versions debate is genuinely unresolved: the French five-act original, the tighter Italian four-act, and the hybrids each have serious advocates, and no single text is definitive.","Start with the King's scene at the beginning of the fourth act, the cello, then the monologue, then the Inquisitor's arrival, and you will know whether this opera is for you. Then hear the duet for Carlos and Posa. Seek the French original if you want the full architecture, the Italian four-act if you want speed."],"verdi-aida":["Four acts, about two and a half hours. An Ethiopian slave in Egypt loves the general sent to destroy her people; he betrays a military secret and the two are sealed alive in a vault beneath the temple. The triumphal scene has straight stage trumpets, ballet and massed chorus at full volume, and then the last act shrinks to two voices, muted strings and a distant priestess, ending almost inaudibly.","Commissioned by the Khedive Ismail of Egypt, not for the opening of the Suez Canal as is often claimed but for Cairo's new opera house; the premiere was delayed to December 1871 because the sets were stuck in Paris during the siege. The Egyptologist Auguste Mariette supplied the scenario and the archaeological detail. Verdi, semi-retired at the Italian premiere, wrote nothing further for the stage for sixteen years.","The straight trumpets in the triumphal scene were built specially, and the second group answers the first a semitone higher, a jolt most listeners feel without identifying its cause. The priestess's opening chant uses a scale Verdi shaped to sound antique rather than any authentic Egyptian source. The final duet is marked to be sung at the softest dynamics almost throughout, which few casts observe.","Cairo and Milan both cheered; some critics accused Verdi of going Wagnerian, which annoyed him. Edward Said's later reading of the opera as an artefact of European empire has become part of any serious discussion of it. Performance debate concerns scale: whether the elephants and armies help or bury a work whose real subject is three people in a private trap.","Endure or enjoy the triumphal scene, then listen to the last twelve minutes at the volume you would use for chamber music: that contrast is the piece. Notice the priestess singing above the sealed tomb. Compare Puccini's Turandot for the same move from spectacle to intimacy. Look for a conductor who honours the quiet markings."],"verdi-messa-da-requiem":["Seven movements for four soloists, chorus and orchestra, about ninety minutes. It opens almost inaudibly with muttered cellos, then the Dies irae detonates, bass drum on the offbeat, brass, chorus at full cry, and returns four times as the work's recurring nightmare. Offstage trumpets answer trumpets across the hall for the last judgment. The final movement gives the soprano a long solo plea and ends without a resolving Amen, the chorus whispering the word deliverance.","Verdi organised a collective requiem for Rossini in 1868 that collapsed in recrimination; he kept his own contribution, the Libera me. When the novelist Alessandro Manzoni, whom he revered as a national conscience, died in 1873, Verdi built a full setting around that movement and conducted it in Milan's church of San Marco on the first anniversary, then at La Scala. He was an unbeliever writing for a country where church and new state were at war.","The Libera me is the oldest music in the score, written six years earlier for another man's memorial, and everything else grows outward from it; its fugue subject comes from that draft. The Lacrymosa takes its melody from a scene Verdi had cut out of Don Carlos. Notice that the work ends not with the requiem's grant of rest but with a request, half spoken, that is never answered.","Hans von Bulow called it an opera in ecclesiastical dress before hearing a note, and apologised in writing eighteen years later. The charge of theatricality is usually answered now by pointing out that the theatre was where Verdi thought about death. Debate persists over chorus size, over whether the piece belongs in a church or a concert hall, and over how literally to take the extreme quiet markings.","Listen to the opening ninety seconds, and how quiet they are, so that the Dies irae lands as a shock. Then follow the Dies irae each time it comes back and notice it is never quite the same. End with the Libera me and hear the piece refuse to close. A large chorus and a conductor unafraid of the bass drum are essential."],"verdi-otello":["Four acts, about two and a quarter hours, with no overture: the curtain rises on a storm made of organ pedal, brass and shouting chorus. Iago talks Otello into murdering Desdemona; the last act is a woman alone with her servant, a willow song, a prayer, and then a low string line under the murderer's entrance. Music runs continuously across scenes, and the love duet that closes the first act returns, broken, at the end.","Verdi was seventy-four and had written no opera since Aida sixteen years earlier. His publisher Giulio Ricordi engineered the collaboration with Arrigo Boito, composer, poet, and once a young critic who had insulted Verdi in print; the two worked slowly from 1879 to 1886. The premiere at La Scala in February 1887 was a national event, with the world's press gathered in Milan.","Otello's entrance to kill Desdemona is announced by the double basses alone, very low and almost without vibrato, a sound scarcely any other opera uses. Boito gave Iago a creed with no source in Shakespeare, so that the villain has a philosophy rather than a motive. And the theme of the love duet, scored for four solo cellos, comes back in the closing bars as the last thing Otello hears.","The premiere was a triumph, and the persistent question since has been whether Verdi absorbed Wagner or arrived independently at continuous music drama; Verdi denied the debt, and most scholars now trace the technique to his own Don Carlos and Aida. Casting is the practical problem: the title role wrecks voices, and each generation has perhaps two credible exponents. Boito's libretto is regarded as among the finest ever written.","Listen to the opening five minutes without knowing the plot: the storm arrives before any character does. Then the first-act love duet, then the willow song and prayer. Notice the solo double basses before the murder. Compare Verdi's Iago with Shakespeare's. Any recording stands or falls on its tenor and its baritone."],"verdi-falstaff":["Three acts, about two hours, on The Merry Wives of Windsor with speeches from Henry IV grafted in. Nothing stops for an aria: the music runs in short glinting fragments, the orchestra full of quick woodwind figures and sudden silences. There is a laundry basket tipped into the Thames, a midnight scene in Windsor forest with fairy music for high voices, and a closing fugue in which the whole cast sings that all the world is a joke.","Verdi was seventy-nine when he finished it, having insisted for fifty years that he would never attempt comedy again after a failure in 1840. Boito supplied the libretto and coaxed him through the work; the two men wrote to each other about it as a private amusement, with Verdi repeatedly threatening to abandon the thing. It was first performed at La Scala in February 1893, eight years before his death.","The final number is a strict academic fugue, the form Verdi had been told as a young man he had not mastered, placed at the end of his life as both a joke and a demonstration. Listen for the orchestra mocking characters behind their backs with a trill or a dropped phrase, and for the single strand of pure lyricism given to the young lovers, deliberately cut short every time it appears.","Audiences applauded and were also puzzled: there were few tunes to take home, and early box office was modest. Toscanini championed it and made its reputation in the twentieth century. It is now routinely called the finest Italian comic opera after Rossini's, though some critics argue that its brilliance is more admirable than lovable. Performance debate is about tempo, since the score can sound frantic if pushed.","Listen to the last five minutes first, the fugue, then start at the beginning and hear how the whole opera has been preparing that shrug. Follow the young lovers' snatched phrases, which never get a full song. Compare Don Pasquale for what Verdi is refusing to do. A crisp, chamber-scaled recording serves it better than a heavy one."],"boito-mefistofele":["A prologue, four acts and an epilogue, about two and three-quarter hours, covering both parts of Goethe's Faust: the seduction and death of Margherita, and the Greek episode with Helen of Troy that other composers leave out. It begins in heaven with offstage brass, chorus and boys' voices, and ends there. The title role is a bass who whistles on stage. Choral fresco matters far more here than solo display.","Boito was twenty-six, a leader of the Milanese Scapigliatura, the young artists who despised what they saw as the complacency of Italian opera and looked north to Germany. He wrote his own libretto, as he always did. The 1868 premiere at La Scala lasted more than five hours, provoked fistfights and was withdrawn after two performances; he cut and rewrote it heavily for Bologna in 1875, where it succeeded.","The devil's whistle is written into the score, not a stage tradition. Boito removed a great deal in 1875, including much of Faust's own music, which is why the tenor is the least developed of the three principals. He then spent the rest of his life on a second opera, Nerone, and never finished it: the polemical young man became the librettist who supplied Verdi with Otello and Falstaff.","The 1868 disaster was as much a cultural quarrel as a musical judgment, since Boito had publicly insulted the older generation and the audience repaid him. The revision's success established the work in Italy, where it has hovered at the edge of the repertory ever since, kept alive by great basses. Critics generally rate the libretto above the music, a verdict his later career rather supports.","Start with the prologue in heaven, the most ambitious thing in the score: offstage bands, chorus, and a long orchestral build. Then hear the prison scene for Margherita. Compare Gounod's Faust, which keeps only the love story and drops the metaphysics. Look for a recording with a bass who relishes both the whistle and the sneer."],"ponchielli-la-gioconda":["Four acts, close to three hours, in seventeenth-century Venice: a street singer sacrifices herself for a rival, a blind mother is saved from a mob, a spy poisons his wife's supposed corpse, and there is a ballet, the Dance of the Hours, that everyone knows without knowing its source. The score piles on effects, from a bass aria of jealous rage to an offstage barcarolle and a monastery scene, with strong tunes and heavy orchestral colour.","Ponchielli was a provincial bandmaster and church musician who came late to major success. The libretto is by Arrigo Boito under the anagram Tobia Gorrio, adapted from Victor Hugo's Angelo. It opened at La Scala in 1876, five years after Aida, and was revised twice before reaching its final shape in 1880. As professor of composition in Milan, Ponchielli taught both Puccini and Mascagni.","The Dance of the Hours is a working plot device, an entertainment staged at a villain's party while the conspiracy tightens, not a decorative divertissement, though it is almost always heard alone. Listen for how Boito's text pushes Ponchielli toward long through-composed scenes: the fourth act is nearly continuous. The bass aria has the shape of a Verdi monologue with darker instrumentation under it.","It was a great success and remains the only Ponchielli opera in the repertory; critics have always treated it as transitional, more skilful than original. Its twentieth-century popularity owed much to Callas, whose first complete commercial recording it was, and to the ballet's afterlife in cartoons and light arrangements. Some argue it deserves better than its reputation as a vehicle for six large voices.","Hear the Dance of the Hours in its place, surrounded by plot, and it stops being a cartoon soundtrack. Then take Alvise's aria and the soprano's fourth-act monologue. Compare Aida for scale and Cavalleria rusticana for what came next. A cast of six genuinely large voices is what this opera actually needs."],"mascagni-cavalleria-rusticana":["One act, about seventy-five minutes, in a Sicilian village on Easter morning. A soldier home from service resumes an affair with a married woman, is challenged by her husband, and is killed offstage; the news arrives as a scream from the crowd. The orchestral intermezzo between the two halves is a slow, warm string melody played while the stage stands empty. Before the violence, the chorus sings an Easter hymn with organ.","Mascagni was twenty-six, conducting a small municipal band in Puglia, when he entered a one-act opera competition run by the publisher Sonzogno in 1889. He set a story by Giovanni Verga, whose stark tales of Sicilian poverty had given Italian literature the word verismo. He won, the Rome premiere in May 1890 caused a sensation, and he spent the remaining fifty-five years of his life failing to repeat it.","The tenor sings his first number before the curtain rises, offstage and accompanied only by a harp, a serenade in Sicilian dialect that places the whole opera geographically before anything is seen. Listen for the fact that the intermezzo's tune is not new: it comes from the Easter hymn we have just heard, taken out of church. The murder itself is never shown, only reported by a woman's shriek.","It was the biggest operatic success of its decade and made Sonzogno's fortune. Critics soon complained that verismo substituted sensation for craft, and Mascagni's later obscurity, together with his compromised position under Fascism, coloured judgment further. The intermezzo's ubiquity in films has damaged the work as much as it has helped. Recent scholarship treats the compression and the use of ritual as deliberate rather than crude.","Listen to the offstage serenade before the curtain and then to the Easter hymn, so that the intermezzo arrives as a memory rather than a tune. Then follow the duet between the two women. It is nearly always paired with Pagliacci, so hear both in one sitting. Any recording with genuinely idiomatic Italian choral singing is worth having."],"leoncavallo-pagliacci":["A prologue and two acts, about seventy-five minutes. A travelling theatre troupe arrives in a Calabrian village; the leader discovers his wife's affair and, performing a comedy about a cuckolded clown that evening, breaks character and stabs her in front of the audience. A baritone steps through the curtain before it starts to explain that actors are people too. The famous tenor aria comes as the clown puts on his costume and weeps.","Leoncavallo, a Neapolitan who had scraped a living as a cafe pianist, wrote his own libretto after Cavalleria rusticana's success made short violent operas commercially irresistible. He claimed the story came from a murder trial his father, a magistrate, had presided over. Sonzogno published it; the premiere in Milan in May 1892 was conducted by Toscanini, then twenty-five and near the start of his career.","The prologue is a manifesto: it announces that the author has taken his story from life, which was the whole verismo programme, and it quotes two themes from the opera to come. The closing line, that the comedy is ended, is written in the score for the baritone but is almost always sung by the tenor, a tradition Caruso is usually blamed for. A plagiarism claim over the plot dogged Leoncavallo for years.","It succeeded immediately and has been inseparable from Cavalleria rusticana ever since; the double bill dates from 1893 in New York. Critics have generally rated Leoncavallo below Mascagni in refinement and above him in theatrical instinct, and his later career, including a rival Boheme that lost to Puccini's, sank quickly. The clown's aria is so famous that the whole opera is hard to hear freshly.","Listen to the prologue first as an argument, not a warm-up. Then take the play within the play in the second half, and notice the moment the orchestra abandons the pretty commedia music. Compare it directly with Cavalleria rusticana in the same evening. Any recording with a tenor who resists sobbing through the big aria is preferable."],"giordano-andrea-chenier":["Four acts, just under two hours, on the poet executed in 1794. An aristocratic party, a revolutionary tribunal, and a final duet sung in a prison courtyard as the carts wait outside. Giordano writes long surging tenor lines over thick strings, and threads authentic revolutionary tunes, the Carmagnole and the Marseillaise among them, through the crowd scenes, so that the politics arrives as noise from the street rather than as argument.","Giordano was twenty-eight and nearly destitute; an earlier opera had failed and his publisher had dropped him. The librettist Luigi Illica, who also worked for Puccini, gave him a subject with a real historical figure at a moment when Italy was preoccupied with its own political violence. It opened at La Scala in March 1896, six weeks after Puccini's La boheme, and made Giordano's name at once.","The servant Gerard's transformation is the opera's real argument: he begins as a footman resenting his masters and ends as a prosecutor who cannot bring himself to use his power, and his third-act monologue is the strongest writing in the score. Listen for the eighteenth-century dance music at the opening party, which stops mid-phrase when the poet begins to speak, and never returns.","It was a hit and stayed in the Italian repertory, sustained by tenors from Caruso to Corelli to Domingo who relish its four big solos. Critics have often called it crude beside Puccini, and the historical Chenier's poetry is barely present in it. Interest has revived through Gerard, whose politics productions now take seriously, and through the score's documentary use of real revolutionary song.","Skip ahead to Gerard's third-act monologue before you hear the tenor's famous first-act improvisation; it will change how you hear the rest. Listen for the revolutionary tunes surfacing in the crowd. Then take the final duet, which is short and unusually direct. Recordings by Corelli or Domingo show what the tenor writing is for."],"cilea-adriana-lecouvreur":["Four acts, about two and a quarter hours, in the Paris theatre world of 1730. A famous actress and a princess love the same man; the princess sends a bouquet of poisoned violets. Cilea's sound is lighter than most verismo, with chamber-scaled woodwind, restrained strings and recurring melodies attached to characters. The heroine's first aria has her declare that she is only the servant of the poet's words, sung almost conversationally, and she later declaims Racine on stage as an insult.","Cilea wrote it for Milan in 1902, at the height of the vogue for verismo, from a play by Scribe about the real actress Adrienne Lecouvreur, who died suddenly in 1730 amid rumours of poisoning. Enrico Caruso created the tenor role at twenty-nine, just before his international career took off. Cilea taught for most of his life, wrote only one further opera, and outlived his own movement by four decades.","The recurring melodies work like calling cards: the actress, the soldier and the princess each have one, and Cilea reshapes rather than merely repeats them. Notice how much of the vocal writing sits in a half-spoken middle register, closer to stage declamation than to aria, which is right for an opera about an actress. The poisoned violets have their own quiet figure, first heard as a gift of love.","It succeeded and then drifted, kept alive largely by sopranos, among them Muzio, Olivero, Tebaldi, Scotto and Gheorghiu, who find in it a role that rewards acting as much as tone. Critics have called the plot creaky and the last act sentimental, and few defend the ballet. The counter-argument is that the score's restraint and its portrait of theatrical life are genuinely unusual for the moment.","Listen to the entrance aria and note how little Cilea lets the singer show off. Then the confrontation between the two women, and finally the death scene. Compare it with Tosca, written two years earlier, to hear how differently the same world can sound. Any recording built around a great actress-soprano is the right one."],"puccini-la-boheme":["Four acts, about an hour and fifty, among students in a Paris garret. The first act is mostly banter, a stove fed with a manuscript and a landlord thrown out, until a neighbour knocks for a light and two arias and a duet follow within fifteen minutes. The second act is a crowded Christmas Eve street scene with children's chorus and a military band. The last act repeats the first act's jokes, then Mimi dies while nobody is looking.","Puccini and his librettists Illica and Giacosa worked from Murger's Scenes of Bohemian Life, quarrelling for three years. Leoncavallo was setting the same book and the two composers fell out publicly; Puccini's version reached Turin first, in February 1896, conducted by the twenty-eight-year-old Toscanini. Puccini had known real poverty as a student in Milan and said he was writing partly from memory.","The last act is built out of the first, the same tunes and the same jokes, now hollow; and at the moment of death the orchestra plays Mimi's first-act melody while Rodolfo is still talking, so the audience knows before he does. Listen for the passages where the two voices move in bare octaves, a Puccini fingerprint. The manuscript burned for warmth in the opening scene is kept from the novel.","Turin's critics were lukewarm and the public was not; within three years it was everywhere. The persistent charge, made by Benjamin Britten among others, is that Puccini manipulates his listener. The defence is that the manipulation is a technique of extraordinary precision, and analysis of the score's motivic economy has largely won the argument. Toscanini's 1946 recording, fifty years after he conducted the premiere, remains a document of first importance.","Listen to the first fifteen minutes for the conversation rather than the tunes, so that the arias arrive as an interruption. Then compare the opening of the fourth act with the opening of the first. Notice the orchestra giving the news of Mimi's death before any character speaks. Toscanini's recording, or any with unforced young voices, suits it."],"puccini-tosca":["Three acts, about two hours, in Rome on a single day in June 1800. A painter hides an escaped prisoner, a police chief tortures him and demands the singer Tosca's body as the price of his life, she stabs the chief, and the promised mock execution turns out to be real. It opens with three loud chords for the villain before a note of anything else, and closes with the heroine jumping from the battlements.","Puccini pursued Sardou's play for years and finally secured it after another composer gave it up. The action is tied to real Roman locations and to a real day in the Napoleonic wars, and Puccini went to Rome to check the exact pitch of the city's church bells for the dawn scene. The premiere at the Teatro Costanzi in January 1900 took place under bomb threats and a nervous police presence.","The third act begins with a shepherd boy singing in Roman dialect over a soundscape of matins bells that Puccini transcribed on site and tuned to the actual instruments: a documentary recording in orchestral form. Scarpia's three chords belong to no key and simply sit next to each other, which is why they land like a door slamming. The tenor's last aria was rewritten after his librettists objected to it.","Joseph Kerman's phrase about a shabby little shocker has followed the opera since 1956 and has been answered more often than agreed with. Early critics disliked the violence; audiences never did. The modern discussion is about Scarpia, and how much sadism a production may show, and about Tosca herself, whose aria protesting that she has only ever lived for art and love reads as both self-pity and self-knowledge.","Listen to the first thirty seconds, the three chords, then wait for them to come back. Take the whole of the second act in one sitting: it is a single unbroken increase of pressure. Then the dawn bells at the start of the third. Callas's scenes with Tito Gobbi remain the benchmark for the acting side of this piece."],"puccini-madama-butterfly":["Three acts, about two and a half hours, in Nagasaki. An American naval officer marries a fifteen-year-old geisha under a lease he can break monthly, leaves, and returns three years later with an American wife to collect the child; Butterfly kills herself. The long vigil contains a wordless humming chorus while she watches through the night, and the score threads real Japanese melodies and the opening of an American anthem through Western harmony.","Puccini saw the play in London in 1900 without understanding the English and knew at once that he wanted it. He researched Japanese music through recordings, a book of transcriptions and conversations with the wife of the Japanese ambassador in Rome. Composition was interrupted by a car crash in 1903 that left him with a broken leg and a diagnosis of diabetes. The first performance at La Scala in February 1904 was destroyed by an organised hostile claque.","Puccini quotes a number of authentic Japanese tunes, and uses the opening of The Star-Spangled Banner as Pinkerton's calling card, a sour joke most audiences hear without registering. The vigil is staged in real time: the humming chorus, then an orchestral dawn, then birdsong, with no action at all for several minutes. The revision of 1906 softened Pinkerton and split the enormously long second act.","The Milan fiasco was almost certainly organised by rivals; three months later, revised and given in Brescia, the opera triumphed. Puccini made five versions in all, and which to perform is a live question, since the harsher 1904 original has advocates. The politics are now central to discussion: whether the work endorses the fantasy it depicts or exposes it, and how a production should handle the racial dynamics.","Listen to the entrance of Butterfly with her friends, a long offstage crescendo of women's voices climbing before she appears. Then the humming chorus and the dawn that follows, in one uninterrupted stretch. Notice the American anthem whenever Pinkerton is pleased with himself. A soprano who sounds young in the first act and shattered in the last is the ideal."],"puccini-turandot":["Three acts, about two hours, in a fairy-tale Peking. A princess beheads any suitor who fails her three riddles; an unknown prince answers them, then offers her his name and his life if she can discover it before dawn. The slave girl who loves him dies under torture rather than tell. The sound is harder than earlier Puccini, with grinding parallel dissonances, gongs and bells, a huge chorus, and three ministers who sing in fast brittle counterpoint.","Puccini began it in 1920, after the First World War had changed the musical world around him, and worked on it while ill with the throat cancer that killed him in November 1924. He left the final duet incomplete, sketched but unorchestrated. Toscanini conducted the premiere at La Scala in April 1926 and, at the point where Puccini stopped writing, laid down the baton and told the audience the composer had died there.","Puccini took Chinese tunes from a music box belonging to a friend who had served in China, and the folk song Mo Li Hua becomes the princess's own music. The slave girl Liu, who has no counterpart in the source play, was Puccini's invention; her death is the last music he completed, and many listeners hear the whole opera tilting toward her rather than toward the title character.","Franco Alfano's completion, cut down by Toscanini, was standard for decades and was long criticised as inadequate. Luciano Berio wrote a subtler alternative in 2001, and some conductors now simply stop where Puccini stopped. The larger debate is whether the ending can work at all: an opera about a woman thawed by a kiss, written by a dying man who could not decide how she should change.","Listen to Liu's two arias and her death scene, then to the riddle scene for the sheer noise. Notice the clattering, dissonant music for the three ministers, unlike anything else Puccini wrote. Then hear where the composer's own music stops. Compare Alfano's ending with Berio's if you can find both on record."],"auber-la-muette-de-portici":["Five acts on the Naples fish-market revolt of 1647, and the first opera whose heroine never sings: Fenella is a mute girl played by a dancer, her thoughts carried by mime and by orchestral melody. Around her Auber sets choral crowds, a market scene, a sleeping-scene barcarolle, a tarantella and the sword duet 'Amour sacré de la patrie'. It ends with Vesuvius in eruption and Fenella throwing herself into the lava.","Auber wrote it for the Paris Opéra, where Eugène Scribe's librettos and the machinist's workshop mattered as much as the singers. February 1828, two years before the July Revolution: a chorus of working men on stage singing about liberty was new and combustible. In August 1830 a Brussels performance sent the audience into the streets and helped start the Belgian revolution — the only opera ever credited with founding a country.","Listen for how Auber gives Fenella a voice she does not have. Each of her mimed speeches is a labelled orchestral tune, so a returning clarinet or violin phrase means she is repeating an accusation the audience has already heard. The device is an ancestor of the leitmotif, and Wagner, who wrote admiringly of the score, was paying attention.","The premiere was a hit that held the stage for decades, then vanished; by 1900 the work survived mainly as a footnote about Brussels. Modern revivals argue over the ballet, over cuts to the long processional finales, and over whether Fenella should be a dancer or an actress. Critics who return to the score are usually surprised by the speed and hardness of the choral writing.","Start at the Act 2 market chorus and the duet that follows, the two men swearing revenge, to hear where French opera got its taste for crowds. Then watch a staged version rather than listening blind: Fenella's scenes make little sense as sound alone. Compare the eruption finale with the end of Meyerbeer's Le prophète."],"meyerbeer-robert-le-diable":["Five acts about a Norman duke's son whose father turns out to be a devil. Meyerbeer surrounds him with a gambling scene, a sicilienne, a huge third act in the ruined cloister of Sainte-Rosalie where dead nuns rise from their tombs and dance, and a final trio in which mother, lover and demon pull Robert three ways. The scoring is dark — bassoons, low brass, timpani rolls — and the whole runs close to three and a half hours.","Paris, November 1831, a year after the July Revolution, at an Opéra newly run as a commercial venture by Louis Véron. Meyerbeer, a Berlin banker's son who had learned his trade writing Italian operas, put his own money behind the production. Gaslit sets, Marie Taglioni dancing the ghostly abbess, and Adolphe Nourrit's tenor made it the most profitable French opera of its era.","The ballet of the nuns is scored at first almost without violins — violas, cellos and low winds, with harp and brass entering as the tombs open — so its effect is a matter of colour rather than melody. Listen too for the cor anglais that shadows Alice, the good foster-sister, everywhere she appears; it is the score's one warm timbre in a work otherwise built from cold ones.","It made Meyerbeer the richest and most imitated composer in Europe and was played thousands of times; Chopin wrote home about it with astonishment. Then the twentieth century turned on him, partly through Wagner's antisemitic polemic, and the operas left the repertory for a century. Recent recordings and stagings have restored the ballet and the uncut fifth act, and the argument now is whether the drama can carry the spectacle.","Go straight to Act 3: the cloister scene is where opera learned to be frightening, and it still works. Then hear Bertram's invocation 'Nonnes, qui reposez' for the sound of a bass voice used as a supernatural instrument. A modern recording with the full ballet is worth the length; compare the nuns with the Wolf's Glen in Weber's Der Freischütz."],"halevy-la-juive":["Five acts set in Constance in 1414, during the church council that burned Jan Hus. Éléazar, a Jewish goldsmith, has raised a Christian cardinal's lost daughter as his own; the opera ends with her thrown into a cauldron of boiling oil as he tells the cardinal whose child she was. Halévy writes a Passover seder of quiet chamber intimacy inside a work otherwise built on processions, bells, anvils and massed choruses.","Commissioned by the Paris Opéra under Véron and premiered in February 1835, with real horses in the first-act procession and a budget that made it the most expensive production the house had mounted. Halévy, born Élie Halfon Lévy to an Ashkenazi family in Paris, was writing about religious persecution less than fifty years after French Jews had gained civil rights, for an audience that read the piece as a plea for tolerance.","The tenor aria 'Rachel, quand du Seigneur' was reportedly added late at Nourrit's urging, and its cor anglais obbligato gives Éléazar the only line in the opera that sounds unaccompanied. Listen also to the seder in Act 2: Halévy sets the Hebrew-flavoured chant in bare unison, then lets a Christian tune outside the window cut across it. The clash of textures is the whole plot in thirty seconds.","It held the Opéra's stage for a century — over five hundred performances — and Wagner and Mahler both admired it. It then disappeared, partly for the cost of staging it, partly because European audiences after 1945 found its subject unbearable. Revivals since the 1970s have restored music cut almost immediately after the premiere, and Éléazar has become a role tenors take as a summit late in a career.","Begin with Éléazar's Act 4 aria, then go back for the Act 2 seder that prepares it. Caruso's recording of 'Rachel', made in his final year, is the reason many people know the opera at all. Compare the crowd writing with Meyerbeer's Les Huguenots of the following year; the two share a librettist and a theology of intolerance."],"meyerbeer-les-huguenots":["Five acts ending in the St Bartholomew's Day massacre of 1572. Meyerbeer builds the first two acts as comedy and courtly display — a drinking song, a bathing chorus, a page's rondo — then narrows to the great fourth-act love duet and the blessing of the daggers, where three choirs of Catholic nobles consecrate the murder to come. Luther's chorale 'Ein feste Burg' threads through the whole as the Protestant signature. Uncut it runs close to four hours.","Paris Opéra, February 1836. Meyerbeer worked at it for five years, revising as the cast changed, and the production demanded seven principal singers of the first rank — the 'night of the seven stars' became the test of whether a house could mount it at all. France in the 1830s had a live argument about religious tolerance, and Scribe's libretto keeps the politics blurred enough for everyone to feel implicated.","Two rare instruments do the dramatic work. Raoul's first-act romance is accompanied by a viola d'amore, an obsolete instrument Meyerbeer revived for its sweet, slightly antique sound. In Act 5 a bass clarinet, barely used in opera before, plays the long solo under Marcel's blessing of the lovers. Listen too for how 'Ein feste Burg' arrives fully harmonised only once the killing has started.","For seventy years it was among the most performed operas in the world; the Paris Opéra gave it more than a thousand times. Wagner learned his stagecraft from it and then spent his career denying it. The twentieth century judged Meyerbeer's manner calculating and dropped him. Recordings from the 1960s onward, and complete stagings since, have restored the case for the fourth act as one of the strongest in nineteenth-century opera.","Go to Act 4 first: the conspiracy chorus, then the duet — roughly forty minutes that need no context. If it grips you, start again from the beginning for the comedy that makes the ending land. Look for a recording that keeps the ballet and the third-act street scene rather than the old heavily cut versions."],"berlioz-benvenuto-cellini":["Two acts about the Renaissance goldsmith casting his bronze Perseus under threat of arrest. Berlioz writes carnival crowds in Rome's Piazza Colonna that split into competing metres, a pantomime played on stage while the real plot happens around it, and a foundry finale where the workmen throw Cellini's own silver into the furnace to complete the pour. Fast, loud and crammed with instrumental detail; about two and three quarter hours.","Berlioz's first opera, staged at the Paris Opéra in September 1838 after a difficult rehearsal period; the players reportedly resisted the rhythms and the singers the tessitura. It survived a handful of performances. Berlioz was thirty-four, living on journalism, and had chosen a hero who was a defiant artist harried by patrons — a self-portrait obvious to everyone in the house. Liszt revived a rewritten version at Weimar in 1852.","The carnival scene puts a stage band in a different metre from the pit and asks the audience to hear both, with a chorus counting off the bells of the Ave Maria over it. Berlioz salvaged the best of this into two concert overtures, so the Roman Carnival Overture is not an introduction to the opera but a rescue from it; its long cor anglais tune comes from Cellini's first-act love music.","Reviewers in 1838 called it unsingable and the Opéra dropped it; Berlioz blamed a cabal and was probably not wrong. Two performing versions now compete — the original Paris score and the shorter Weimar revision — and conductors mix them. Since Colin Davis's recordings the piece has been treated as a flawed, exhilarating work rather than an embarrassment, though it remains hard to cast.","Start with the overture, then the first-act carnival finale to hear the same material at full size with voices. Cellini's aria 'Sur les monts les plus sauvages' shows the lyrical Berlioz. Pick a version and stay with it; a recording under Colin Davis or John Nelson will tell you which edition it uses."],"adolphe-adam-giselle":["Two acts. A peasant girl loves a disguised nobleman, learns he is engaged elsewhere, goes mad and dies; in the second act she rises among the Wilis, ghosts of jilted brides who dance men to death, and saves him from them. Adam writes bright, danceable numbers for the village act and then close to an hour of pale, sustained string writing for the moonlit graveyard. About two hours with an interval.","Paris Opéra, June 1841, choreographed by Jean Coralli and Jules Perrot for Carlotta Grisi. Théophile Gautier built the scenario from a paragraph in Heine about Slavic ghost-brides. Adam, a fluent theatre professional, wrote the score at speed. Gas lighting, trapdoors and the new pointe technique made the ballet blanc possible: a corps of women in white who appear to float rather than step.","Adam gives characters recurring tunes and then breaks them. In the mad scene Giselle's own love melody returns distorted and in the wrong key, played by a solo instrument while she stops dancing altogether — one of the first places a ballet score carries psychology rather than steps. Listen for the viola solo in Act 2 that comes back from Act 1 as a memory, thinner and slower.","It has never left the stage, unusual for an 1840s ballet, but the score has been cut, reordered and interpolated for nearly two centuries — the Petipa revisions in St Petersburg and Minkus's added numbers among them. Musicologists now separate Adam's 1841 text from the accretions, and companies choose. Critics who dismiss the music as merely pretty tend not to have heard Act 2 without the dancing.","Watch rather than listen, at least the first time: the second act is the reason the piece exists, and the moment the Wilis enter is one of theatre's great changes of temperature. Then hear the mad scene again for how the orchestra tells you her mind is going. Compare with the lakeside acts of Swan Lake, which grew out of this."],"berlioz-la-damnation-de-faust":["Four parts, about two hours, for tenor Faust, bass Méphistophélès, mezzo Marguerite, chorus and a large orchestra. It is a concert opera: scenes without connecting action — Hungarian plains, a Leipzig beer cellar with a fugue on the word Amen, a dream of sylphs, Marguerite's ballad of the king of Thule, a headlong ride to the abyss with a chorus of gibbering demons, and a final apotheosis with harps.","Berlioz began with eight Faust scenes in 1828, then wrote most of the rest on tour — in coaches, in German inns — and premiered the whole at the Opéra-Comique in December 1846. Paris stayed away. The losses were severe enough to push him into a concert tour of Russia to recover. Goethe reached him through Gérard de Nerval's translation, a book he carried for years.","The Hungarian March is in the piece for a commercial reason: Berlioz had scored the Rákóczi tune for a concert in Pest, it brought the house down, so he moved Faust to Hungary in the opening scene to justify keeping it. Listen also to the demons' chorus, sung in an infernal language Berlioz invented and wrote out himself, and to 'D'amour l'ardente flamme', shadowed by a cor anglais that never lets Marguerite finish.","The 1846 failure hurt Berlioz badly and he blamed Parisian indifference. The work returned through Édouard Colonne's concerts and became a choral-society staple. Raoul Gunsbourg staged it in Monte Carlo in 1893, and the argument over whether it belongs in a theatre has run ever since — Berlioz called it a dramatic legend and made no provision for scenery.","Start with the Ride to the Abyss and the Pandaemonium that follows, then go back for the beer-cellar fugue to hear Berlioz being funny. The Dance of the Sylphs is the quietest orchestral music he wrote. A French-language cast matters here more than usual; the prosody is half the characterisation."],"meyerbeer-le-prophete":["Five acts on the Anabaptist takeover of Münster in 1534. An innkeeper is talked into playing a prophet-king by three agitators, crowned in the cathedral while his own mother is made to deny she knows him, and blows up the palace with everyone inside at the end. The score's centre is Fidès, a contralto mother's part of unusual size, and a Coronation March that became a nineteenth-century standard.","Paris Opéra, April 1849, months after the 1848 revolutions had swept Europe and collapsed; an opera about a popular uprising run by cynics landed on a nervous city. Meyerbeer had held the score back for years waiting for the right contralto and got Pauline Viardot. The production used carbon arc lamps for a sunrise — an early use of electric light in a theatre — and put the corps de ballet on roller skates to mime skating.","Fidès is the real protagonist and Meyerbeer knew it: she has the prison scene, the cathedral denial and a final duet closer to a mother's argument than a love scene. Listen for the way the Coronation March keeps returning in fragments, hollowed out, once the crowd has been betrayed. Viardot reportedly asked for changes as they rehearsed, and the part sits unusually wide as a result.","It was an immediate international success and then the first Meyerbeer opera dismissed as machinery; Wagner's Opera and Drama attacks it directly. Twentieth-century revivals were rare and usually cut. Recordings from the 1970s with Marilyn Horne as Fidès made the case that the contralto writing alone justifies the piece. Stagings still argue over the skating ballet, which can look absurd or prophetic.","Hear Fidès's Act 5 scene 'O prêtres de Baal' first, then the cathedral coronation for the sound of a crowd being managed. The march is familiar from wedding programmes and brass arrangements; hearing it in place, with the chorus and the mother's interruption, changes it entirely. Look for a recording that keeps the third act complete."],"offenbach-orphee-aux-enfers":["An opéra bouffe in two acts, later expanded to four, in which Orpheus is a bored violin teacher, Eurydice is glad to be abducted, and the Olympian gods go on strike over a diet of nectar. A character called Public Opinion forces the husband to go and fetch his wife. The score parodies everything to hand — Gluck, patriotic anthems, ballroom dances — and ends with the galop infernal, the tune the world knows as the can-can.","Bouffes-Parisiens, October 1858, a small theatre Offenbach ran himself under a licence that had long limited him to a handful of singers on stage. Paris under Napoleon III was rich, censored and cynical, and an operetta showing the gods as venal bureaucrats was read at once as the Second Empire in fancy dress. A hostile review accusing him of profaning antiquity gave the run the publicity it needed.","Offenbach quotes Gluck's 'Che farò senza Euridice' straight, so the most solemn tune in French operatic memory becomes a joke about a husband delighted to be rid of his wife. Listen for the fly duet, where Jupiter, disguised as an insect, courts Eurydice in buzzing — a virtuoso number built almost entirely from one repeated consonant.","It ran for well over two hundred nights and made Offenbach's fortune. Nineteenth-century moralists disapproved; later writers took it seriously as a diagnosis of its society. The can-can's afterlife in cabaret and cartoon has obscured the rest of the score. Modern productions usually restore the extra numbers of the 1874 four-act version while trying to recover the original's speed.","Listen past the galop to the second-act revolt of the gods, where a chorus of deities sings a mock-Marseillaise. The overture most often played is a later arrangement, not Offenbach's; seek a complete recording in French with the spoken dialogue intact. Compare with La belle Hélène, which does the same joke with more affection."],"berlioz-les-troyens":["Five acts and about four hours, in two halves: the fall of Troy, with Cassandra watching a city refuse to believe her, and Dido's Carthage, where Aeneas breaks off a love affair to sail for Italy. Berlioz writes an orchestral Royal Hunt and Storm with off-stage horns and nymphs, a septet over a quiet sea, a night duet, and a Trojan March that returns three times, meaning something different each time.","Written between 1856 and 1858 in Paris, to Berlioz's own libretto after Virgil, whom he had read as a boy in his father's study. No French theatre would stage it whole. He accepted a cut version of the Carthage half at the Théâtre Lyrique in November 1863, watched it trimmed further through the run, and never heard the Troy acts at all. Princess Carolyne Sayn-Wittgenstein had pushed him to write it.","The Trojan March is the work's memory. It arrives as a triumph while the wooden horse is dragged in, comes back in the minor as the survivors leave the ruins, and returns off stage at the very end, distant, as Dido dies cursing Rome. Listen too for the lovers' duet 'Nuit d'ivresse', whose text Berlioz lifted from The Merchant of Venice rather than Virgil.","For a century the piece existed only in fragments and a reputation; the first complete staging in a single evening came in the twentieth century, and Colin Davis's 1969 recording made it repertory. Scholars have since untangled Berlioz's own cuts from the theatre's. The argument now is casting: the work needs a dramatic soprano for Cassandra, a mezzo for Dido and a heroic French tenor in the same building.","Do not start at the beginning. Try the Royal Hunt and Storm, then the love duet, then Dido's final monologue; if those hold you, the four hours will. A complete recording under Colin Davis or John Nelson is the standard entry. Compare the Troy acts with Gluck, whose heir Berlioz believed himself to be."],"gounod-faust":["Five acts. An old scholar sells his soul for youth, seduces Marguerite, kills her brother, and loses her to a prison and a final trio in which she refuses to escape with him. Gounod's manner is lyric rather than grand: the Jewel Song, a garden scene built as one long unbroken duet, a soldiers' chorus, and a church scene where Marguerite's prayer is answered by a hostile organ and a demonic bass.","Théâtre Lyrique, March 1859, with spoken dialogue and a company far smaller than the Opéra's. Gounod had spent time in a seminary and nearly took orders; the religious scenes are not decoration. Recitatives replaced the dialogue in 1860, and a Walpurgis Night ballet was added when the Paris Opéra took the work over in 1869 — the version that then travelled the world.","Marguerite's spinning-wheel ballad of the King of Thule is deliberately archaic, a plain strophic tune over bare accompaniment, and Gounod interrupts it twice with her own distracted thoughts before the jewels arrive to sweep it away. Listen for the church-scene organ, which is not accompanying her prayer but blocking it, and for Méphistophélès's serenade, whose laugh is written into the score as notes.","It became the most performed opera of the later nineteenth century — the Metropolitan Opera opened its doors with it in 1883 — and then suffered for that ubiquity. German critics complained that Goethe had been reduced to a love story and renamed it Margarethe. Recent productions have gone back to the 1859 dialogue version, which is faster and less pious, and the ballet is now usually cut.","The garden scene, roughly half an hour from 'Salut! demeure chaste et pure' to the end of Act 3, is the work in miniature. Then hear the final trio for how Gounod raises the key twice to force the ending open. A French cast and a conductor who keeps the tempos moving make the case; older, slower recordings can turn it into a hymn."],"berlioz-beatrice-et-benedict":["Two acts, about an hour and a half, made from the comic half of Much Ado About Nothing: two people who mock love are tricked into admitting they are in love, and marry protesting. Spoken dialogue links the numbers. The score's centre is not a joke but a nocturne for Héro and Ursule, two women's voices over muted strings at dusk, and a wedding-day sicilienne that sounds like relief.","Berlioz's last work, written for the opening of a new theatre at Baden-Baden in August 1862, commissioned by the impresario Édouard Bénazet, who had been paying him well for years. He was fifty-eight, ill, and had just been widowed a second time; he conducted the premiere himself. He wrote his own libretto, cut Shakespeare's villain and his plot entirely, and invented a pedantic music master, Somarone, to mock choir directors.","Somarone's wedding ode is a joke aimed at Berlioz's own profession: a laboured fugue rehearsed on stage, with its composer stopping the singers to demand more expression. Listen for how the nocturne avoids a full cadence for minutes at a time so the evening never quite closes, and for the overture's habit of starting a tune and refusing to finish it — the same refusal the two lovers keep making.","It succeeded at Baden-Baden and was liked in Germany, then sat permanently in the shadow of Les Troyens. Critics have long argued whether the spoken dialogue should be trimmed, translated or replaced, and some conductors perform it as a concert piece with narration. The consensus now is that its lightness is deliberate and hard-won, written by a man with little left to prove and less time.","Start with the duo-nocturne 'Nuit paisible et sereine' — among the loveliest ten minutes Berlioz wrote, and it needs no plot. Then the overture, then Béatrice's second-act aria. A recording with French speakers is worth hunting for; the timing of the dialogue carries the comedy."],"bizet-les-pecheurs-de-perles":["Three acts on a beach in Ceylon: two friends who once swore off the same woman meet her again as a veiled priestess, and one of them betrays the oath. The famous number is their first-act duet 'Au fond du temple saint', whose long unison melody returns at the end. Around it Bizet writes a nocturnal invocation for the priestess, choral prayers to Brahma, and a fire at the finale.","Théâtre Lyrique, September 1863. Bizet was twenty-four, back from Rome on the Prix de Rome stipend, and the theatre's director Léon Carvalho was obliged to stage a work by a Prix winner. The librettists relocated a Mexican scenario to Ceylon because exotic settings sold; almost nothing in the score is Sinhalese. Bizet was paid poorly and the piece closed after eighteen performances.","The great duet's ending is not Bizet's. He wrote a fast, agitated close; the reprise that lets the two men sing the big tune again in harmony was pasted in for an 1893 revival, eighteen years after his death, and it is the version almost everyone knows. Listen also to Nadir's 'Je crois entendre encore', floating in a high half-voice over muted strings with almost no bass line to stand on.","Berlioz reviewed it kindly, most others did not, and it vanished until posthumous revivals rewrote it. For a century the performing material was corrupt — added recitatives, altered endings, transposed numbers. Critical editions since the 1970s have restored Bizet's text, and conductors must now decide whether to give audiences the duet ending they expect or the one Bizet actually wrote.","Everyone starts with the duet; start instead with Leïla's second-act cavatina and Nadir's romance, then come to the duet already knowing the two voices. Choose a recording that says which edition it uses. Compare the temple choruses with Delibes's Lakmé, which borrowed the recipe twenty years later."],"offenbach-la-belle-helene":["Three acts in which Helen of Sparta, resigned to a fate already written for her, waits for Paris to arrive and take her to Troy. Offenbach gives her one serious invocation to Venus, then surrounds her with a foolish husband, two Ajaxes who sing in matched idiocy, a high priest running a swindle, and a parlour game of goose played by kings. About two and a quarter hours with dialogue.","Théâtre des Variétés, December 1864, with Hortense Schneider, the biggest star in Paris, as Hélène. The librettists Meilhac and Halévy would later write Carmen. The Second Empire was at its most self-satisfied, and an operetta in which royalty is stupid and adultery is destiny was understood exactly as intended; the censors objected to the treatment of religion rather more than to the sex.","Paris's account of the judgement on Mount Ida is a mock-heroic ballad with a refrain, 'Evohé', that Offenbach keeps bringing back in the wrong places, so a hymn to Venus turns into a drinking cry. Listen for the trio of kings, three men singing about their own greatness in music far too small for it, and for the fact that Hélène's serious aria is the only number with no joke in the accompaniment.","It was a large success and travelled everywhere, though foreign versions bowdlerised the text. Later French critics treated Offenbach as a symptom of imperial decadence, and after the defeat of 1870 some blamed him for it. The modern view, argued by conductors such as Marc Minkowski, is that the craft is Mozartian and the satire has aged better than most of what it was mocking.","Start with the overture and Hélène's 'Amours divins', then the first-act finale. Spoken dialogue in French, delivered by performers who can time a joke, is essential — recordings that cut it lose half the piece. Compare with Orphée aux enfers for the earlier, wilder version of the same idea."],"gounod-mireille":["Five acts in Provence: a farmer's daughter loves a basket-weaver, is refused, and crosses the burning Crau desert on foot to a chapel by the sea, where she dies. Gounod builds the score from Provençal material — a farandole, a tambourin rhythm, the chanson de Magali sung as a duet — and reserves his plainest writing for the desert scene, a long stretch for one soprano and a thinning orchestra.","Théâtre Lyrique, March 1864. Gounod went to Provence to write it, staying near Saint-Rémy and reading Frédéric Mistral's epic poem Mirèio with its author, part of a movement to revive Occitan as a literary language. The tragic ending frightened the theatre; within weeks Gounod was cutting, and versions in three acts with a happy ending circulated for the next seventy years.","The Crau scene is the reason the opera exists and the first thing every revision removed. Gounod paints the heat by subtraction — high strings, silence, an off-stage shepherd's pipe — and lets Mireille hallucinate a mirage before the orchestra returns. Listen for the tambourin rhythm, a Provençal drum pattern, running under the village scenes and stopping entirely once she leaves them.","Audiences in 1864 wanted the Faust formula and did not get it. Reynaldo Hahn led a restoration in 1939 that returned to the original five acts, and a 2009 Paris production continued the recovery. The opera is now regarded, in France at least, as Gounod's most personal score, though outside France it remains rare and is usually known only through its soprano waltz.","Listen to the Chanson de Magali early on for the folk manner, then jump to the fourth-act desert scene. The valse-ariette 'O légère hirondelle' is the coloratura number but the least characteristic music in the piece. Seek out a recording of the restored five-act version rather than the old three-act arrangement."],"meyerbeer-l-africaine":["Five acts following Vasco da Gama from a Lisbon council chamber to a shipwreck and an island where the enslaved queen Sélika, who loves him, lets him go and then dies under a poisonous manchineel tree. The third act is staged on a full-size ship that is boarded and destroyed. Uncut the score runs close to four hours; the tenor's 'O paradis', sung at the sight of land, is its one universally known page.","Meyerbeer worked at it on and off for nearly thirty years and was still revising when he died in May 1864, the day after finishing the copying of the score. The Paris Opéra handed the manuscript to the Belgian critic François-Joseph Fétis, who cut about an hour, reordered scenes and renamed the piece; that version premiered in April 1865 and is the one the nineteenth century knew.","Meyerbeer's own title was Vasco de Gama, and his heroine is not African: the geography drifts between India and an unnamed island because the libretto was rewritten around star singers across decades. Listen for the ship music in Act 3, where sailors' calls, a Latin prayer and a war cry are stacked on top of one another — Meyerbeer's habitual trick of making three groups sing past each other at once.","It was the last great grand opéra success and then the clearest casualty of Meyerbeer's fall. Scholars have long known that Fétis's text is not the composer's. A performing edition of the complete original was staged at Chemnitz in 2013 under the title Vasco de Gama, running well over four hours and reviving music no audience had heard. Which version is the opera remains unsettled.","'O paradis' is the way in, but the second-act sleep scene, where Sélika sings a lullaby over the man she has been captured with, is better music. Compare the shipwreck with the storm in Les Troyens, written at almost the same time. A recording of the Chemnitz reconstruction shows exactly what the old cuts removed."],"ambroise-thomas-mignon":["Three acts from Goethe's Wilhelm Meister: a girl stolen as a child from an Italian estate, kept by a travelling troupe, rescued by a student, and eventually restored to a mad father who does not know her. Thomas writes her a plain, folk-shaped song of homesickness, 'Connais-tu le pays?', and gives the rival actress a glittering polonaise. Spoken dialogue links the numbers; about two and three quarter hours.","Opéra-Comique, November 1866, the house's characteristic mixture of song and speech aimed at a bourgeois family audience. Thomas was fifty-five, a Conservatoire professor who would soon become its director. Paris wanted Goethe softened, so Mignon marries her rescuer; when the work reached Germany the ending was changed to her death, and Thomas supplied recitatives for the Italian houses that would not tolerate spoken dialogue.","Mignon barely sings a florid note in the entire opera — Thomas defines her by withholding ornament, then surrounds her with characters who cannot stop decorating. Philine's polonaise 'Je suis Titania' is the deliberate contrast, all trills and top notes. Listen for the styrienne, a mock-Austrian dance Mignon sings in male clothing, and for the harp that follows the mad harper Lothario like a leash.","It reached its thousandth performance at the Opéra-Comique in 1894, a run few operas of the century matched, and 'Connais-tu le pays?' was sung in every drawing room in Europe. German critics were offended by the treatment of Goethe. The work faded after 1945 and now appears mainly as a vehicle for a mezzo willing to take the title role; the several endings still confuse revivals.","Hear 'Connais-tu le pays?' and Philine's polonaise back to back — the whole design of the opera is in that contrast. Then listen to the second-act finale, where the theatre burns. Marilyn Horne's recording under Antonio de Almeida makes the case; look for a version that keeps the dialogue rather than the Italian recitatives."],"gounod-romeo-et-juliette":["Five acts that reduce Shakespeare to the lovers: four duets carry the opera, from the balcony through the wedding night to the tomb, where Gounod lets Juliet wake before Romeo dies so the two can sing together one last time. A choral prologue tells the audience the ending in advance. Juliet's waltz 'Je veux vivre' and Romeo's 'Ah! lève-toi, soleil' are the set pieces.","Théâtre Lyrique, April 1867, timed for the Exposition Universelle, when Paris was full of visitors and the theatres competed for them. Gounod was returning to the librettists of Faust, Barbier and Carré, and to a formula that had made him rich. It was an immediate success, and he revised it twice more as it moved to the Opéra-Comique and then to the Opéra, adding a ballet for the larger house.","The tomb scene departs from Shakespeare deliberately: Gounod follows an eighteenth-century stage tradition, current in France through Garrick's adaptation, in which Juliet wakes in time for a last duet. Listen for how the orchestra recalls the balcony music there, slowed and moved into the lower strings, so the final duet is literally the first one dying. The prologue's chorus enters in bare, chant-like harmony before the orchestra arrives.","Critics then and since have complained that Gounod wrote four love duets and no drama, and that Mercutio and the feud are barely sketched. Audiences did not care; it entered the repertory at once and has stayed. Performance practice now debates the added ballet and the later Opéra revisions, and the altered tomb scene is sometimes trimmed back toward Shakespeare.","Start with the balcony duet, then jump to the tomb and listen for what comes back. 'Je veux vivre' is the calling card but tells you least about the opera. Singers who can float a line over the orchestra matter more than volume here; try recordings under Alain Lombard or Michel Plasson with native French casts."],"ambroise-thomas-hamlet":["Five acts. Hamlet is a baritone, Ophelia a coloratura soprano whose mad scene — twenty minutes beside a stream, the orchestra breaking into waltz fragments — is the opera's centre. Thomas keeps the ghost, the play within the play and the graveyard, and ends, in the Paris version, with Hamlet alive and crowned. A ballet, a Danish spring festival, sits in the fourth act. About three hours.","Paris Opéra, March 1868, written for two specific singers: the baritone Jean-Baptiste Faure, who wanted a Hamlet, and the Swedish soprano Christine Nilsson. Shakespeare had become fashionable in France through the English touring companies of the 1820s that also changed Berlioz's life. The Opéra's house rules required a ballet and a spectacular finale, which is why a French Hamlet ends up king.","The score carries an early orchestral saxophone part, an instrument Adolphe Sax had patented in Paris two decades before and was still trying to sell to theatres. Listen also to Ophelia's mad scene: the tunes she sings are not new but scraps of what she and Hamlet sang earlier, reassembled in the wrong order — Donizetti's technique for Lucia, applied with a French ear for orchestral colour.","It succeeded enormously and then became the standard example of French presumption toward Shakespeare; Covent Garden refused the happy ending and Thomas wrote an alternative in which Hamlet dies. The work vanished for most of the twentieth century and returned through Thomas Hampson and Simon Keenlyside, who argued that the baritone Hamlet is a real dramatic creation rather than a compromise.","Go to the mad scene first, then Hamlet's drinking song and his monologue 'Être ou ne pas être', which is short and unexpectedly bare. Decide which ending you want before choosing a recording — Richard Bonynge's, with Sherrill Milnes and Joan Sutherland, uses the Paris version. Compare Ophelia with Lucia's mad scene."],"delibes-coppelia":["Two acts and a closing divertissement. A village girl discovers that the beauty reading on Doctor Coppélius's balcony is a clockwork doll, hides in his workshop and pretends to be the doll come to life. Delibes writes character dances — a mazurka, a czardas, a bolero, a Scottish jig — and a Waltz of the Hours, all scored with a lightness French ballet music had not had before.","Paris Opéra, May 1870, choreographed by Arthur Saint-Léon to a scenario after E. T. A. Hoffmann's story of the sandman, with the uncanny horror removed. The sixteen-year-old Giuseppina Bozzacchi created Swanilda. Two months later France was at war; Saint-Léon died in September, Bozzacchi died during the siege of Paris on her seventeenth birthday, and the Opéra closed its doors.","The czardas and mazurka are not decoration: Delibes uses Hungarian and Polish dance to place Swanilda's village on a border, and the ballade of the ear of corn is a real folk-divination game set as a slow waltz. Listen for the automaton music in Act 2 — thin, ticking, deliberately mechanical scoring that Tchaikovsky studied closely before writing his own toy and doll music.","Tchaikovsky said openly that Delibes's ballets were better than his own early efforts, and Coppélia is the score that made ballet music worth a composer's full attention. It has never left the repertory, though the third-act divertissement is often cut and the male lead, originally danced by a woman in trousers, is now always a man. Restagings still argue over how sinister Coppélius should be.","Watch Act 2: the workshop scene is a comic performance for a dancer pretending to be a machine pretending to be alive. Then hear the Waltz of the Hours on its own for the orchestration. Compare with Giselle for how far ballet music travelled in thirty years, and with The Nutcracker for where it went next."],"bizet-l-arlesienne":["Twenty-seven numbers of incidental music for Alphonse Daudet's play about a Provençal farm boy destroyed by love for a woman the audience never sees. Bizet had twenty-six players, so the writing is chamber-sized and sharp: an alto saxophone as the voice of the simple-minded younger brother, a harmonium behind off-stage voices, and orchestral variations on a Provençal Christmas march. The two concert suites drawn from it run about forty minutes.","Théâtre du Vaudeville, October 1872. Daudet's play ran fifteen performances and closed; the boulevard audience wanted comedy and got a suicide. Bizet, then thirty-four and short of money, took a commission for a small pit orchestra and wrote some of his most economical music in it. He arranged the first suite for full orchestra within weeks; the second was assembled after his death by Ernest Guiraud.","The march that opens the score is a real Provençal carol, the 'Marcho dei Rèi', varied as a set of orchestral variations before it returns. In Guiraud's second suite the Farandole superimposes that march on a whirling dance tune so the two run at once — a stroke that belongs to Guiraud's arrangement rather than to Bizet. The Minuet everyone knows from that suite comes from a different opera altogether, La jolie fille de Perth.","The play failed and took the music down with it; Bizet's first suite rescued the score within a month and it has been popular ever since, usually without a word of Daudet. Purists object that the familiar suites misrepresent the original — different scoring, added arrangements, changed order. Complete recordings with the spoken text exist and reveal a leaner, stranger piece than the concert hall knows.","Hear the Adagietto, ninety seconds of muted strings, then the Farandole. Then find a recording of the complete incidental music with narration to hear the saxophone in its dramatic place rather than as a novelty. Compare the Provençal colour with Gounod's Mireille, written eight years earlier from the same landscape."],"bizet-carmen":["Four acts. A soldier throws over his career, his mother and his fiancée for a woman who tells him at the outset exactly what she is, and kills her outside a bullring when she refuses to pretend she still wants him. Bizet writes Spanish-flavoured numbers — habanera, seguidilla, gypsy song — inside a French opéra comique with spoken dialogue, and threads a short chromatic fate motif through the whole.","Opéra-Comique, March 1875. The house existed for family entertainment and marriage plots; the management fought the ending for months, and the chorus complained at being asked to act as individuals rather than stand in lines. Bizet was thirty-six and died of a heart condition three months after the premiere, believing the work had failed. It had not: it ran for over thirty performances that season.","The Habanera is not Bizet's tune. He took it from 'El arreglito' by the Basque composer Sebastián Iradier, thinking it a folk song, and added an acknowledgement to the vocal score when he learned otherwise. Listen for the fate motif's stretched interval, which sounds neither major nor minor, and for the card scene, where Carmen stops performing and the orchestra drops to a bare, slow tread.","Reviews called it obscene and undramatic; within three years Vienna had made it a triumph, in Ernest Guiraud's version with sung recitatives replacing the dialogue. Nietzsche used it as a stick to beat Wagner. The twentieth century restored the dialogue, and the critical editions of Fritz Oeser and his successors reopened questions about what Bizet cut in rehearsal and what others cut after him.","Everyone knows the tunes; listen instead for the structure — how each act ends with Carmen winning and José losing more. Start with the third-act card trio, the least famous and most frightening scene. Choose a recording with spoken dialogue and a mezzo who sings rather than growls, and compare a French-language cast with the old recitative versions."],"saint-saens-samson-et-dalila":["Three acts, about two hours. The first is nearly an oratorio — Hebrew crowds, a fugal chorus, a prophet rousing them — before the opera becomes a seduction: Dalila's three approaches to Samson, ending with 'Mon cœur s'ouvre à ta voix', a slow duet in which he never quite answers. The last act sets Samson blinded at a mill, a Bacchanale for the Philistines, and the temple pulled down in twenty seconds.","Begun in 1867 as an oratorio on Delilah, expanded into an opera, and refused by every Paris theatre — a biblical subject on the French stage was still close to scandal. Liszt, whose influence ran through the Weimar court theatre, arranged a German-language premiere there in December 1877. Rouen staged it in French in 1890; the Paris Opéra only in 1892. Saint-Saëns was by then France's most decorated musician.","The oratorio origins never disappear: the first act's choral fugue is written in a deliberately old-fashioned manner, closer to Handel than to Gounod, and the contrast with Dalila's chromatic, coiling lines carries the entire moral argument. Listen for the Bacchanale's opening oboe solo, built on a scale with a stretched step that Saint-Saëns had heard on his travels in North Africa, and note that Samson gets no aria until he is blind.","German audiences took it at once; French critics found it neither properly sacred nor properly theatrical. It became a repertory staple through the twentieth century largely on the strength of one mezzo aria and the Bacchanale, which encouraged the charge that the rest is inert. Recent stagings work against the biblical-postcard tradition, and conductors argue whether the first act should be staged at all or presented as ritual.","Start with 'Mon cœur s'ouvre à ta voix' but stay for the end of the duet, where Samson's answers get shorter and shorter. Then hear Act 1 complete to understand what Dalila is dismantling. A great mezzo — Rita Gorr, Marilyn Horne — matters more than anything else here; compare the Bacchanale with Borodin's Polovtsian Dances."],"offenbach-les-contes-d-hoffmann":["A prologue, three acts and an epilogue in which the poet Hoffmann, drunk in a tavern, tells the stories of three loves — a clockwork doll, a singer forbidden to sing, a Venetian courtesan — each destroyed by a different form of the same villain, sung by one bass-baritone. The Barcarolle opens the Venice act; the doll's aria winds down twice and has to be rewound before she can finish.","Offenbach had spent his life on operetta and wanted, at the end of it, a serious opera. He worked through 1880, rehearsals began at the Opéra-Comique, and he died in October with the orchestration incomplete and the order of the acts unsettled. Ernest Guiraud finished the score; the premiere came in February 1881. The Venice act was cut before opening night and parts of it went missing for decades.","The Barcarolle is second-hand: Offenbach lifted it from his own failed German opera Die Rheinnixen of 1864, where it was a chorus of elves. The famous Venice septet is not by Offenbach at all but a later addition by other hands. Listen for the four villains sharing one vocal signature — the same low, mocking manner — and for the doll's aria sagging in pitch and slowing as her spring runs down.","The performing text has been contested for over a century. Fritz Oeser's 1976 edition restored a great deal of cut music but also invented some; Michael Kaye and Jean-Christophe Keck later found and published hundreds of pages of Offenbach's autograph, changing the picture again. Whether the Venice act comes second or third, and which of the many finales to use, remains a decision every production must make.","Listen to the doll's aria and the Barcarolle first, then the Antonia act complete — the most sustained and least comic music Offenbach wrote. Check which edition a recording uses before committing; the differences are large. Compare the four villains with Méphistophélès in Gounod's Faust, whom they are quietly mocking."],"delibes-lakme":["Three acts in British India: a Brahmin priest's daughter falls in love with an English officer who has trespassed in her father's sacred garden. The Flower Duet for two women's voices opens the opera, the Bell Song is Lakmé's coloratura display in a market square, and the ending is a quiet self-poisoning with datura leaves. Delibes scores it with harps, high woodwind and a great deal of silence.","Opéra-Comique, April 1883, at the height of French colonial expansion. The librettists worked from Pierre Loti's autobiographical novel about a European officer and a Polynesian girl, and moved it to India. Delibes was a ballet composer by trade and the score shows it — dances, processions and orchestral colour carry more weight than the recitative. Marie van Zandt, an American soprano in her twenties, created the title role.","The Bell Song is a story within the story: Lakmé is made to sing a legend in public to flush out the intruder, so a coloratura display piece is also a trap, and its bell figures return in the orchestra later when the trap closes. Listen for the way Delibes writes the sacred garden with no bass instruments at all for long stretches, and for a drooping figure that appears in Act 1 before anyone mentions poison.","It succeeded immediately and stayed, mostly for two soprano numbers. The Flower Duet's afterlife in advertising has made the opera itself hard to hear. Modern criticism reads the piece as a colonial artefact — the English are absurd, the Indians are scenery — and stagings either confront that or retreat into orientalist prettiness. The craft of the score is rarely disputed by either side.","Hear the Flower Duet in its place at the start rather than as an excerpt: it is two women washing at a river, not a hymn. Then the Bell Song, then the last scene. Look for a Lakmé who can sing softly — Mady Mesplé and Natalie Dessay are the French reference recordings. Compare with Bizet's Les pêcheurs de perles."],"massenet-manon":["Five acts, about two hours fifty. A girl on her way to a convent runs off with a student, leaves him for money, and comes back to drag him out of a seminary in a scene where prayer turns into seduction; they are separated at the end on the road to Le Havre. Massenet writes conversationally, with speech over orchestra as often as aria, giving Manon a gavotte at her most brittle and a farewell to a table at her most human.","Opéra-Comique, January 1884, on Abbé Prévost's novel of 1731. Massenet went to The Hague to write part of it in the house where Prévost had lived. The Opéra-Comique's format — spoken dialogue, an intimate house, a middle-class audience — suited a story about money and appetite. Massenet was forty-one, a Conservatoire professor, and this is the work that made him the most successful French opera composer of his generation.","Massenet uses melodrama constantly: the singer speaks in rhythm while the orchestra carries the tune, so Manon's decisions happen in the pit rather than in her mouth, and the Saint-Sulpice scene is built on the device. Listen for the small descending phrase attached to her from her first entrance, which returns in the last scene played by strings so slowly it is barely recognisable.","A success from the start and never out of the repertory, though critics have always divided over Massenet's sweetness — Vincent d'Indy's circle thought it fatally soft, and the charge of perfumed sentiment has followed him since. Puccini's Manon Lescaut of 1893 covers the same novel and invited comparison for a century. Recent scholarship treats Massenet's dramatic pacing as sharper than the reputation suggests.","Start with 'Adieu, notre petite table' — three minutes that hold the whole character — then the Saint-Sulpice duet. A soprano who can colour the words matters more than one who can fill a hall: Victoria de los Ángeles and Beverly Sills are contrasting classics. Compare Massenet's Manon with Puccini's for two national ideas of the same woman."],"chabrier-le-roi-malgre-lui":["Three acts of opéra comique about Henri de Valois, elected King of Poland and desperate not to be, who conspires against himself and is undone by the plot's success. The music is far ahead of the joke: a Fête polonaise that is really a symphonic mazurka, a Danse slave, choral writing full of unexpected side-steps, and harmony that keeps landing a semitone from where the ear expects it.","Opéra-Comique, May 1887. Chabrier had been a civil servant at the Ministry of the Interior until he was thirty-nine, resigning to compose full time after hearing Tristan at Munich and weeping through the prelude. The libretto, patched together from an old comedy by several hands, was a mess and he knew it. Three performances in, the Salle Favart burned down with heavy loss of life and the run ended.","Ravel said the premiere of this opera changed the direction of French harmony, and claimed he could play the score from memory. The evidence is in the Fête polonaise, where Chabrier keeps a dance rhythm going while the chords slide sideways underneath it — a habit Debussy, Ravel and Poulenc all took up. Listen for the offbeat accents in the choral writing, which sound like mistakes until you count them.","The fire made the piece a legend before it could be judged. Revivals are rare because the libretto defeats directors, and various rewritings have been attempted, including versions that replace the spoken dialogue entirely. Composers have always ranked it higher than the public has: it is the standard example of a score that everyone influential admired and almost nobody has seen on a stage.","Start with the Fête polonaise on its own — it works as a concert piece and tells you what the fuss is about. Then the second-act choral ensembles. John Eliot Gardiner's recording is the usual recommendation. If it appeals, go on to Chabrier's España and the Trois valses romantiques for the same harmonic ear in smaller frames."],"lalo-le-roi-d-ys":["Three acts on the Breton legend of Ys, a city built behind sea-gates: a king's elder daughter, passed over in love, opens the gates and drowns everyone, then throws herself into the water to appease the saint. The overture, with its long cello solo, is played far more often than the opera itself. Mylio's aubade 'Vainement, ma bien-aimée' is the tenor's page.","Opéra-Comique, May 1888, after more than a decade of rejections; Lalo had a version finished by 1878 and could not get it staged. He was sixty-five, known chiefly as an instrumental composer — the Symphonie espagnole, the Cello Concerto — and had spent years as a violist in a string quartet. Breton legend was fashionable in a France rediscovering its provinces after the defeat of 1871.","Lalo composes the sea as an absence: for two acts there is almost no water music at all, so that when the gates open the orchestral flood has nothing to compete with. Listen for the modal inflections in the choral writing, borrowed from Breton hymn tunes, and for the discovery that the overture's cello solo is Margared's music — the villain's, not the lovers'.","It was Lalo's one great theatrical success and reached its hundredth performance within a few years. Outside France it never took hold, and Lalo remains an instrumental composer in most listeners' minds. Critics praise the orchestration and the concision — the piece is short by the standards of its decade — while conceding that the drama is legend rather than character.","The overture first, then the aubade, then the final scene. Try a French recording: Rita Gorr's Margared under André Cluytens is the historic reference. Compare the drowned city with Debussy's piano prelude The Sunken Cathedral, written from the same Breton legend a generation later."],"massenet-werther":["Four acts, about two hours ten. A young poet loves a woman engaged to another man, watches her marry, and shoots himself at Christmas while children rehearse a carol outside. Massenet writes almost no ensemble and no crowd: the opera is four long conversations. 'Pourquoi me réveiller' sets a translated Ossian poem, and Charlotte's letter scene has her read his letters aloud while the orchestra remembers what he sounded like.","Rejected by the Opéra-Comique's director Léon Carvalho, who wanted another Manon and found the subject too gloomy, the opera went instead to Vienna and was premiered there in German in February 1892; Paris heard it a year later. Massenet had visited Wetzlar, where Goethe set the novel, and read it on the spot. Goethe's book of 1774 had once made suicide fashionable across Europe, and the shadow still clung to it.","The orchestra includes an alto saxophone, used not for local colour but for one grey timbre in the darker scenes. Listen for the desolation music that opens the third act and returns beneath Charlotte's letters, and for the children's Christmas song that Massenet plants in the sunlit first act precisely so it can come back through a window over the dying man in the last.","Vienna liked it, Paris was slower, and the work then settled into the repertory as Massenet's most serious score. A baritone version was made for Mattia Battistini in 1902 and is still occasionally staged, which shifts the balance of the piece considerably. Critics who resist Massenet elsewhere usually make an exception here, on the grounds that the sentiment is the subject rather than the manner.","Begin with the third act entire, the letter scene through 'Pourquoi me réveiller' — self-contained and devastating. Then go back to the first act to hear how much of it is quoted there in advance. Georges Thill, Nicolai Gedda and Jonas Kaufmann are the reference Werthers; a French-speaking Charlotte matters just as much."],"massenet-thais":["Three acts. A monk crosses the desert to convert a famous Alexandrian courtesan, succeeds, and discovers at her deathbed that he has fallen in love with her and lost his faith. Between the two scenes of the second act the orchestra plays the Méditation, a solo violin over harp and muted strings, standing in for her conversion. Massenet set a prose libretto, so the vocal lines follow the rhythm of speech.","Paris Opéra, March 1894, on Anatole France's anticlerical novel of 1890. Massenet wrote it for the American soprano Sibyl Sanderson, around whose voice and reputation the piece was designed; the first run gained extra notoriety from a reported costume mishap. He revised it in 1898, adding the oasis scene and expanding the ballet. France's reputation as an enemy of the Church did the box office no harm at all.","The Méditation is not a religious interlude but a portrait of a decision, and its violin theme returns in the third act in the mouths of both characters, so the melody changes owner as the faith does. Listen for how Athanaël's music grows more sensual as hers grows plainer — the two exchange styles across the opera, and by the last scene he is singing what she used to.","The Méditation escaped into salons and violin encores almost immediately and has kept the opera's name alive while obscuring the rest of it. Critics have called the work a luxury item and the theology a pose. Revivals depend on finding a soprano who can carry the title role's mixture of glamour and stillness — Beverly Sills and Renée Fleming both built productions around it. Both the 1894 and 1898 versions are performed.","Hear the Méditation in context, with the scenes on either side of it, rather than as a violin encore. Then the final duet, for the reversal. Renée Fleming's recording under Yves Abel is the modern reference and Beverly Sills's the historic one. Compare with Saint-Saëns's Samson for another French opera about a holy man undone."],"saint-saens-swan":["The thirteenth of fourteen movements in Le carnaval des animaux, and the only one that is not a joke. A cello sings a single unbroken melody of about three minutes over two pianos that ripple in even broken chords from the first bar to the last. There is no contrasting middle and no development, only one arc that climbs to a held high note and sinks back. The pianos suggest water; the cello glides above it without ever seeming to move its feet.","Saint-Saens wrote the whole suite in a few days in February 1886 while resting in a small Austrian village, between an exhausting concert tour and the London premiere of his Third Symphony. It was a private amusement for a Shrove Tuesday concert given by the cellist Charles Lebouc. Fearing that a menagerie of musical jokes would damage his standing as a serious composer, he forbade publication of everything except this movement, and the rest appeared only after his death in 1921.","Listen to the two pianos: they are not playing the same water. One lays down a steady rocking figure while the other adds a faster, higher shimmer above it, and the two rhythms never quite lock. The cello's tune, meanwhile, is built from a phrase that keeps beginning on a weak beat, which is what makes it sound as though it is being carried rather than propelled.","Fokine's 1905 solo for Anna Pavlova, The Dying Swan, attached a death to a piece that contains none, and the association has held for over a century. Cellists still argue about it: whether to use the long, freely stretched phrasing of the ballet tradition or the steadier line the score asks for. Its ubiquity in recitals, funerals and advertising has also made critics undervalue the suite around it, which is sharper music than its reputation suggests.","Hear it first with the two pianos rather than a harp or a string orchestra; the original texture is drier and stranger. Then notice how little vibrato the best players use. Follow the cello's breathing: the phrases are long, and the whole effect depends on the player not taking an audible breath where you expect one."],"saint-saens-danse-macabre":["A symphonic poem of about seven minutes. The harp strikes twelve for midnight, a solo violin tunes itself into a deliberately sour interval and starts a waltz, and the xylophone clatters through it like bones. Two ideas alternate, the lurching dance and a smoother second tune, building through the whole orchestra to a shrieking climax. Then an oboe crows as the cock, the noise evaporates, and the violin gets a last thin word.","Saint-Saens first set Henri Cazalis's poem as a song in 1872, then rewrote it for orchestra two years later. Paris in the 1870s had almost no home for French orchestral music that was not opera; the Societe Nationale de Musique, which he had helped found in 1871 under the motto Ars Gallica, existed to build one. The tone poem itself was a German import taken from Liszt, and using it was a statement about what French concert music could become.","The soloist retunes the violin's top string down a semitone before playing, so the open strings sound a hollow, unstable interval that carried a devilish nickname in medieval theory. The main dance tune is a disguised version of the Dies irae, the plainchant for the dead, twisted into a jaunty triple metre. Saint-Saens quoted the same waltz again years later in the Fossils movement of the Carnival of the Animals, xylophone and all.","The 1875 Paris premiere went badly; listeners found the xylophone grotesque and the subject vulgar, and opinion only turned after repeated hearings. Liszt, to whom the piece owes its form, admired it enough to make a piano transcription. It is now the one French tone poem everyone can hum, which has cost it critical respect. Writers who take Saint-Saens seriously point instead to its economy: nothing in the seven minutes is wasted.","Count the harp's twelve strokes at the start, then wait for the xylophone, which is the sound the piece is remembered by. Near the end a solo oboe interrupts: that is dawn, and everything after it is the dancers going back underground. Try a French orchestra, then compare Liszt's Totentanz, which takes the same chant entirely seriously."],"saint-saens-symphony-no-3-organ-finale":["The last of the symphony's four linked sections, about ten minutes. It opens with one enormous organ chord in the major that arrives after a long minor-key struggle, and from there the orchestra builds a broad hymn-like theme while two players share a single piano, scattering fast figuration over the top. A fugal passage tightens the argument, the theme returns in full brass, and the movement ends with the organ holding the whole hall.","The Royal Philharmonic Society in London commissioned the symphony, and Saint-Saens conducted the premiere at St James's Hall on 19 May 1886. He was fifty, at the height of his authority, and said afterwards that he had given everything he had to give. Liszt died that July, and the published score carries a dedication to his memory. Large concert halls with permanent organs were a new civic ambition across Europe, and the piece was written to use one.","The whole symphony grows from a single restless theme heard at the very start, itself a bent version of the Dies irae chant. By the finale that theme has been straightened into the major-key hymn: the same notes, walking instead of running. The piano part is for four hands at one keyboard, a domestic instrument dropped into an orchestral score, and its glitter exists to make the organ's weight audible by contrast.","It succeeded immediately in London and remains the only one of his symphonies in the repertory; he never wrote another. The standing charge against Saint-Saens, brilliant craftsmanship without depth, has attached to this piece too, and Gounod's remark that he was the French Beethoven gets quoted as often to mock as to praise. Modern performances argue mostly about organs: the hall's own instrument, a borrowed one, or an electronic substitute.","Do not start here. Play the third section first so that the finale's opening chord lands as the release it is designed to be. Then listen for the piano, easily lost under the organ. Seek a recording made in a hall with a real pipe organ, and afterwards go back to the symphony's opening to hear the same theme before it was straightened out."],"saint-saens-introduction-rondo-capriccioso":["One movement of about nine minutes in two joined halves. The introduction is slow and brooding, the soloist entering low over held strings. Then the rondo begins: a springy, Spanish-tinged tune in swinging triple metre that returns four times, separated by episodes that ask the violinist for something harder each time, including rapid bouncing bow-strokes and a long passage in harmonics, before a coda that simply accelerates to the finish.","Saint-Saens wrote it for Pablo de Sarasate, the Spanish virtuoso who had turned up at his door as a teenager to ask, quite casually, for a concerto. The Spanish colouring is a portrait of the player. Paris in the 1860s was full of violin showpieces stitched from opera tunes; this one borrows no melodies at all, which was part of its point. Sarasate's playing was famous for sweetness and ease rather than force, and the writing suits that exactly.","The introduction is not decoration. Its falling phrase is the rondo tune slowed down and turned minor, so the whole piece is one idea in two moods. Notice how little the orchestra does: plucked strings and light wind chords, scored to stay out of the soloist's way so that even the quietest passagework carries. Bizet, then still an unsuccessful theatre composer, made the piano reduction that violinists learned it from.","Published in 1870, it became a standard test piece almost at once, which has led critics to file it as display rather than composition. Recordings divide sharply between players who take the rondo as an elegant dance and those who race it; the score's markings favour the dancers. Scholarship now reads it as evidence of how early Saint-Saens arrived at his mature manner, since he was twenty-eight when he wrote it.","Listen first for the join, the exact moment the slow introduction gives way to the dance. After that, follow the returns of the rondo tune and notice how the accompaniment changes underneath each one. Compare Heifetz for speed with a French player for lilt, and put it beside Ravel's Tzigane to hear the same idea of a dance carrying a virtuoso."],"saint-saens-carnival-animals-aquarium":["The seventh of the suite's fourteen movements and barely two minutes long. Two pianos run continuous rippling scales up and down while the strings play a slow, drifting melody above and below them, and a glass harmonica drops single bright notes into the texture like light striking a surface. Nothing develops. The piece hangs, repeats itself with small changes of colour, and stops without arriving anywhere.","Written in February 1886 alongside the rest of the suite as private entertainment, at a moment when public aquaria were a new civic spectacle: Paris, London and Berlin had all built them, and glass tanks of live fish drew crowds. Saint-Saens, a lifelong amateur naturalist and astronomer, was as interested in the phenomenon as in the joke. He suppressed the suite anyway, and it went unpublished until after his death.","The instrument specified is the glass harmonica, a set of spinning glass bowls played with wet fingers, invented by Benjamin Franklin and nearly obsolete by 1886. Most performances substitute a celesta or glockenspiel, which is brighter and less eerie. Listen for the two pianos playing the same scale a fraction apart, so that the water shimmers rather than glides, and for a melody that never lands on a stable close.","Aquarium has had a long second life in cinema, where it signals wonder, menace or dream depending on the cut, and that ubiquity obscures how strange its harmony actually is. Performance practice has moved towards restoring the glass harmonica wherever an instrument and a player can be found. Critics who once dismissed the suite as trivia now treat it as Saint-Saens's most inventive scoring, done at chamber scale.","Listen for the little bell-like notes on top first, then deliberately ignore them and follow the strings, which carry a real melody most listeners never hear. Play it directly after The Swan to feel how differently the same two pianos can be used. A recording using an actual glass harmonica is worth hunting down."],"saint-saens-samson-et-dalila-mon-coeur-s":["Dalila's aria in the second act, about six minutes, sung to Samson in the ravine outside her house while a storm gathers. The vocal line unwinds in long, low, curving phrases over a rocking accompaniment, the orchestra doubling her sweetly rather than pushing. Samson interjects twice, and in the second verse his voice joins hers. There is no display writing at all: the effect comes from the mezzo's lowest register and from how slowly the melody turns.","Saint-Saens began the work as an oratorio and turned it into an opera; French theatres wanted nothing to do with a biblical subject staged with singers in costume. Liszt, then at Weimar, arranged the premiere there in December 1877, sung in German. Paris did not stage it until 1892. The Second Empire's appetite for oriental subjects and the older French tradition of sacred drama both feed the score, which sits between opera and oratorio throughout.","The aria is a seduction that is also a lie, and the music says so. Dalila's melody is exquisitely sincere while the accompaniment underneath keeps sliding downward by half-steps, refusing to settle where the tune implies it should. Note too that the orchestra states her opening phrase before she sings it: she is following a script. The act ends soon afterwards with a thunderclap and Samson's cry of betrayal.","Reception has always been split between admiration for the melody and unease at what the opera does with it: a Philistine woman characterised by sinuous chromatic music, a habit later critics have named as orientalism. The staging problem is genuine, since the piece was conceived as oratorio and the second act is its only real drama. As a concert item the aria has been claimed by every great mezzo since Marian Anderson, in every convenient key.","Hear it inside the opera before you hear it as a recital piece; the aria means something different when Samson is standing there. Listen for the second verse, where the tenor enters over her line. Then compare two mezzos, one who sings it as love and one who sings it as calculation. Both readings are in the notes."],"saint-saens-havanaise":["Ten minutes for violin and orchestra in a single span, built on the habanera's limping rhythm, a long note followed by two short ones, which the orchestra keeps running under almost everything. The soloist alternates between languid, sliding song high on the top string and sudden bursts of dry brilliance, then returns to the dance. The two characters trade places three or four times, and the piece fades out rather than finishing loudly.","The story attached to the piece is that it began in a hotel room in Brittany, watching a log fire flicker while on tour with the Cuban violinist Rafael Diaz Albertini, and that the alternation of flame and glow became the alternation of fast and slow. Saint-Saens wrote it for Albertini and dedicated it to him. The habanera had travelled from Havana to Spain and then to France, where Bizet had used it in Carmen a decade earlier.","The rhythm is Cuban rather than Spanish, and it reached Paris as a ballroom craze before it reached the concert hall. Listen for the mute: the violin is directed to play muted at the opening, which is why the first tune sounds as though heard from another room. Notice too that the fast episodes are in the same metre as the slow ones, only subdivided, so the dance never actually stops.","It has never been more than a recital favourite, and reviewers have often filed it beside Sarasate's Spanish showpieces as tourism. The defence is that Saint-Saens writes the dance straight, without picture-postcard flourishes, and that holding two opposite characters together with one rhythm is tighter construction than it sounds. Violinists argue about tempo, since taken too fast the habanera stops being a dance at all.","Tap the rhythm before listening, long then short-short, and you will hear it running under nearly every bar. Notice the change of colour when the mute comes off. Then pair it with the Introduction and Rondo Capriccioso to hear how often Saint-Saens returned to the idea of a dance as a vehicle for a violinist."],"saint-saens-piano-concerto-no-2-first-movement":["The concerto's opening movement, about eleven minutes, and shaped unlike any other. It begins with the piano alone in a long, grave, improvised-sounding solo in the manner of a Bach organ fantasia, before the orchestra enters with one heavy chord. The main theme, when it arrives, is broad and dark, and the movement stays slow and serious throughout, ending by returning to the same unaccompanied piano writing it began with.","Anton Rubinstein wanted to conduct in Paris and asked Saint-Saens for a new piece to fill the programme. There were three weeks. Saint-Saens wrote the concerto in seventeen days and played the premiere himself at the Salle Pleyel on 13 May 1868, under-rehearsed; he admitted afterwards that the scherzo had gone badly. He was thirty-two, already the most feared sight-reader and improviser in France, and the opening solo is essentially a written-down improvisation.","The theme of that opening solo is said not to be his: it is reported to come from an abandoned Tantum ergo by his pupil Gabriel Faure, borrowed with permission and used as the movement's frame. The larger joke about the concerto, that it begins with Bach and ends with Offenbach, is a fair description of a work whose three movements get faster and lighter as they go, inverting the usual concerto shape entirely.","Rubinstein's Paris audience liked it, the piece has never left the repertoire, and for a century it was the Saint-Saens concerto pianists learned. The complaint, made from the start, is that the three movements do not belong to the same work. Defenders reply that the mismatch is deliberate and that the first movement's return to solo piano at the close is what holds the design together. Cortot, Rubinstein and Cherkassky each argued a different case on record.","Start with the first two minutes and simply listen to the piano alone; it sounds like an organist thinking aloud. Then wait for the orchestra's first chord, which lands like a door closing. Afterwards play the finale to hear what the joke about Offenbach means. Look for a pianist with a big singing tone: this movement is a slow argument, not a sprint."],"delibes-lakme-flower-duet":["The second number of the first act: Lakme and her servant Mallika go down to the river to gather flowers. Two women's voices move almost entirely in parallel, a third apart, over a gently rocking accompaniment with harp and low woodwind. There is no conflict and almost no harmonic movement. The duet circles the same phrase, opening out a little at each return, and ends as quietly as it began, about six minutes later.","Delibes wrote Lakme for the Opera-Comique, where it opened on 14 April 1883, on a subject drawn from a French vogue for stories of colonial India: a Brahmin priest's daughter loves a British officer and dies for it. Third Republic audiences wanted exotic settings that flattered European assumptions, and the score obliges with temple bells and modal colouring while the vocal writing stays entirely French in manner.","The two voices are not equals. Mallika, the servant, mostly shadows Lakme at a fixed distance, which is a way of showing rank in sound. Listen for the moments when the harmony shifts underneath an unchanged melody, so the same tune is suddenly lit differently. The duet's calm is also dramatic irony: nothing in the opera afterwards is peaceful, and the river described here is where the British officer will find her.","The opera has survived on two numbers, this duet and the Bell Song, and is rarely staged outside France. From 1989 the duet became the sound of British Airways advertising and has been used in dozens of films since, generally to signal serenity, which has made it hard to hear as theatre at all. Recent productions have had to confront the colonial plot rather than decorate it.","Pick out the lower voice and follow only that for a chorus; the piece changes completely. Then listen to how much of the effect comes from the harp underneath. Compare a French recording from the 1950s with a modern one, since older singers take it slower and straighter, and afterwards find the Bell Song, where the same soprano does the opposite thing."],"delibes-coppelia-waltz":["The waltz from the first act, about four minutes: Swanilda dances in the village square below the toymaker's window, watched by a doll she takes for a girl. The tune arrives on strings over a plain three-beat accompaniment, slows into a held phrase, then presses forward again. The scoring keeps thinning and thickening, so the same melody sounds by turns private and public, and a brisk coda closes it off.","Coppelia opened at the Paris Opera on 25 May 1870, with choreography by Arthur Saint-Leon and a sixteen-year-old Italian, Giuseppina Bozzacchi, as Swanilda. Six weeks later France declared war on Prussia. Paris was besieged, Saint-Leon died of a heart attack, and Bozzacchi died of smallpox on her seventeenth birthday in November. The ballet is the last work of the Second Empire's theatre, and it outlived everything around it.","The waltz is built to be danced in character rather than in the abstract: its stops and hesitations are cues for mime, and a listener who knows the plot can hear where Swanilda is pretending. Delibes also threads national colours through the act, setting a mazurka and a czardas beside this Viennese waltz, so that the square feels like a real border town rather than a stage.","Coppelia established that a ballet score could be composed rather than assembled from serviceable dance numbers, and Tchaikovsky said as much in print. For a century the ballet was treated as a charming trifle; recent scholarship takes the music seriously as the model for Swan Lake and everything after it. Productions still cut and reorder the score freely, and no two agree on what belongs in the first act.","Listen for the moment the waltz hesitates: that is choreography, not indecision. Then play the czardas from the same act to hear Delibes change nation without changing style. If you know Tchaikovsky's ballets, put this beside the Sleeping Beauty waltz; the debt is obvious in both directions of listening."],"delibes-sylvia-pizzicato":["Two minutes from the third act in which the entire string section plays plucked, never bowed, with a triangle and a light wind counter-tune above. The melody is short and repeats with tiny variations; the effect depends on the dryness of the sound and on absolute precision of ensemble. Halfway through the harmony darkens for a few bars and swings back. It stops abruptly, on the beat, like a mechanism running out.","Sylvia opened at the Palais Garnier on 14 June 1876, a year after the new opera house had opened its doors and at a moment when French ballet was in decline as a serious art. Delibes was in his forties and had moved from chorus master to house composer. The subject, Diana's nymph and a shepherd, is late-classical pastoral, but the writing is orchestral first and danceable second, which is why the score outlived the choreography entirely.","Plucked passages existed before this one, but they were short effects inside longer pieces. Here the device is the whole movement, which forces Delibes to make plucked strings carry melody, bass and rhythm at once. Listen for the triangle marking the phrase ends, without which the ear loses the beat, and for the dry wind commentary underneath that supplies the only sustained sound in the number.","Tchaikovsky heard Sylvia in 1877 and wrote that had he known it earlier he would not have dared to write Swan Lake, a remark quoted ever since as the score's certificate. The Pizzicati escaped into the salon and the encore repertoire immediately and has been arranged for almost everything. Full stagings of Sylvia were rare until Frederick Ashton's 1952 version, which is now the usual route back to the score.","Listen for the ensemble itself: the pleasure is in dozens of players plucking as one, and a good recording lets you hear fingernails on gut. Then hear it staged in Ashton's version. Compare it with the Nutcracker's Dance of the Sugar Plum Fairy, another miniature built entirely on one unusual sound."],"delibes-coppelia-mazurka":["The first act's Polish dance, about four minutes, and the ballet's grandest ensemble number. A firm stamping rhythm throws its accent off the first beat, brass and drum land on the strong steps, and a tune keeps rising by step before falling back. Delibes states it plainly, repeats it louder, drops into a quiet trio for wind, then brings the whole orchestra back. It is written for a stage full of villagers, not for soloists.","The plot is set in a village on the old Galician borderlands, and the first act's dances are its geography: a mazurka from Poland, a czardas from Hungary, a waltz from Vienna. Paris in 1870 followed Polish politics closely, the January Uprising being seven years past and Chopin's mazurkas standard salon currency, so the dance carried a political flavour French audiences would have caught immediately.","Set it beside a Chopin mazurka and the difference is instructive: Chopin writes the dance's give and take into the melody, while Delibes keeps the beat rigid so that a corps de ballet of thirty can stay together. The accent lands on the second or third beat almost throughout, which is what makes it a mazurka rather than a waltz. Listen for the moment the brass takes the tune and the strings become pure rhythm.","For most of the twentieth century the mazurka was the number cut when Coppelia was shortened, on the grounds that it holds up the story. Restored productions have made the case that the act's three national dances are the point, since the village is a border and its dances say so. French orchestras keep the tempo brisk; Russian ones tend to make it heavier and grander.","Count in threes and notice that the weight is never on one. Then listen to the quiet middle section and hear how much softer the same tune becomes. Play it back to back with the act's czardas, and afterwards with a Chopin mazurka, to hear the gap between a dance for a ballroom and a dance for a stage."],"bizet-carmen-habanera":["Carmen's entrance aria in the first act, about four minutes. Over a cello line that slides down the scale again and again, hypnotically, she sings a descending chromatic melody in the habanera's limping rhythm, first alone and then with the chorus of soldiers and townspeople answering. Each verse turns to the major for a swung refrain. She sings it to a crowd, throws a flower at Don Jose, and walks away.","Carmen opened at the Opera-Comique on 3 March 1875, a theatre whose subscribers expected family entertainment with spoken dialogue between the numbers. Bizet gave them a cigarette-factory worker who chooses her own lovers and is murdered on stage. He reportedly rewrote this aria a dozen times before the first Carmen, Celestine Galli-Marie, accepted it. He died three months later at thirty-six, believing the opera a failure.","The tune is not Bizet's. He took it, thinking it a folk song, from El arreglito by the Basque composer Sebastian Iradier, and added an acknowledgement to the score once he learned it was under copyright. The accompaniment is the giveaway to her character: it descends by half-step, never resolving, so the music refuses to settle exactly as she does. The opera's fate motif is kept out of this number entirely.","The premiere was received coldly and the earliest reviews attacked the subject as immoral. Within a decade Carmen was the most performed opera in Europe; Nietzsche used it as a weapon against Wagner, and Brahms is said to have seen it twenty times. Modern debate turns on the versions, Ernest Guiraud's sung recitatives written after Bizet's death against the original spoken dialogue, and on how the character is staged now.","Follow the cellos, not the voice, on a first hearing; that sliding line is the whole character. Then listen to the chorus's answers and how they crowd around her. Seek a recording with spoken dialogue, Beecham's or a modern period-aware one, to hear the piece as the opera comique it was written to be."],"bizet-carmen-toreador-song":["Escamillo's entrance in the second act, in the smugglers' tavern. He begins with a swaggering verse in the minor, describing the bullring from inside, and then the refrain arrives: a broad marching tune in the major that the whole chorus takes up and repeats. Five minutes, two verses, and a great deal of noise. The audience has heard the refrain already, since it opens the opera's prelude before a word of the drama.","The Opera-Comique needed a set piece for a visiting star, and Escamillo exists largely to supply one and to give Carmen a rival for Jose's place in the story. Bizet reportedly had no affection for the number and dismissed it as filth written to order. Bullfighting was a live French fashion in the 1870s, legal across the Spanish border and increasingly staged in the south, so the character read as contemporary rather than picturesque.","Planting the refrain in the prelude means that when Escamillo sings it the audience already knows the tune without knowing why, which is exactly how celebrity works. The verse's minor-key strut and the refrain's major-key swing use the same rhythm; only the harmony changes, which is why the switch feels like a light coming on. Note how little the orchestra does under the verse: he is nearly unaccompanied, boasting into a quiet room.","It has always been the number that non-opera-goers know, and that has done it no favours critically; Bizet's own contempt is regularly quoted as evidence against it. The counter-argument is dramatic. The crude tune is characterisation, not carelessness, since Escamillo is a celebrity performing a celebrity, and a subtler aria would tell the wrong truth. Baritones divide over whether to sing it beautifully or with calculated vulgarity.","Listen to the prelude first and register the tune, then hear it arrive in the second act. Notice how thin the accompaniment is in the verse and how the chorus swamps him in the refrain. Compare a Russian-school baritone's plush version with a lighter, faster French one; the character changes completely between them."],"bizet-l-arlesienne-farandole":["Four minutes that end by running two tunes at once. It opens with the Provencal Christmas carol known as the March of the Kings, hammered out in the minor by the full orchestra. Then a fast chain dance starts quietly on flutes over a drum. The two alternate and accelerate, and in the last minute they sound together, the carol in long notes underneath while the dance races above, driving to a hard close.","Daudet's play L'Arlesienne opened in Paris in October 1872 with Bizet's incidental music for a pit band of twenty-six players. The play failed within weeks. Bizet salvaged a first orchestral suite immediately; the second, containing this movement, was assembled after his death by his friend Ernest Guiraud, who also wrote the sung recitatives for Carmen. Provence was then being reinvented as a cultural nation by the poet Mistral, whose movement the play drew on.","The March of the Kings is a real Provencal carol, so Bizet did not write the tune he is most famous for using. The dance over it is a genuine farandole, a chain dance led through the streets to pipe and drum, and the piccolo and long narrow drum in the score stand in for those street instruments. The trick of the last minute works because the two tunes share the same underlying step.","The suites, not the play, kept the music alive, and Bizet's original theatre scoring for a small band went unheard for a century; it now has advocates who argue that Guiraud's full-orchestra dress coarsens it. The Farandole's popularity as an encore, a school piece and film music has led to it being played faster every decade. It is a dance, and dancers cannot move at some modern tempos.","Learn the carol's shape in the first thirty seconds, because everything after depends on recognising it. Then wait for the last minute, when both tunes run together, and try to hold on to the slow one. Hear a period-minded performance of the original theatre score alongside a big symphonic recording; they are almost different pieces."],"bizet-pearl-fishers-au-fond-du-temple":["A duet for tenor and baritone in the first act, about six minutes. Two men who once loved the same woman recall the moment they saw her, and the memory arrives as a hushed melody sung first by the tenor over a harp, then taken by the baritone, then by both in close harmony. The middle turns agitated as they swear never to let her divide them, and the great tune returns, louder, at the end.","Bizet was twenty-four, recently back from the Prix de Rome in Italy, and given a commission by the Theatre Lyrique on condition that the work be a full-length opera by a young composer. He had roughly four months. The libretto, with its Ceylon setting, pearl divers and veiled priestess, is standard exotic material of the period. The opera ran eighteen performances in 1863 and then disappeared until after his death.","The version everyone knows is not what Bizet wrote. His ending to the duet is quieter and harmonically stranger; the plush unison finish familiar from recordings was added by an editor in 1893, and only in the later twentieth century did critical editions restore the original. Listen for which ending a performance uses. The theme's return late in the opera is Bizet's own, an early trial of the recurring motif he would handle far better in Carmen.","Berlioz praised the score's colour; most Paris critics dismissed the piece. The opera has never quite entered the core repertoire and survives chiefly on this duet, which for a century was sung in the corrupted version. Debate now is textual, since several nineteenth-century editions compete, and dramatic, since the exotic frame is harder to present straight than it once was. The duet itself has never needed defending.","Listen for the point where the second voice enters, and then for the moment the two lines run parallel rather than answering each other. Compare an old recording, Bjorling and Merrill being the famous one, with a modern performance using the restored ending. The difference in the final twenty seconds tells you a great deal about how opera texts get made."],"bizet-carmen-seguidilla":["Four minutes in the first act, sung with her hands tied. Carmen, under arrest, describes a tavern outside the city walls where she means to dance, and claims she is singing to herself rather than to the soldier guarding her. The music is a light Spanish dance in triple time, high and quick, the orchestra flicking at her between phrases. Don Jose orders her to be silent and then unties her. The dance never breaks stride.","Bizet had never been to Spain. His Spanish material came from printed collections and from Paris cafe-concert versions of Andalusian song; the seguidilla is a real dance form, and he uses its metre and guitar-like accompaniment fairly accurately. The scene's problem was the Opera-Comique's: a woman seducing a soldier while under arrest was not what the house's family subscribers had bought their tickets for.","Carmen never addresses Jose directly here. She sings about a place, and about an unnamed lover she has just discarded, and lets him do the work of applying it to himself, which is why the aria is in the third person. Listen for the exact bar where he capitulates: the orchestra changes register, and her line, which has been circling one note for most of the number, finally rises.","The Seguidilla is the number that convinced early critics the opera was indecent, and Galli-Marie's staging of it, the tied hands and the closeness, was singled out in reviews. It is now read as the clearest demonstration of how the opera works, since Carmen's music is always a public performance and the drama lies in who believes it. Directors still disagree about whether she is calculating here or improvising.","Watch it rather than only hearing it if you can; the aria is choreography as much as song. Listen for Jose's interruptions and how little they disturb the rhythm. Then play the Habanera again and notice that both are dances, and that she has no number in the whole opera which is not one."],"bizet-symphony-c-adagio":["The second of four movements, about nine minutes. It opens with plucked strings and then an oboe alone, playing a long, plain, faintly foreign-sounding melody that seems reluctant to end. Strings take it up; a busier middle section turns briefly contrapuntal; the oboe returns. The scoring is spare throughout, so the movement is essentially one wind solo with an orchestra listening, and it fades out on the same plucked bass it began with.","Bizet wrote the symphony in November 1855, aged seventeen, as a Conservatoire student, weeks after making a piano arrangement of Gounod's First Symphony, whose influence is unmistakable. He never mentioned the piece again, never heard it played, and the manuscript sat unexamined in the Paris Conservatoire library after Reynaldo Hahn deposited it in the 1930s. Felix Weingartner conducted the premiere in Basel in February 1935, eighty years after it was written.","The melody's foreign colour comes from a scale with one step flattened, the same device Bizet would later use to conjure Spain in Carmen and Ceylon in The Pearl Fishers. Notice too that the movement contains a short, strict fugue in its middle section, a seventeen-year-old showing his teachers he had done the homework. The plucked bass at the opening is the only accompaniment for a long while; everything else is melody.","Nobody heard it for eighty years, so it has no nineteenth-century reception at all. When it appeared in 1935 the reaction was astonishment at its poise, and the comparison with Mendelssohn's teenage output was made at once. Balanchine choreographed the whole symphony in 1947 as Le Palais de Cristal, later renamed Symphony in C, which is how most people now encounter it. Scholars still weigh how much is Gounod and how much is Bizet.","Listen for the oboe and count how long the phrase runs before it comes to rest; the length is the point. Follow the movement into its busier middle and notice how quickly the texture thickens and thins again. Compare it with the slow movement of Mendelssohn's Italian Symphony, and if you can, watch Balanchine's ballet, where this music is the great adagio duet."],"bizet-l-arlesienne-minuet":["A slow minuet, about four minutes, for solo flute over harp and muted strings. The flute carries almost the entire melody and barely stops for breath; the harp supplies a rocking accompaniment underneath, and the middle section hands a countermelody to another wind instrument before the flute returns. It is the quietest number in either L'Arlesienne suite and the least danceable: the triple step is there, but nobody could move to it at this tempo.","The second L'Arlesienne suite was assembled in 1879, four years after Bizet's death, by Ernest Guiraud, who had access to the manuscripts and a publisher who wanted a sequel to a profitable first suite. Guiraud's method was to arrange and, where necessary, to import. Bizet had died at thirty-six leaving a drawer of unstaged theatre music, and posthumous editing shaped nearly everything of his that stayed in the repertoire.","This minuet is not from L'Arlesienne at all. Guiraud lifted it from Bizet's opera La jolie fille de Perth of 1867 and dropped it into the Provencal suite, where it has sat ever since under the wrong title. The original L'Arlesienne score is separately notable for one of the earliest orchestral uses of the alto saxophone, an instrument barely thirty years old, which Bizet treated as a singing voice rather than a novelty.","The number's beauty has never been in question; its provenance is the awkward part, and modern programme notes increasingly say so. The larger argument concerns Guiraud, whose posthumous interventions in Bizet, this suite and the sung recitatives for Carmen among them, kept the music alive and simultaneously misrepresented it. Performances of Bizet's original 1872 theatre score for twenty-six players have grown steadily more common since the 1980s.","Follow the flute's breathing: the phrases are long, and the player's control is the performance. Then listen for the harp, which does nearly everything else. Play the Farandole immediately afterwards for maximum contrast, and if you find a recording of the original theatre music, notice how different Bizet's own small-band sound is from Guiraud's."],"mussorgsky-pictures-at-exhibition-great-gate-kiev":["The last of the suite's ten pictures, about six minutes, and the only one that ends in triumph. A slow, heavy, hymn-like theme in block chords, written as if for massed brass, alternates with quiet passages in bare octaves that imitate Russian chant sung by men's voices. Bells begin, rendered on the piano as clanging repeated chords, and the walking theme that has linked the whole suite returns, now grand, before the final chords.","Viktor Hartmann, architect and designer, died of an aneurysm in 1873 at thirty-nine. Mussorgsky, who had lost his closest friend, wrote the suite in about three weeks in June 1874 after a memorial exhibition of Hartmann's drawings in St Petersburg. Among them was a design for a city gate at Kiev in old Russian style, with a bell tower shaped like a warrior's helmet, made for a competition that was cancelled. The gate was never built.","The picture is of a building that does not exist, which changes what the music is doing: this is construction, not description. The quiet passages between the fanfares are a genuine Russian Orthodox chant type, a repertoire Mussorgsky knew well, so the gate is being consecrated as it is imagined. And the walking theme returning at the end is the visitor himself, finally standing inside the thing he has been looking at.","The suite went unpublished until 1886, after Mussorgsky's death, in a text corrected by Rimsky-Korsakov; the composer's own version only became standard in the twentieth century. Ravel's 1922 orchestration for Koussevitzky is now more famous than the original and has been criticised for making rough, deliberately awkward piano writing sound expensive. There are more than twenty other orchestrations, and pianists increasingly argue the piece belongs on the instrument it was written for.","Hear it on the piano first and notice how hard the writing is to make sound like bells; the awkwardness is the point. Then hear Ravel's version and decide what has been gained and lost. Listen for the walking theme's return near the end, which you will recognise instantly if you have followed the whole suite, and which has been completely changed."],"mussorgsky-night-bald-mountain":["About eleven minutes of orchestral noise-making that begins with strings scurrying high and low at once while trombones bellow over them. A witches' sabbath assembles: a stamping dance in the strings, a heavy processional for the arrival of a dark god, a long crescendo of massed brass. Then a bell tolls, the storm evaporates, and the piece ends with clarinet and a solo flute over shivering strings as dawn comes up.","Mussorgsky finished the score on St John's Eve, 23 June 1867, in a burst of confidence, writing to a friend that he had produced something entirely his own and would not revise a note of it. Balakirev, whose approval the Mighty Handful required, refused to perform it and demanded changes. Mussorgsky refused to make them. The piece was never played in his lifetime, and he recycled its material twice into unfinished stage works instead.","What is played today is essentially Rimsky-Korsakov's piece. In 1886 he took a later version Mussorgsky had made for an opera project, smoothed the harmony, rescored it, and added the calm ending with the bell, so that dawn is Rimsky's invention rather than Mussorgsky's. The 1867 original, published only in 1968, is harsher, shorter and stops without consolation. Both are worth knowing, and they are not the same work.","For a century the piece was heard only in Rimsky's dress and taken as proof that Mussorgsky needed editing. The recovery of the 1867 score reversed that, and it is now a standard example in the argument about how much of Mussorgsky the world has actually heard. Stokowski's arrangement for Disney's Fantasia in 1940 fixed the Rimsky version in popular memory, dawn included, and made the piece a Halloween fixture.","Start with Rimsky's version, since it is the one everything else refers to, and wait for the bell, the moment the whole texture switches off. Then find a recording of Mussorgsky's 1867 original, which Abbado championed, and hear how much rougher and stranger it is. Neither is the definitive text, and the pair is the lesson."],"mussorgsky-boris-godunov-coronation-scene":["The end of the prologue: the new Tsar processes out of the cathedral to be acclaimed. Bells dominate, real ones and the orchestra imitating them, over two chords that alternate endlessly without ever resolving into a key. The crowd sings a massive acclamation. Then everything drops away and Boris himself sings, quietly and alone, that his soul is heavy with foreboding, before ordering the feast and walking off into the noise.","Mussorgsky wrote a first version in 1869 on Pushkin's play; the Imperial Theatres rejected it, partly for having no leading female role. He revised it in 1872, adding a Polish act, and the Mariinsky staged it in February 1874. The subject, a Tsar who may have had a child murdered to take the throne and a people who suffer whoever rules, was legible as contemporary politics, and the censors read it exactly that way.","The famous sound is two chords built on notes as far apart as the scale allows, swung back and forth so that neither can be heard as home. Mussorgsky wrote it to imitate the way Russian church bells beat against each other out of tune. Listen for what happens once the crowd falls silent: the orchestra thins to almost nothing and Boris's first words are a confession of dread. He is crowned and frightened within three minutes.","Rimsky-Korsakov reorchestrated the whole opera twice, in 1896 and 1908, and for most of the twentieth century that is what audiences heard, brighter and better balanced and quite unlike Mussorgsky's deliberately raw scoring. Shostakovich made another version in 1940. Since the 1970s the composer's own versions have been performed and recorded increasingly often, and which Boris is the real one is now the standard case study in editing dead composers.","Listen to the alternating bells at the start and try to decide what key the music is in; the fact that you cannot is the composer's intention. Then wait for the crowd to fall silent and hear how small Boris's voice is. Compare Rimsky's orchestration with Mussorgsky's own, both of which Gergiev has recorded, and hear how much politeness the editing adds."],"mussorgsky-pictures-at-exhibition-promenade":["The suite's opening and its connective tissue: a bare, sturdy tune stated first in single notes and then harmonised in thick chords, as though one voice were joined by a choir. It lasts under two minutes and comes back four more times between the pictures, each time in a different key, at a different speed and in a different mood, until it is finally absorbed into the last picture. The metre keeps changing, five beats then six, so the walk limps.","Mussorgsky wrote to the critic Vladimir Stasov in June 1874 that he was moving through the Hartmann memorial exhibition and putting himself into the piece, and the Promenade is that self-portrait: the composer walking between his dead friend's drawings. Stasov, who had organised the show and who named the Mighty Handful, is the suite's dedicatee. Mussorgsky was thirty-five and had seven years left to live.","The irregular metre is not carelessness. Russian folk song moves in uneven groups of beats, and Mussorgsky is writing the way speech and song actually go in his country rather than in a German textbook. Follow the Promenade's returns and you can track the visitor's state of mind: hesitant after the catacombs, hurried after the market, and at the end not walking at all but standing still inside the final picture.","Because the Promenade is a frame rather than a picture, orchestrators have treated it as expendable; Ravel omits one of its appearances entirely, a decision argued over ever since, since the walk is the suite's narrative. Pianists made the same mistake for decades by playing individual movements as detachable encores. The current consensus is that Pictures is a single span and that the Promenade is what makes it one.","Play the opening thirty seconds and learn the tune, then listen to the whole suite and count how many times it comes back and how far it has changed. On the piano the first statement should sound almost clumsy: heavy, unhurried, deliberately plain. Compare it with the trumpet opening of Ravel's orchestration, which is beautiful and, arguably, wrong."],"mussorgsky-pictures-at-exhibition-baba-yaga":["Two hammered chords, then a headlong ride. The ninth picture, about three minutes, is the suite's most violent music: the outer sections pound in the bass while the right hand leaps across huge intervals, and a quieter middle creeps, with a repeated figure like something circling in the dark. Then the ride returns and drives straight, without a break, into the final picture. It is the only movement that does not stop.","Hartmann's drawing was a design for a clock in the shape of the hut of Baba Yaga, the witch of Russian folklore, who lives in a house standing on hen's legs and travels in a mortar, steering with a pestle and sweeping away her tracks with a broom. Mussorgsky ignored the clock entirely and wrote the witch instead, which tells you what he thought a picture was for.","Listen to the quiet middle section, where a single high note repeats over a slow murmur: that is usually heard as the flight through the forest, and it is the only soft writing in the movement. Then notice the join at the end, where the last chords do not resolve but rise straight into the Great Gate, so that the witch delivers the visitor to the gate and the suite's two biggest movements form one span.","The movement's difficulty made it a favourite of virtuoso pianists, which for a long time meant it was played as a display piece detached from the suite. Orchestrators, Ravel included, have found the leaping right hand hard to translate, and comparing their solutions is a good way to hear what orchestration adds and removes. Scholars point out that Mussorgsky's own title refers to the hut, not the witch: the horror is architectural.","Take the first ten seconds loud. Then listen for the quiet middle and notice how little material it uses. Follow the movement all the way into the Great Gate without pausing the recording, which is how it was written. Try a live piano recording against Ravel's orchestration for two opposite kinds of savagery."],"mussorgsky-khovanshchina-prelude":["Six minutes of orchestral dawn before the curtain. A single quiet melody in the strings, folk-shaped and unhurried, is passed around the orchestra and varied five or six times, gaining a little light at each return: woodwind, then horns, then full strings. Bells sound distantly, a cockerel is heard, and the music never once raises its voice. It is a set of variations disguised as sunrise over a city.","Mussorgsky worked on Khovanshchina from 1872 until his death in 1881, writing his own libretto about the violent Moscow of the 1680s, when the old Russia of the musketeers and the Old Believers was destroyed by Peter the Great's new one. He was drinking heavily and destitute and left the opera unfinished, with this prelude among the few sections fully scored. Repin painted him in a hospital ward days before he died at forty-two.","The dawn is a lie the opera then dismantles. This is the last peaceful music in a work that ends with a mass self-immolation, and the tune's calm folk shape belongs to the world about to be destroyed. Listen for how little harmony there is: the melody is nearly modal and the accompaniment often just a held chord, so the sense of brightening comes almost entirely from changing instruments rather than changing chords.","Every performance is an edition. Rimsky-Korsakov completed and rescored the opera in 1883, cutting heavily; Ravel and Stravinsky prepared parts of another version for Diaghilev in 1913; Shostakovich made the version most used today in 1959, restoring Mussorgsky's harmonies where he could. The prelude survives all of them nearly intact, which is why it is the work's calling card. What the whole opera should sound like remains genuinely unsettled.","Count the returns of the tune and notice which instruments take it each time, since that sequence is the entire structure. Listen for the bells near the end. Then hear Shostakovich's orchestration against Rimsky's and note how much darker and thinner Shostakovich keeps it, which most scholars think is closer to what Mussorgsky wanted."],"tchaikovsky-piano-concerto-no-1":["Three movements, about thirty-five minutes. The opening is the most famous misdirection in the repertoire: four horn notes, then huge piano chords under a broad tune in the wrong key, lasting three minutes and never returning. What follows is a fast, argumentative first movement; a slow movement that sets a flute solo against a scampering middle section; and a finale built on a Ukrainian dance song that drives to a blazing coda in the major.","Tchaikovsky finished it in February 1875 and played it through for Nikolai Rubinstein, director of the Moscow Conservatory and the finest pianist he knew, expecting advice. Rubinstein called it worthless, unplayable and vulgar. Tchaikovsky refused to change a note, withdrew the dedication, and offered it instead to Hans von Bulow, who gave the premiere in Boston on 25 October 1875, the first major Russian work launched in America.","The famous opening theme is in a different key from the concerto and is abandoned after the introduction; a century and a half of listeners have waited for it to come back. Two of the other tunes are borrowed: the first movement's main subject came from blind beggar-musicians Tchaikovsky heard at a Ukrainian market, and the finale takes a Ukrainian spring dance. Rubinstein later learned the concerto and played it often.","Rubinstein's verdict is the most famous bad review in music, and Tchaikovsky's account of it, written three years afterwards, is the only source for it. The concerto became the popular test of pianistic size, and the Horowitz-Toscanini performances of 1943 fixed it as a heroic set piece; cuts and thickened orchestration crept into practice. Since the 1990s scholars have argued for Tchaikovsky's own final 1888 text against the posthumous editions most pianists use.","Accept that the opening will not return, and listen to the rest for what it is. Try the slow movement's middle section, which is a fast waltz hidden inside an Andante. Then compare a big, slow, romantic reading with a lean and quick one; Argerich takes it at a speed that changes the character of the whole piece."],"tchaikovsky-swan-lake-scene":["Three minutes that open the lakeside act. Harp and trembling strings set up a shimmer, and a solo oboe plays a long curving melody in the minor with nothing to support it but that shimmer. Strings take the tune up and swell, brass enter heavily, and it breaks off unresolved. There is no dancing in it at all; it is scene-setting, played while the swans arrive, and it returns at the ballet's end as the music of the whole story.","The Bolshoi commissioned a ballet in 1875 and paid him eight hundred roubles. Tchaikovsky, then thirty-five and teaching harmony he disliked teaching, wrote it in under a year. The 1877 Moscow premiere, choreographed by Julius Reisinger with a conductor who could not manage the score, was judged a failure; numbers were replaced with music by other composers, and the ballet stayed in that condition until after his death.","The oboe theme is the swan and the curse at once, which is why it returns at the moment the lovers die. Listen to what lies underneath it: the strings play no tune, only a hovering texture, so the melody has nothing to lean on, and the harp's rippling figures are the water. Tchaikovsky is said to have drawn on material from an earlier, destroyed domestic ballet on the same subject written for his sister's children.","The 1895 St Petersburg revival by Marius Petipa and Lev Ivanov, two years after Tchaikovsky's death, is the version the world inherited: reordered, shortened, and partly recomposed by Riccardo Drigo. Almost no production uses the 1877 score as written. Scholars now treat the ballet as the moment dance music stopped being functional accompaniment, and the argument is over which text best supports that claim.","Listen for the oboe entering over the shimmer and follow it to the end without expecting resolution. Then find the ballet's final scene and hear the same tune again with the full orchestra behind it. If you can, watch it staged; the music was written to be seen, and the swans arriving is part of the sound."],"tchaikovsky-symphony-no-6-pathetique-finale":["The symphony's fourth movement is slow, which almost no symphony before it had been. Ten minutes marked Adagio lamentoso: a descending string melody, a second theme that briefly gathers warmth, a climax that collapses, a single stroke on the gong, and then a chorale for low brass. The final three minutes sink through the orchestra into the cellos and basses, getting quieter and lower until the sound simply stops being audible.","Tchaikovsky conducted the premiere in St Petersburg on 28 October 1893 to a puzzled reception and died nine days later, officially of cholera contracted from unboiled water. He had told his nephew the symphony had a programme he would not disclose. He was fifty-three, internationally famous, and living a life in which his homosexuality had to remain concealed; the coincidence of this ending and that death has shaped every hearing since.","The famous descending melody is not played by anyone. The first and second violins alternate notes, each section taking every other note of the tune, so the melody exists in the listener's ear and in no player's part. Nobody knows why he wrote it this way. The first movement also quotes, quietly in the trombones, the Russian Orthodox chant sung for the dead.","The second performance, conducted by Eduard Napravnik three weeks after the composer's death, was overwhelming, and the symphony's reputation was made by the funeral. A long argument about whether Tchaikovsky killed himself, involving a supposed court of honour and poison, has run since the 1980s and is unresolved, though most specialists now doubt it. What is not in doubt is that ending a symphony by fading into silence permanently changed the form's options.","Listen for the gong: one stroke, easy to miss, and everything after it is aftermath. Then go back and try to hear the interlocking violins in the opening theme, which you will not, and that is the point. Compare Mravinsky's cold, fast reading with a slower Western one; the piece survives both, but they are different works."],"tchaikovsky-1812-overture":["Fifteen minutes in a single span. It opens with violas and cellos playing a Russian hymn in the manner of chanted prayer, then builds to battle music in which two national tunes fight: the Marseillaise, appearing and reappearing in the brass, and Russian material answering it. The French tune is gradually beaten down, cannon fire enters, and the opening hymn returns in full brass over carillon bells, with the Tsarist anthem crowning the close.","Moscow was preparing the 1882 Arts and Industry Exhibition and the consecration of the Cathedral of Christ the Saviour, built to commemorate the defeat of Napoleon in 1812. Nikolai Rubinstein asked Tchaikovsky for an occasional piece. He wrote it in six weeks, complaining that he felt no warmth for it and that it would have no artistic merit whatever. The first performance took place in a tent near the cathedral in August 1882.","Both anthems are anachronisms. God Save the Tsar was written in 1833, twenty-one years after the campaign, and the Marseillaise was banned in Napoleon's France, so neither could have been heard on the battlefield the piece depicts. The opening hymn is a genuine Orthodox troparion. The cannon are written into the score as an instrument, though Tchaikovsky is unlikely to have imagined the outdoor artillery performances that later became standard.","Tchaikovsky's own dismissal is quoted in nearly every programme note, usually to excuse enjoying the piece. Soviet performances replaced the Tsarist anthem with a chorus from Glinka; American Independence Day concerts adopted the work wholesale, cannon included, so that a Russian imperial commemoration became a fixture of the Fourth of July. Critics who defend it point to the craftsmanship of the tune-against-tune construction rather than to the noise.","Learn the two anthems first, the Marseillaise and the Orthodox hymn at the opening, and the piece becomes legible as an argument rather than a barrage. Listen for the moment the French tune stops recovering. Indoors, choose a recording with real bells; outdoors, accept the cannon and enjoy them for what they are."],"tchaikovsky-violin-concerto-d":["Three movements, about thirty-five minutes. The orchestra begins with a few bars that turn out not to be the theme; the soloist enters and supplies it, a long singing tune that keeps climbing. The first movement is huge, with its written-out cadenza placed before the recapitulation rather than after. A short muted slow movement in the minor follows without a real ending, and the finale is a Russian dance, fast, stamping and relentless.","Tchaikovsky wrote it in under a month in spring 1878 at Clarens on Lake Geneva, recovering from the catastrophe of his brief marriage, with the young violinist Iosif Kotek at hand to advise on the solo writing. He had just heard Lalo's Symphonie espagnole and wanted its freshness and its lightness of orchestral touch. He replaced the original slow movement with the one now played and published the discarded one separately.","Leopold Auer, dedicatee of the first version, refused to play it and called it unviolinistic, so the dedication passed to Adolph Brodsky, who gave the premiere. Auer later taught it to Heifetz, Elman and Zimbalist in his own edited version with cuts, and most twentieth-century performances used that text. In the finale, listen for a folk tune that keeps being interrupted by a slower drooping idea; the dance never gets a clear run at it.","Eduard Hanslick's Vienna review of the 1881 premiere is notorious: he wrote that the finale stank to the ear and that here was music one could hear being played badly. Tchaikovsky memorised the review and repeated it for the rest of his life. The concerto is now among the four or five most played, and the argument has narrowed to whether to restore the passages Auer removed.","Wait for the soloist's first entry, since the orchestra's opening is a doorway rather than a theme. In the first movement, notice that the cadenza arrives early. Then go straight to the finale for the dance. Try Milstein or Oistrakh for classical line, and a modern uncut performance to hear exactly what Auer's edition removed."],"tchaikovsky-nutcracker-dance-sugar-plum-fairy":["Two minutes, and among the shortest famous pieces in music. Plucked low strings tick like a clock, and over them a celesta, a small keyboard whose hammers strike steel bars, plays a descending, dripping melody in the minor. A bass clarinet answers with a curling phrase. The dance repeats with slightly fuller scoring and ends in a flourish. Nothing develops: the number exists to introduce a sound nobody in the theatre had heard.","Tchaikovsky came across the celesta in a Paris workshop in 1891 and had one shipped to St Petersburg in secret, writing to his publisher that Rimsky-Korsakov and Glazunov must not learn of the instrument before he had used it. The Nutcracker opened at the Mariinsky on 18 December 1892 in a double bill with his opera Iolanta. He thought the ballet much weaker than The Sleeping Beauty and said so plainly.","The instrument had been patented in Paris in 1886 by Auguste Mustel, and this dance is its first celebrated orchestral use, which is why the secrecy was a genuine matter of professional priority. Listen for the bass clarinet's reply, which is the movement's other half and is almost always overlooked. The plucked accompaniment never varies, and that is what makes the two minutes feel mechanical: a dance for a figure not quite alive.","The premiere was received without enthusiasm, and the ballet's reputation was built in the twentieth century, largely in America, where Balanchine's 1954 production turned it into an annual institution. The Sugar Plum Fairy's music has since been sampled, arranged and advertised past the point of hearing it. Critics who return to the full score are usually surprised by how dark and thinly scored much of the ballet is.","Listen for the bass clarinet and follow that rather than the celesta. Then hear the number in the concert Suite, where it sits between the Chinese and Russian dances and the orchestra sounds much smaller. Compare a Russian recording, usually slower and heavier, with a fast Western one."],"tchaikovsky-nutcracker-waltz-flowers":["Seven minutes, the longest number in the ballet's second act. A harp cadenza opens it alone; four horns then state the main waltz theme quietly, and the strings take over and expand it. Three or four further tunes follow, each broader than the last, with a rising clarinet phrase and a cello countermelody among them. The whole accelerates into a coda that piles the themes on top of one another for the corps de ballet's exit.","Petipa gave Tchaikovsky exact specifications for the ballet, down to the number of bars and the character of each dance, and this waltz is one of the few places where he had room to expand. The commission followed the success of The Sleeping Beauty, on a Hoffmann story as adapted by Dumas. He worked on it through 1891, a year that also took him to New York to conduct at the opening of Carnegie Hall.","The harp cadenza at the start is a survival of an older theatrical convention, an instrumental flourish to quieten the house, and it does no structural work at all. Listen for the horns' first statement, four playing in unison and softly, which was unusual scoring and gives the tune its particular blurred warmth. Then count the waltz's tunes; there are more of them than memory retains, and the coda is a catalogue of them.","As part of the 1892 concert Suite the waltz was a success months before the ballet opened, and it has carried the score commercially ever since. Ballet critics have long argued that the second act is a divertissement without drama and that this waltz is where Tchaikovsky simply writes a symphonic movement instead. Recordings differ mainly over whether the conductor lets the corps dance or lets the orchestra sing.","Listen through the harp opening to the horns and fix that tune in mind. Then, as the piece goes on, try to notice each time a genuinely new tune arrives rather than a repeat. Compare it directly with the Sleeping Beauty waltz and with Strauss's Blue Danube: three answers to the same question about how long a waltz can grow."],"tchaikovsky-sleeping-beauty-waltz":["The Garland Waltz from the first act, about five minutes, danced by villagers carrying flower hoops. A short introduction leads to the main tune on strings with brass punching the second and third beats; a quieter section for woodwind follows, and the opening returns more fully scored. The melody is long, taking many bars to finish a single sentence, and its repetitions are what allow a stage full of dancers to move in formation.","Ivan Vsevolozhsky, director of the Imperial Theatres, proposed a ballet on Perrault and handed Tchaikovsky Petipa's minutely detailed scenario, with bar counts, characters and tempi specified. Tchaikovsky, who loved eighteenth-century France and the court the ballet evokes, accepted the constraints happily and later called the score one of his best works. The premiere at the Mariinsky in January 1890 drew from the Tsar only the remark that it was very nice.","Petipa's written instructions survive, and comparing them with the score shows where Tchaikovsky obeyed and where he overshot; this waltz is longer and more symphonic than was asked for. Listen for the offbeat brass punctuation, which is not decoration but a metronome for thirty-odd dancers holding garlands. The tune's phrases are deliberately unequal in length, which is what stops a five-minute waltz from becoming a loop.","The Sleeping Beauty is now generally judged Tchaikovsky's finest ballet score and the high point of Imperial Russian ballet, though its first audience was lukewarm. Diaghilev's lavish 1921 London production nearly bankrupted his company and did not convert the West immediately; the ballet's Western canonisation came later, through Nikolai Sergeyev's notated records of the Petipa choreography, carried out of Russia after the revolution.","Let the tune complete itself before judging it, because the first phrase is only half a sentence. Listen for the brass on the offbeats, and then for the quieter woodwind section. Play it against the Nutcracker's Waltz of the Flowers to hear one composer solving the same problem twice, and watch a staging if you can, since the garlands explain the music."],"tchaikovsky-romeo-juliet-fantasy-overture":["Twenty minutes in one movement built from three ideas. It opens with a slow chorale for clarinets and bassoons in the manner of church music, then breaks into a fast, jagged, hammering section for the street fighting, with cymbal crashes landing off the beat. Between the fights comes the love music: a wide rising melody first given to cor anglais and muted violas. All three collide, the love theme is broken off, and hard final chords close it.","Balakirev handed Tchaikovsky the subject, the key scheme and the shape in 1869, then criticised the result and made him rewrite it; a third version followed in 1880. Tchaikovsky was twenty-nine at the first attempt, still finding his manner, and this is where he found it. His engagement to the soprano Desiree Artot had recently collapsed, though how much of that is in the music is guesswork rather than evidence.","The love theme is not the tune most people remember. What films usually quote is the second, gentler idea that follows it; the actual first statement of the love music is the wide cor anglais and viola melody, and it appears only three times in the whole piece. The ending was rewritten more than once, and the drum roll and hard chords that now close the work only arrived with the final version.","The 1870 Moscow premiere failed, reportedly because the audience was preoccupied with a court case involving the conductor, and Balakirev remained unsatisfied with the piece for years. It is now the earliest Tchaikovsky work in permanent repertory and the model for a genre: the single-movement concert overture that tells a play's plot without words. Analysts note that the fight music and the love music share intervals, which is why the collision works.","Learn the three ideas separately, chorale then fight then love, and listen straight through marking where each returns. The moment to wait for is the climax where the love theme tries to sing and the fight music cuts it off. Compare the 1869 original, occasionally recorded, with the standard version to hear how much a composer can improve on his own idea."],"tchaikovsky-serenade-strings-waltz":["The second of the Serenade's four movements, about four minutes for strings alone. A short rising phrase leads into a waltz that hovers and hesitates rather than swinging; the accompaniment is light, often plucked or barely touching the string, so the movement stays airborne throughout. A slightly more urgent middle section builds and subsides, the tune returns, and it closes softly. No wind, no brass, no percussion, only violins, violas, cellos and basses.","He wrote the Serenade in autumn 1880 at the same time as the 1812 Overture and told his patron Nadezhda von Meck that the difference in feeling could not have been greater: the overture made to order and without love, the Serenade out of inner compulsion. It revives an eighteenth-century form, the outdoor serenade, as deliberate homage to Mozart, whom Tchaikovsky revered above every other composer.","The waltz is written so that no single line is ever quite the melody: the tune keeps being handed between first violins and cellos in mid-phrase. Notice also how rarely the strong beat is actually played, so the dance is implied by what is missing. The Serenade's opening movement is headed in the form of a sonatina, and its grave introduction returns at the very end of the finale, framing all four movements including this one.","It succeeded immediately, and Tchaikovsky reported the players applauding at the rehearsal. It has never fallen out of favour, which for a long time meant it was patronised as light music. Balanchine's Serenade of 1934, his first ballet made in America, used the score complete and permanently changed how it is heard. The performance argument now concerns size: a chamber group of twenty players against a full symphonic string section.","Listen for the handover of the tune between violins and cellos; once you hear it happen you cannot stop hearing it. Then play the first movement to catch the grave opening that frames everything else. Compare a small period-minded ensemble with a big modern string body, and the waltz becomes a different dance entirely."],"tchaikovsky-symphony-no-5-andante-cantabile":["The symphony's second movement, about thirteen minutes. Low strings open with a dark, hymn-like passage, and then a solo horn plays one of the longest and most exposed melodies ever given to the instrument, unhurried and in the major. Oboe and clarinet answer, the strings take the tune and swell. Twice the movement is broken into by loud brass playing the symphony's motto theme, the second time destroying the climax outright. The end is quiet and unresolved.","Tchaikovsky wrote the Fifth in the summer of 1888 at Frolovskoye, his first symphony in a decade, after years of believing himself written out. He was forty-eight and newly established as a conductor of his own works across Europe, and he was deeply unsure of the piece. His sketchbook contains a note about submission before fate or providence, which is generally taken as the symphony's undisclosed programme.","The motto that interrupts twice is the same tune heard in the clarinets at the very opening of the symphony, and it returns in every movement, transformed each time from funeral march to triumphal march. Here it functions as an intrusion, so the movement is never allowed to finish its own thought. Listen also to how the horn melody avoids an obvious cadence for minutes together, which is what makes it feel endless.","The 1888 St Petersburg premiere was received without warmth, and Tchaikovsky decided the symphony was insincere and a failure, writing so to von Meck; Hamburg audiences a year later persuaded him otherwise. Twentieth-century critics attacked the finale's major-key triumph as bombast, a charge the slow movement escaped entirely. It is now the most-played of his symphonies, and horn players regard the solo as among the two or three hardest in the repertoire.","The horn solo begins after about a minute and a half of dark string writing; wait for it, and notice that it never repeats itself exactly. Listen for the two brass interruptions and how the second arrives at the worst possible moment. Then play the symphony's first bars to hear the interrupting tune in its original quiet form."],"tchaikovsky-marche-slave":["Ten minutes. A slow, weighted funeral march in the minor opens on low strings and bassoons, built from a Serbian folk song and repeated with growing force. A brighter middle section brings a second Serbian tune, and then the Russian imperial anthem enters in the brass. The march returns, the anthem comes back in full, and the piece ends in a fast, blazing major-key coda with the whole orchestra at maximum volume.","In summer 1876 Serbia went to war with the Ottoman Empire and lost badly. Russian public opinion swung behind the Slavic cause and Russian volunteers crossed the border. Nikolai Rubinstein asked Tchaikovsky for a piece for a Moscow benefit concert for the wounded, and he wrote it in a few days. The concert took place in November 1876, and by several accounts the audience wept and shouted.","The opening theme is a real Serbian folk song and the second one is Serbian too, so the piece stages an alliance in its material as well as in its rhetoric: Serbian tunes rescued by the Russian anthem. That anthem later made the work unperformable in the Soviet Union in its original form, and Soviet editions replaced it. Listen for the way the funeral march's rhythm survives underneath the triumph at the end.","It was a sensation in 1876 and has been an embarrassment at intervals since, as the politics it celebrates have moved in and out of acceptability. Musically it uses the same construction as the 1812 Overture, written four years later and generally judged the better piece. Conductors treat it as a concert closer, and those who take it slowly find more in the march than the coda's noise suggests.","Listen for the two Serbian tunes before the anthem arrives, and notice how much of the piece stays in the minor. Then wait for the brass to bring the anthem in. Play it beside the 1812 Overture, which uses the same device with better material, and try a Russian orchestra for the sheer weight of the opening march."],"tchaikovsky-none-but-lonely-heart":["Three minutes for one voice and piano. The piano begins alone with a rocking figure that then continues almost unbroken; the voice enters low with a short phrase that rises and falls back, and the whole song is built from that one shape, repeated and lifted higher each time. There is a single climax near the end, where the voice reaches its top note on the word for longing, and then the piano finishes alone.","Written in 1869, the last of six songs published as Opus 6, when Tchaikovsky was twenty-nine and had just completed the first version of Romeo and Juliet. The text is Lev Mey's Russian rendering of Mignon's song from Goethe's Wilhelm Meister, the poem that begins only one who has known longing knows what I suffer. Beethoven, Schubert, Schumann and Wolf all set it, so the competition was formidable and the choice deliberate.","Mignon in Goethe is a child, and the German settings mostly treat the poem as a child's grief. Tchaikovsky's is an adult's, and it works as a love song rather than a lament, partly because the Russian translation shifts the emphasis. Listen to the piano's left hand, which keeps sinking by half-steps beneath a melody that has not moved, so the ground gives way under a voice standing still.","It became his most widely known song almost immediately and was arranged for every available instrument; a Hollywood film of 1944 took its English title, and Sinatra and others later recorded it as a popular ballad. That crossover has made critics wary of it. Specialists in Russian song generally rate the later Opus 60 and Opus 73 sets higher, while conceding that this one solves the problem of setting Mignon in a way the German settings do not.","Listen first in Russian, then in a transposition for a different voice type; the song changes character completely between a soprano and a baritone. Follow the piano's descending bass line throughout. Then hear Schubert's and Wolf's settings of the same poem back to back with it: three composers, one text, three entirely different griefs."],"tchaikovsky-eugene-onegin-polonaise":["The orchestral opening of the third act, about five minutes: a ball in a St Petersburg mansion, years after the events of the first two acts. A polonaise, the Polish processional dance in three beats with its characteristic snap on the first, played by full orchestra at full brightness. Two contrasting episodes, one for strings and one lighter, separate returns of the main tune. The curtain rises on it and the guests enter to it.","Tchaikovsky wrote Onegin in 1877 and 1878, during and after his disastrous marriage, calling it lyric scenes rather than an opera because Pushkin's novel in verse offered no plot fit for the stage. He did not want stars; the first performance was given by Moscow Conservatory students at the Maly Theatre in March 1879 under Nikolai Rubinstein. The polonaise was the Russian court's ceremonial dance, opening every formal ball, so its use here is social documentation.","The dance's job is dramatic. It announces that Onegin has re-entered a world of protocol he once despised, and its glitter is precisely what he cannot join. Compare it with the waltz that opens the second act, played by a provincial band at a country name-day party: the same composer writing two social classes. The third act's second dance, an ecossaise, closes the ball, and almost nobody ever plays that one.","The opera's early Russian reception was uneasy, with critics objecting to putting Pushkin's famous verse on stage at all, and it took Tchaikovsky's own conducting and a Bolshoi production to establish it. The polonaise escaped into concert programmes immediately and is now performed far more often than the opera is staged outside Russia. Recent productions have reduced its pageantry, treating the ball as cold rather than splendid.","Listen to the rhythm of the first bar and count three; the snap on the first beat is what makes it a polonaise rather than a waltz. Then find the second-act waltz and hear the class difference between the two parties. In the theatre, watch Onegin during this music, since the best productions leave him standing still while everyone else moves."],"weber-der-freischutz":["Three acts of Singspiel — sung numbers separated by spoken dialogue. A marksman, Max, must win a shooting trial to marry Agathe, and takes seven magic bullets cast at midnight. The Wolf's Glen scene that closes Act II is what you remember: an owl with glowing eyes, an incantation spoken and counted over shuddering strings, low clarinets, drum rolls and a wild hunt. The overture gathers four horns, the same shivering harmony and Agathe's soaring melody before ending in blazing C major.","Weber wrote it while running the German opera at Dresden, in a court that still treated Italian opera as the serious kind. The premiere on 18 June 1821 at Berlin's new Schauspielhaus came a few years after the Napoleonic wars, when German audiences wanted something recognisably their own: forest, folk song, huntsmen, village superstition. Friedrich Kind's libretto came out of a collection of ghost stories. Within a year the piece was playing across the German-speaking lands.","Weber ties the drama to a scheme of keys and instrumental colours rather than to tunes alone. The demonic Samiel never sings — he speaks, over a hollow low chord and muffled drums, so the ear learns to dread a sound rather than a character. Listen for the horn writing: warm and four-part for the human world, stopped and snarling in the Glen. The clarinet, Weber's own instrument, carries Agathe's hope.","The premiere was a sensation, and within months the score had been pirated, parodied and arranged for guitar, military band and piano duet. Berlioz set the spoken dialogue as recitative for Paris in 1841 and inserted Weber's Invitation to the Dance as the compulsory ballet, a version now abandoned. Debate continues over the Hermit who arrives to overturn the sentence in the last minutes, and over how much dialogue a modern staging should keep.","Start with the overture, then jump straight to the end of Act II. In the Wolf's Glen, count the seven bullets with Kaspar and notice how each casting brings a new horror out of the orchestra. Then hear Agathe's Act II scene, \"Leise, leise\", for the other side of the same score. Carlos Kleiber's recording is the classic; a period band brings out the raw natural horns."],"weber-euryanthe":["Three acts sung throughout, with no spoken dialogue — which for a German opera in 1823 was the whole point. A medieval wager on a wife's fidelity, a secret about a dead girl's ghost, and two of the best villains in the repertory in the jealous Eglantine and the scheming Lysiart. The overture is the most played part: a hard-driving Allegro that stops dead for eight muted solo violins, hushed and very high, before the drive resumes.","After Der Freischütz made him famous, Vienna's Kärntnertortheater asked for a grand heroic opera, and Weber took the chance to prove that German opera could sustain continuous music. He was already ill with the tuberculosis that killed him three years later. His librettist, Helmina von Chézy, produced a plot he revised repeatedly and still could not make clear. It opened on 25 October 1823 in a city where Rossini was the reigning taste, with Schubert in the audience.","Weber threads short recurring figures through the score — the ghost music, a motto for Euryanthe's innocence — so that the orchestra remembers what the characters conceal. Those eight muted violins in the overture are not decoration: they are the vision of Emma's ghost, lifted out of Act III and planted in the first minutes, so the audience hears the secret before anyone speaks it. Wagner studied the device closely.","It ran briefly and then faded, blamed almost entirely on the libretto; the music has never been in doubt. Nineteenth-century admirers from Schumann to Wagner argued that the score was ahead of anything else in German opera, and later conductors tried to rescue it by rewriting the text. None of those versions held the stage. It survives mostly through the overture, with occasional concert performances making the case for the whole.","Hear the overture first and wait for the moment the orchestra thins to eight muted violins — thirty seconds Wagner never forgot. Then try Eglantine's Act I scene, a genuine mezzo villain aria, and the Act II duet where the trap closes. Play the first act of Lohengrin straight afterwards and the family resemblance is unmistakable, down to the vocal types."],"weber-oberon":["An English romantic opera in three acts, with spoken dialogue and a plot that shuttles between fairyland, the court of Charlemagne, Baghdad and Tunis. The overture is the famous part: a solo horn call of three quiet notes answered by muted strings, then a headlong Allegro built from tunes heard later on. The other high point is Reiza's Act II scena, \"Ocean, thou mighty monster\", a storm and a rescue written for a big dramatic soprano.","Covent Garden commissioned it, and Weber, coughing blood and knowing he had little time, learned English at a lesson a day so that he could set James Robinson Planché's text himself. He travelled to London in 1826, conducted the premiere on 12 April and died there on 5 June, aged thirty-nine. The English form he was handed — songs and dialogue wrapped around scenic spectacle — was not the continuous drama he had been reaching for in Euryanthe.","The horn call that opens the overture is Oberon's magic horn, and it returns at every turn of the plot, so an instrument works as a character. Weber also made the fairy music deliberately thin and high — piccolo, triangle, quiet divided strings — against heavy brass for the human world. Listen for how little the orchestra doubles the voices; the scoring is transparent enough to sound decades later than 1826.","London loved it and Weber despised its shape, calling the assignment a job forced on him by money. Later conductors tried to repair the structure, replacing the dialogue with recitative or with a narrator, and none of those versions has become standard. Most houses now avoid the staging problem altogether and give the overture alone, which has become one of the most played concert overtures ever written.","Begin with the overture and listen for the horn's quiet call returning inside the fast music. Then find \"Ocean, thou mighty monster\" sung by a big-voiced soprano — Birgit Nilsson's recording is the benchmark — and hear the storm built from the orchestra alone. Compare the fairy writing with Mendelssohn's Midsummer Night's Dream overture from the same year."],"weber-clarinet-concerto-no-1":["Three movements, about twenty minutes. The first is a stormy Allegro in which the soloist enters not with a flourish but with a long quiet line over hushed strings. The slow movement holds the surprise: the clarinet sings a hymn, then falls silent while three horns play a chorale alone, and answers them in a veiled low register. The finale is a bounding rondo full of leaps, trills and rapid figures across the instrument's whole compass.","Weber wrote it in Munich in 1811 for Heinrich Baermann, the virtuoso of the Bavarian court orchestra, having produced a concertino for him within days of first hearing him play. The clarinet was still a young instrument, its key mechanism changing decade by decade, and Baermann's soft singing tone shaped what Weber asked of it. Weber was twenty-four, drifting between courts after a financial scandal in Stuttgart and living largely on what touring earned.","The chorale in the slow movement is the moment to watch. Weber reduces the orchestra to three horns and stops the soloist entirely, a piece of theatre imported from opera into a concerto — the Wolf's Glen sonority ten years early. Notice too how often he writes in the clarinet's lowest register, the chalumeau, which composers of 1811 mostly avoided as thick and unclear, and which here carries the concerto's private voice.","Baermann carried both concertos across Europe and made them the backbone of clarinet repertory, where they have stayed. Nineteenth-century editions carry so many of his own alterations that players still must choose between Weber's text and the tradition of the man it was written for. Performances on period clarinets, with fewer keys and a woodier sound, have revived arguments about tempo and about how much freedom the singing lines want.","Go to the slow movement first and wait for the three horns; the passage lasts under a minute and explains the whole concerto. Then take the finale for the athletics. Compare a modern instrument with a period clarinet to hear how much of the colour comes from the hardware, and follow it with Mozart's concerto to hear exactly what Weber added."],"weber-konzertstuck-f-minor":["A single seventeen-minute span for piano and orchestra in four linked sections that run without a break: a brooding slow opening, an agitated fast movement, a distant march that grows from almost nothing into a blaze, and a headlong final Presto in the major. There is no double exposition and no classical cadenza. The piano plays almost throughout, sometimes accompanying and sometimes leading, and the joins between sections are dramatic events rather than pauses.","Weber finished it on the morning of 18 June 1821, the day Der Freischütz opened in Berlin, and played it in public a week later. He told his wife Caroline the story behind it: a woman waiting on a castle tower for a husband gone to the crusades, her fear that he is dead, the distant sound of his returning troop, reunion. Printing a programme with a score was still unusual, and he never published this one.","The march is the mechanism. It begins at the edge of audibility and grows louder in steady stages, so the listener hears an army approaching in real time — an effect built out of dynamics and orchestration rather than harmony. Liszt played the piece constantly and took the whole design from it: one movement made of contrasted linked sections, with material transformed rather than repeated. The Romantic concerto and the symphonic poem both start here.","It was popular through the nineteenth century, then dropped away as larger concertos crowded the programmes, and it now returns mostly as a short second item on a concert. Scholars point to it as the missing link between Beethoven's Emperor concerto and Liszt's. Weber's own programme has been treated both as the key to the piece and as a story attached afterwards to music that stands perfectly well without it.","Listen for the four sections without watching a clock and try to hear where each begins — the joins are the point. Wait for the march to appear out of silence; the crescendo is longer than it feels. Then play Liszt's First Concerto straight afterwards and the debt is obvious. Choose a recording that dares to begin the march genuinely near inaudibility."],"weber-invitation-to-the-dance":["Nine minutes for solo piano, and the first waltz written to be listened to rather than danced. It opens with a conversation in the low register — a phrase, a lighter answer, both repeated with growing warmth — then breaks into a chain of waltzes, fast, slow and brilliant, before the dancing stops and the opening dialogue returns as a farewell. The frame is the innovation: an introduction and an epilogue wrapped around music that would otherwise be purely functional.","Weber wrote it at Hosterwitz in the summer of 1819 and dedicated it to his wife, Caroline Brandt, a singer he had married the year before. Vienna's waltz craze was at its height and the dance was still faintly scandalous, a couple turning in a close hold. Putting one on a concert programme, with a little drama attached, claimed the ballroom for the recital room — the same move Schubert, Chopin and Liszt would each make in turn.","Weber wrote a running commentary into his wife's copy, naming each phrase: the invitation, her evasion, his insistence, her acceptance, the walk to the floor. Knowing it changes the piece, because the descending phrases at the start and at the end are the same music with opposite meanings. Listen to the low register at both ends — the only place where the piano sounds like a speaking voice rather than a small orchestra.","Berlioz orchestrated it in 1841 to serve as the ballet Paris demanded inside Der Freischütz, and that version, brilliant and a little glossy, is now heard more often than the original. Diaghilev's company used the same orchestration for Le Spectre de la rose in 1911, with Nijinsky leaping out of a window, so the piece is now as much ballet score as piano work. Pianists complain that the orchestral glamour has flattened its intimacy.","Play the piano original before the orchestral version. Listen only to the first minute and the last minute — the same phrases, one an approach and one a goodbye — and the shape falls into place. Then hear Berlioz's orchestration to understand what nineteenth-century Paris wanted from a dance, and compare a Chopin waltz for the private version of the same idea."],"marschner-der-vampyr":["Two acts of German Singspiel about Lord Ruthven, a vampire granted one more year of existence if he can destroy three brides before midnight. The score's centre of gravity is its baritone villain, who gets the best music: a scene of self-loathing exultation over churning low strings before the first murder. Around him sit sweet-toned lovers, a drinking chorus, a storm, and a spectral opening in which spirits gather on a Scottish moor.","Marschner was thirty-two and building a career in Leipzig when he took up a subject that had been circulating for a decade. Polidori's story The Vampyre, written during the Geneva summer that also produced Frankenstein and long misattributed to Byron, had reached the German stage by way of French melodrama. Wilhelm August Wohlbrück, Marschner's brother-in-law, supplied the libretto, and the premiere came at the Leipzig Stadttheater on 29 March 1828.","Ruthven is the missing step between the villains of Der Freischütz and Wagner's Dutchman: damned, magnetic, and singing about his own condition in a way that asks for sympathy. Marschner gives his appearances a fixed sound — low clarinets, tremolo violas, a rasping trombone — and then reuses it wherever his influence reaches. Wagner knew the opera well enough to compose a new allegro ending for one of its tenor arias in 1833.","It succeeded immediately across Germany and reached London the same year in an English adaptation with a rewritten plot. Then Wagner's own supernatural operas absorbed its audience and it vanished, dismissed as a curiosity standing in the shadow of Der Freischütz. Revivals since the 1980s, helped by a British television version that transplanted the story into modern politics, treat it as more than a footnote, though it is still rarely staged whole.","Go straight to Ruthven's Act I scene and listen for the moment the vocal line stops being villainous and turns almost lyrical — that ambivalence is what Wagner took. Then hear the opening spirit chorus for the Wolf's Glen inheritance. If you know Der fliegende Holländer, put the Dutchman's first monologue beside Ruthven's; the resemblance is startling."],"marschner-hans-heiling":["A prologue and three acts. Hans Heiling, king of the earth spirits, gives up his kingdom and his powers to live among humans and marry Anna, who comes to fear what he is. The prologue is the striking stroke: it happens before the overture, underground, with the spirits and Heiling's mother pleading with him not to go, so the opera has already begun when the curtain-raiser finally arrives. Heiling is a bass-baritone whose great scenes are monologues of exclusion.","Eduard Devrient wrote the libretto, offered it first to Mendelssohn, who declined it, and then sang the title role himself at the Berlin premiere on 24 May 1833 with Marschner conducting. The subject came from Bohemian legend attached to rock formations near Karlsbad. Marschner had recently become court Kapellmeister in Hannover, a post he held for nearly thirty years, and this was the height of his reputation in the German theatres.","The plot is Wagner's before Wagner: a supernatural being who wants a mortal woman's love, a woman who cannot bear what he is, and a renunciation at the close. Listen for how the prologue's music returns at the end, framing the whole opera as a journey back underground. Marschner also gives the spirit world a fixed orchestral colour, so you can hear Heiling's other nature seeping into human scenes before anyone on stage notices.","It held the German stage until the First World War and then largely disappeared outside German-speaking countries, where it still surfaces. Critics divide the same way they always have: the dramatic monologues are admired, the conventional Singspiel numbers around them are not, and the reputation now rests heavily on the opera being an ancestor rather than on being itself. Recordings are few, which keeps the argument mostly theoretical.","Start with the prologue, before any overture, and notice that you are already inside the story. Then find Heiling's Act I monologue and listen to a bass-baritone being asked to carry an opera's sympathy. Play the Dutchman's first monologue next: the vocal type, the harmonic language and the predicament are nearly identical, ten years apart."],"lortzing-zar-und-zimmermann":["Three acts of comic Singspiel set in a Dutch shipyard, where Tsar Peter the Great is working incognito as a carpenter and a self-important burgomaster, Van Bett, mistakes another Peter for him. Van Bett is why the piece survives: a buffo bass who introduces himself with mock-legal Latin and later drills a chorus of townspeople through a rehearsal that collapses. The Tsar gets one genuinely moving aria, and there is a clog dance in the finale.","Lortzing wrote his own libretto, as he always did, adapting a French comedy, and composed the piece while working as an actor and singer at the Leipzig Stadttheater — he sang Peter Ivanov himself at the premiere on 22 December 1837. German theatres of the 1830s ran on exactly this repertory: middle-class comedy with spoken dialogue, tunes an audience could take home, and no aristocratic patron. He wrote a dozen such operas and died broke in Berlin.","The joke of Van Bett's mock-Latin entrance is that a man with no Latin and no real authority is trying to sound like a magistrate, and Lortzing has the orchestra keep tripping him — accompaniment figures that mock rather than support the singer. Listen also to the Tsar's Act III aria, which is not comic at all: a ruler admitting he was happier before the crown, scored with a restraint the rest of the opera never asks for.","It is among the most performed operas in Germany and almost unknown elsewhere, a gap that says more about language and spoken dialogue than about the score. Lortzing has long been patronised as a purveyor of homely tunes, and postwar German criticism was uneasy about how comfortably his work fitted mid-century nostalgia. Recent stagings push back against the cosiness by taking the politics of disguised power seriously.","Try Van Bett's entrance aria first; if the bass is enjoying himself, the whole opera works. Then hear the Tsar's Act III aria for the other register, and the clog dance for the tune every German school choir knows. Look for a recording with German-speaking singers who relish the dialogue, since the spoken scenes carry as much comedy as the music does."],"lortzing-der-wildschutz":["Three acts of comedy built on mistaken identity: a village schoolmaster believes he has shot the count's deer, and a chain of disguises follows through a country house where nobody is who they claim to be. The best-known number is a billiard quintet, in which five people play a game and negotiate a bribe at the same time while the music keeps the cue strokes going underneath. Lortzing writes ensembles rather than showpiece arias; the plot moves in music.","Lortzing adapted a Kotzebue comedy himself and had it staged in Leipzig on 31 December 1842. Germany then had no single opera capital, only dozens of court and municipal theatres needing new work in the vernacular, and he wrote directly for that market as an insider — actor, tenor, librettist and composer at once. He never held a secure post for long, and the royalty arrangements of the time meant that a piece played everywhere earned him almost nothing.","The quintet is a small structural marvel. Lortzing keeps a pattern of short repeated notes running through the whole number as the rhythm of the game, and lets five contradictory intentions ride on top of it without the pulse ever breaking. Elsewhere he parodies the schoolmaster's amateur cantata rehearsal, which is a joke at the expense of exactly the choral societies who filled the theatre for the premiere.","German critics have generally called this his best score, more finely worked than Zar und Zimmermann, and it remains repertory in German houses while being almost never staged elsewhere. The persistent argument is whether Lortzing is a real comic dramatist in the line from Mozart or a skilled provider of theatre music with a good ear. Recent scholarship, noticing the sharpness of the class satire, has been kinder than the nineteenth century was.","The billiard quintet is the way in — listen for the ticking figure that never stops while five people argue over money. Then take the schoolmaster's first scene for the comic bass writing, and the Countess's music for the parody of grand-opera manners. An idiomatic German ensemble matters more here than a famous conductor on the box."],"nicolai-die-lustigen-weiber-von-windsor":["Shakespeare's Merry Wives in three acts of German Singspiel, with Falstaff tipped into the Thames, a moonlit forest finale and two women running the entire plot. The overture is the piece everyone knows: a long hushed introduction with divided strings and a slowly rising line, as though a wood were waking, then a scampering Allegro that keeps threatening to break into song. The score is light and fast, full of ensembles, and the fairy scene is genuinely magical.","Nicolai spent his twenties in Rome and his thirties in Vienna, where he founded the orchestra that became the Vienna Philharmonic, then moved to Berlin as court Kapellmeister. Salomon Hermann Mosenthal's libretto trimmed Shakespeare hard, keeping the wives and cutting the sub-plots. The premiere took place at the Berlin opera house on 9 March 1849, in a city still unsettled by the previous year's revolution, with Nicolai conducting. He died of a stroke two months later, aged thirty-eight.","The overture's slow opening is scene-painting placed before the curtain: the same music returns for the Windsor Forest finale, so the audience hears the ending first without knowing it. Listen for how Nicolai keeps Falstaff out of the best tunes — the wives get the arias and duets, while the fat knight is characterised by a lumbering figure in the low winds that never quite becomes a melody of its own.","It has been a German repertory staple ever since, while English-speaking countries prefer Verdi's Falstaff of 1893 and Vaughan Williams's Sir John in Love. The comparison has done Nicolai no favours: Verdi's is the greater score, and the standard verdict makes this the charming lesser version. Defenders argue that it belongs to a different genre — a domestic comedy with dialogue, not a through-composed music drama — and should be judged as one.","Start with the overture, which is far more familiar than the opera, and wait for the transition out of the slow introduction into the Allegro. Then find Frau Fluth's Act I aria for the coloratura comedy, and the forest finale for the Mendelssohnian fairy writing. Hearing it beside Verdi's Falstaff is instructive, so long as you are not scoring the match."],"cornelius-der-barbier-von-bagdad":["A comic opera in two acts, through-composed and under two hours: a young man in love, a garrulous barber who will not stop talking, a chest, a caliph and a chain of misunderstandings. The barber is a deep bass whose great number is a catalogue of his brothers' virtues, delivered with mock solemnity over an orchestra that refuses to hurry him. Cornelius writes fine ensembles and a muezzin's call at dawn, and keeps the scoring light enough for every word to land.","Cornelius wrote his own libretto — he was a poet before he was a composer — while living in Weimar inside Liszt's circle. Liszt conducted the premiere on 15 December 1858 at the court theatre, and the evening turned into a demonstration against him by the anti-Weimar faction in the house. He resigned his post there soon afterwards and the opera was withdrawn. Cornelius later moved to Vienna and then Munich, drawn into Wagner's orbit without ever adopting his manner.","The comedy is mostly rhythmic. Cornelius sets the barber's endless talk as patter that keeps colliding with a slow, dignified accompaniment, so the joke is a mismatch of tempo rather than of words. Listen for the recurring greeting figure he passes around the cast until it becomes a running gag in sound. The Baghdad setting is suggested by scale and instrumental colour rather than by pastiche of anything actually Middle Eastern.","Felix Mottl reorchestrated and expanded the opera in 1884, adding Wagnerian weight and a new overture, and that version carried the piece for decades as the only one heard. Twentieth-century scholarship restored Cornelius's own lighter scoring, which most modern performances use. The two versions embody the argument about him: whether he was a miniaturist swamped by the New German School, or a composer working deliberately at his own scale.","Start with the barber's catalogue aria, which decides whether the whole piece charms you, then the dawn muezzin scene for Cornelius's ear for atmosphere. If you can find both the Mottl and the original scoring, compare five minutes of each; the difference in weight is the story of the opera's reception. His Christmas songs make a good companion piece."],"humperdinck-hansel-und-gretel":["Three acts, under two hours, on the Grimm story: two hungry children, a broom-maker's cottage, a forest, a gingerbread house and a witch baked in her own oven. Humperdinck sets folk-style tunes — a counting song, a dance duet — inside a full late-Romantic orchestra with Wagnerian harmony and continuous texture. The two set pieces are the Evening Prayer, in which fourteen angels are named as the children fall asleep, and the orchestral Dream Pantomime that follows it.","It began as four songs written for a family puppet play: Humperdinck's sister, Adelheid Wette, wanted music for verses she had made for her children, and over two years the project grew into an opera. Humperdinck had spent 1880 and 1881 at Bayreuth as Wagner's assistant, copying and preparing Parsifal, and the fluency of that language is everywhere in the score. Richard Strauss, then twenty-nine, conducted the premiere in Weimar on 23 December 1893.","The trick of the piece is that its folk tunes are almost all Humperdinck's own inventions, written to sound inherited. Listen to how the Evening Prayer melody is announced by horns in the prelude before you know what it is, then returns transformed through the Dream Pantomime as the angels descend — full Wagnerian motivic technique applied to a nursery song. The Witch's Ride uses the same technique for chromatic mayhem instead.","It succeeded immediately, spread across Europe within two years and became the German Christmas opera. The Metropolitan Opera's broadcast of it on Christmas Day 1931 was the first opera relayed live from that house. Critics have never quite settled whether the Wagnerian apparatus dignifies the fairy tale or overwhelms it, and the Witch — written for a mezzo, often sung by a tenor in drag — remains a staging problem that productions solve very differently.","Begin with the prelude and its horn chorale, then jump to the end of Act II for the Evening Prayer and the Dream Pantomime, fifteen minutes that work as a concert piece on their own. Listen for how rarely the orchestra actually rests. Then compare the Witch's Ride with Wagner's Ride of the Valkyries to hear what a pupil does with a teacher's machinery."],"wagner-rienzi":["Five acts of grand opera in the Parisian manner: crowds, processions, a ballet, a battle and a heroic tenor who barely leaves the stage. Cola di Rienzo raises the Roman people against the nobles, is made tribune, is abandoned by them and burns with the Capitol. The overture, still played, lays out the plan: a single sustained trumpet note, then Rienzi's prayer sung by the strings, then a march that keeps getting louder. Uncut, the opera runs close to five hours.","Wagner began it in Riga and finished it in Paris between 1838 and 1840, in the poverty of his failed assault on the French capital, deliberately imitating Spontini and Meyerbeer to produce something the Opéra might stage. Paris refused it. Dresden accepted it, and the premiere on 20 October 1842 was the greatest success of Wagner's life to that point, winning him the post of Royal Saxon Kapellmeister a few months later.","Everything Wagner later argued against is here on purpose: separate closed numbers, a ballet where the plot simply stops, choral spectacle for its own sake. He excluded the work from the Bayreuth canon and would not allow it there. The manuscript full score has its own dark afterlife — presented to Hitler, who prized the opera, and lost at the end of the war, so the autograph of Wagner's first success no longer exists.","Nineteenth-century houses cut it hard, sometimes splitting it across two evenings. Wagner's own dismissal has largely stuck, and the standard verdict treats Rienzi as the work of a gifted imitator, worth hearing for the overture and the Act V prayer. Revivals since the 1970s and a full critical edition have made the case that the score is better than its reputation, but it remains rare outside Germany.","The overture is the way in: listen for the quiet trumpet call at the start and then for the same march returning, transformed, at the end. If you go further, find Rienzi's Act V prayer, which a great heroic tenor can make overwhelming. Then hear Der fliegende Holländer, written immediately afterwards, and the change of direction is unmistakable."],"wagner-der-fliegende-hollander":["Wagner's shortest mature opera, often given without an interval in a single span of about two and a quarter hours. A Norwegian captain's daughter, Senta, is obsessed with the portrait of a Dutchman condemned to sail until a woman is faithful to death; he lands, they meet, and the bargain destroys them both. The sound is sea and storm: bare open fifths on horns and bassoons, howling strings, a male chorus of sailors, and a ballad that stops the second act dead.","Wagner fled Riga in 1839 with creditors behind him and took a small ship towards London; a storm drove it into the Norwegian fjords, and the sailors' shouts echoing off rock walls stayed with him. He wrote the opera in Paris in poverty, selling the scenario to the Paris Opéra for a few hundred francs when he could not obtain the commission himself. Dresden staged it on 2 January 1843, ten weeks after Rienzi's triumph, to a markedly cooler reception.","Senta's Ballad, in the middle of Act II, was the first music Wagner composed for the opera, and the whole score grows out of it — the Dutchman's rising call and the redemption melody are both already inside it. He said as much himself, describing the ballad as the thematic seed. Listen also to the closing bars: Wagner rewrote them in 1860 with a soft glowing coda from the harmonic world of Tristan, and most performances now use that ending.","Dresden found it dark and short of spectacle after Rienzi, and it ran only four times. Its standing rose with Wagner's, and the argument now is over versions: the original score or the 1860 revision, three separate acts or one continuous span, the early orchestration or his later thickening of it. Productions divide over whether Senta's obsession is redemptive or pathological, and recent stagings usually take the second view.","Start with the overture, then Senta's Ballad, and listen for the ballad's phrases reappearing everywhere else. Notice that the Dutchman's music sits almost always in the low register and the Norwegian sailors' almost always bright and rhythmic — two worlds that never blend. Look for a performance given in one unbroken act; the piece gains enormously from never stopping."],"wagner-tannhauser":["Three acts, about three hours. A minstrel knight has been living inside the Venusberg with the goddess of love; he returns to the world, joins a song contest at the Wartburg, blurts out what he has known, is sent to Rome for absolution and is refused. The music divides into two sound worlds, the chromatic swirling Venusberg and the diatonic pilgrims and knights. The overture states both, and the Pilgrims' Chorus returns at the end to settle the argument.","Wagner wrote it as Dresden's Kapellmeister, pulling two separate medieval legends together into one plot. In 1861 the Paris Opéra staged it at the emperor's request, and Wagner rewrote the Venusberg scene in the harmonic language he had just invented for Tristan — but refused to put the compulsory ballet in the second act, where the Jockey Club members arrived after dinner to watch it. They whistled the performances down and the production was withdrawn after three nights.","The Paris rewrite left a seam that no version smooths over. The Bacchanal is post-Tristan music, harmonically decades ahead of the 1845 score around it, so the Venusberg literally sounds like a later world than the Wartburg — an accident that turns out to serve the drama. Listen for the pilgrims' melody arriving three times, at the opening, in Act III and in the final bars, each time differently scored and differently meant.","Dresden was confused by it in 1845, Paris rioted in 1861, Vienna received a third version in 1875, and Wagner said near the end of his life that he still owed the world a Tannhäuser. Modern houses choose between the Dresden and Paris scores or blend them, and every choice draws criticism. The opera's moral scheme, in which a woman dies to redeem a man, has become the main subject of contemporary productions.","The overture tells the whole story; hear it first as a concert piece. Then take Wolfram's Act III song to the evening star, quiet and almost strophic, and Elisabeth's Act II entrance for the opposite kind of blaze. If you want to hear the seam, put the Paris Bacchanal directly beside the Act II march and listen for the sixteen-year gap between them."],"wagner-lohengrin":["Three acts, about three and a half hours. A knight arrives in a boat drawn by a swan to defend Elsa of Brabant, on one condition: she must never ask his name or where he comes from. She asks. The prelude is the signature sound — violins divided into eight parts, entering at the very top of their range, building to a full-orchestra blaze and receding to nothing across eight minutes. Ortrud and Telramund, the pagan schemers, supply the darkness.","Wagner finished the score in Dresden in 1848 and then took part in the May 1849 uprising, after which a warrant was issued for his arrest and he escaped to Switzerland. Liszt conducted the premiere in Weimar on 28 August 1850, on Goethe's birthday, while the composer was in exile and unable to attend. Wagner did not hear his own opera until 1861, in Vienna, eleven years after it had made him famous across Germany.","The whole opera turns on a distinction of register. The Grail world sits high — divided violins, flutes, harmonics — and Ortrud's world sits low, in dark brass and bass clarinet, so you can hear which force is winning at any moment without following a word. Listen also to the wedding scene: the famous chorus is not a triumph but precisely the point at which the question that destroys everything becomes unavoidable.","This was the opera that made Wagner's name while he was banned from the country, and the last he wrote before the theoretical essays that produced the Ring. Nietzsche loved it and later used it against him. Nineteenth-century audiences turned the Bridal Chorus into a wedding standard, to the lasting irritation of Wagnerians, since in context the marriage collapses within minutes. Ludwig II built Neuschwanstein around its imagery.","Listen to the prelude by itself, twice, in a recording with a large string section — the effect depends entirely on how quietly eight violin parts can enter. Then Ortrud and Telramund's Act II scene in near darkness, and the Grail Narration in Act III. Compare the prelude with the opening of Das Rheingold to hear Wagner build a world out of a single chord."],"wagner-das-rheingold":["The Ring's prologue: one act in four scenes, two and a half hours without an interval. It opens on a single low chord held for more than four minutes while the Rhine gathers out of it. A dwarf renounces love to steal the river's gold and forge a ring of absolute power; a god steals it from him and pays it out to giants as a debt. Eighteen tuned anvils hammer the descent into Nibelheim, and the gods cross a rainbow bridge into a fortress they cannot afford.","Wagner composed it in Zurich in 1853 and 1854, in exile, having written the poem of the whole cycle backwards from Siegfried's death. It then waited fifteen years for a stage. Ludwig II of Bavaria, who had rescued Wagner financially, ordered the premiere in Munich on 22 September 1869 over the composer's furious objection that no part of the Ring should be given before all of it existed. Wagner boycotted the performance.","The famous opening holds one chord for 136 bars, and the point is that it contains no melody until the Rhinemaidens sing — the cycle begins in a state of nature, before music has taken shape. Wagner had the Wagner tuba built for this score, a horn-and-tuba hybrid giving the gods their own colour, and it enters with the Valhalla music. Listen for how Alberich's renunciation and the ring's own motif are nearly the same figure turned inside out.","The Munich premiere was a shambles: the machinery failed at the dress rehearsal, the conductor and the Rhinemaidens resigned, and Wagner tried to have it stopped. Its proper first performance came at Bayreuth in August 1876. Critics have argued ever since over whether the cycle's politics are Feuerbach's, Bakunin's or Schopenhauer's, and George Bernard Shaw's reading of it as an anti-capitalist parable remains the most influential single interpretation.","Listen to the first ten minutes alone: the low sustained note, the rising figure, the Rhinemaidens. Then Alberich's renunciation, then the anvils, then the entry into Valhalla. Rheingold is the best way into the Ring because it is short, continuous and almost entirely narrative. Georg Solti's studio recording makes the machinery audible; a Bayreuth performance gives you the theatre."],"wagner-die-walkure":["Three acts and roughly four hours, the most self-contained and most performed part of the Ring. Act I: two strangers sheltering from a storm in a hut discover that they are twins, and fall in love. Act II: a god argues himself into destroying his own son, across a monologue lasting nearly half an hour. Act III: eight warrior women ride, and a father puts his daughter to sleep inside a wall of fire. The famous orchestral music sits at both ends; the centre is talk.","Wagner composed it between 1854 and 1856, at the point in his exile when he read Schopenhauer and the Ring's philosophy shifted from revolution towards renunciation. Ludwig II again forced an early premiere, in Munich on 26 June 1870, once more against Wagner's wishes and again before the cycle was finished. Wagner stayed away. Bayreuth's first proper performance followed six years later, inside the theatre he had built for the purpose.","Wotan's Act II monologue is the structural key to the entire cycle: he narrates the events of Rheingold and predicts what is to come, while the orchestra supplies motifs from music the audience has already heard and music it has not heard yet. Careless performances cut it and always damage the tetralogy by doing so. Listen also for the sword motif, a bare trumpet call, arriving before anyone on stage knows there is a sword.","The Ride of the Valkyries left the opera house almost at once and has had a strange second life in film, most famously in Apocalypse Now, which has made it hard to hear in context. Die Walküre is often staged alone, which purists resist and box offices favour. Debate continues over how the incest of Act I should be presented, and over whether Wotan is a tragic figure or a politician trapped inside his own contracts.","Act I works as a self-contained ninety-minute drama for three singers; start there and listen for the door blowing open on the spring moonlight. Then Act III's opening for the Ride, and the last twenty minutes for Wotan's farewell and the fire music. Compare a Brünnhilde like Birgit Nilsson with a lighter modern voice to hear what the part can be made to do."],"wagner-siegfried":["Three acts, about four hours, and the strangest opera in the Ring: for most of two acts there are almost no women on stage and no love plot at all. A boy raised by a dwarf forges a sword, kills a dragon, tastes its blood, understands birdsong, shatters a god's spear and walks through fire to wake a sleeping woman. The forging scene is a hammering, bellows-driven set piece; the Forest Murmurs are the cycle's quietest and most detailed nature painting.","Wagner broke off composition in 1857, in the middle of Act II, and did not return to it for twelve years. In between he wrote Tristan und Isolde and Die Meistersinger, founded a new harmonic language, married Cosima, acquired a king as patron and built a theatre. He completed the score in 1871. The premiere came at Bayreuth on 16 August 1876, inside the first complete cycle, conducted by Hans Richter.","You can hear the twelve-year gap. The music resumes partway through Act II, and from there the harmony thickens, the orchestral texture grows denser and the chromatic language of Tristan floods in — by Act III the score sounds like a different opera from Act I, which in effect it is. Listen for the moment the Wanderer's music turns weary in Act III: Wotan is being written out of his own cycle by a composer who has changed his mind.","The heroic tenor part is the hardest in the repertory — five hours of singing, mostly loud, mostly high, against an enormous orchestra — and casting it has limited performances for a century and a half. Siegfried himself has become the cycle's problem: a hero designed as free nature, read after 1945 as a brutal innocent, and staged with increasing scepticism. Productions since the 1970s often present him as a damaged child.","Start with the Forest Murmurs in Act II, which works as a concert piece and shows Wagner's ear for quiet orchestral detail. Then the forging songs in Act I for the noise, and the last twenty minutes of Act III for the awakening. If four hours is daunting, listen to Act I and Act III one after the other and hear the harmonic gap open between them."],"wagner-gotterdammerung":["A prologue and three acts, four and a half hours, and the Ring's ending. Three Norns spin the rope of fate until it breaks; Siegfried is drugged into forgetting Brünnhilde, betrays her and is murdered by Hagen; she rides into his funeral pyre, and the Rhine reclaims the ring as Valhalla burns. It is the only part of the cycle with a chorus, and the only one that feels like grand opera again — oaths, a wedding, a conspiracy trio in the old manner.","Wagner wrote its text first, in 1848, as Siegfrieds Tod, then worked backwards through three more librettos in order to explain it. The music was finished at Wahnfried in November 1874, twenty-six years later. The first complete Ring ran at Bayreuth from 13 to 17 August 1876 with Richter conducting and the Kaiser in the audience, and left the new festival with a deficit that took years to clear.","Because the poem came first, the score uses motifs as memory rather than as labels — the Funeral Music is a compressed biography of Siegfried and his parents told entirely in earlier themes, with no words sung at all. Listen also to the closing minutes: the orchestra ends on a melody heard only once before, in Die Walküre, sung by Sieglinde. Wagner never named it, and what it means is still argued over.","The premiere cycle was underfunded and technically rough, and Wagner himself declared the staging a failure. The 1976 centenary Ring, directed by Patrice Chéreau in nineteenth-century industrial dress, was booed and then became the model for nearly every production since. Scholars still dispute the ending: whether the fire destroys the gods' order or renews the world, and whether Brünnhilde's final act is submission or judgment.","The three orchestral spans — Dawn and Siegfried's Rhine Journey, the Funeral Music and the Immolation — make a concert programme of their own and are the best way in. Then hear Hagen's Watch, the darkest bass music Wagner wrote. Chéreau's filmed Bayreuth production remains the standard first encounter with the staged cycle."],"wagner-tristan-und-isolde":["Three acts, roughly four hours, in which very little happens: a sea crossing, a night of talk, a dying man waiting. Act II is a love duet lasting most of an hour, interrupted once; Act III is a wounded man's delirium over a shepherd's tune played on the cor anglais. The opera opens with a rising cello line and a chord that refuses to resolve, and the whole score is built on that withholding — a four-hour delay whose resolution arrives only in the final bars.","Wagner broke off the Ring in 1857 to write it, living in a cottage on the Zurich estate of his patron Otto Wesendonck and in love with Wesendonck's wife. He intended something short and performable that would earn money. Vienna took it into rehearsal in 1862 and abandoned it after seventy-seven attempts as unsingable. Ludwig II paid for the premiere in Munich on 10 June 1865 under Hans von Bülow, whose wife Cosima was by then carrying Wagner's child.","The opening chord — four notes that could belong to several keys and settle in none — has been analysed more than any other chord in music, and its function is dramatic rather than technical: desire that cannot be satisfied, written into harmony. Listen for the cor anglais tune in Act III, played offstage, returning three times with different meanings. And notice that the Liebestod's melody is the love duet's, recomposed to reach the resolution the duet was denied.","Ludwig Schnorr von Carolsfeld, the first Tristan, died five weeks after the premiere at twenty-nine, and a legend of the opera as lethal attached itself immediately. Nietzsche called it the great intoxication and then spent years trying to escape it; Debussy mocked it while quoting it. Every account of modernism's harmonic breakdown starts here, and productions now argue mainly over whether this is a love story or a study of self-annihilation.","The Prelude and Liebestod are usually played together as a concert pair — start there, and note that they are the beginning and the end with four hours removed from between them. Then hear Act II from the great nocturnal duet, and the opening of Act III. Look for a recording where the orchestra never covers the singers; Kirsten Flagstad under Wilhelm Furtwängler remains the reference."],"wagner-die-meistersinger-von-nurnberg":["Wagner's only mature comedy and his longest opera: three acts, four and a half hours, in bright diatonic C major. A young knight wants to marry a goldsmith's daughter and must win a song contest judged by a guild of tradesmen. The score is contrapuntal rather than chromatic — the overture ends by stacking three separate themes on top of one another — and the cobbler-poet Hans Sachs, a bass-baritone with two long monologues, is Wagner's warmest character.","Wagner sketched the subject in 1845 as a comic pendant to Tannhäuser and wrote it twenty years later, mostly in Switzerland, while Ludwig II paid the bills and Cosima von Bülow left her husband for him. The premiere took place in Munich on 21 June 1868 under Hans von Bülow, with the king in the royal box and Wagner seated beside him, an unheard-of breach of court protocol that the Munich press noticed at once.","Beckmesser, the pedantic town clerk humiliated in public, was called Hans Lick in an early draft — a jab at the critic Eduard Hanslick, who was present when Wagner read the libretto aloud and left early. Whether the character also carries antisemitic caricature has been argued fiercely since the 1990s. Listen too for the Act III quintet, which stops the comedy dead for five minutes of complete stillness.","The opera's celebration of German art made it the Third Reich's favourite Wagner, opened the cultural programme of the Nuremberg rallies, and was the only opera staged at Bayreuth in the festival's final wartime seasons — a history that Sachs's closing warning against foreign influence makes impossible to ignore. Postwar German productions confront it directly, most famously Katharina Wagner's in 2007. Its musical standing has never been in doubt.","The overture is the best five-minute demonstration of counterpoint in the repertory: listen for three themes arriving separately and then combining at the end. Then the Act III quintet and Sachs's cobbling song. If the length daunts you, Act III alone runs about two hours and contains most of the great music, including the contest itself."],"wagner-parsifal":["Three acts, over four hours, called by Wagner not an opera but a festival play for the consecration of a stage. A wounded king cannot die, a sorcerer holds the sacred spear, and a young man who understands nothing arrives, learns pity and heals him. The pace is slower than anything else Wagner wrote: Act I contains a scene change during which the orchestra plays for several minutes while the scenery moves. Four low bell notes toll through the whole work.","Wagner wrote it last, at Bayreuth and in Palermo, for the theatre he had built, with the sunken pit and covered acoustic he had designed for the Ring. The premiere on 26 July 1882 was conducted by Hermann Levi, a rabbi's son, whom Wagner tried and failed to have baptised first. Wagner took over the baton for the final scene of the last performance that summer, and died in Venice six months later.","Wagner meant the work never to leave Bayreuth, and the copyright held until 1913. The Metropolitan Opera staged it anyway in 1903 from published materials, in what German newspapers called a theft, and Cosima's attempts to stop it failed. Listen for the four bell notes, which needed a purpose-built instrument because no church bell would give them at the pitch he wanted, and for the Grail motif, which is the old Dresden Amen.","Nietzsche took the work as Wagner's capitulation to Christianity and broke with him over it; Debussy called the orchestration among the most beautiful ever written while detesting the plot. Its language of blood and purity has been read as racial doctrine, an argument that has intensified rather than settled. Traditional houses still discourage applause after Act I, a convention that seems to have begun at Bayreuth by accident.","Start with the Prelude and the Good Friday Music from Act III, both often played in concert. Then take the Act I transformation scene to feel the tempo of the whole work. Parsifal rewards an uninterrupted first hearing more than any other Wagner opera; Hans Knappertsbusch's Bayreuth recordings are famously slow and famously right."],"wagner-wesendonck-lieder":["Five songs on poems by Mathilde Wesendonck, about twenty minutes, for a woman's voice and piano: \"Der Engel\", \"Stehe still!\", \"Im Treibhaus\", \"Schmerzen\" and \"Träume\". The manner is Wagnerian rather than song-like — long unbroken vocal lines over a piano part that behaves like an orchestra, harmony that slides instead of cadencing, almost no strophic repetition. \"Im Treibhaus\", the hothouse, is the strangest of them: heavy, humid, barely moving for four minutes.","Wagner was living in a cottage on the Zurich estate of the silk merchant Otto Wesendonck, who was subsidising him, and was in love with Wesendonck's wife, who wrote poems in his own manner and gave them to him. He set five of them over the winter of 1857 and 1858, while beginning Tristan und Isolde. The situation broke that summer, when Wagner's wife Minna intercepted a letter and he left for Venice alone.","Wagner labelled two of the songs studies for Tristan und Isolde, and that is exactly what they are. The music of \"Träume\" becomes the Act II love duet, and \"Im Treibhaus\" becomes the prelude to Act III, so an opera is hiding in draft inside a private album. He orchestrated \"Träume\" alone for a small ensemble and had it played under Mathilde's window on her birthday; Felix Mottl orchestrated the other four after Wagner's death.","These are almost the only songs Wagner wrote as an adult, and the standard view treats them as opera by other means rather than as lieder — a criticism when they are set beside Schubert and Wolf, a virtue when they are set beside nothing. Singers argue over versions: the piano original is more intimate and harder to sustain, the Mottl orchestration lusher and slightly generic. Recent performances increasingly choose the piano.","Take \"Träume\" first, then find the Act II love duet from Tristan and hear the same music grown enormous. Then \"Im Treibhaus\" for the strangest four minutes of the set. A great Wagnerian soprano gives it weight, a lieder singer gives it words; try an orchestral recording against a recital version with piano and decide which you prefer."],"richard-strauss-salome":["One act, about a hundred minutes, no interval. Herod's stepdaughter demands the head of the imprisoned prophet Jochanaan, dances for it, receives it, and sings a fifteen-minute love scene to the severed head before the soldiers crush her. Strauss uses an orchestra of over a hundred, and the writing runs from oily chromatic slither to blazing tonal chords. The end is famous for one grinding dissonance arriving immediately after the most rapturous music in the score.","Strauss saw Wilde's play in Max Reinhardt's Berlin production and set Hedwig Lachmann's German translation directly, cutting the text himself rather than commissioning a libretto — one of the first operas made straight from a spoken play. Wilde had died in disgrace five years earlier, and the subject was scandalous in every direction at once, biblical and sexual together. Ernst von Schuch conducted the premiere in Dresden on 9 December 1905.","Listen to the very last chord. Strauss builds an ecstatic passage as Salome kisses the mouth of the head, then drops a mass of unrelated notes on top of it — the moment the opera's sensuality is refused. Earlier, Jochanaan is given plain, almost churchy diatonic music inside a chromatic world, so the prophet sounds not holy but stiff and out of place, which is exactly the point being made.","The Vienna censor blocked it despite Mahler's advocacy, and the city did not see it until 1918. The Metropolitan Opera gave it once in January 1907 and withdrew it after protests from the board. Strauss said the royalties built his villa at Garmisch. The Dance of the Seven Veils is now the awkward part: written as a striptease, it is choreographed today as anything from trauma flashback to refusal, and it is the weakest music in the score.","Listen to the last twenty minutes first, from the dance onward, and stay for the final chord — the whole opera is engineered to arrive there. Then go back for Jochanaan's music, sung from inside the cistern below the stage. The title role needs a soprano with the weight of Isolde and the agility of a teenager; recordings by Birgit Nilsson and Ljuba Welitsch mark the two poles."],"richard-strauss-elektra":["One act, under two hours, for an orchestra of about a hundred and ten players. Agamemnon's daughter waits in the courtyard for her brother to come home and kill their mother. There is no subplot and almost no relief: three enormous confrontations for Elektra, with her sister, her mother and her brother, then a recognition, two murders offstage, and a dance in which Elektra falls dead. The first sound is a bare unison figure, the murdered king's name in music.","Strauss saw Hofmannsthal's version of Sophocles in Berlin and asked to set it, beginning a partnership that produced six operas across twenty years. Freud and Breuer's Studies on Hysteria had appeared in 1895 and Vienna was full of the new psychology; Hofmannsthal's Elektra is a case study as much as a Greek heroine. Ernst von Schuch again conducted the Dresden premiere, on 25 January 1909, three years after Salome from the same house.","Klytämnestra's scene is where Strauss goes furthest. Her account of the dreams that keep her awake sits over harmony that hardly settles anywhere for ten minutes, with the orchestra imitating a sleepless body in heartbeat figures and sudden shudders in the low brass. Listen for the recognition scene straight afterwards, which is diatonic and tender, and notice that Strauss makes plain tonality itself sound like relief from illness.","It was widely called ugly and hysterical in 1909, and it is still the point at which Strauss's admirers and detractors separate. He never went this far again: Der Rosenkavalier followed two years later, and the charge of retreat has been argued over ever since, with defenders pointing out that he had reached a dead end and knew it. The title role is among the heaviest in the soprano repertory and is rarely sung twice in a week.","Begin with the opening minute and the maids' scene, then Klytämnestra's monologue, then the recognition. Ninety minutes is short for an opera of this weight, so a single sitting is the right way to take it. Compare it with Schoenberg's Erwartung, written the same year, to see two different roads out of Wagner. Solti's recording with Birgit Nilsson is the ferocious benchmark."],"richard-strauss-der-rosenkavalier":["A comedy in three acts, over three hours, set in an imagined Vienna of the 1740s and full of waltzes that would not be invented for another century. A princess takes a seventeen-year-old lover, arranges his marriage to a girl his own age, and gives him up. Two moments carry the opera: the presentation of the silver rose in Act II, scored with celesta, flutes and harps at the very top of the orchestra, and the Act III trio for three sopranos that resolves everything.","Hofmannsthal proposed the subject and wrote the text in an invented Viennese idiom mixing Maria Theresa's court with Molière and Hogarth. Strauss, coming off two one-act shockers, wanted something warm and popular and got it: special trains ran from Berlin to the Dresden premiere on 26 January 1911, and the opera was in every German house within a year. Max Reinhardt supervised the staging without official credit in the programme.","The anachronism is deliberate and systematic. The waltz did not exist in 1740, and Strauss uses it not as local colour but as the sound of time running wrongly, which is the Marschallin's whole subject. Listen for the scoring of the silver rose: no strings carry the melody, only high winds, harps and celesta, a shimmer meant to suggest silver and the smell of rose oil. The same colour returns in the final duet.","It made Strauss rich and permanently annoyed the modernists, who read it as a retreat from Elektra into nostalgia. Adorno's hostility set the tone for decades of academic disdain that audiences comprehensively ignored. Recent criticism has been more interested in what the opera does with gender — a woman playing a boy who disguises himself as a woman — and in the Marschallin's clear-eyed account of ageing at thirty-two.","Go to the presentation of the rose at the start of Act II and then the trio near the end of Act III; if those two passages work on you, the rest will follow. Then hear the Marschallin's Act I monologue about clocks and time. Karajan's 1956 recording with Elisabeth Schwarzkopf is the classic; look for a Marschallin who sounds younger than the tradition suggests."],"richard-strauss-ariadne-auf-naxos":["An opera about an opera. In the Prologue, backstage at a rich Viennese house, a young composer learns that his tragedy will be performed at the same time as a comedy troupe's routine, because the patron wants the fireworks to start on schedule. The Opera that follows does exactly that: Ariadne mourns on her island while four commedia dell'arte clowns interrupt with jokes, and Zerbinetta delivers a long coloratura lecture on getting over men. Thirty-six players only.","The first version, in 1912, was an afterpiece to Hofmannsthal's adaptation of Molière's Le Bourgeois gentilhomme, staged by Max Reinhardt in Stuttgart — an evening needing both an acting company and an opera company, which no theatre could afford twice. Strauss and Hofmannsthal replaced the play with a sung Prologue in 1916, adding the Composer as a soprano trouser role. The Vienna Hofoper gave that version on 4 October 1916, in wartime.","The small orchestra is the argument, not an economy. After the hundred-piece scores of Salome and Elektra, Strauss writes for thirty-six players with piano and harmonium, and every line is audible. Listen for how he keeps the two styles harmonically apart — Ariadne's music long-breathed and modal-leaning, the clowns' crisp and dance-based — and then for the places where they begin to infect each other, which is the opera's real subject.","The 1912 version failed for practical reasons and is now a curiosity, though it is occasionally revived; the 1916 version is standard. Critics have long preferred the Prologue to the Opera, finding the quick conversational writing for the Composer the freshest music Strauss ever produced. Zerbinetta's aria, shortened in the revision, is still among the hardest coloratura pieces in the repertory and dominates any performance in which it succeeds.","Start with the Prologue, which is fast, funny and almost conversational, then Zerbinetta's aria for the fireworks and Ariadne's monologue for the opposite pole. This is the Strauss opera to try if the big ones feel overwhelming: two hours, small forces, every word audible. Look for a recording with a Composer who sounds genuinely young and genuinely furious."],"richard-strauss-die-frau-ohne-schatten":["Three acts, over three hours, for the largest orchestra Strauss ever assembled, including a glass harmonica, Chinese gongs, a wind machine and offstage ensembles. A spirit Empress casts no shadow, which means she can bear no children, and has three days to obtain one from a human woman or lose her husband to stone. Two couples, one supernatural and one poor, are set against each other, and the scoring moves between fairy-tale glitter and the plain domestic sound of a dyer's workshop.","Hofmannsthal conceived it as a counterpart to The Magic Flute — two couples, ordeals, a temple — and worked on the symbolist text for years while Strauss composed between 1914 and 1917. The war made a premiere impossible; when it finally came, at the Vienna Staatsoper on 10 October 1919 under Franz Schalk, Strauss had just taken up co-direction of the house. Austria was hungry and impoverished, and the production's sheer scale was itself controversial.","The shadow is not a metaphor the opera pauses to explain; it is a fertility image on which Hofmannsthal built the entire allegory, and the piece turns on the Empress refusing to take another woman's shadow in a single decisive refusal. Listen for the falcon's cry, a high woodwind figure that recurs like a conscience, and for the Barak scenes, where Strauss suddenly writes the plainest and warmest music in the score.","Long thought unstageable and unaffordable, it was heavily cut for decades and dismissed as Hofmannsthal's most obscure libretto weighing down a fine score. Postwar productions and complete recordings changed that: Karl Böhm's advocacy and the Vienna and Salzburg stagings established it as one of Strauss's major works. Strauss himself called it his child of sorrows. The symbolism still divides audiences sharply, production by production.","Try Act III first, where the great orchestral interlude and the Empress's decision arrive; it is the emotional pay-off and works well with a synopsis in hand. Then Barak and his wife's Act I scenes for the human half of the story. Böhm's Vienna recording is the classic. Follow a libretto closely, because the plot is genuinely hard to reconstruct by ear."],"glinka-a-life-for-the-tsar":["Four acts and an epilogue, about three hours. The peasant Ivan Susanin, a bass, leads a Polish detachment into the winter forest and to their deaths, saving the young tsar. Glinka separates the two nations by musical dialect: the Russians sing long modal melodies over held, drone-like harmony, the Poles get a whole act of polonaise, krakowiak and mazurka. The epilogue's massed chorus 'Slavsya', with bells and brass, is the sound most listeners carry out of the theatre.","Glinka came home to St Petersburg in 1834 after three years in Italy and Berlin, wanting music in which his countrymen would feel at home. Until then Russian opera meant imported Italian troupes and vaudeville with tunes attached. Nicholas I accepted the dedication, and the working title Ivan Susanin was changed to flatter him. The premiere in December 1836 was a court occasion, with the imperial family present and the theatre's Italian singers retrained in Russian declamation.","Susanin's music is built from short repeating cells that behave like folk song rather than aria: Glinka lets a melody stand still while the orchestra shifts underneath it, a device Russian composers used for the next hundred years. Notice too that the Poles never once sing a Russian tune and the Russians never dance a mazurka. The clash of the two idioms is the plot argued in sound.","Aristocrats reportedly called it coachman's music, which Glinka took as praise. Soviet theatres could not stage a title glorifying a tsar, so from 1939 it played as Ivan Susanin with a rewritten libretto that removed the monarchy altogether; the original text only came back in the 1990s. Scholars still argue whether the true climax is Susanin's forest scene or the epilogue, and productions cut the Polish act at their peril.","Start with Susanin's Act IV scene in the forest, then play the Polish act straight through to feel the stylistic gulf Glinka engineered. Compare it with Mussorgsky's Boris Godunov, which took from it the idea of the chorus as the real protagonist. Look for a Russian cast with a genuinely dark bass; the role sits low and needs weight rather than beauty."],"glinka-ruslan-and-lyudmila":["Five acts from Pushkin's mock-epic poem: a bride snatched from her wedding feast, three rival suitors, a dwarf sorcerer whose power lives in his beard, and a garden of enchantresses. Musically it is a catalogue of invented colours, including a descending whole-tone scale for the giant Chernomor, a rising and falling head for the severed Head, and Persian and Caucasian dances in Act IV. The overture's headlong string writing is the best-known five minutes.","Glinka worked at it for five years while his marriage collapsed, assembling the libretto piecemeal from several hands after Pushkin's death in a duel ended any hope of the poet writing it himself. The 1842 premiere at the Bolshoi Theatre in St Petersburg drew a cool response; the court left before the end. Glinka soon went abroad to Spain, where he collected the folk material that fed his later orchestral pieces.","The whole-tone scale that announces Chernomor may be the first sustained use of that scale in European opera, and it became shorthand for the supernatural: Rimsky-Korsakov, Debussy and Stravinsky all drew on it. Listen also to Act I, where a five-beat bar keeps the wedding chorus off balance, and to Ratmir's Act III music, which is where the Russian 'oriental' style, all languid triplets and drones, begins.","Contemporaries complained that the plot is a string of tableaux with no dramatic pressure, and they were right; the work survives on its sound rather than its pacing. Cuts are routine, and no two productions agree on which. Since the 1990s Mariinsky stagings have restored much of the score, and scholars now treat the opera less as a failed drama than as the quarry from which the Mighty Handful took its materials.","Hear the overture, then jump to the Act IV dances and Naina's magic castle music. Follow that with Borodin's Polovtsian Dances and Rimsky-Korsakov's Sadko to hear the same colours being refined. A complete recording under a Russian conductor pays off, since the choral writing and the low brass need idiomatic weight rather than polish."],"dargomyzhsky-the-stone-guest":["Three short scenes on the Don Juan legend, under two hours, set to Pushkin's verse play with not a word altered and almost nothing repeated. There are no arias in the usual sense and no ensembles to speak of; the voices follow the rise and fall of spoken Russian over a thin, watchful orchestra. Two guitar-accompanied songs for Laura are the only set pieces. The Commendatore's statue arrives to grinding whole-tone chords.","Dargomyzhsky, already ill, wrote it in his last years as a deliberate experiment, telling friends he wanted the sound to express the word directly. The young men of the Balakirev circle gathered at his flat to hear each scene as it appeared, and treated the project as their manifesto. He died in January 1869 with the piano score nearly finished; Cui completed the missing passages and Rimsky-Korsakov orchestrated it for the Mariinsky premiere in 1872.","Count how rarely a phrase comes back. Dargomyzhsky refuses the repetitions that normally give opera its shape, so the ear has to hold on to tiny recurring intervals instead: the falling figure attached to Don Juan, the hollow whole-tone sonority for the statue. Notice also that the orchestra frequently contradicts the singer, supplying the irony Pushkin leaves between the lines.","It has always divided listeners: an austere landmark to some, dry recitative to others. Rimsky-Korsakov thought it needed help and re-orchestrated it again in 1902, a version now generally set aside in favour of something closer to the original thin scoring. Its real influence is indirect, running through Mussorgsky's Marriage and Boris Godunov to Debussy, who admired the principle of setting a play as it stands.","Follow a libretto with translation; the whole point is the fit between word and note, and it vanishes if you cannot track the text. Start with Laura's two songs, the only conventionally tuneful moments, then go back to the opening scene. Compare it directly with an Italian Don Giovanni to hear what Dargomyzhsky refuses to do."],"balakirev-islamey":["Nine minutes of solo piano, subtitled an oriental fantasy. A quick Caucasian dance tune, hammered out in repeated notes and hand-crossing leaps, frames a slow central song of a very different character, wide and drooping. The dance returns faster and thicker each time, ending in a coda that asks the player to keep two hands doing three things at once. The sound is closer to a plucked and struck folk band than to a nineteenth-century piano.","Balakirev collected the two themes on travels through the Caucasus in the 1860s, one from a Kabardian dance he heard played on a stringed instrument, the other a Crimean Tatar melody sung to him in Moscow. He wrote the piece quickly in 1869 while his personal circumstances were collapsing; within a year he had lost his conducting posts, and he withdrew from music for most of the 1870s to work as a railway clerk.","The difficulty is not decorative. Balakirev spaces the notes to imitate instruments the piano cannot become, so the hands are forced into positions no keyboard tradition had used, and the famous repeated-note figure is really a drum. Nikolai Rubinstein gave the first performance; Liszt took the piece up and taught it. Ravel later said he had set out to write something harder still.","For a century it served mainly as a stunt, the piece pianists used to prove they could play anything, and its musical substance was underrated as a result. Recordings by Julius Katchen and Boris Berezovsky helped move the argument on. Scholars now read it as the clearest early statement of the Russian orientalist manner, and note that Balakirev's own later revision made it marginally more, not less, awkward.","Listen once for the noise and the speed, then again for the slow middle section, which is where the piece earns its keep. Watch a video performance if you can: the hand-crossing tells you more than the sound alone. Then hear Lyapunov's Lezghinka, which is openly modelled on it, and Borodin's In the Steppes of Central Asia for the same landscape at walking pace."],"balakirev-tamara":["A symphonic poem of about twenty minutes after Lermontov. A low, sluggish opening paints the Daryal gorge with the river running through it; a distant horn call becomes the queen's summons; the centre is a long accelerating dance of seduction built on drones and snaking woodwind lines; and at the end everything drains away, leaving the gorge as it was. Balakirev keeps the harmony almost static and lets colour and rhythm do the work.","Balakirev sketched it in the late 1860s, abandoned music entirely after his professional collapse, and only finished the score in 1882 after returning to composition a changed and more devout man. Fifteen years separate the first idea from the premiere he conducted in St Petersburg in 1883. He sent the score to Liszt, who admired it; Diaghilev later had it staged in Paris as a ballet for Karsavina, with designs by Bakst.","Almost nothing in the piece modulates in the textbook sense. Balakirev repeats short phrases over held bass notes and changes only the instruments and the speed, so the sense of intoxication comes from accumulation rather than harmonic argument. Listen for the two-note falling sigh that opens the work and closes it, and for the moment near the end where the dance simply stops mid-gesture rather than cadencing.","It was admired by musicians well before audiences caught up, and it has never quite entered the standard repertoire, partly because it demands a conductor willing to let a very slow opening be slow. Stravinsky, who found much Balakirev tiresome, conceded that this score taught him things about orchestral colour. Today it is heard mostly on recordings by conductors with a Russian specialism.","Give the first three minutes patience; the piece is designed so that nothing happens for a long time. Then track how the dance is assembled instrument by instrument. Pair it with Rimsky-Korsakov's Scheherazade, written six years later, to hear how much of that famous score was already sitting in Balakirev's gorge. Seek out a recording with a wide dynamic range."],"cui-orientale":["Four minutes for violin and piano, the ninth of the twenty-four short pieces Cui gathered as Kaleidoscope. Over a rocking, quietly repeated piano figure the violin spins a single long melody with a persistent drooping turn at the end of each phrase, climbing gradually into the high register before sinking back. There is no development and no contrasting section: one tune, one accompaniment pattern, one shape rising and falling.","Cui was a military engineer by profession, a professor of fortification who taught the future Nicholas II, and a composer and critic in the hours left over. By the 1890s the Mighty Handful had dispersed, and Cui, its most combative reviewer, was writing chiefly small forms and songs for the domestic market that flourished with the spread of pianos in Russian middle-class homes. Kaleidoscope was published as teaching and salon material.","The oriental flavour comes from one narrow interval, an augmented step inside an otherwise plain minor scale, which Cui touches on every few bars and never resolves conventionally. The piano part never changes its pattern, so the whole effect depends on the violinist's control of a slow crescendo. In the original set the piece sits between two brisk numbers, and much of its languor is a matter of context now lost.","Cui's reputation has suffered from his own criticism: he savaged Tchaikovsky, Rachmaninoff and others, and posterity has repaid him by treating his music as a footnote to his opinions. Even sympathetic scholars concede that his large works, including several operas, lack sustained invention. This miniature outlived them all through violinists' encore programmes and early recordings, which is not the legacy he wanted.","Take it as an encore, not a monument: three or four minutes, ideally after something loud. Compare Heifetz's clipped, forward-moving account with slower modern readings to hear how much the tempo changes the character. Then listen to Rimsky-Korsakov's Song of India for the same idiom handled by a better orchestrator, and to Saint-Saëns's Havanaise for the French version of the same fashion."],"borodin-symphony-no-2":["Under half an hour, four movements, and almost no padding. It opens with a heavy unison string figure, seven notes stamped out in bare octaves, which controls the whole first movement. A scherzo in very fast one-in-a-bar follows, then a slow movement that begins with a solo horn over harp, one of the loveliest things Borodin wrote. The finale is a stamping dance in irregular metre with tambourine and triangle cutting through.","Borodin was Professor of Chemistry at the Medico-Surgical Academy in St Petersburg and composed in gaps between lectures, laboratory work and founding a medical school for women. The symphony took seven years, overlapping with Prince Igor, from which some material migrated. Stasov, the circle's publicist, attached the nickname Bogatyr, after the warrior-heroes of Russian epic, and encouraged a programme of feasting princes that Borodin never fully endorsed.","Listen to how the opening seven-note figure keeps reappearing in disguise: slowed into the second subject, buried in the bass under the scherzo, and finally straightened out in the finale's dance. The scherzo's speed comes partly from writing the first horn a passage that repeats one note so fast it becomes a texture. The slow movement's harp is tuned to imitate the gusli, the Russian plucked zither.","The 1877 premiere in St Petersburg went badly; the scoring was thick and the players underprepared, and Rimsky-Korsakov and Borodin revised the orchestration afterwards. Once the balance was fixed its reputation rose steadily, and conductors from Beecham onwards used it to argue that Borodin was the most naturally symphonic of the Mighty Handful. Debate now centres on tempo: how fast the scherzo can go before it stops being playable.","Begin with the slow third movement and its horn solo, then go back for the first movement's opening bars, which are startling if you come to them cold. Notice how short everything is; nothing outstays its welcome. Compare with Kalinnikov's First Symphony for the warmer, later version of the same manner, and look for a recording that keeps the brass from swamping the strings."],"borodin-string-quartet-no-2":["Half an hour in four movements, unusually gentle for its date. The cello leads almost throughout, opening the first movement with a long singing line answered by the first violin. A light scherzo in waltz rhythm follows, then the Nocturne, in which the cello sings high above the others and the violin answers in canon. The finale is a chase built from a rising two-note question that the players keep tossing between them.","Borodin wrote it in the summer of 1881 at a country retreat and is generally said to have offered it to his wife Ekaterina as a memento of their courtship in Heidelberg twenty years earlier. He was fifty-one, overloaded with teaching and administration, and complained that he could compose only when too ill to go to the laboratory. He died six years later, in the middle of a fancy-dress ball, leaving Prince Igor unfinished.","The whole quartet grows from intervals of a rising second and a falling third, which is why such relaxed music never sounds loose. In the Nocturne the cello's melody returns in the viola an octave lower while the violin traces the same shape above, so three voices sing one tune at once. The finale's opening question is answered by silence for two full bars before the movement takes off.","Its popularity has been a mixed blessing: the Nocturne was extracted, orchestrated, and turned into the song 'And This Is My Beloved' in the 1953 musical Kismet, which made Borodin a posthumous hit and encouraged the view that he was a melodist and nothing more. Quartet specialists argue the opposite, pointing to the tight motivic working, and the piece is now standard repertoire on its own terms.","Everyone starts with the Nocturne; do it, then immediately play the first movement, which is the same music at greater length. Listen to the cello rather than the top line, since Borodin gives it most of the tunes. Compare a Russian quartet's warmer, more portamento style with a leaner modern reading, and follow it with Tchaikovsky's Souvenir de Florence for the same generation at fuller strength."],"borodin-prince-igor":["A prologue and four acts drawn from the medieval Lay of Igor's Campaign. A Russian prince marches against the nomadic Polovtsy, is defeated and captured, is treated with courtesy by his captor Khan Konchak, and escapes home to a ruined city. The score alternates block-like Russian choral writing with the chromatic, drone-based music of the Polovtsian camp, whose long danced sequence at the end of Act II is the most famous half-hour in Russian opera.","Stasov proposed the subject in 1869. Borodin worked at it for eighteen years around his chemistry, abandoning it, resuming it, and cannibalising it for his symphonies. He died suddenly in 1887 leaving the opera in fragments, some of it never written down at all. Rimsky-Korsakov and the young Glazunov assembled and orchestrated a performing version, Glazunov reportedly reconstructing the overture from memory of Borodin playing it. The premiere came at the Mariinsky in 1890.","The two worlds are separated by tuning habits rather than by tunes: the Russians sing in plain diatonic scales harmonised in blocks, the Polovtsy in scales with flattened and raised steps over unmoving bass notes. Konchak, the enemy, gets the warmest music in the opera, and Igor the most self-doubting, which quietly undercuts the patriotic frame. Act III is largely not Borodin's and is often cut entirely.","Every production must decide what the opera is, since no authoritative order of scenes exists. The Rimsky-Glazunov edition dominated for a century; more recent scholarship, and the 2014 Metropolitan Opera staging, rearranged the acts and restored material the editors had dropped. Critics disagree about whether the completed score misrepresents Borodin's harmony. What nobody disputes is the strength of the choral writing.","Start with the Polovtsian Dances, then hear Igor's Act II soliloquy, which is the emotional core and rarely excerpted. Read a synopsis first: the dramaturgy is loose and the names come fast. Choose a complete recording from a Russian house, and if you can, watch a staging that gives the dances real dancers rather than a concert chorus standing still."],"mussorgsky-boris-godunov":["A prologue and four acts, about three and a quarter hours. A tsar who may have murdered a child to reach the throne is destroyed by guilt and by a pretender claiming to be that child. The vocal writing follows Russian speech rhythm rather than song shapes; the chorus, a starving and volatile crowd, is on stage for much of the opera. Bells dominate the coronation, and the score ends with a Holy Fool alone, weeping for Russia.","Mussorgsky, a civil servant in the forestry department, wrote the first version in 1868–69 and had it rejected by the Imperial Theatres, reportedly for lacking a leading female role. He added a Polish act with a soprano, a love scene and a revolutionary crowd scene, and the revision reached the Mariinsky stage in 1874 after a partial performance drew enough support to force the issue. Censorship kept the tsar's murder implicit.","The tolling of the coronation is built from two chords that do not belong to the same key, alternating so that neither ever resolves; the ear hears bells because it cannot hear a tonic. Boris's music narrows as he declines, his phrases shortening until he is speaking on a monotone. The Holy Fool's tune appears earlier in the opera in a crowd scene, so his final lament is something the people have already sung without noticing.","Rimsky-Korsakov's smoother 1896 and 1908 orchestrations made the work internationally famous and were long assumed to be improvements. Since the 1970s houses have mostly returned to Mussorgsky's own scoring, harsher and thinner, and the argument has shifted to which of his two versions to stage, or whether to conflate them. Shostakovich's 1940 orchestration is a third option, still occasionally heard.","Start with the Coronation Scene and Boris's monologue, then hear the final scene with the Holy Fool. Pick a recording in Mussorgsky's own orchestration first, then sample Rimsky's to hear what a century of listeners knew instead. The title role suits a bass who acts with the voice; compare Chaliapin's old recordings with any modern account and the tradition explains itself."],"mussorgsky-pictures-at-an-exhibition":["Ten pieces for piano describing pictures and designs, linked by a recurring Promenade that represents the visitor walking between them. The range is wide: a gnome lurching in fits and starts, an ox-cart grinding closer and away, chicks in shells, two Jewish men arguing, a market at Limoges, catacombs, Baba Yaga's hut on hen's legs, and a final Great Gate of Kiev built from the Promenade tune in full peal.","The architect and painter Viktor Hartmann, a close friend, died suddenly in 1873 aged thirty-nine. Stasov organised a memorial exhibition of some four hundred of his works in St Petersburg in early 1874, and Mussorgsky wrote the suite in about three weeks that June. Several of the pictures he chose are now lost, known only from his titles. He published nothing of it in his lifetime; it appeared in 1886, edited by Rimsky-Korsakov.","The Promenade is written in shifting bar-lengths so the walk keeps stumbling, and it changes character according to the picture just seen, brisk after the chicks, heavy after the catacombs. It stops appearing after Limoges and then returns transformed as the Great Gate, so the visitor and the monument turn out to be the same music. The catacombs movement is subtitled in Latin, with a note that the dead speak in a dead language.","The piano original was long treated as raw material, especially after Ravel's 1922 orchestration for Koussevitzky, which is what most listeners still mean by the piece. There have been dozens of other versions, from Stokowski to synthesizer and rock arrangements. Pianists have gradually reclaimed it, arguing that the awkward, unpianistic textures are the point and that Ravel's polish removes the grief.","Hear the piano version before the orchestral one, then Ravel's, and note what each gains and loses, especially in the Great Gate. Follow the Promenade as a character rather than a link. Then listen to Night on Bald Mountain for the same composer's other side. Richter's live 1958 Sofia recital is the classic piano account, rough sound and all."],"mussorgsky-songs-and-dances-of-death":["Four songs, about twenty minutes, in which Death appears in person and takes someone. In Lullaby he sits by a sick child's cradle and outsings the exhausted mother. In Serenade he courts a dying girl beneath her window. In Trepak he dances a drunken peasant to sleep in the snow. In The Field Marshal he rides over a battlefield at night and claims both armies. The piano writing is spare and often harsh.","The poems are by Mussorgsky's friend Arseny Golenishchev-Kutuzov, written to the composer's specification; a fifth song was planned and never finished. Mussorgsky wrote them between 1875 and 1877 as his drinking worsened and his civil-service career disintegrated. He was dismissed from his post, lived on the charity of friends, and died in a military hospital in 1881, four days after his forty-second birthday.","Death adopts a different musical disguise each time and always borrows a familiar genre: cradle song, serenade with guitar-like strumming, folk dance, military march. Listen for the moment in each song when the vocal line stops belonging to the human character and becomes Death's, usually marked by the accompaniment stiffening into a repeated pattern. The Field Marshal ends on a phrase drawn from a Polish revolutionary song.","The cycle was slow to travel, partly because it needs a singer who will act rather than beautify. Shostakovich orchestrated it in 1962 for Galina Vishnevskaya, and his own Fourteenth Symphony is unimaginable without it. Debate continues over voice type: written for bass, it has been claimed with success by mezzos, and some argue the female voice sharpens the horror of Lullaby.","Begin with Trepak, the most immediately gripping, then Lullaby, the cruellest. Follow the text closely; every effect depends on who is singing which line. Compare Boris Christoff with Vishnevskaya in Shostakovich's orchestration to hear two entirely different readings, and set it beside Schubert's Erlkönig, which invented the situation half a century earlier."],"mussorgsky-khovanshchina":["Five acts on the political chaos in Moscow around 1682: rival princes, the musketeer regiments, and the Old Believers who reject the reformed church. The opera opens with a quiet orchestral dawn over the Moscow River, one of the gentlest preludes in the repertoire, and ends with the Old Believers walking into a fire they have built themselves. The choral writing is broad and modal, and there is no conventional hero anywhere in it.","Mussorgsky began in 1872 with Stasov, who supplied historical material, and worked on it for eight years while his health collapsed. He wrote his own libretto. At his death in 1881 the vocal score was almost complete but the orchestration barely begun and the ending unwritten. Rimsky-Korsakov produced a performing version, cutting substantially, and the first staging was given by an amateur company in St Petersburg in 1886.","The Old Believers sing in the old church modes and almost never move by leap, while the court characters use wider, more modern intervals; the historical argument is coded into the shape of the melodies. Listen for Marfa's divination scene, where a repeating bass pattern turns prophecy into something mechanical. The famous opening prelude is titled Dawn over the Moscow River, and its tune reappears, altered, at moments of political sunrise.","There is no definitive text. Rimsky's version held the stage for decades; Shostakovich made a fuller and darker orchestration in 1959, now often preferred, and Stravinsky and Ravel jointly supplied a final chorus for Diaghilev in 1913. Scholars generally accept Shostakovich's orchestration with Stravinsky's ending as the best compromise. Whether the opera is unfinished by accident or by nature is still argued.","Start with the prelude, then Marfa's divination and the final immolation chorus. Choose a recording in Shostakovich's orchestration, ideally Abbado's, and read the historical background first because the politics are dense. Compare the choral writing with Boris Godunov: the same composer, but slower, wider and less angry, as if watching from further off."],"rimsky-korsakov-the-snow-maiden":["A prologue and four acts on Ostrovsky's fairy play. The daughter of Frost and Spring longs to live among humans, is given the capacity to love, and melts in the first sunlight of summer; her mortal lover drowns himself, and the villagers greet the sun god anyway. The score runs on birdcalls, shepherd's pipes and calendar rituals, with the Dance of the Tumblers as its best-known page and a long hymn to the sun at the end.","Rimsky-Korsakov spent the summer of 1880 at a rented country house and wrote most of it there in a rush of enthusiasm; he later called this the happiest composing of his life. He had recently spent years drilling himself in counterpoint and orchestration after realising how much technique the self-taught Balakirev circle lacked, and this was the first large work in which the new discipline and his ear for folk material worked together.","Each supernatural character carries a fixed instrumental colour and a short recurring motif, a system Rimsky refined for the rest of his career. The Snow Maiden's own music grows warmer and more chromatic exactly as she becomes capable of love, which is to say as she approaches death. Much of the villagers' material comes from a folk-song collection Rimsky himself edited, and the wedding rituals follow real calendar practice.","Ostrovsky disliked what the opera did to his play, and outside Russia the work has never had the currency of Scheherazade or the later fairy-tale operas, mainly because the pacing is leisurely and the ritual scenes need staging that takes them seriously. Russian critics consider it his warmest score. Stravinsky, his pupil, absorbed the pagan spring ritual and used it very differently thirty years later.","Try the Dance of the Tumblers and the final Hymn to the Sun first, then listen to the prologue whole for the birdsong and the frost music. Compare the spring ritual here with the opening of The Rite of Spring to hear how one generation's charm became the next one's violence. A Russian-language recording with a genuine folk-timbred mezzo for Lel is worth seeking out."],"rimsky-korsakov-sadko":["Seven tableaux rather than acts, on the Novgorod legend of a poor musician who wins a wager, sails away, is taken to the bottom of the sea, marries the Sea King's daughter, and is returned home. The centrepiece is a contest of three merchant guests, from Varangia, India and Venice, each singing a song in his own idiom. Human Novgorod is plain and diatonic; the underwater kingdom is built from a scale of alternating steps that keeps sliding.","Rimsky had used the Sadko story once before, in an orchestral tableau of 1867, and returned to it thirty years later at the height of his powers as an opera composer. The Imperial Theatres turned it down, reportedly on the tsar's own instruction, so the premiere in 1897 went to Savva Mamontov's private opera in Moscow, a company backed by an industrialist and designed by painters including Vrubel and Korovin.","The sea music uses a scale alternating whole and half steps, later known as the octatonic scale, which Rimsky treated as the sound of the supernatural. His pupil Stravinsky took the same scale and built The Firebird and much else from it. Note also that the epic recitation of the bylina singers is set in a slow five-beat metre throughout, imitating the way the old narrative poems were actually chanted.","The Song of the Indian Guest was detached almost immediately, arranged for every instrument imaginable, and became a light-music staple, which distorted the opera's reputation abroad. Full stagings are rare outside Russia because of the scale and the seven-tableau structure; the Metropolitan Opera's 2020 broadcast was its first in nearly a century. Scholars now treat it as the pivot between Russian nationalism and Stravinsky's modernism.","Hear the three merchants' songs first, in order, as a set piece; then the underwater scene, where the harps and the sliding scale do their work. Read the tableau titles beforehand, since the story moves in jumps. Then listen to The Firebird and try to spot the inheritance: it takes about thirty seconds. Russian casts and Russian conductors remain the safest choice."],"rimsky-korsakov-tsar-saltan-suite":["Three orchestral tableaux drawn from the opera after Pushkin, each opened by the same brisk trumpet fanfare, as though a herald were announcing the next chapter. The first shows the tsar leaving for war, the second the queen and her child adrift in a barrel on the sea, the third the fabulous city of Ledenets with its squirrel cracking golden nuts and its thirty-three armoured knights rising from the waves. Twenty-two minutes, brilliantly scored.","Rimsky wrote the opera in 1899–1900 for the centenary of Pushkin's birth, again for Mamontov's private company in Moscow rather than the Imperial stage. He was in his mid-fifties, professor at the St Petersburg Conservatory, and had settled into a run of fairy-tale operas after the deaths of Borodin, Mussorgsky and Tchaikovsky left him the senior figure in Russian music and the editor of much of his friends' unfinished work.","The recurring fanfare is a structural joke: it is the same in all three movements, so the suite is organised the way a book of illustrations is, by frames rather than by argument. The squirrel's tune in the third tableau is the Russian folk song 'In the Garden', which Rimsky lets a xylophone play. The knights emerge over a chromatic sea figure that Rimsky reused from his own earlier seascapes.","Overshadowed by Scheherazade and by the orchestral interlude next to it in the opera, the Flight of the Bumblebee, which became one of the most-played fragments in music and detached itself entirely from its context. The suite is heard less often than it deserves; conductors who champion it argue that it shows Rimsky's orchestration at its cleanest, with fewer of the thick doublings of his earlier scores.","Take the third tableau first for the sheer noise of the magic city, then go back and hear all three in order to catch the fanfare joke. Compare it with Lyadov's Enchanted Lake for the quieter Russian fairy tale, and note how much of Stravinsky's Petrushka fairground sound is already here. Any orchestra with a good percussion section will do."],"rimsky-korsakov-the-golden-cockerel":["A prologue, three short acts and an epilogue, under two hours. A lazy tsar is given a magic cockerel that will warn him of danger; he loses both his sons, is seduced by an eastern queen, refuses to pay the astrologer who supplied the bird, and is pecked to death by it. The tsar's music is deliberately crude, all lumbering repeated figures; the Queen of Shemakha's is sinuous and highly chromatic, with a wordless coloratura hymn to the sun.","Rimsky wrote it in 1906–07, in his sixties and openly angry: he had been dismissed from the Conservatory for supporting striking students after the 1905 revolution, and his music was briefly banned from performance. The censor blocked the opera, seeing the point of a stupid tsar destroyed by his own complacency after a military disaster. Rimsky died in June 1908; the premiere came in Moscow in 1909, with cuts.","The astrologer sings in an extremely high tenor register, close to falsetto, and both opens and closes the opera by stepping outside the story to tell the audience that only he and the queen were real people. The cockerel's cry, a rising interval on a muted trumpet offstage, is the seed of much of the score. The two harmonic worlds, the tsar's plain and the queen's chromatic, never mix even when the characters sing together.","Diaghilev staged it in Paris in 1914 as a hybrid of opera and ballet, with singers at the sides and dancers miming, which the composer's family bitterly opposed but which spread the work internationally. Critics have argued ever since about whether the satire is political, philosophical, or simply Pushkin's irony left intact. Stravinsky, present at the Paris production, took the orchestral bite of the score straight into his own work.","Listen to the astrologer's prologue and the Queen of Shemakha's Hymn to the Sun, then hear the tsar's music immediately after, to feel how brutally the two are opposed. The orchestral suite makes a good introduction if the full opera is too much. Look for a production that plays the satire straight-faced rather than as pantomime, and expect the ending to be abrupt."],"tchaikovsky-symphony-no-4":["Four movements, about forty-three minutes. Horns and bassoons open with a jagged fanfare that Tchaikovsky called Fate, and it interrupts the movement twice more, the second time to shut down the recapitulation. A wistful slow movement is led by a solo oboe over pizzicato strings; the scherzo is played entirely plucked by the strings, with wind and brass entering only in the middle; and the finale is a whirling folk-tune movement into which the Fate fanfare crashes near the end.","Written across the worst year of Tchaikovsky's life. In 1877 he married a former student, fled within weeks, and suffered a breakdown; he finished the symphony in Italy and Switzerland while recovering. Its dedication reads simply 'to my best friend': Nadezhda von Meck, the wealthy widow who supported him for thirteen years and whom, by mutual agreement, he never met. The premiere came in Moscow in February 1878 under Nikolai Rubinstein.","The finale's tune is the folk song 'In the field there stood a birch tree', quoted almost unaltered, and Tchaikovsky sets it as a public crowd celebrating while the private self is excluded, which is what the returning Fate fanfare says. The plucked scherzo was designed, he told von Meck, as music for a mind wandering after too much wine. The whole first movement is in a lilting nine-beat metre, unusual at that length.","Taneyev objected that the symphony was really a ballet, and the criticism of Tchaikovsky as a symphonist who thinks in dance rhythms has followed the work ever since. German critics long treated the seams between sections as structural failure. Recent scholarship reads the interruptions as the point rather than a flaw, and the detailed programme Tchaikovsky supplied in letters to von Meck is now taken as an after-the-fact rationalisation.","Listen for the fanfare's three appearances and note what each one destroys. Take the scherzo on its own to hear the plucking as a texture. Then compare Beethoven's Fifth, which Tchaikovsky had in mind, and follow with the Pathétique to see the same argument reach the opposite conclusion. Mravinsky's Leningrad recordings remain the reference for sheer attack."],"tchaikovsky-eugene-onegin":["Seven scenes across three acts, called lyric scenes rather than an opera. A country girl writes a love letter to a visiting dandy, who rejects her kindly; he flirts with her sister, kills his friend in a pointless duel, and years later begs the now married woman to take him, and is refused. The Letter Scene, a twelve-minute soliloquy that starts and stops as she drafts and destroys, is the heart of it.","Tchaikovsky began sketching in May 1877, at the moment he was receiving and answering a real love letter from Antonina Milyukova, whom he then married disastrously. He refused a professional first performance, giving the premiere to students of the Moscow Conservatory at the Maly Theatre in March 1879, because he wanted young voices and unshowy acting. Educated Russians knew Pushkin's verse novel by heart, which raised the stakes considerably.","Onegin has no lyrical music of his own until the final scene, where he sings the melody Tatyana used in the Letter Scene almost twenty years of stage time earlier; his emotional life is borrowed and arrives too late. Listen also to the way social music encroaches: a peasant chorus during Tatyana's first appearance, a provincial waltz around the duel, a grand polonaise before her refusal.","Early critics complained that turning Pushkin's ironic verse novel into a sentimental opera was a desecration, and Tchaikovsky partly agreed, which is why he called the piece lyric scenes. Its stage life outside Russia grew slowly and is now enormous. Modern arguments centre on the duel scene's tempo and on whether Onegin should be played as cold or as merely young; both readings work.","Start with the Letter Scene, and follow the text; it is the best-written twelve minutes in Russian opera. Then hear Lensky's aria before the duel, and the final scene. Compare a Russian-language recording with a translated one and notice how much of the vocal writing is shaped by the consonants. Try the Levine or Solti sets, or Netrebko's Tatyana on video."],"tchaikovsky-serenade-for-strings":["Four movements for strings alone, half an hour. It opens with a broad chordal introduction, deliberately archaic, before a busy sonata movement takes over. Then a waltz that has outlived the rest of the piece, a short elegiac slow movement, and a finale built on two Russian folk tunes, one slow and one a stamping dance. At the very end the archaic opening returns and is revealed to be the same music as the folk finale, slowed down.","Written in the autumn of 1880, at the same time as the 1812 Overture, and Tchaikovsky told von Meck that the Serenade came from inner compulsion while the overture was noisy work done to order. He described it as a homage to Mozart, whose music he loved above all others and whose supposed clarity he was trying to reach. A private read-through was arranged as a surprise for him in Moscow before the public premiere in 1881.","That final transformation is the whole design: the solemn opening chords and the peasant dance of the finale are one melody at two speeds, so the piece turns out to be a single argument disguised as a suite. In the waltz, listen for the way Tchaikovsky keeps the melody a beat behind where the ear expects it. He asked for as many string players as possible, wanting weight rather than chamber transparency.","It has never been controversial, which has occasionally counted against it; critics who want Tchaikovsky tragic tend to file it under charm. Balanchine's 1934 ballet Serenade, the first work he made in America, attached a second life to it. Performance debate is mostly about size: Tchaikovsky's own request for a large body of strings sits awkwardly with the modern taste for lean chamber orchestras.","Everyone knows the waltz; the reward is the finale, and specifically the last ninety seconds, where the opening returns. Listen to the first movement and the last back to back to catch the trick. Compare it with Dvořák's String Serenade of five years earlier, and with Mozart's Eine kleine Nachtmusik for the model Tchaikovsky said he was chasing."],"tchaikovsky-piano-trio":["Three quarters of an hour in only two movements. The first is a long elegiac piece opening with the cello alone over rolling piano, its main theme returning again and again in different lights. The second is a theme with eleven variations, including a fugue, a mazurka and a waltz, followed by an enormous final variation that becomes a movement in its own right, and then a coda in which the opening elegy comes back as a funeral march and fades out.","Nikolai Rubinstein, founder of the Moscow Conservatory and the man who had first savaged and then championed Tchaikovsky's First Piano Concerto, died in Paris in March 1881. Tchaikovsky, who had told von Meck that he disliked the sound of piano with strings, wrote the trio in Rome that winter and inscribed it 'to the memory of a great artist'. It was first played privately in Moscow on the first anniversary of Rubinstein's death.","The piano part is written as a memorial to a pianist, so it is deliberately grand and exposed, and the variations are arguably portraits of episodes in Rubinstein's life, though Tchaikovsky never confirmed any programme. Listen for the moment in the coda when the elegy returns over a marching bass: the same notes that opened the work in lament are now being carried out. The fugue variation is the composer answering his old teacher's demands.","It founded a Russian tradition of memorial trios: Rachmaninoff wrote one for Tchaikovsky himself, Shostakovich another for Sollertinsky. Performers argue about cuts, since Tchaikovsky sanctioned optional ones in the variations and many ensembles take them; purists play everything and accept the length. The piece is also famously exhausting, and reviewers have complained since the 1880s that the piano writing is concerto-sized.","Set aside forty-five minutes and do not dip in. If you must sample, take the theme and first few variations, then jump to the last five minutes. Compare an uncut performance with one that takes the sanctioned cuts to judge for yourself. Then hear Arensky's First Trio and Shostakovich's Second, both descendants. Older Russian recordings favour intensity over polish."],"tchaikovsky-queen-of-spades":["Three acts, about two and three quarter hours. A poor officer obsessed with an old countess's secret of three winning cards courts her ward to reach her, frightens the old woman to death, is visited by her ghost, and destroys himself at the gaming table. Between these scenes Tchaikovsky inserts a full eighteenth-century pastoral entertainment and a children's march, so the opera keeps stepping out of its own nightmare into brittle period pastiche.","Commissioned by the Imperial Theatres and written in Florence in about six weeks early in 1890, to a libretto by the composer's brother Modest which departs sharply from Pushkin's cold, ironic story. Tchaikovsky wept while composing Herman's death. The premiere at the Mariinsky in December 1890 was a success, and the opera reached Kiev and Prague within a year. Three years later he was dead at fifty-three.","The three-card motif, a short rising figure, is planted in the orchestral introduction before anyone mentions cards, and thereafter appears whenever Herman's mind slips. The dying Countess sings not Tchaikovsky but a real aria by Grétry from 1784, so the eighteenth century intrudes as an actual quotation rather than an imitation. Listen for the offstage church choir at the end, which turns a gambling den into a funeral.","Purists have complained since 1890 that Modest's libretto sentimentalises Pushkin, replacing detachment with melodrama. Directors have responded by staging the pastiche scenes as hallucination, and Meyerhold's 1935 production, which moved the action back to Pushkin's own time, still shapes debate. Musically the score is now often ranked above Onegin for sheer theatrical control, though it is performed less.","Begin with Herman's Act I arioso and the Countess's scene in her bedroom, then the final gaming scene. The pastoral interlude will sound like a mistake until you realise it is meant to. Compare it with Mozart's Don Giovanni for the earlier version of a statue-and-ghost reckoning. Gergiev's Mariinsky recording and the Ozawa Boston set are both strong."],"tchaikovsky-souvenir-de-florence":["Four movements for six string players, about thirty-five minutes, and written to sound like far more than six. The first movement launches without preamble on a driving figure; the second is a long duet for first violin and first cello over plucked accompaniment; the third and fourth are unmistakably Russian, the scherzo built on a stamping village dance and the finale on a folk-like tune that ends in a fast fugue and a headlong coda.","Tchaikovsky sketched a theme in Florence in early 1890 while writing The Queen of Spades, hence the title, and completed the sextet that summer as a thank-you piece for the St Petersburg Chamber Music Society, which had made him an honorary member. He found the six-part texture difficult, complained about it in letters, withdrew the work after a private run-through and rewrote the last two movements before publication in 1892.","Only the second movement's melody has anything Italian about it; the title commemorates where the piece began, not what it sounds like. Listen to how the two violas are used, often as the harmonic engine rather than as filler, which is what gives the sextet its orchestral weight. The finale's fugue is brief and almost comic, a nod to the academic training Tchaikovsky had and mostly declined to display.","Critics have long noted the mismatch between the Italian title and the Russian material, and some hear the four movements as belonging to two different pieces. Ensembles disagree about whether to play it one to a part or with a full string orchestra; both are common, and the orchestral version, which Tchaikovsky did not authorise, arguably suits the writing better. Its popularity has risen sharply since the 1990s.","Start with the second movement's violin and cello duet, the most immediately beautiful thing in it, then take the finale for the opposite reason. Hear a one-to-a-part performance and a string-orchestra version back to back; the difference is startling. Pair it with Borodin's Second Quartet, written nine years earlier, for the gentler side of the same repertoire."],"tchaikovsky-symphony-no-6-pathetique":["Four movements, about forty-seven minutes, ending in the wrong place. A bassoon mutters out of near-silence to begin; the first movement contains one of Tchaikovsky's broadest tunes and one of his most violent developments, entered without warning after the quietest bar in the piece. The second is a waltz that cannot be danced, having five beats to the bar. The third is a march that invites applause. Then the finale, slow, sinking, and dying away on the double basses.","Composed in the first half of 1893 with unusual speed and secrecy; Tchaikovsky told his nephew Bob Davydov, the dedicatee, that the work had a programme he would not disclose. He conducted the premiere in St Petersburg on 28 October 1893 to a puzzled reception, and died nine days later, officially of cholera contracted from unboiled water. The title Pathétique was suggested by his brother Modest the morning after the premiere.","In the first movement's quiet passage the trombones play a phrase from the Orthodox requiem, 'With thy saints give rest', which is the closest thing to a statement of the hidden programme. The finale's opening melody does not exist in any one instrument: the notes are split between the two violin sections, so each player is sawing a line that makes no sense alone, and only the audience hears the tune.","The premiere's coolness was reversed within weeks by the composer's death, and the symphony has been read ever since as a suicide note, a reading fed by the persistent rumour that he was forced to poison himself after a homosexual scandal. Most scholars now reject that story for lack of evidence. The interpretive argument that remains is practical: how long to wait after the third movement before the finale.","Play the third and fourth movements together without pausing, and notice the trap: the march ends so triumphantly that audiences still clap, and the finale has to begin in that wreckage. Listen for the split violin tune, then look at a score if you can, because it is hard to believe. Mravinsky is the classic account; Bernstein's very slow late recording is the argument against it."],"arensky-piano-trio-no-1":["Four movements, half an hour, and grateful to play. The violin opens with a long singing theme that the cello answers; the scherzo is a light, glittering piece with a waltz in the middle; the slow movement is an elegy in which the cello is given the best of the writing; and the finale gathers speed before pulling up short to recall the opening theme and the elegy in turn. The piano part is brilliant but never swamps the strings.","Arensky studied with Rimsky-Korsakov in St Petersburg, then taught harmony in Moscow, where Rachmaninoff and Scriabin passed through his classes. He wrote the trio in 1894 in memory of Karl Davydov, the cellist and former director of the St Petersburg Conservatory who had died in 1889, which explains the prominence of the cello throughout. Arensky's own life was disorderly; he drank and gambled heavily and died of tuberculosis at forty-four.","The elegy's melody is prepared much earlier, in the first movement's second theme, so the memorial is built into the structure rather than added at the end. Listen for the scherzo's opening, where the piano's rapid figuration imitates a mandolin or balalaika before the strings enter. The finale's abrupt halt before the recollection is marked in the score as a full silence, and its length is left to the players.","Rimsky-Korsakov wrote coolly of his pupil that he would soon be forgotten, and for much of the twentieth century that looked accurate. The trio survived where the symphonies and operas did not, kept alive by conservatory students and chamber societies. Modern advocates argue that Arensky's fluency is a virtue rather than a symptom, and recordings by the Beaux Arts Trio and others have brought it back to concert programmes.","Start with the scherzo, which is over quickly and hard to dislike, then the elegy for the cello writing. It makes an ideal first Russian chamber piece: nothing in it is difficult to follow. Compare it with Tchaikovsky's Trio, its obvious model, and notice how much shorter and lighter Arensky is willing to be. The Borodin Trio recording is a good starting point."],"kalinnikov-symphony-no-1":["Four movements, about thirty-five minutes, unusually melodic even by Russian standards. The strings begin at once with a long modal tune that will govern the whole symphony. The slow movement floats a cor anglais melody over muted strings and harp; the scherzo is a fast dance with a lilting trio; and the finale gathers up themes from all three earlier movements and ends with the opening melody blazed out by the full orchestra.","Kalinnikov grew up poor in the Oryol province, studied in Moscow on a scholarship at a school for the underprivileged after failing to afford the Conservatory, and played bassoon and timpani in theatre orchestras to survive. Tchaikovsky recommended him for a conducting post, but tuberculosis forced him south to Yalta, where he wrote this symphony in 1894–95 on very little money. The premiere in Kiev in 1897 was a triumph he was too ill to enjoy for long.","Almost every theme in the symphony can be traced back to the opening melody, reshaped rather than developed in the German manner; Kalinnikov varies the mode and the rhythm and lets the shape stay recognisable. Listen in the finale for the moment the cor anglais tune from the slow movement reappears in the brass. The scoring uses harp throughout, which is what gives the slow movement its particular glow.","It spread quickly through Russian and European orchestras before 1914 and then almost vanished, partly because Kalinnikov left too little else to sustain a reputation and partly because the Soviet period preferred other names. Revival has been slow and record-driven, led by Svetlanov and later Järvi. Critics who resist it call it sentimental; its defenders reply that few symphonies of the 1890s are so tightly unified.","Start at the beginning; the first thirty seconds tell you whether the piece is for you. Then take the slow movement on its own. Follow it with Borodin's Second Symphony for the tougher version of the same idiom, and with Tchaikovsky's Fifth for the cyclic finale done at greater length. Svetlanov's recording is the traditional recommendation and still hard to beat."],"taneyev-symphony-no-4":["Four movements, close to forty minutes, and the only one of Taneyev's four symphonies he allowed to be published. A terse opening motif in the strings drives the first movement; the slow movement is a broad, hymn-like piece with long-breathed woodwind lines; the scherzo is fleet and contrapuntal; and the finale takes the opening motif and puts it through fugal treatment before the coda turns it into a major-key affirmation.","Taneyev was Tchaikovsky's pupil and the pianist in the premieres of his concertos, then director of the Moscow Conservatory, then its most demanding teacher of counterpoint; Scriabin, Rachmaninoff and Medtner all passed through his classes. He spent decades on a treatise about invertible counterpoint and composed slowly and self-critically. This symphony, finished in 1898 and premiered by Rimsky-Korsakov in St Petersburg, was his largest orchestral statement.","The same four-note idea underlies every movement, sometimes inverted, sometimes stretched, so the symphony is a demonstration of the contrapuntal principles Taneyev spent his life codifying. Listen in the finale for the entries of the fugue subject stacking up under the main tune. He also lets Russian melodic shapes sit inside strict Western procedures, which is precisely the synthesis he argued Russian music needed if it was to last.","Taneyev's reputation has always been that of a teacher and theorist first, and critics have called his music learned rather than felt, an accusation he anticipated and resented. Soviet scholarship rated him highly; Western programmers have largely ignored him. Recent recordings, notably by Neeme Järvi and Mikhail Pletnev, have made the case that the fluency is real and that the counterpoint serves the melodies rather than smothering them.","Take the slow movement first to establish that this composer can write a tune, then the finale for the fugal machinery. Listen for the four-note idea returning in each movement. Compare it with Brahms, whose combination of severity and warmth Taneyev admired, and with Tchaikovsky's Fourth, written by his teacher twenty years earlier on very different principles."],"glazunov-violin-concerto":["Twenty minutes played without a break. A dark opening theme for the soloist over low strings, a warmer second idea, and a central slow section grow into one another; the cadenza, instead of coming at the end of the first movement, sits at the centre and serves as the bridge into the finale, a brisk march in the major announced by a solo trumpet. The violin writing is high, sweet and continuously singing rather than aggressive.","Glazunov wrote it in 1904 for Leopold Auer, the Hungarian-born professor in St Petersburg who trained Heifetz, Elman and Zimbalist and had once declared Tchaikovsky's concerto unplayable. Auer gave the premiere in St Petersburg in 1905. Glazunov was then in his late thirties, a fixture of Russian musical life; the following year he became director of the Conservatory, a post he held through the revolution until leaving Russia in 1928.","The three sections share material, so what sounds like a suite is a single sonata design folded over itself; the finale's march theme is the opening theme turned major and squared off. The cadenza is not a display panel but the structural hinge, and it introduces the finale's tune before the orchestra does. Note how rarely the full orchestra plays at once, which keeps the soloist audible without any thinning of the harmony.","It sits slightly awkwardly in history, too late to be a straightforward Romantic concerto and too conservative to belong to 1904, and critics have used it to define Glazunov as an accomplished conservative. Violinists never agreed: Heifetz recorded it repeatedly and made it a signature. Its position in the teaching repertoire is secure, and recent scholarship treats the compressed single-span form as genuinely original rather than a shortcut.","Twenty minutes with no natural stopping point, so listen straight through and let the cadenza tell you where you are. Then compare Heifetz's fast, silvery recording with a modern broader one. Set it beside Sibelius's Violin Concerto, written the same year, to hear two entirely different answers to the same problem of what a concerto could still be in 1904."],"lyapunov-transcendental-etudes":["Twelve studies, over an hour if played complete, though they are usually taken in groups. Each has a title and a character: a whirling Terek river, a bell-study, a nocturne, a Caucasian Lezghinka in stamping dance rhythm, a summer night, and a long final elegy. The writing demands leaps, rapid repeated notes, and hands crossing in ways that make the piano imitate plucked and struck folk instruments as much as it sings.","Lyapunov trained in Moscow, then attached himself to Balakirev in St Petersburg and became the last serious composer of that circle. In 1893 the Imperial Geographical Society sent him and Balakirev to collect folk songs in the northern provinces, and the material fed everything he wrote afterwards. He composed the studies between 1897 and 1905, dedicating the set to the memory of Liszt, whose pupils he had known.","Liszt's own Transcendental Studies use only the flat keys; Lyapunov wrote his twelve in the sharp keys Liszt left untouched, so the two sets together complete the cycle of all twenty-four. The tenth, Lezghinka, is openly modelled on Balakirev's Islamey down to the dance rhythm. The last study is titled an elegy in memory of Liszt and quotes his manner of writing rather than any specific tune.","The set went almost unheard for most of the twentieth century, dismissed as derivative Liszt with a Russian accent, and its length works against complete performance. Louis Kentner and later Konstantin Scherbakov made recordings that changed the assessment. Pianists now argue that the folk material gives the studies a harmonic character Liszt does not have, and several numbers have entered recital programmes on their own.","Do not attempt all twelve at once. Start with Lezghinka and Terek, the two most extrovert, then the closing elegy. Play Balakirev's Islamey immediately before Lezghinka and the debt is unmistakable. Compare with Liszt's own set to hear what Lyapunov keeps and what he replaces. Scherbakov's complete recording is the standard modern reference."],"lyadov-the-enchanted-lake":["Eight minutes in which almost nothing happens, deliberately. Muted strings shimmer in a slow tremolo from the first bar to the last; flutes, harp and celesta drop single points of light across the surface; a soft horn call rises once and subsides. There is no theme in the usual sense, no climax worth the name, and no development. The piece begins as though it had already been going on and ends by fading below hearing.","Lyadov taught at the St Petersburg Conservatory, where Prokofiev sat unwillingly in his harmony class, and was known for miniatures, folk-song arrangements and an inability to finish large projects. Diaghilev commissioned The Firebird from him for the 1910 Paris season and, when nothing arrived, gave it to the young Stravinsky, which is how the Ballets Russes found its composer. The Enchanted Lake dates from just before that episode.","Lyadov called it a fairy-tale picture with no people in it, only water and stars, and refused to attach a story. The harmony barely moves: he holds a single chord for long stretches and changes only the instruments playing it, so the sense of motion comes entirely from colour. Listen for the celesta, still a new instrument, and for the fact that no melody is ever completed.","It has been claimed both as the last flowering of Russian nationalism and as an independent Russian route to impressionism arrived at without French influence; Lyadov himself denied knowing Debussy's music well. The piece's brevity and quietness keep it off concert programmes, where it works only as a deliberate contrast. Modern writers use it as evidence that Lyadov's small output reflects taste rather than idleness.","Play it loud enough that the quietest bars are audible, and in a quiet room; on a bus it will simply disappear. Do not wait for the tune. Follow it with Debussy's Reflets dans l'eau for the French version of the same idea and Sibelius's Swan of Tuonela for the northern one. Any recording with real dynamic range will do; Svetlanov's is the traditional choice."],"smetana-ma-vlast":["Six symphonic poems, about seventy-five minutes played complete. Vyšehrad opens with two harps sounding a four-note motto for the ruined castle above Prague. Vltava traces a river from two springs through a forest hunt, a village wedding and a moonlit dance of water sprites to the St John rapids. Šárka tells of a warrior maiden's revenge; From Bohemia's Woods and Fields builds a fugue out of rustling strings; Tábor and Blaník hammer a Hussite battle hymn into a final blazing march.","Smetana began the cycle in 1874, the year syphilis destroyed his hearing, and finished Blaník in 1879 having never heard a note of any of it played. Prague then had a Czech-language theatre only a decade old and no orchestra of its own, and the Habsburg administration ran the province in German. He dedicated the six poems to the city of Prague. The complete cycle was first performed there in November 1882.","The cycle is locked together by two recurring themes. Vyšehrad's harp motto returns quietly at the end of Vltava, where the river passes the castle rock, and comes back in full at the close of Blaník. The Hussite chorale 'Ye Who Are Warriors of God', a fifteenth-century battle hymn still politically charged in the 1870s, drives Tábor and is transformed into the major-key march that ends everything.","Individual poems were performed as they were written; the six together waited until 1882. Vltava quickly outgrew the cycle and is still often played alone, which annoys Czech musicians who hear the last two poems as the argument the first four were building towards. Critics outside Bohemia long treated Tábor and Blaník as crude patriotism. Since 1946 the complete cycle has opened the Prague Spring festival every year on 12 May, the anniversary of Smetana's death.","Hear it whole at least once, in order, so the harp motto that opens Vyšehrad can come back and mean something an hour later. If you know only Vltava, go next to From Bohemia's Woods and Fields for its wide-open string writing. Czech Philharmonic recordings under Talich, Ančerl or Kubelík carry an accent other orchestras rarely find."],"smetana-bartered-bride":["A comic opera in three acts, about two and a half hours. In a Bohemian village on a feast day, Mařenka is promised by her parents to the son of a rich neighbour; her sweetheart Jeník appears to sell his claim on her to the marriage broker Kecal for three hundred crowns, with a clause nobody reads properly. The score runs on dances: a polka closing the first act, a furiant opening the second, and the whirling Dance of the Comedians for a travelling circus in the third.","Prague's Provisional Theatre, opened in 1862, needed Czech operas and had almost none. Smetana, newly its conductor, set a libretto by Karel Sabina and staged the two-act version in May 1866, weeks before the Austro-Prussian war brought enemy troops into the city. It failed. He rewrote it three times over four years, adding an act, replacing spoken dialogue with sung recitative and inserting the circus scene; the 1870 version has never left the repertory.","Almost none of the tunes are folk tunes. Smetana wrote them, then borrowed the rhythms and turns of phrase of the polka and the furiant so exactly that Czech audiences assumed he was quoting. Listen to Vašek, the stammering younger brother: his repeated notes are a real speech defect set to music, and Smetana treats him with unusual tenderness rather than mockery. The overture was written before most of the opera.","The first night lost money and the composer blamed the heat and the war. By 1882 it had reached its hundredth Prague performance; within another decade it was in Vienna and then across Europe, usually in German as Die verkaufte Braut, which is how most of the world met Czech music. Critics have argued ever since whether its sunny peasant world is honest portraiture or a comfortable fiction, and modern stagings often push back against the postcard.","Start with the overture's scurrying fugue for strings, then jump to the Dance of the Comedians. After that, take the second act's drinking chorus and furiant in sequence to feel how the dances carry the plot. Look for a recording sung in Czech; the German versions smooth away the short, punchy first syllables the rhythms are built from."],"dvorak-symphony-no-7":["Four movements, about thirty-seven minutes, and the darkest of his symphonies. The first movement rises out of a low murmur in violas and cellos into a theme that keeps trying to climb and falling back. A quiet slow movement in F major, full of solo woodwind, is interrupted twice by outbursts. The scherzo sets a cross-rhythmed Czech furiant against a gentler trio. The finale drives in D minor to a last-page turn into the major that sounds fought for rather than granted.","The Philharmonic Society of London made Dvořák an honorary member in 1884 and asked him for a symphony. He was forty-three, newly famous in England, and determined to write something that would stand beside Brahms, whose Third Symphony he had recently heard. His mother had died in 1882. Czech politics were at their most strained, and he told a friend the new work should shake the world. He conducted the premiere in London in April 1885.","Dvořák said the first movement's opening theme came to him as a train pulled into Prague's station carrying delegates from Hungary to a national demonstration; he was a lifelong railway enthusiast who copied out locomotive numbers for pleasure. The theme never settles into a comfortable major key. After the first performances he cut a long passage out of the slow movement, judging it too discursive, and the printed score is the shortened one.","London liked it; Brahms's publisher Simrock did not, and haggled over the fee because long symphonies sold worse than dances. For decades the Seventh sat in the shadow of the Eighth and Ninth. Donald Tovey's judgement that it belongs beside Brahms's Fourth and Schubert's Great C major among post-Beethoven symphonies helped turn opinion, and conductors now often name it the finest of the nine.","Follow the opening theme: eight bars, quiet, in the violas, then track how often it comes back louder and more desperate. The scherzo is the easiest way in, two rhythms fighting over the same bar. Compare it with Brahms's Third, written two years earlier, and then with Dvořák's own Eighth to hear how quickly the mood lifted."],"dvorak-rusalka":["A lyric fairy tale in three acts, about two and three-quarter hours. A water nymph asks a witch to make her human so she can love a prince; the price is her voice on land, and failure will damn them both. The first act gives her the Song to the Moon, a slow prayer over rippling strings. The second is a court ball at which she cannot speak. The third returns to the pool for a kiss that kills.","Dvořák was fifty-nine, back from three years in America and running the Prague Conservatory. Jaroslav Kvapil had offered his libretto around before it reached him; Dvořák set it in about seven months. Prague was steeped in Symbolist theatre and in the folk collections of Erben and Němcová, and the story braids Andersen's mermaid with Czech water-spirit legend. The National Theatre gave the premiere in March 1901 and it was a success at once.","Every character has a motif, and the water goblin Vodník's — a heavy falling figure in low brass and woodwind — keeps intruding on the human scenes to remind you which world owns Rusalka. Her voicelessness is composed, not merely acted: through most of the second act she is silent while the orchestra sings her line for her. The Song to the Moon's accompaniment is the same rippling figure that opens the opera.","Dvořák's most-played opera at home, it took eighty years to travel: Britain did not stage it professionally until the late 1950s and the Metropolitan Opera not until 1993. English-speaking audiences met the Song to the Moon long before they met the work it belongs to. Directors since the 1980s have read it as a story about sexual awakening, exile or enforced muteness rather than a children's fable, which the score's harshness supports.","Start with the Song to the Moon, then go back and hear the opening five minutes so you recognise the water music it grows out of. The moment to wait for is the final duet, where the same melody returns transformed. Look for a Czech cast; the language's stresses shape the vocal lines. Renée Fleming's recording is the usual gateway."],"dvorak-stabat-mater":["Ten movements for four soloists, chorus and orchestra, about eighty-five minutes: the longest setting of the medieval poem in the repertory. It opens with the strings holding a single high note that sinks slowly downward through a long orchestral introduction before any voice enters. The movements alternate massed chorus, solo quartet and individual arias, moving from B minor through a tenor's plea to a final Amen fugue that lifts into D major.","Dvořák sketched the first movements in 1876 after his daughter Josefa died two days old. He put the sketch away and returned to it in 1877, in the weeks after his eleven-month-old daughter Růžena swallowed a phosphorus solution and his three-year-old son Otakar died of smallpox. Within a few months he had lost all three of his children. He was thirty-six, poor, and still barely known outside Bohemia.","The falling figure the strings play at the very start returns at the end of the last movement, so the work closes the circle it opened. Dvořák never sets the Crucifixion dramatically; there is no thunder and no storm, and the only real violence is the tenor's 'Fac me vere'. He keeps unaccompanied choral writing in reserve for the moments of greatest quiet, so the plainest textures arrive last.","The Prague premiere came in 1880, but England made the work: a London performance in 1883 and Dvořák's own conducting there in 1884 turned him into a celebrity in the country of the great choral festivals. Some later critics found the length and the sweetness at odds with the subject; others hear the repetitions as the shape grief actually takes. It remains his most-performed sacred music.","Give the opening minutes your full attention with nothing else happening; the slow descent is the whole piece in miniature. Then skip to the bass solo of the fourth movement and to the final Amen. A large chorus and a Czech orchestra suit it, and Kubelík's recording is a standard reference. Compare it with Verdi's Requiem of three years earlier to hear how differently grief can sound."],"dvorak-piano-quintet-no-2":["Piano and string quartet, four movements, about forty minutes. The cello sings the opening melody over quiet piano arpeggios, and within twenty bars the mood has darkened into the minor; that swing between warmth and shadow runs through the whole piece. The second movement is a dumka, a lament that keeps breaking into fast dance. The third is a furiant labelled as a scherzo, and the finale is a polka-driven rondo that pauses for a hushed chorale before its last flourish.","Dvořák spent the summer of 1887 at Vysoká, the country house in southern Bohemia where he kept pigeons and walked for hours. He had dug out a piano quintet in A major written fifteen years earlier, tried to revise it, gave up, and wrote a new one in the same key in a few weeks. He was forty-six and at the height of his powers, with the Seventh Symphony behind him; Prague heard the quintet in January 1888.","The viola, not the violin, is the emotional centre: it announces the dumka's theme and the finale's second subject. Dvořák marks the dumka's faster sections as only a little quicker rather than genuinely allegro, so the accelerations feel like someone trying and failing to cheer up. The scherzo is not really a furiant either; it has the title's swagger but keeps the characteristic cross-rhythm hidden inside the accompaniment.","It was popular immediately and has stayed the most played Romantic piano quintet after Schumann's and Brahms's. Critics who wanted Dvořák to be a symphonist once treated his chamber music as relaxation, a view that has collapsed. The live argument now is about tempo in the dumka, where an over-slow reading makes the movement sag and a brisk one loses its ache.","Start at the second movement and let the two speeds alternate; by the end you will hear the form without being told it. Then go back for the cello's first entry. Compare it with the Dumky Trio of four years later, where the same idea takes over an entire work. Any recording pairing a good quartet with a pianist willing to play quietly will serve."],"dvorak-dumky-trio":["Six movements for violin, cello and piano, about thirty-two minutes, and not a sonata form anywhere in it. Each movement is a dumka: a slow, brooding song that breaks into a fast, bright dance and then sinks back. The keys wander from E minor through C sharp minor, A major, D minor, E flat major and C minor without ever settling on a home. The last three movements run together, and the cello leads more often than the violin, usually high and exposed.","Dvořák wrote the trio in 1890 and 1891, between the Requiem and his departure for New York. He played the piano part himself at the first performance in Prague in April 1891, on the evening Charles University made him an honorary doctor, and then took the piece on a farewell tour of some forty Bohemian and Moravian towns with the violinist Ferdinand Lachner and the cellist Hanuš Wihan before sailing for America.","The dumka is Ukrainian in origin, a ballad form Dvořák met through Slavonic literary collections rather than in Bohemian villages, so the trio's Czech colour is partly borrowed from further east. Nothing modulates conventionally; movements simply begin in a new key with no preparation. Wihan, the cellist of that farewell tour, is the reason the cello writing sits so high and so exposed, and he would later be given the Cello Concerto too.","Audiences took to it at once and scholars have argued about it since: is a work of six linked laments a piano trio at all, or a suite pretending to be one? Some hear a deliberate dismantling of Germanic form on the eve of the American journey. Performers disagree about how far to stretch the slow sections; too much rubato and the six movements blur into one long sigh.","Play the first movement twice before going on, because the slow-fast-slow pattern it sets is repeated five more times in different weather. Notice how often the piano is left alone. Then hear the dumka movement of the Piano Quintet to find the seed. Czech players, the Suk Trio above all, give the fast sections a lift that others turn into mere speed."],"janacek-jenufa":["Three acts, about two hours, sung in Moravian dialect. A village girl is pregnant by the feckless Števa; his half-brother Laca slashes her cheek in jealousy; her stepmother the Kostelnička hides her through the winter, drowns the newborn under the ice and is exposed at the thaw. The orchestra runs on short repeated cells rather than long melodies, and the first act turns on a xylophone ticking like a mill wheel behind the opening scene.","Janáček worked on it from 1894 to 1903, setting Gabriela Preissová's prose play himself, which was almost unheard of then, and finishing as his twenty-year-old daughter Olga was dying; the score is dedicated to her memory. He was a provincial choirmaster and teacher in Brno, nearly fifty, with no reputation beyond Moravia. The Brno theatre staged it in January 1904 and Prague's National Theatre refused it for twelve years.","Janáček had spent years notating the pitch and rhythm of ordinary Czech speech in pocket notebooks — a market seller's shout, a child's question — and built the vocal lines out of those speech tunes. The Kostelnička sings almost entirely in fragments and is given no aria. Listen for the short figure first heard with the mill wheel, which returns whenever the child is mentioned, long after the xylophone has fallen silent.","Brno's small company gave a rough premiere. Karel Kovařovic, who had rejected the opera in Prague, finally staged it there in 1916 in his own re-orchestration, smoothing the harmony and thickening the doublings, and that version travelled the world for sixty years. The Mackerras and Tyrrell edition of the original Brno score, published in 1996, restored the sparer sound, and most houses now use it. Which version is the real one is still argued.","Give the first four minutes your attention: the xylophone, the rising figure in the strings and Jenůfa's first phrase set up everything that follows. Then go straight to the end of the second act, the Kostelnička's scene with the ice. Seek a recording in Czech using the original version; the language's front-loaded stresses are the rhythm of the piece."],"janacek-katya-kabanova":["Three acts and about a hundred minutes, set in a town on the Volga. Káťa, married into a household ruled by her mother-in-law Kabanicha, takes a lover while her husband is away, confesses in public during a thunderstorm and drowns herself in the river. The prelude sets eight slow timpani strokes against sleigh bells, and both return at the end. The orchestra includes a viola d'amore, an obsolete instrument Janáček wanted for Káťa's most private music.","Janáček was sixty-six, suddenly famous after Prague finally staged Jenůfa, and in love with Kamila Stösslová, a married woman thirty-eight years younger whom he had met in 1917 and to whom he wrote hundreds of letters. He said Káťa was her. The source is Ostrovsky's play The Storm, in Vincenc Červinka's translation, which Janáček cut to the bone. Brno gave the premiere in November 1921.","The eight timpani strokes of the prelude are a death knell sounded before anything has happened; they return as she goes into the water, so the opera closes a circle. The viola d'amore's sympathetic strings are nearly inaudible in a theatre, which Janáček knew: he wanted the colour rather than the volume. During the confession the offstage voices may be townspeople or Káťa's own conscience, and the score does not decide.","Brno's premiere went well; Prague's the following year divided critics who found the manner too abrupt to be opera. Max Brod's German translation carried it into Berlin in 1926 and made Janáček an international name. Charles Mackerras's Decca recording of 1976 began the wave that put all the late operas into the repertory. Productions now argue over whether Kabanicha is a monster or a product of the same system that destroys Káťa.","Listen to the four-minute prelude on its own, then again after the last scene, and the design becomes audible. Káťa's long first-act monologue about flying is the aria she never quite gets to sing. Compare it with Jenůfa: the same short cells, but the orchestra now carries most of the feeling. Mackerras's recording with Elisabeth Söderström is the usual starting point."],"janacek-cunning-little-vixen":["Three acts, about ninety-five minutes, and roughly half the cast are animals. A forester catches a vixen cub; she escapes, raids a farmyard, courts a dog fox, raises a litter and is shot by a poacher. Between the animal scenes the humans age and their world narrows. The score is short, bright and dance-driven, full of orchestral interludes, and it closes with the forester alone in the wood in a slow rising melody.","The story came from a newspaper: Rudolf Těsnohlídek's serial in Brno's Lidové noviny, written to caption a set of drawings by Stanislav Lolek, and pointed out to Janáček by his housekeeper. He was seventy, at the peak of a very late career, and spent the summers of 1922 and 1923 at Hukvaldy, the village he came from, watching the forest he was writing about. Brno gave the premiere in November 1924.","The forester's closing music is not new: it is the vixen's own theme, slowed and handed to him, so the ending's consolation is her music outliving her. Janáček wanted the animal parts sung by children and danced where possible, blurring the two casts on stage. The frog who has the last word is the grandson of the frog from the first scene, and says almost the same thing.","It puzzled its first audiences, who expected either a children's piece or a comedy and got a work about dying. Max Brod's German version turned the vixen into a gypsy girl and the opera into an allegory, distorting it for decades. Janáček asked that the final scene be played at his funeral in 1928, and it was. Since Walter Felsenstein's Berlin staging of 1956 it has been recognised as one of the century's strangest and most humane operas.","Start at the end: the last six minutes, the forester's monologue, work on their own. Then go back to the opening, which is almost a ballet before anyone sings. Notice how much of the story is told by the orchestra between scenes. Mackerras's recording, or any staging that resists making the animals cute, will serve; the piece is funny and merciless at once."],"janacek-makropulos-case":["Three acts, about ninety-five minutes, built around a lawsuit. Emilia Marty, a famous and glacial singer, knows too much about a century-old inheritance case; she is in fact Elina Makropulos, born in 1585 and kept alive by an alchemist's formula she now wants renewed. The prelude piles offstage trumpets and timpani over a churning orchestra. The last twenty minutes, in which she decides to let herself die, are among the most sustained stretches Janáček wrote.","Janáček saw Karel Čapek's play in Prague in 1922 and asked for the rights; Čapek doubted a legal comedy could be sung. Much of the first act is documents and dates, and Janáček set the text almost verbatim, at seventy-two, in the same burst of work that produced the Glagolitic Mass and the second string quartet. Brno gave the premiere in December 1926, less than two years before his death.","Almost the whole opera is conversation over an orchestra repeating short figures obsessively. Marty's coldness is written as an absence: she is denied the warm speech-melody Janáček gives everyone else, and only in the final scene does her line open out. An offstage chorus sings wordless vowels at the end, and the prelude's fanfares return there too, framing three hundred years of plot inside four minutes of music.","Early audiences found the first act impenetrable and the subject cold. The score Janáček left had unclear orchestration in places, and Břetislav Bakala's performing version was standard for decades until Mackerras and others returned to the manuscript. Since the 1990s it has been staged constantly, partly because the title role suits singing actresses of a certain age, and partly because the price of immortality has become a live question again.","Do not fight the first act's legal detail; let it wash past and listen to the orchestra, which is doing the feeling. The reward is the final scene, which needs no context at all. Read a synopsis first. Elisabeth Söderström and Anja Silja both recorded Marty memorably, and Mackerras's Decca set is the reference."],"janacek-string-quartet-no-1-kreutzer-sonata":["Four short movements, about eighteen minutes, played with barely a pause. It opens with a rising phrase in the first violin that is then broken up and hurled around for the rest of the piece. Janáček asks the players to bow on the bridge, so whole passages hiss rather than sing; tremolos, snatched fragments and sudden silences replace conventional development. The third movement seems to glance at Beethoven's own Kreutzer Sonata before smashing what it has quoted.","Tolstoy's novella has a husband murder his wife out of jealousy after she plays Beethoven's Kreutzer Sonata with a violinist. Janáček had approached the subject twice before in pieces now lost. In late 1923, aged sixty-nine, he wrote the quartet in about nine days for the Bohemian Quartet, telling Kamila Stösslová he was thinking of a poor woman tormented and beaten to death. Prague heard it the following year.","Janáček identifies with the murdered wife, not with Tolstoy's narrator, so the violence in the piece is what is done to her rather than by her. The opening phrase reappears in every movement, each time more damaged, and by the finale it is being interrupted before it can finish. The bowing-on-the-bridge passages are unusually long for 1923, and players still argue about how ugly to let them sound.","The Bohemian Quartet's premiere in 1924 was well received and the piece entered the repertory quickly, helped by its brevity. Scholars have assumed that the two lost earlier Kreutzer works were recycled into it, but the manuscript evidence is thin. Performances divide between those that smooth the joins into a continuous argument and those, following Czech tradition, that leave every fragment raw and unblended.","Eighteen minutes and no real slow movement, so hear it twice in a row. Track the first violin's opening four notes and count their disguises. Then go to the Second Quartet, Intimate Letters, written five years later on the same nervous principle but about desire rather than violence. The Škampa and Pavel Haas quartets record it with the necessary rawness."],"bartok-violin-concerto-no-2":["Three movements, about thirty-seven minutes. Harp chords and plucked strings set up a guitar-like strumming before the violin enters with a broad theme in the manner of the verbunkos, the old Hungarian recruiting dance. The slow movement is a set of six variations on a quiet folk-like tune. The finale is a free variation of the first movement, following it almost bar for bar in places, and ends with a soloist's flourish Bartók added only under protest.","Zoltán Székely, a Hungarian violinist living in the Netherlands, commissioned the work in 1936 and wanted a concerto; Bartók wanted a set of variations. They compromised, and he wrote a concerto whose finale is a variation set. He was fifty-six, watching fascism close over Hungary, refusing to let his music be broadcast in Germany or Italy, and he would leave for America in 1940. Székely gave the premiere in Amsterdam in March 1939.","The first movement's second theme uses all twelve notes of the chromatic scale — Bartók's answer to the Second Viennese School, and his demonstration that a twelve-note theme need not sound atonal, since he harmonises it in the plainest way available. The original ending kept the soloist silent for the last bars; Székely objected and Bartók wrote the alternative that is almost always played. Both versions survive in print.","It was received as his most approachable large work and it displaced the withdrawn First Concerto of 1907, which stayed unpublished until 1958 and is why this one is usually just called 'the' Bartók concerto. Yehudi Menuhin championed it. Arguments now centre on the two endings, on how much freedom the verbunkos theme takes, and on whether the harp-and-strings opening should sound like a cimbalom band.","Begin with the slow movement's theme and follow the six variations; it is the clearest lesson in variation form in twentieth-century music. Then play the outer movements back to back and hear the finale rewriting the first. Compare it with Berg's Violin Concerto, finished three years earlier. Menuhin's and Isabelle Faust's recordings sit at opposite ends of the interpretative range."],"bartok-sonata-two-pianos-percussion":["Two pianos, two percussionists and about twenty-six minutes. The battery is timpani, xylophone, side drums with and without snares, cymbals, bass drum, triangle and tam-tam, and Bartók drew the exact floor plan for the four players into the score. A slow introduction grows out of a timpani roll into a driving first movement; the second is one of his nocturnal slow movements; the third is bright, almost cheerful, and fades away to nothing.","Paul Sacher commissioned it for the tenth anniversary of the Basel section of the International Society for Contemporary Music, having commissioned Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta from the same composer the year before. Bartók played the first piano at the Basel premiere in January 1938 with his wife Ditta Pásztory-Bartók at the second. Weeks later Germany annexed Austria and he began arranging to get his manuscripts out of Hungary.","The pianos are treated as percussion and the percussion as melody. The xylophone carries tunes, the timpani are retuned with their pedals in mid-phrase to play glissandos, and the cymbal is specified to be struck with different sticks at named points on the metal. Bartók's directions run down to the size of the beaters. The quiet second movement is his first fully worked night music for this particular ensemble.","Basel took to it, and Bartók made an orchestral version in 1940, the Concerto for Two Pianos and Percussion, which he thought less successful and which is rarely played. The sonata is now standard repertoire and a fixture of percussion education. Its influence runs from Boulez to film scoring, and the practical argument is about balance: whether the pianos should dominate or dissolve into the battery.","Listen for the timpani glissando near the start, a pedal-drum effect most listeners have never consciously noticed. The slow movement rewards a dark room. Then compare it with Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta, its twin. Live performance is worth seeking out, since the piece is partly a spectacle of four people surrounded by equipment."],"bartok-cantata-profana":["About nineteen minutes for tenor, baritone, double mixed chorus and orchestra, in three continuous parts. Nine sons who hunt instead of working are turned into stags; their father goes to find them and raises his gun; the eldest tells him they cannot come home, because their antlers will not pass through the door and their mouths now drink from mountain springs. A rising scale opens the work in the chorus and closes it, quietly, in the tenor.","Bartók built the text himself from two Romanian colinde — winter solstice ballads he had recorded on phonograph cylinders in Transylvania before 1918 — and translated them into Hungarian. In 1930, with Hungarian nationalism hardening after the loss of Transylvania, publishing a Hungarian-language work openly derived from Romanian peasant song was a political act. He said his guiding idea was the brotherhood of peoples, and planned a larger cycle he never wrote.","The rising and falling scale that opens the piece has often been heard as a deliberate echo of the opening chorus of Bach's St Matthew Passion, which Bartók knew intimately, and the stags' transformation is set as a fugue. The chorus is split into two bodies that answer each other across the hall, and the tenor's last line floats almost free of the orchestra, as if the singer had walked away from it.","The BBC gave the first performance in London in 1934; Hungary did not hear it until two years later. Choruses find it brutally hard — the divisions, the range, the language — which is why it stays rarer than its quality warrants. Scholars disagree about how far to read the stags as Bartók's own refusal to return to a country he no longer recognised. He left Hungary five years after finishing it.","Twenty minutes, one story, no interval: read the text first and then listen straight through. The moment to wait for is the father lowering his gun. Compare it with Kodály's Psalmus Hungaricus, written seven years earlier from a Hungarian text, to hear two ways of being a Hungarian composer. Recordings under Boulez and under Solti take very different tempos."],"bartok-string-quartet-no-6":["Four movements, about thirty minutes. Each is introduced by the same slow theme marked Mesto — sad — heard first on solo viola, then in two parts, then in three, and finally taking over the whole last movement, which never arrives at the fast music the pattern has promised. Between these introductions come a sonata movement, a heavy-footed Marcia and a mocking Burletta in which two violins are made to play deliberately out of tune with each other.","Bartók began the quartet at Saanen in Switzerland in August 1939 and finished it in Budapest that November, weeks after the war began. His mother, whom he had refused to leave, died the following month. The sketches show he first planned a fast finale and replaced it with a fourth Mesto. It was the last work he wrote in Europe: he sailed for New York in October 1940 and never came back.","The Mesto theme returns four times, always the same melody but differently harmonised and differently scored, so it carries a different weight on each appearance. In the Burletta one violin is instructed to play a quarter-tone flat against the other — real out-of-tuneness, precisely notated. The finale is built entirely from material already heard, so nothing new arrives in the last ten minutes, and that absence is the point.","The Kolisch Quartet gave the premiere in New York in January 1941, with Bartók in the audience and close to destitute. The six quartets were slowly recognised as the most important cycle since Beethoven's, and the Sixth as its bleak close. Performers argue about whether the Mesto should sound like grief or like exhaustion, and about whether the Burletta's quarter-tones should be audibly ugly or merely queasy.","Listen to the four Mesto passages alone, in order, before hearing the whole quartet; five minutes will teach you the piece. Then play it complete and wait for the finale, where the introduction refuses to end. Compare it with Janáček's Intimate Letters. The Takács Quartet's cycle is the usual recommendation and the Végh Quartet's the historic one."],"bartok-mikrokosmos":["One hundred and fifty-three piano pieces in six graded books, running from five-finger exercises a child can manage in a first week to the Six Dances in Bulgarian Rhythm that close the last book and belong in recitals. Complete performance takes about two and a half hours. The titles name what each piece teaches or evokes: Chromatic Invention, From the Island of Bali, Village Joke, Boating, Diary of a Fly. Several exist in versions for two pianos or with an added second part.","Bartók began in 1926 for his son Péter, then six, and finished in 1939, so the collection spans his most concentrated period as a composer. No piano method then taught modern harmony, irregular metre and folk modes from the first lesson; teachers still started children on Czerny. He was himself a working pianist and a professor at the Budapest Academy who had edited Bach and Mozart for students, and he knew exactly what small hands can do.","The set is a composition treatise in disguise. Whole-tone, pentatonic, Lydian and octatonic collections are introduced one at a time as though they were ordinary, and mirror writing, canon and inversion arrive before a child has met a plain triad. The last book's Bulgarian dances use metres of seven, five and nine beats grouped unevenly, the same rhythms as the string quartets. Several pieces quietly rehearse material from the larger works being written alongside them.","Some teachers found it too difficult and too dry, and it never replaced conventional methods; others treat it as the most important body of piano teaching material of the century. Pianists long thought the early books unperformable in concert. Since György Sándor and later Zoltán Kocsis and Jenő Jandó recorded the whole cycle, complete performances have shown that the miniatures work as music when they are played as music.","Do not start at number one. Go to the sixth book and the Six Dances in Bulgarian Rhythm, then work backwards into books four and five, where the pieces last a minute and each has a single idea. From the Island of Bali and Diary of a Fly are the ones you will remember. Kocsis's recording has the lightest touch."],"kodaly-psalmus-hungaricus":["About twenty-four minutes for tenor, mixed chorus and orchestra, with an optional children's choir. It is one continuous movement in rondo form: a keening choral refrain returns several times between the tenor's increasingly agitated verses. The tenor carries most of the text, a sixteenth-century Hungarian paraphrase of Psalm 55, the psalm of a man betrayed by his friends. Bare modal choral writing is set against sudden brass, and the ending dissolves rather than triumphs.","Budapest commissioned the work for the fiftieth anniversary of the union of Buda, Pest and Óbuda into one city, and it was first performed in November 1923 in a concert that also introduced Bartók's Dance Suite. Hungary had lost two-thirds of its territory at Trianon three years earlier, and both Kodály and Bartók had been pushed out of official positions after 1919. He chose a psalm about betrayal and set it in archaic Hungarian.","The refrain is Kodály's own invention, not a folk tune, but its shape follows the pentatonic contours of the old Hungarian songs he had been collecting since 1905, so it passes for ancient. Mihály Kecskeméti Vég's sixteenth-century verse is left unmodernised, its grammar strange even to Hungarians. The tenor part lies low and declamatory rather than lyrical, closer to a cantor's chant than to opera: Kodály wanted a witness, not a hero.","It made Kodály internationally famous within two years, taken up by Furtwängler, Toscanini and Mengelberg. Between the wars it was heard as Hungary's answer to other nations' war requiems; under communism it was awkward, and abroad it faded as Bartók's reputation rose. Choruses that sing it in translation lose most of the point, and the shortage of good Hungarian tenors keeps performances rarer than the work deserves.","Follow the refrain: hear it once, then wait for its returns and notice how differently each is scored. Read the text in translation first — a man surrounded by liars — and the shape becomes obvious. Compare it with Bartók's Cantata Profana. Any recording under Kodály's own baton or Antal Doráti's, sung in Hungarian, is the place to begin."],"kodaly-duo-violin-cello":["Three movements, about twenty-five minutes, for two players and no piano. The instruments spend much of the piece imitating a larger ensemble: double stops, held drones and plucked chords make two strings sound like four or five. The first movement is a rhapsodic sonata form, the second a slow, free lament that seems to be improvised, and the finale a driving folk dance that begins with a long unaccompanied cello recitative before the violin joins it.","Kodály wrote it in 1914, in the months when Europe went to war. He was thirty-one, teaching at the Budapest Academy, and had spent nine summers with Bartók walking Hungarian and Slovak villages recording peasant singers onto wax cylinders. The collecting stopped. The Duo, the Solo Cello Sonata of the following year and the first string quartet all come from this narrow, isolated period, when Hungarian musicians were cut off from Paris and Vienna alike.","The tunes are Kodály's own, built on the pentatonic scale of the oldest Hungarian songs, whose characteristic falling interval he had traced as far east as the Volga; the Duo's melodies keep dropping the same distance. The slow movement is written as free recitative in which the barlines are only advisory. In the finale the cello is asked to strum across its strings like a village fiddler's accompanist.","It waited four years for a first performance and much longer for a reputation, partly because a violin-and-cello duo has almost no repertoire and no audience habit. Performers treat it as one of the hardest things they play: exposed intonation, no keyboard to hide behind, and rhythms that must sound improvised while staying exactly together. It is now the standard work of its medium and the reason others were written.","Two instruments, no piano — listen for how full it nonetheless sounds, especially in the finale's strummed chords. Start with the slow movement if you want the Hungarian colour at its plainest. Compare it with Kodály's Sonata for Solo Cello, written a year later. Duos who have played together for years, rather than star pairings, tend to find its rubato."],"fibich-poeme":["A slow single melody a little over four minutes long, rising and falling in gentle arcs over a rocking accompaniment that never changes its tread. It began as the lyrical centre of the orchestral idyll At Twilight, a piece about a summer evening, and Fibich made a piano version too. Almost every violinist plays it in arrangement, and it has been sung, transcribed for cello and scored for salon orchestra and for cinema pit bands.","Zdeněk Fibich was Prague's third composer after Smetana and Dvořák, and the most cosmopolitan of them: trained in Leipzig and Paris, drawn to Wagner and to the spoken melodrama. In 1892 he fell in love with his pupil Anežka Schulzová and began a private diary in music that eventually ran to 376 short piano pieces called Moods, Impressions and Reminiscences. At Twilight dates from 1893, in the middle of that obsession.","The Poème is not a free-standing piece but an episode lifted out, and inside At Twilight it returns transformed at the very end, after the woodwind have suggested nightfall. Its melody is one of the diary entries, so a tune the world knows as a genteel encore began as a coded private note about a particular woman on a particular day. Fibich indexed those pieces by her gestures and features.","It became a hit and then a liability: its popularity in cafés and on early records fixed Fibich in the public mind as a miniaturist, while his three symphonies, the melodrama trilogy Hippodamia and the opera Šárka went unplayed outside Bohemia. Czech scholarship has been trying to reopen that case for fifty years. Beyond the Czech lands the recovery has barely begun, and most listeners still know him by four minutes of music.","Play it once as the violin encore it has become, then find a recording of the complete At Twilight and hear it in its proper surroundings, where it means considerably more. After that try one of the symphonies. Older Czech Philharmonic recordings are where Fibich still sounds like a native speaker rather than a salon guest."],"suk-asrael-symphony":["Five movements in two parts, about fifty-five minutes, in C minor. The first part is a funeral symphony: a slow processional opening movement, a bleak Andante, and a scherzo whose trio is almost unbearably tender. The second part turns inward, with a fourth movement built from a small piece written years before for the composer's wife, and a finale that gathers everything and ends not in triumph but on a long, quiet major chord after the strings have thinned to almost nothing.","Suk was Dvořák's favourite pupil, second violin of the Bohemian Quartet, and married to Dvořák's daughter Otilie. Dvořák died in May 1904 and Suk began a symphony in his memory. While he was writing the fourth movement, in July 1905, Otilie died at twenty-seven. He stopped, restarted, and made the second part about her. He was thirty-one, and his music never went back. Prague heard the work in February 1907.","Asrael is the angel who separates the soul from the body in Jewish and Islamic tradition, and his motif — a bare descending figure heard in the first bars — runs through all five movements. Suk also draws on music from his own incidental score Radúz and Mahulena, and the fourth movement grows from a short piece written for Otilie long before her death. The final chord is held so long it seems to be waiting for something.","The Czech Philharmonic has treated Asrael as a sacred object ever since Talich. Abroad it took until the 1980s, and the advocacy of Kubelík, Pešek and later Mackerras, for it to be heard as one of the great symphonies of the years before the First World War. Critics who want it shorter miss that the length is the subject. Recordings now appear regularly, though live performances remain uncommon outside Prague.","Fifty-five minutes with no obvious tune to hold on to, so listen for the falling figure in the first minute and follow it. The scherzo's trio is the easiest way in. Then hear Dvořák's Seventh, the world Suk grew out of, and Mahler's Ninth, the world he arrived at. Talich, Kubelík and Bělohlávek all recorded it with the Czech Philharmonic."],"novak-in-the-tatra-mountains":["A single-movement symphonic poem of about sixteen minutes. It opens in near-silence, low strings and distant horn calls suggesting altitude and empty space, builds a long crescendo into a mountain storm with full brass and percussion, and then subsides into a calm, radiant coda as the weather clears. There is no story beyond the landscape; the shape is the shape of a day in high country, and the scoring keeps finding cold, thin colours high in the woodwind.","Vítězslav Novák studied with Dvořák and then found himself by walking: summers spent collecting song in Moravia, Slovakia and the High Tatras gave him a music of weather and altitude rather than legend. He wrote this piece in 1902, aged thirty-two, at the moment Czech music was choosing between Dvořák's lyricism and the orchestral extravagance of Richard Strauss. He took the second road, and the piece made his name.","Novák avoids quoting actual Slovak folk melody here, though his notebooks were full of it; instead he builds his themes out of its intervals, so the mountains sound inhabited without anyone singing. The storm is raised over a single held bass note that lasts several minutes before it moves. He became the most influential Czech teacher of his generation, and this piece's atmospheric writing is audible in what his pupils wrote.","It was played widely in Bohemia before 1914, when Novák was spoken of alongside Suk as Dvořák's true heir. Two world wars, then the communist period's preference for accessible music, then the postwar avant-garde's preference for anything else, left him stranded between categories. Recordings of his orchestral music have appeared steadily since the 1990s, and the reassessment is still in progress.","Sixteen minutes in one arc: listen without pausing and let the crescendo do its work. The moment to wait for is the return of quiet after the storm, which is scored quite differently from the quiet at the start. Compare it with Smetana's From Bohemia's Woods and Fields, and with Sibelius's Second Symphony, written the same year at the other end of Europe."],"moniuszko-halka":["Four acts, about two and a quarter hours. Halka, a peasant girl, has been seduced and abandoned by the nobleman Janusz, who is marrying a woman of his own class; Jontek, a highlander who loves her, watches her lose her reason and drown herself while the wedding party celebrates indoors. The score alternates drawing-room ensembles with mazurkas, polonaises and highlander dances, and gives Jontek the opera's best number, a lament about rustling fir trees.","Moniuszko wrote the first, two-act version in Vilnius, then a Russian provincial city, and it was heard there in concert in 1848; Warsaw's censors refused it, unwilling to have peasants and nobles set against each other onstage so soon after failed uprisings. He expanded it to four acts and added the highlander dances, and Warsaw finally staged it on New Year's Day 1858. It made him Poland's national composer overnight.","The class politics are in the music, not only the plot: the nobles sing polonaises and salon duets, Halka and Jontek sing in the modal, ornamented manner of village song, and the two idioms hardly ever mix. Halka's line grows less regular as her mind goes. Moniuszko had already published volume after volume of Songs for the Home, teaching the same plain national manner to amateurs around Polish kitchen tables.","Halka became a fixture in Poland and remained almost unknown outside it, partly a language problem and partly because the nineteenth century's opera map ran through Paris and Milan. Critics abroad who do encounter it usually note how much better the folk-derived music is than the salon music. Recent stagings, including one realised in Haiti with a Polish-descended community, have pressed hard on the colonial and class readings the plot invites.","Go straight to Jontek's fourth-act aria, the one about the fir trees rustling, and then to the highlander dances. After that start at the beginning and hear how differently the nobles are made to sing. A Polish cast is essential. Compare it with Smetana's Bartered Bride, written eight years later on the other side of the same idea."],"wieniawski-violin-concerto-no-2":["Three movements in about twenty-two minutes, played without a break. A long orchestral introduction sets up the soloist's entry high on the top string; the first movement is stormy and thick with double stops and rapid octaves. The middle movement, a Romance in B flat, is a single long-breathed melody over muted strings and horn. Without pausing, the finale turns into an Alla zingara, all snapped rhythms, harmonics and imitation gypsy-band swagger.","Wieniawski was one of the two or three greatest violinists of the century, a prodigy admitted to the Paris Conservatoire at eight. By 1862 he was solo violinist to the Tsar and the first professor of violin at the new St Petersburg Conservatory, working alongside Anton Rubinstein. He wrote the concerto for himself and dedicated it to Sarasate. He died at forty-five, worn out by touring, in a Moscow hospital bed.","The technical fireworks are placed to hide the joins: each movement runs into the next through a bridge that is also a display passage, so the concerto never actually stops. The high right-elbow bow grip Wieniawski taught in St Petersburg, later called the Russian bow hold, is what makes the finale's bouncing bow-strokes practical; violinists who use it find the piece easier than it looks from the page.","The Second has entirely eclipsed the First, and its Romance is among the most recorded pieces in the violin repertory. Critics dismissive of virtuoso concertos have called it a display vehicle with a good tune; players answer that the cantabile writing is genuinely fine, and note that Tchaikovsky was in St Petersburg while it was new. The five-yearly Wieniawski Competition in Poznań has kept the rest of the output alive.","Start with the Romance and let it play out whole, then hear the concerto in order to feel how the finale releases what the first movement builds. Compare it with Tchaikovsky's Violin Concerto of 1878 and with Paganini's First. Older recordings by Heifetz, Milstein and Perlman take the finale faster than seems possible; recent ones dig further into the tone."],"paderewski-piano-concerto":["Three movements, about thirty minutes, for a large Romantic orchestra and a soloist who is busy from the second bar. The first movement is restless and minor-key with an unusually long development; the second is a Romanza, a slow song in which the piano decorates rather than declaims; the finale is a krakowiak, the syncopated duple-time dance of the Kraków region, which gallops to a bravura close. The piano writing runs on thick chords and wide leaps.","Paderewski wrote it in 1888, aged twenty-eight, just before the Paris and London debuts that would make him the most famous pianist alive. He had studied with Theodor Leschetizky in Vienna, who told him he had begun far too late to become a virtuoso. He went on to be the most celebrated musician of his generation, Poland's prime minister in 1919, and a fundraiser for Polish relief who died in New York in 1941.","The concerto is dedicated to Annette Essipoff, Leschetizky's wife and one of the great pianists of the age, who took it up early. The krakowiak finale is the tell: Paderewski's Polishness is national dance rather than collected folksong, the Chopin inheritance carried into the concert hall. Listen for how often the orchestra is reduced to accompaniment — this is a pianist's concerto, written by a man who knew exactly what he could do.","It sold, it was played, and then Paderewski the politician and the celebrity swallowed Paderewski the composer. For most of the twentieth century his music was written off as salon confection on the strength of the Minuet in G alone. Since the 1990s pianists have recorded the concerto, the Polish Fantasy and the enormous B minor symphony, and the verdict now is that he was a real composer given far too little time.","Begin with the finale if you want to know quickly whether the piece is for you; the krakowiak rhythm is unmistakable. Then hear the Romanza. Compare it with Grieg's and Chopin's concertos, both audible ancestors. Piers Lane's recording gives it the sweep it needs without turning every climax into thunder."],"szymanowski-king-roger":["Three acts, about ninety minutes, one for each of three worlds: a Byzantine cathedral, a courtyard with a Persian tinge, and an ancient theatre at dawn. King Roger of Sicily is asked to judge a young Shepherd who preaches a religion of ecstasy; his queen Roxana is drawn to the stranger, and then Roger is too. The first act opens with massed chorus and organ, the second glitters and clatters, and the third ends with Roger alone, singing a hymn to the rising sun.","Szymanowski travelled to Sicily and North Africa before 1914 and came back obsessed with the twelfth-century Norman kingdom where Latin, Greek and Arab cultures met. He wrote the libretto with his cousin Jarosław Iwaszkiewicz and then rewrote the ending himself. The war and the revolution destroyed his family's estate in Ukraine; he finished the opera in a newly independent Poland, and Warsaw staged it in June 1926.","The Shepherd is Dionysus and Roger is Pentheus: Euripides' Bacchae lies under the Sicilian story. Szymanowski's original ending had Roger follow the Shepherd; he changed it so that Roger stays and greets the sun alone, turning a story of surrender into one of self-possession. Each act keeps its own harmonic world — modal, then chromatic and orientalised, then a bright diatonic C that arrives like daylight.","Poland treated it as a national monument, but the outside world took eighty years to catch up: Britain did not stage it until the 1970s, the Royal Opera not until 2015, and the Metropolitan Opera still has not. Its standing rose with the general reassessment of Szymanowski in the 1990s. Directors disagree fundamentally about whether the ending is liberation or defeat, and many read Roger's crisis as a coded account of the composer's own sexuality.","Read a synopsis, then listen to the second act first: Roxana's wordless song over swaying strings is the most seductive thing Szymanowski wrote. Then start at the opening chorus and play the opera through. Compare it with Parsifal, which it argues with directly. Simon Rattle's recording and Mariusz Treliński's stagings are the standard modern points of reference."],"szymanowski-violin-concerto-no-1":["One continuous movement of about twenty-five minutes with no traditional first-movement form anywhere in it. The soloist plays almost throughout, mostly high, in long ecstatic lines over an orchestra of unusual make-up — celesta, piano, two harps, glockenspiel, a large percussion section — that shimmers rather than accompanies. A single cadenza, written by the violinist Paweł Kochański, sits near the end, and the work closes very high and very quietly.","Szymanowski wrote it in 1916 at Tymoszówka, his family's estate in Ukraine, during the war, having recently absorbed Debussy, Scriabin and Stravinsky and travelled through Sicily and North Africa. Kochański, his close friend, worked out much of the violin writing with him. The revolution destroyed the estate two years later. The concerto had to wait until November 1922 for its first performance, in Warsaw, in a country that had meanwhile come into existence.","The starting point was Tadeusz Miciński's poem 'May Night', which describes insects, birds and lovers in a heated spring darkness; Szymanowski copied lines from it into the score. Kochański devised the fingerings and the high harmonics that give the piece its glassy upper register, and the cadenza is entirely his work. There is no key signature for most of the piece, yet it is not atonal — it hovers rather than wanders.","It was among the first violin concertos to abandon three movements and the contest between soloist and orchestra altogether, and for that reason took decades to be programmed regularly. Players who make it sound like Romantic display miss it; the tone needs to be silvery rather than large. Since the 1990s, with recordings by Zehetmair, Mordkovitch and Frank Peter Zimmermann, it has become a repertory piece.","Listen in the dark, once, without trying to follow a structure — this is a piece to be surrounded by rather than tracked. Then listen again for the moment the orchestra thins to celesta and harp. Compare it with Debussy's Prélude à l'après-midi and with Scriabin. Thomas Zehetmair's recording under Rattle is a good first choice."],"erkel-bank-ban":["Three acts, about two and a half hours. In thirteenth-century Hungary the viceroy Bánk returns to court to find that the foreign queen's brother has dishonoured his wife Melinda and that the nobles are plotting; he kills the queen, and Melinda drowns herself with their child. The score fuses Italian vocal melody with verbunkos, the dotted recruiting-dance idiom of Hungarian bands, and puts a cimbalom in the pit. Bánk's second-act aria about his homeland is the most famous tenor solo in Hungarian.","József Katona's play of 1815 was banned for decades because it shows Hungarians killing a foreign queen. Erkel, conductor of the National Theatre and composer of the music for the Hungarian national anthem, worked on the opera through the 1850s, in the repression that followed the crushed revolution of 1848, and could stage it only in March 1861, when Habsburg censorship briefly relaxed. The audience understood exactly what it was watching.","The two musical languages are assigned by allegiance: the Hungarian characters sing in verbunkos rhythms with the cimbalom behind them, while the queen's imported court sings Italianate coloratura. Melinda's mad scene borrows the shape of a bel canto scena and fills it with Hungarian melody, so the heroine loses her mind in two styles at once. The cimbalom, a folk instrument, is sitting in an opera orchestra here among the first times it ever did.","It became Hungary's second national opera after Erkel's own Hunyadi László and is still performed there every year, often on national holidays; outside Hungary it is nearly unknown. The 1861 score was heavily revised in 1940 by Kálmán Nádasdy and his collaborators, and that version was standard for decades. A return to Erkel's original has been under way since the 1990s and remains contested by singers and by audiences who grew up with the revision.","Find the tenor aria about the homeland first — three minutes, and every Hungarian knows it. Then listen to the nobles' chorus in the first act for the verbunkos swing. Read a synopsis, because the plot is dense with medieval politics. Hungarian State Opera recordings and the 2002 film version are the practical options, and the language, stressed on first syllables, drives every rhythm in the score."],"hubay-hejre-kati":["Six minutes for violin and piano, also heard with orchestra. It follows the two-part shape of the csárdás: a slow, heavily ornamented lassú in which the violin sighs and slides in imitation of a Hungarian band's leader, then a sudden switch to a fast friss that accelerates through plucked notes, harmonics and rapid scales to a headlong finish. The title is the sort of shout of encouragement a band leader calls out to a girl named Kati.","Jenő Hubay studied with Joseph Joachim in Berlin and with Vieuxtemps in Paris before returning to Budapest, where he taught at the Academy of Music from 1886 and eventually ran it. Between the 1880s and the 1900s he published fourteen Scènes de la csárda, of which this is the fourth. Budapest then had famous Roma bands playing in every restaurant and coffee house, and the pieces are a conservatory violinist's transcription of what he heard there.","The slides, the bent notes and the deliberately uneven rhythm are not sloppiness but notation of a specific performance style, and violinists who play them cleanly kill the piece. What Hubay called Hungarian is really the urban Roma band repertoire — the same conflation Liszt made and Bartók spent a career untangling. Hubay's teaching mattered more than his composing: Szigeti, Vecsey, Székely and Zathureczky all came out of his class.","Hejre Kati was a staple of the early recording era and of every violinist's encore list, then fell out of fashion with the salon repertoire in general and now returns mainly as a display piece. Musicologists use it as evidence in the long argument about what Hungarian music meant before Bartók and Kodály's fieldwork — an argument in which Hubay's whole aesthetic turned out to be the losing side.","Six minutes: hear the slow section's first phrase and notice how far the violinist bends the pitch, then wait for the switch into the fast half. Compare it with Brahms's Hungarian Dance No. 5 and Liszt's Second Hungarian Rhapsody, which come from the same source, and then with Bartók's Romanian Folk Dances to hear the correction being made."],"dohnanyi-variations-nursery-song":["Piano and large orchestra, about twenty-five minutes. It opens with several minutes of the grandest, most portentous orchestral introduction imaginable — thunderous brass, tolling chords, a whole Romantic tragedy promised — and then the piano enters alone, in single notes, with the tune English speakers know as 'Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star'. Eleven variations follow, parodying a waltz, a chorale, a march and a Viennese classical minuet in turn, before a fugue and an unashamedly grandiose finale.","Ernő Dohnányi was Hungary's most celebrated pianist after Liszt, a contemporary of Bartók at the Budapest Academy who chose the conservative road and became the country's dominant musical administrator. He wrote the variations in Berlin and gave the first performance there in February 1914, months before the war. The dedication reads, roughly, for the enjoyment of people with a sense of humour and the annoyance of everybody else.","The joke is structural. The introduction imitates the language of Wagner and Strauss so convincingly that the tune's arrival is genuinely shocking, and each variation afterwards impersonates a composer or a genre without ever naming one. The nursery tune is French in origin, 'Ah! vous dirai-je, maman', and Mozart had already written variations on it, which Dohnányi's audience knew perfectly well. The closing fugue takes the whole thing entirely seriously.","It was a hit at once and has never left the repertory, which irritated Dohnányi, whose serious works — two symphonies, a large body of chamber music, an opera — went unplayed. His reputation collapsed after 1945 over accusations about his conduct in wartime Hungary; he emigrated to the United States and taught in Florida until his death. Those charges have since been examined and largely rejected, and the music is being recorded again.","Do not read anything about it beforehand if you can help it, because the introduction only works once. Then listen again for the disguises: the waltz, the chorale, the march. Compare it with Brahms's Academic Festival Overture for the same trick of high style applied to low material. Howard Shelley's recording plays the opening completely straight, which is the point."],"grieg-peer-gynt":["Grieg wrote roughly ninety minutes of music for Ibsen's sprawling verse drama: twenty-six numbers of preludes, dances, choruses and songs threaded through five acts. The score runs from the pastoral flute tune that opens Act Four to the goblin stampede of the mountain king's hall, where a plodding bassoon theme accelerates until the orchestra seems to fall over itself. A soprano sings Solveig's waiting song twice, essentially unchanged, at opposite ends of Peer's wasted life.","Ibsen wrote to Grieg from Dresden in January 1874 asking for the music; Grieg, then thirty, accepted and then spent two years complaining that the play resisted setting. Christiania's theatre wanted spectacle, and the production of February 1876 supplied it, with painted trolls and a shipwreck. Norway had no independent state and no orchestral tradition of its own; Grieg was already the country's musical figurehead, and the commission made the association permanent.","Solveig's Song puts a lilting Norwegian dance rhythm underneath a melody in the minor, so the tune consoles and mourns at once. Listen also in the mountain king's hall for the trolls' theme given to muted low strings creeping upward: Grieg said the music reeked so of cow-pats and Norwegian provincialism that he could hardly bear to hear it. Much of the exotic Act Four music is invented orientalism, not borrowed material.","Grieg thought parts of the score dreadful and said so in letters, yet audiences disagreed, and the two concert suites he assembled in 1888 and 1891 made him internationally famous while detaching the music from Ibsen's savage satire. Recent decades have brought recordings of the complete theatre score with the spoken text, which restores the irony the suites sand away and shows how much music is never heard in the concert hall.","Hear the full score once, with narration, before returning to the suites: Peer's swindling and self-deception make the tenderness of Solveig's music land differently. Notice how little the famous Morning Mood has to do with Norway, since it accompanies a sunrise in Morocco. For a sense of the theatre, seek a Norwegian recording that keeps the original text."],"grieg-lyric-pieces":["Sixty-six short piano pieces published in ten books across thirty-four years, most of them two or three minutes long: waltzes, nocturnes, marches of dwarfs and trolls, folk dances, a butterfly, a bird, a brook. Grieg keeps to simple ternary shapes and rarely asks for large hands, but he builds harmonies from open fifths, drone basses and sudden chromatic sideslips that turn a parlour tune strange. The last book closes with a waltz called Remembrances.","Grieg's publisher Peters in Leipzig wanted saleable domestic music, and the Lyric Pieces were the backbone of his income for four decades. He wrote many of them at Troldhaugen, the villa above Bergen he built in 1885, working in a small hut by the fjord. The upright piano was the entertainment technology of the age; a Grieg album on the music desk was as ordinary across Europe and America as a gramophone would later be.","The set is a private diary in disguise. The Arietta that opens Book One returns in Book Ten, transposed and dressed as a waltz under the title Remembrances, so the whole thirty-four-year sequence closes a circle. Several pieces imitate the Hardanger fiddle: listen for ringing open strings and a slightly sour second in the springar and halling numbers, and for the bell-like repeated notes of Klokkeklang, which is built almost entirely from fifths.","Debussy sneered that Grieg's music tasted of pink bonbons stuffed with snow, and the pieces suffered for decades from their association with the parlour and the piano lesson. Ravel and Delius knew better and said so. Modern scholarship treats the harmonic experiments of the late books as a real influence on French music, and complete recordings have made the case that the set repays being heard whole rather than in famous excerpts.","Do not try all sixty-six at once. Start with Book Five, Op. 54, where Notturno and March of the Dwarfs sit side by side, then hear Klokkeklang from the same book for the strangest sound Grieg ever made. Emil Gilels's selection is the classic recording; for the complete set, seek a Norwegian pianist who lets the dance rhythms limp."],"grieg-haugtussa":["Eight songs for voice and piano, about twenty-five minutes, on poems from Arne Garborg's verse novel about Veslemøy, a poor herd-girl on the west-Norwegian moors who sees the hidden folk. The cycle moves from an opening summons through a blueberry-picking scene and a skipping goat dance to betrayal and a long final song beside a brook, where the piano's rippling right hand runs almost unbroken while the singer talks herself into calm.","Garborg published Haugtussa in 1895 in Landsmål, the New Norwegian assembled from rural dialects rather than the Danish-derived written language of the towns. Grieg read it at once, called it a book of unparalleled musical possibilities, and sketched far more settings than he kept. He was in his early fifties, chronically ill and increasingly political: choosing Landsmål declared which Norway he belonged to, ten years before the union with Sweden was dissolved.","Veslemøy is second-sighted, and Grieg keeps the supernatural almost entirely out of the vocal line and puts it in the piano. In the goat dance the skipping is a limping folk rhythm that the voice never quite lands on. The brook in the last song never resolves its accompaniment figure; it simply thins out and stops, as though the water carried on after the girl had stopped listening.","Grieg thought these his best songs and said so repeatedly; the wider world took a century to agree, partly because the dialect text defeated foreign singers, who long sang German or English versions that flattened it. Kirsten Flagstad's advocacy and later recordings by Norwegian sopranos and mezzos rebuilt the case. Scholars now treat the cycle as his most sustained large-scale design apart from the concerto.","Read a translation of the poems first, since the story only works if you know Veslemøy is jilted. Then listen for the seam where love collapses into the goat dance. Seek a Norwegian singer who keeps the dialect consonants intact, and compare a mezzo with a soprano: the cycle sounds like a different story in each voice."],"sibelius-kullervo":["Five movements, about seventy-five minutes, for orchestra with a male chorus and two soloists who appear only in the third. That central movement is the largest: the chorus narrates in a chanting Kalevala metre while Kullervo seduces a girl during a sleigh ride and both discover she is his sister. The outer movements are purely orchestral, from a grinding opening in the low strings to a war march built on a repeated brass figure and a bleak death scene.","Sibelius was twenty-six, back from study in Berlin and Vienna, engaged to Aino Järnefelt and unknown outside Helsinki. Finland was a Grand Duchy of the Russian Empire, and the Kalevala, the runic epic Elias Lönnrot assembled from oral poetry in the 1830s, had become the country's founding document. Sibelius had gone to hear the runic singer Larin Paraske recite. The premiere in April 1892 made him a national figure overnight.","The chorus does not sing melodies so much as recite: Sibelius sets the Kalevala's five-beat trochaic line close to how it would have been chanted, with the accent falling awkwardly against the bar. Listen for the moment the sister realises who has seduced her, where the orchestra simply stops and a single line of chanting continues over nothing. The war march's brass idea is a rhythm rather than a tune, which is how much of the later Sibelius works.","After a handful of performances Sibelius withdrew the score and kept it locked away for the rest of his life, releasing only one movement once. It was not heard complete again until 1958, a year after his death, and entered the repertoire only in the 1970s. Some hear an unformed sprawl; others argue it is the most Finnish thing he wrote, and that the mature symphonies bought their concentration at a price.","Come to it after the Second Symphony rather than before, and let the length be part of the point. The third movement is the reason to listen: follow a text translation line by line. Look for a recording with a genuine Finnish male choir, since the language's long vowels and doubled consonants are half the sound, and for a conductor who takes the war march at a walking pace."],"sibelius-symphony-no-1":["Four movements, roughly thirty-eight minutes. It opens with something no symphony had done before: a solo clarinet, alone over a quiet timpani roll, playing a long melancholy line for over a minute before the strings tear in. The scherzo is hammered out on timpani and pizzicato strings. The finale is headed Quasi una fantasia, brings the clarinet melody back on massed strings as a broad tune, and then ends with two abrupt plucked chords instead of a peroration.","Sibelius finished it in the spring of 1899 and conducted the premiere in Helsinki that April, in the year Tsar Nicholas II's February Manifesto began stripping Finland of its autonomy. The same season produced the patriotic tableau music that became Finlandia. He revised the symphony in 1900 before the Helsinki Philharmonic took it on tour as far as Paris. Tchaikovsky's Pathétique, which he admired almost to embarrassment, was seven years old.","The clarinet's opening melody is not really an introduction: it never returns in the first movement at all, and reappears only at the start of the finale, which makes the symphony a frame rather than an arch. Listen too for the harp, used sparingly and pointedly here and almost abandoned in the later symphonies. The two closing plucked chords are marked to be dry and unemphatic, and conductors who linger on them spoil the joke.","Critics then and since have heard Tchaikovsky in it, and Sibelius was defensive about the charge for years. The rehabilitation argues that the Russian surface hides a very different method, one in which themes grow out of fragments rather than being stated and then developed. Conductors still disagree about the finale's big string tune, whether to swell it as late-Romantic melody or hold it in reserve, and about what the 1900 revision gained.","Listen to the first ninety seconds twice: one clarinet, one drum and nothing else is the whole of Sibelius in embryo. Then jump to the finale and hear the same tune arrive in a completely different world. Compare a heart-on-sleeve Russian-style reading with a cooler Finnish one; the difference is instructive, and the ending will catch you out either way."],"sibelius-symphony-no-3":["Three movements, about thirty minutes, scored for a classical-sized orchestra without harp or extra brass. The first movement starts with a walking figure low in the cellos and basses, plain as a folk tune, and stays brisk and clear throughout. The middle movement is a slow, slightly limping dance that turns a small phrase over for ten minutes without settling. The finale begins as a scherzo made of fragments and gradually becomes a hymn-like march that simply arrives.","Sibelius completed it in 1907 and conducted the premiere in Helsinki that September, having promised it to London two years earlier and missed the date. He had left the city in 1904 for Ainola, the house at Järvenpää; he was drinking heavily and deep in debt. The dedication went to Granville Bantock, who had championed him in England. Later that year Mahler visited Helsinki, and the two argued about what a symphony should contain.","The famous exchange with Mahler, Sibelius wanting severity of style and profound logic and Mahler answering that the symphony must be like the world and contain everything, happened while this score was fresh, and the Third is that argument in sound. The finale's trick is that nobody can say where the scherzo ends and the hymn begins: the same fragments merely speed up, thicken and coalesce until a tune is unmistakably there.","The Third disappointed listeners who wanted another Second, and it remains the least played of the seven. Its defenders call it the hinge of the cycle, the point at which Sibelius stopped writing nationalist tone poetry and began building symphonies out of process. Conductors argue about the finale's tempo, since taking the coalescence too fast makes the closing march sound abrupt rather than inevitable.","Give the finale your full attention and try to mark the exact moment the fidgeting turns into a hymn. You will fail, and that is the point. Follow it with the Second Symphony's finale to hear how much rhetoric has been stripped away in five years. Both British Sibelians and recent Finnish conductors make persuasive and quite different cases."],"sibelius-symphony-no-4":["Four movements, about thirty-five minutes, and the darkest orchestral music of its decade. It opens with cellos, basses, bassoons and contrabassoon growling a four-note figure, after which a solo cello sings alone. There is no big tune anywhere. The scherzo breaks off mid-gesture; the slow movement builds a long melody three times and abandons it each time; the finale rings tuned percussion against a string orchestra that will not agree with it, then ends on bare chords without ceremony.","In 1908 surgeons in Berlin removed a tumour from Sibelius's throat after more than a dozen operations, and for years he lived under a death sentence, forbidden alcohol and cigars. He wrote the Fourth at Ainola across 1910 and 1911 and conducted the premiere in Helsinki that April, on a programme that also offered lighter fare. Schoenberg's freely atonal pieces and Stravinsky's Petrushka come from the same two years.","The interval that governs the whole symphony is the tritone, the gap between C and F sharp that medieval theorists called the devil in music. It is in the opening four notes, in the harmony that refuses to settle, and in the finale's quarrel. Listen for the finale's tuned percussion: Sibelius wrote for glockenspiel, but conductors have long substituted tubular bells, which turns the ending from glinting to funereal.","The premiere baffled the Helsinki audience, and in New York two years later listeners hissed. Sibelius called it a protest against the compositions of today and refused to soften it. It is now the symphony that critics who dislike Sibelius are most likely to admire, and the one performers approach most carefully. The bells-versus-glockenspiel choice is still argued in print, as is how much vibrato the closing chords should carry.","Do not expect a tune to hold on to; hold on to the four opening notes instead, and count how often they return disguised. Start with the slow movement if the whole seems forbidding. Then listen to the last thirty seconds and notice that the symphony does not end so much as stop being played. A spare, unsentimental recording serves it better than a plush one."],"sibelius-symphony-no-6":["Four movements, under thirty minutes, opening with strings alone in slow polyphony that could almost be sixteenth-century church music until a flute and a harp change the light. Nothing is loud for long. The second movement is fragmentary and pastoral, the third a fast scherzo of barely two minutes, and the finale a chorale that grows, reaches a single climax, and then recedes for several minutes until the strings are left murmuring and simply cease.","Sibelius worked on it alongside the Fifth and Seventh through the war years, Finland's independence and its civil war, finishing in 1923 and conducting the Helsinki premiere that February. He was fifty-seven, world-famous and drinking again after years of abstinence. Two rival ideas of modern music were now unavoidable, the Mahlerian and the neoclassical pieces coming out of Paris. He wanted, as he put it, to offer pure cold water rather than cocktails.","The key signature says D minor, but the music mostly moves in the Dorian mode, the old church scale with a raised sixth, which is why it sounds neither major nor minor but lit from outside. Two harps function almost as a continuo. Listen for the flute's first entry: it is the earliest sound that does not come from the string choir, and it changes the temperature instantly.","It was received politely and then ignored, since audiences wanting another Fifth found nothing to applaud. Sibelius said it always reminded him of the scent of the first snow. Later critics, Tovey among them and after him a generation of British Sibelians, argued that it is the subtlest of the seven. Conductors struggle with the finale's long fade, which can sound either like a decision or like a battery running down.","Listen to the first two minutes as if they were a Renaissance motet, then wait for the harp. This is a symphony for when you want no argument made at you. Follow it immediately with the Seventh, written a year later, to hear similar materials fused into a single movement. A cool, clear recording serves it better than a grand one."],"sibelius-symphony-no-7":["One continuous movement of about twenty-two minutes that contains, compressed, an adagio, a scherzo and a finale without ever stopping. It begins with a rising scale on the strings over a soft timpani stroke. Three times a solo trombone rises out of the orchestral texture with a broad, grave theme, each time in a different context and tempo; the third arrival, over churning strings, is the climax. The last minute settles onto a single held C major chord.","Sibelius conducted the premiere in Stockholm in March 1924, announcing the piece as Fantasia sinfonica No. 1; only when he published it the following year did he call it a symphony. He was fifty-eight and had five years of major work left. Finland was six years independent, and Europe was full of manifestos about what music should now be, none of which he answered. Tapiola and the music for The Tempest followed, then three decades of silence.","The whole work grows from the scale that opens it: the same stepwise rise, slowed down, becomes the trombone theme, and sped up becomes the scherzo material. Sibelius alters tempo continuously rather than in blocks, so the joins between what would elsewhere be separate movements are invisible, and the piece behaves as one long accelerating and decelerating line. The final chord is preceded by an ache that resolves only at the last instant.","It was slow to be understood, partly because listeners kept hunting for the four movements. Analysts have argued for decades about how many sections it contains and where they begin, which is itself the strongest evidence for how thoroughly the seams are hidden. It is now widely treated as the summit of the cycle and as one of the century's decisive rethinkings of symphonic form, alongside Schoenberg's single-movement experiments.","Listen for the trombone three times and use it as your map; everything else can wash past on a first hearing. Then play the piece again and try to feel where the tempo changes rather than counting movements. Pair it with the Fourth to hear the two extremes of late Sibelius. The interesting difference between recordings is whether the conductor makes the transitions audible or seamless."],"sibelius-tapiola":["A single eighteen-minute tone poem for large orchestra built almost entirely from one short string figure heard in the opening bars. Sibelius turns it into wind chorales, shivering tremolos and low brass, and at the centre into a passage where the strings whirr in cold patterns like wind rising through trees. A genuine roar breaks near the end, then subsides into a few bare wind chords and a quiet major cadence that sounds less like resolution than exhalation.","Walter Damrosch commissioned it for the New York Symphony Society, which gave the premiere in December 1926. Sibelius was sixty-one, and it proved to be his last major work; he spent the following three decades at Ainola and probably burned the manuscript of an Eighth Symphony in the 1940s. He prefaced the score with a four-line verse about Tapio's forest, where dark dreams stir among the trees: the northern woods of the Kalevala, not a picturesque landscape.","Nearly every bar derives from the opening five-note string idea, so the piece is one continuously mutating cell rather than a sequence of themes. Listen to the harmony at the height of the storm, where Sibelius stacks the orchestra into a texture with no clear chord at all, closer to Ligeti's later clouds than to anything Romantic. The final cadence releases a tension that has been held unbroken for most of the piece.","Early listeners found it monotonous, and even sympathetic critics called it more atmosphere than music. Later composers disagreed, and it is regularly cited as an ancestor of the sustained textural writing that Ligeti and the Polish composers developed after 1960. The argument today is about tempo and weight: whether to let the piece move as narrative, or hold it nearly static so that the storm arrives as shock.","Listen in the dark, and listen for the five notes of the opening in everything that follows. When the whirring wind passage begins, stop trying to hear melody and hear texture instead. Then play Ligeti's Atmosphères and notice the family resemblance. Recordings that take the storm at a broad tempo make the piece frightening rather than merely blustery."],"nielsen-symphony-no-3-espansiva":["Four movements, about thirty-seven minutes. It opens with the whole orchestra hammering a single chord nine times in accelerating rhythm before a great swinging theme takes hold. The second movement is the surprise: over slow strings, a soprano and then a baritone sing without words, floating vowels above a pastoral scene. A brisk third movement follows, and the finale is a broad, plain, confident tune that the strings state and the orchestra simply enlarges.","Nielsen conducted the premiere in Copenhagen in February 1911, on a programme that also introduced his Violin Concerto, an extraordinary evening for a forty-five-year-old who had begun as a house-painter's son from Funen and worked up through the Royal Chapel's second violins. The title means expansive, which he glossed as the outward growth of the mind's scope. Denmark was prosperous and at peace, and the symphony is not embarrassed about it.","The wordless voices can be replaced by clarinet and trombone, and Nielsen says so in the score, a practical concession that also reveals how he heard them, as instruments rather than characters. Listen to the opening hammer blows: the chord stays the same while the rhythm accelerates, so the momentum is purely metrical. His habit of shifting the tonal centre in mid-phrase, which makes the later symphonies so unstable, is already everywhere here.","The Espansiva was Nielsen's international breakthrough, taken up in Amsterdam and Germany before 1914, and it remains the most immediately likeable of the six. Some find the finale's plainness complacent beside the violence of the Fourth and Fifth; others hear the plainness as the point, a deliberate refusal of tragedy. Performance debate centres on whether to use the two singers or the substitute instruments in the slow movement.","Start at the second movement and let the voices arrive without warning; it is one of the few moments in the symphonic repertoire that makes people ask what just happened. Then go back for the nine hammer blows at the opening. Compare a Danish orchestra with a large international one, since the Danes tend to keep the finale walking rather than marching."],"nielsen-wind-quintet":["Flute, oboe, clarinet, horn and bassoon, about twenty-five minutes in three movements. The first is a genial sonata-form conversation, the second a minuet whose trio the oboe leads, and the finale a set of eleven variations on a hymn tune in which a different instrument steps forward each time with its own character. The oboist swaps to cor anglais for one variation. The writing is deliberately awkward in places, as though each player were caught being themselves.","Nielsen wrote it for the Copenhagen Wind Quintet after hearing them rehearse down a telephone line: the story goes that he called a friend, heard the five players in the background, and was struck by their individual voices. He knew them all personally and wrote to their temperaments. It was played privately in the spring of 1922 and publicly in Copenhagen that October. He planned a concerto for each of the five; only two were finished.","The finale's theme is Nielsen's own hymn tune, written for the Danish popular-song movement he was deeply involved in, so a sophisticated chamber work is built on a melody meant for people who could not read music. Listen for the horn's variation, written like a hunting call heard from another room, and for the moment the cor anglais enters and changes the colour of the whole set.","It is the piece that persuaded composers a wind quintet could sustain a serious argument rather than provide light relief, and it is now the cornerstone of the repertoire. Critics have sometimes found the variations uneven; players answer that unevenness is the design, since each variation is a portrait of a colleague. The two surviving concertos, for flute and clarinet, grew directly out of it and are usually programmed alongside.","Listen to the finale first and try to hear which player is being characterised in each variation. Then start again from the beginning and notice how rarely all five instruments play the same kind of music at once. Any good ensemble will serve, but Danish groups tend to bring out the humour: the piece is funnier than its reputation."],"nielsen-clarinet-concerto":["One continuous movement of about twenty-five minutes, falling into four linked sections, for solo clarinet and a lean orchestra of two bassoons, two horns, strings and a snare drum that behaves like a second soloist. The clarinet is pushed to the top and bottom of its range and made to snarl. The drum interrupts, mocks and eventually duels with it. Two cadenzas break the argument open, and the ending is sudden, quiet and unresolved rather than triumphant.","Nielsen wrote it in 1928 for Aage Oxenvad of the Copenhagen Wind Quintet, a player famous for a rough, unvarnished tone and an abrupt temper. Nielsen said the clarinet could be both warm-hearted and wild, and Oxenvad reportedly grumbled that Nielsen must play the instrument himself, since he had found all its worst notes. Nielsen had suffered a heart attack in 1926 and would die three years after finishing the concerto.","There are two tonal centres a semitone apart, and the concerto never chooses between them, so the argument that sounds temperamental is actually structural. The snare drum is not colour: it is written into the form, entering at the seams and forcing the music to change direction. Listen to the second cadenza, where the drum refuses to let the soloist finish, and to how little the orchestra ever supports the clarinet.","It baffled its first audiences and remains the hardest of Nielsen's works to love, yet players regard it as a summit of the clarinet repertoire and a piece by which soloists are measured. Debate concerns the drum: how loud, how aggressive, how far forward in the balance. Recent scholarship reads the concerto's instability as a portrait of a mind at odds with itself rather than a caricature of Oxenvad.","Follow the snare drum rather than the clarinet on a first hearing; it tells you where the piece is going. Then listen again for the two cadenzas and notice how differently the soloist behaves after each. Pair it with the Flute Concerto, written three years earlier and far more genial. Danish clarinettists tend to keep Oxenvad's rough edge, which suits the music."],"arvo-part-passio":["Seventy minutes of the St John Passion in Latin, sung without a break and almost without change of dynamic. Jesus is a bass, moving slowly and low; Pilate is a tenor, quicker and higher; the Evangelist is shared between four solo voices accompanied by violin, oboe, cello and bassoon; the crowd is the chorus with organ. There are no arias, no chorales and no commentary. The one real event is the final phrase, where the music opens into a major chord.","Pärt had left Soviet Estonia in 1980 and was living in West Berlin on a scholarship, an Orthodox convert writing Latin church music for a Western European audience. He had spent much of the 1970s in near-silence, studying plainchant and early polyphony after abandoning serialism. Passio was first heard in Munich in November 1982, and its recording two years later carried the tintinnabuli style far outside the concert hall.","The score is generated by rules rather than composed phrase by phrase: the pitch of each syllable, the length of each note and the choice of accompanying instrument all follow from the punctuation and grammar of the Latin. A comma, a question and the end of a sentence each have their own musical consequence. That is how the piece can run seventy minutes without repeating itself while sounding as though nothing changes.","Some hear an act of devotion of extraordinary discipline; others hear a system running for over an hour without dramatic incentive, and the objection is not foolish. Comparison with Bach is unavoidable and unfair, since Bach dramatises and Pärt refuses to. Performance practice has settled towards slow tempi, minimal vibrato and resonant church acoustics, though early recordings made under the composer's eye are brisker than most later ones.","Do not sample it. Give it the full seventy minutes, ideally in a resonant space or with the lights off, and let the flatness become the experience rather than an obstacle. Track the two main soloists by range: low is Christ, high is Pilate. The reward is the last thirty seconds, which only works if you have sat through everything before it."],"arvo-part-te-deum":["Thirty minutes on the Latin Te Deum for three choirs, women, men and mixed, with divided strings, a prepared piano and a recorded drone that the score calls a wind harp. The drone sounds almost throughout, a low continuous hum over which the music is built rather than against. The choirs alternate and overlap in slow, mostly stepwise lines, and the prepared piano adds a dry metallic ping at the ends of phrases. The dynamic range is narrow and the pulse barely moves.","West German Radio in Cologne commissioned it, and it was first performed there in January 1985. Pärt had been in the West for five years and was writing sacred Latin texts for broadcasters and concert societies rather than for churches, which was itself a measure of how the position of religious music had shifted. He revised the score twice in the following years, principally tightening the closing pages.","The drone is not electronic ambience but a recording of a wind harp, a stringed instrument sounded by moving air rather than by hands, and it fixes the harmonic centre of the entire piece before a note is sung. The prepared piano is closer to a bell than a keyboard. Listen at the end for the final sung phrase asking for mercy, set lower and quieter than anything before it.","It arrived at the height of the vogue for what critics were beginning to call holy minimalism and drew the same split as the rest of Pärt's output: profound simplicity to some, marketable stillness to others. Performance debate concerns the taped drone, which some conductors reduce almost to inaudibility while others bring it forward. Estonian choirs under Tõnu Kaljuste largely set the interpretive standard.","Locate the drone first, then notice that everything else is measured against it. The piece rewards patience and punishes sampling. Try it after Passio, which is starker, or after a Renaissance setting of the same hymn for the contrast in ambition. Choirs with a straight, vibrato-free tone serve it best, and a resonant recording helps more than a close one."],"berwald-symphonie-singuliere":["Three movements, under thirty minutes, for a Beethoven-sized orchestra. The first movement is fast, wiry and full of abrupt gear changes, with themes arriving from unexpected keys. The strangeness that gave the symphony its name sits in the middle: the broad slow movement is interrupted halfway through by a complete scherzo, after which the slow music resumes and finishes as though nothing had happened. The finale is brisk and terse, and ends almost before it has settled.","Berwald wrote four symphonies in three years in the early 1840s and had almost none of them played. The musical establishment in Stockholm had no use for him; he made his living running an orthopaedic clinic in Berlin and later managing a glassworks and a sawmill in northern Sweden. He composed at night and in the intervals of business. This symphony was finished in 1845 and put away in a drawer.","Nesting a scherzo inside a slow movement is not merely a novelty: it lets Berwald write a three-movement symphony containing four movements' worth of material, and the return of the slow music afterwards sounds like memory rather than repetition. Listen too to the first movement's transitions, where he does not modulate so much as cut, dropping into a remote key without preparation, a habit contemporaries mistook for incompetence.","The score waited sixty years for its first performance, in Stockholm in 1905, decades after Berwald's death. Nineteenth-century Sweden dismissed him as an eccentric amateur; the twentieth century, hearing the same music after Nielsen and Sibelius, found the abruptness deliberate and modern. He is now routinely called the most original Scandinavian composer before Grieg, on the evidence of four symphonies that almost nobody heard in his lifetime.","Go straight to the second movement and wait for the scherzo to burst in, since the joke only works if it surprises you. Then listen to the first movement for the sudden key shifts and notice how quickly Berwald abandons an idea once he has had it. Compare it with a Schumann symphony of the same decade to hear how much stranger it is."],"gade-echoes-of-ossian":["A concert overture of about thirteen minutes. It begins with harp and low strings suggesting a bard tuning up, then builds a long, dark, surging Allegro from a handful of modal-sounding themes, with horn calls threading through. There is no plot to follow: the piece evokes a legendary northern past of mist and heroic memory, and it ends by returning to the opening harp gesture, as though the tale had been told and the fire had gone out.","Gade was twenty-three and a violinist in the Royal Chapel in Copenhagen when he entered the overture for a competition run by the city's music society and won it. Ossian, the supposedly ancient Gaelic bard whose poems James Macpherson had in fact largely written himself in the 1760s, was a European craze that had already produced paintings, novels and Mendelssohn's Hebrides. Gade attached a motto about legend and heroic deeds drifting to us from far-off days.","The Nordic tone contemporaries heard in Gade is mostly a few technical habits: melodies that avoid the leading note, so they sound modal and old; horn writing in bare fifths; and drone basses. None of it is folk material. Listen for the harp, which frames the whole piece and stands for the bard's instrument, an idea Gade took from the pictures and poems rather than from any surviving music.","Winning the prize changed his life. Mendelssohn conducted Gade's First Symphony in Leipzig in 1843 and made him his assistant at the Gewandhaus, and Gade briefly led the orchestra after Mendelssohn's death. He returned to Copenhagen, founded its conservatory and taught both Grieg and Nielsen. Later critics found his mature music too German; the overture, written before he ever saw Leipzig, is where the Danish voice is clearest.","Listen for the harp at the start and at the end, and treat everything between as one long crescendo of atmosphere. Then play Mendelssohn's Hebrides Overture, written a decade earlier, and hear how directly Gade learned from it while making the light colder. Danish orchestras keep the tempo moving; slower readings turn the piece merely picturesque."],"svendsen-romance":["Nine minutes for solo violin and a modest orchestra. The violin states a long singing melody almost immediately, high on its top string, and the piece is essentially that tune, varied, decorated and returned to. A faster middle section gives the soloist some double-stopping and a brief climb, but there is no cadenza and no display for its own sake. The orchestra accompanies with warm, plain harmony and keeps the violin in the foreground throughout.","Svendsen was himself a violinist, trained in Leipzig, who had played and conducted across half of musical Europe before settling as a conductor in Christiania alongside Grieg. He wrote the Romance in 1881, near the end of the composing life he would largely abandon after moving to Copenhagen in 1883 to run the Royal Theatre. Short lyrical concert pieces for a star soloist were a staple of the period's mixed programmes.","The tune's shape is Norwegian without quoting anything: it circles a note, drops a fourth and rises again, in the manner of a folk song rather than an operatic aria. Notice how rarely the orchestra doubles the melody. Svendsen's scoring, which Grieg openly envied, keeps the accompaniment transparent so that the soloist never has to force, and the return of the main theme slips in underneath the strings rather than over them.","It became one of the most played short violin pieces in Scandinavia and is the one Svendsen work that never left the repertoire, which slightly distorts his reputation: he was a symphonist and a conductor of European standing whose two symphonies deserve more than they get. Grieg said plainly that Svendsen orchestrated better than he did. The Romance survives partly because it is short, grateful, and impossible to play badly unnoticed.","Nine minutes and one tune: listen for how the soloist re-enters after the middle section and what has changed. Compare two violinists, one who plays it as salon music and one who plays it as a folk song, since the whole difference lies in the rubato. Then try Svendsen's Second Symphony to hear what else he could do."],"sinding-rustle-of-spring":["Three minutes for solo piano. A rippling figure runs almost without pause from the first bar to the last, split between the hands so that the melody emerges from the middle of the texture while both hands keep the water moving. The tune itself is simple and rises in warm sequences, and the effect is of something heard through continuous motion. A louder middle passage thickens the chords before the opening returns and the piece evaporates upward.","Sinding published it in 1896 in a set of six pieces, and it earned him money for the rest of his life. This was the peak of the domestic piano market: a piece that sounded far harder than it was, fitted onto four pages and flattered an ambitious amateur could sell hundreds of thousands of copies. Sinding was then regarded in Germany as Grieg's natural successor, and wrote symphonies, concertos and hundreds of songs that nobody now plays.","The trick is in the thumbs: the melody notes are taken by alternating thumbs inside the running figure, so the tune seems to float in a register where no one is obviously playing it. The German title, Frühlingsrauschen, is closer to the point than the English, since Rauschen means the rushing of water as much as the rustling of leaves, and the piece sounds far more like a thaw than like foliage.","It became a byword for genteel Victorian and Edwardian parlour taste and is often cited as an example of what serious music escaped from, a judgement that says more about its ubiquity than its quality. Sinding's own reputation collapsed for a different reason: in 1941, aged eighty-five and reportedly no longer competent, he was enrolled in the Norwegian Nazi party, and Norway has argued about him ever since.","Listen once for the melody and once for the accompaniment, and try to work out which hand plays what; you will not manage it. Then put a Grieg Lyric Piece beside it to compare a miniature that means something with one that only wants to please. Historic recordings from the shellac era take it faster and drier than modern pianists do."],"stenhammar-serenade":["Five movements and nearly forty minutes, though the piece is called a serenade and behaves like one: an Overtura that begins with a soft, slowly waking horn call, a light Canzonetta, a fast Scherzo, a long Notturno and a Finale that dissolves rather than concludes. The scoring is unusually clear and chamber-like for the scale, with woodwind solos threaded through string textures, and the closing pages fade to a held note that simply stops sounding.","Stenhammar composed it after visits to Italy, and the warmth is deliberate: the leading Swedish symphonist of his generation wanted, for once, to write about southern light. He had taken over the Gothenburg Symphony in 1907 and spent fifteen years turning a provincial band into a real orchestra while touring as a pianist and playing chamber music. He revised the Serenade in 1919, removing one movement, which is why performances differ in length.","The movement Stenhammar cut in 1919 was published separately and is sometimes restored, so you may hear a six-movement version; check the track listing before deciding the piece is too long or too short. Listen at the very opening for the horn emerging out of nothing, an effect that asks the orchestra to begin below the threshold of hearing and only works in a good hall or a patient recording.","Sweden regards the Serenade as its finest orchestral work and Stenhammar as the peer of Nielsen and Sibelius that history mislaid; outside Scandinavia he is barely known. The claim holds up for the Second Symphony and the late string quartets as well. Debate concerns the two versions, and whether the tighter 1919 revision or the fuller original better balances the enormous Notturno at the centre.","Start with the Notturno, the emotional centre, and hear how much of it is solo woodwind over quiet strings. Then take the whole piece in order and notice that nothing is ever hurried. If it works for you, go on to Stenhammar's Second Symphony and his late quartets. Gothenburg recordings, unsurprisingly, have the idiom in their bones."],"alfven-midsommarvaka":["Fourteen minutes of orchestral rhapsody, subtitled Swedish Rhapsody No. 1, built from folk tunes and dance rhythms Alfvén collected or absorbed. It opens with a swinging polska and keeps piling on dances, each brighter and faster than the last, with a quieter central episode where a clarinet and then the strings sing a slow, sentimental melody over shimmering accompaniment. The dance returns, accelerates, and ends in a whirl of brass and percussion.","Alfvén wrote it in 1903, in his early thirties, out of memories of midsummer nights in the Stockholm archipelago, where the sun barely sets and the fiddling and drinking go on until morning. Sweden had a self-conscious national romanticism in all the arts around 1900, and the piece supplied its soundtrack. Alfvén later became director of music at Uppsala University and conductor of a famous male choir, and wrote five symphonies rarely heard abroad.","The slow central section is not a folk tune but Alfvén's own, and it is the seam where the piece stops being a picture postcard: the dance music around it is bright and public, while this melody is private and a little drunk. Listen to how he uses the strings' upper register there, and to the way the returning dance never sounds quite as innocent afterwards.","It made Alfvén internationally famous and then trapped him. In the 1950s the central melody, arranged and retitled Swedish Rhapsody, became a hit record on both sides of the Atlantic, and the composer vanished behind it. Swedish critics have long treated the piece as light music dressed as art; the counter-argument is that the orchestration is genuinely fine, and that being enjoyable is not a fault.","Play it loud. Follow the dance rhythm, a limping three-beat polska with the weight in the wrong place, and notice how differently the central melody moves. Then hear one of Alfvén's symphonies to see what he did with the same language at length. Swedish orchestras give the polska a swing that others tend to iron flat."],"rangstrom-symphony-no-1":["Four movements and about forty-five minutes, written in memory of Strindberg, who had died two years earlier. It is a symphony by a song composer: the themes are vocal in shape, stated plainly and repeated rather than developed, and the orchestra is handled in blocks of colour, with brass chorales, string unisons and sudden silences. The slow movement is the heart, a long elegy over a repeated tread, and the finale ends darkly rather than in consolation.","Rangström was a Stockholm critic and largely self-taught composer who had studied briefly in Berlin and idolised Strindberg, Sweden's ferocious dramatist and novelist, whose funeral in 1912 drew enormous crowds into the streets. Sweden had no strong symphonic habit of its own; Rangström's models were German, while his own instinct was for song, of which he wrote well over two hundred. He completed the symphony in 1914, as Europe went to war.","Rangström never learned orthodox counterpoint and never wanted to, so the symphony does not develop its themes in the German sense: it restates them in new colours, the way a song strophe returns with a different accompaniment. Once you hear the structure as strophic rather than argumentative, what sounds like technical weakness becomes a method. Listen to the brass chorales, which act as refrains between the vocal paragraphs.","Swedish critics divided immediately and have never fully agreed: one camp hears an original voice using amateur means, the other simply hears an amateur. Recordings since the 1990s have strengthened the first case, and the four symphonies now get occasional airings outside Sweden. Rangström's songs, which never left the Swedish recital repertoire, remain the surer ground of his reputation.","Come to the songs first, a dozen of them in any good Swedish recital, and then hear the symphony as an extension of them; it will make immediate sense. Concentrate on the slow movement. Notice how much of the effect comes from orchestral colour and silence rather than from harmony or counterpoint, which is exactly what his critics complained of."],"madetoja-symphony-no-2":["Four movements, about thirty-eight minutes, the last two running together without a break. It opens quietly with a rising woodwind idea over strings and grows into music that is lyrical rather than declamatory: the melodies are long and folk-inflected, and the orchestra is lighter and more French than Sibelius's. The third movement is a driven, hammering allegro, and the finale gathers the earlier material into a slow, dark processional that ends in resignation rather than victory.","Madetoja composed it between 1916 and 1918 and conducted the premiere in Helsinki that December, months after the Finnish Civil War. His brother had been killed in the fighting, and his close friend the composer Toivo Kuula died in May 1918 after being shot in a quarrel with soldiers. Finland had been independent for a year and was tearing itself apart. Madetoja had studied with Sibelius and then in Paris, and the French training shows.","The finale is a funeral march that never says so. Its material comes from the first movement's opening idea, slowed and darkened, so the symphony ends by remembering its own beginning in mourning. Listen for the Ostrobothnian folk contours in the melodies, narrow, stepwise and circling, drawn from the region Madetoja grew up in and which also fills the opera he set in the same landscape.","It is generally rated the finest Finnish symphony not written by Sibelius, and Finnish critics have argued for a century over whether Madetoja is a follower or an alternative. The French training is the strongest evidence for the second view, since the sonorities are closer to Debussy than to Sibelius. Outside Finland the symphony remains rare, though recordings since the 1990s have built it a following.","Listen to the last two movements as a single span and wait for the moment the driving music gives way to the closing procession. Then compare the opening minutes with Sibelius's Third to hear how different two Finnish symphonies of the same decade can be. Finnish orchestras find the melodic inflection that others tend to miss."],"tubin-symphony-no-5":["Three movements, about thirty minutes, for an orchestra with prominent timpani and brass. The first movement grows from a stark rising figure over a drum ostinato; the slow movement is a long, bleak string melody that keeps being cut off; the finale is a driving toccata that builds through repeated rhythmic cells to a huge and hard-won major ending, with the timpani hammering one note under the entire final climb. Nothing in the piece is decorative.","Tubin fled Estonia by boat in 1944 as the Soviet army returned, leaving manuscripts behind, and settled in Sweden, where he worked at a music museum in Stockholm and composed in the evenings. He wrote the Fifth in 1946, in his first years of exile, and it was premiered in Stockholm. Estonia would remain occupied for another four and a half decades; Tubin never lived there again and died in Stockholm in 1982.","The drive comes from rhythm rather than harmony: whole passages are built by repeating short cells and shifting the accent, a technique closer to Bartók than to the Nordic symphonists Tubin is usually filed beside. Listen to the timpani, which is structure and not colour, and to the finale's arrival in the major, which is not a resolution of the harmony so much as an act of will imposed over an unchanged bass.","Tubin's ten symphonies were almost unknown until Neeme Järvi recorded the cycle in the 1980s, and their rediscovery coincided with Estonia's move towards independence, which loaded them with a symbolism Tubin never claimed. The Fifth is the usual point of entry. Critics divide over whether its triumphant ending earns itself or merely asserts itself; Estonian listeners have generally had less difficulty with it than Western ones.","Start with the finale if you want to know within two minutes whether this composer is for you. Then hear the whole symphony in order so the ending lands as the outcome of everything before it. Järvi's advocacy remains the reference point; after this, the Fourth and the Sixth are the natural next steps."],"rautavaara-cantus-arcticus":["Subtitled Concerto for Birds and Orchestra: eighteen minutes in three movements for orchestra and a tape of real birds. It opens with two flutes imitating birdcalls before the recorded birds themselves enter, first marsh birds over a bog, then the low moaning of shore larks slowed down until they sound like something far larger, and finally migrating swans over a long orchestral crescendo that swells and recedes as the flock passes out of hearing.","The University of Oulu, in northern Finland, commissioned a piece for its first doctoral graduation ceremony in 1972, and Rautavaara went out with a tape recorder to the marshes of Ostrobothnia and to the Arctic Circle. Tape and orchestra had been combined for twenty years by then, almost always in the service of electronic abstraction; using it for field recordings of birds, under unashamedly tonal orchestral writing, was against everything a serious European composer was expected to do.","In the second movement the shore lark recording is played back at half speed, dropping it an octave and turning a small bird into something vast and mournful. It is the piece's one real act of studio manipulation and its emotional centre. Listen also to the opening: the two flutes arrive before the tape, so the human music is heard as birdsong first, and the birds then correct it.","It became Rautavaara's most played work and helped legitimise a return to tonality and atmosphere that the post-war avant-garde had ruled out. Critics who valued that avant-garde called it sentimental, and some still do. Its defenders point out that the tonal writing is functional rather than nostalgic, and that the tape is documentary rather than decorative. It is now a fixture of Finnish concert life and a favourite of film editors.","Listen on headphones so the tape sits in space around the orchestra rather than behind it. Wait for the slowed larks in the second movement, which is the sound people remember years later. Then play some of Messiaen's bird music for the contrast: Messiaen transcribes birds into notation for human players, while Rautavaara simply lets them sing."],"tormis-curse-upon-iron":["Ten minutes for mixed choir with a shaman drum that beats from the first bar to the last, at roughly the speed of a heartbeat and gradually accelerating. The choir chants runic verses telling the origin of iron and then curses it. The music is built from tiny cells of two or three notes repeated with shifting stress, growing from a whisper to shouting, with men's and women's voices splitting into hard, unblended lines. It stops abruptly, on the drum.","Tormis wrote it in Soviet Estonia in 1972, taking the iron-origin runo from the Kalevala and the Estonian oral tradition and adding lines by living poets. The Cold War, Vietnam and the arms race were the unstated subject, and the censors could hardly object to an ancient folk text about metal. Tormis spent his career setting the runic songs of the small Finno-Ugric peoples, several of whose languages were already close to extinction.","The drum is the piece's clock and its argument. It never stops, and its acceleration generates the sense of panic, since the vocal material itself barely changes. Listen to how the choir is asked to sing: straight tone, no vibrato, hard consonants, closer to shouting across a field than to choral blend. That village sound, rather than the concert-hall one, is the point of the curse.","It became the signature piece of the Estonian choral movement and was sung constantly during the Singing Revolution of the late 1980s, when mass choral gatherings became the vehicle of Baltic independence. Western choirs have taken it up but often smooth it into conventional tone, which drains it. Tormis insisted that he did not use folk song but that folk song used him, a formulation critics are still chewing over.","Find an Estonian choir and hear how ugly and thrilling the sound is meant to be. Follow the drum's speed rather than the words on a first hearing, and notice when you first realise it has been getting faster. Then listen to Pärt from the same country and decade for the opposite response to the same silence."],"norgard-symphony-no-3":["Two large movements, about fifty minutes, for a very big orchestra joined later by voices. The sound is unlike any other symphony of the 1970s: bells, glockenspiel, harps and tuned percussion ring through a texture that seems to expand outwards, carrying melodies at several speeds at once, a fast surface with a slower line beneath it and a slower one beneath that. The second movement gathers this into radiant chiming tuttis before the choirs enter and the music opens out.","Nørgård had spent the 1960s working out what he called the infinity series, a way of generating an endless melody from a simple arithmetic rule, and the Third Symphony is its largest application. He was in his forties, teaching in Aarhus, and had moved past both his teacher Vagn Holmboe and the Darmstadt orthodoxy in a single step. Denmark, small and generously funded, could afford to let a composer spend years on a private theory.","The infinity series has a strange property: the melody it generates contains slowed-down copies of itself at every level, so the fast notes, every second note and every fourth note all trace the same shape. Nørgård stacks these layers simultaneously, which is why the music feels like one thing moving at three speeds. He combines them with harmony derived from the natural overtone series, and that is where the bell-like glow comes from.","Danish critics call it the country's most important orchestral work since Nielsen, and it is the piece by which Nørgård is measured. It is also rarely played, because it needs an enormous apparatus and long rehearsal. Some hear a system on display; more hear one of the few genuinely new orchestral sounds of the later twentieth century. Nørgård himself moved on sharply afterwards into darker and more fractured music.","Do not try to follow the theory. Listen instead for the layers of speed, picking out a fast line and then noticing a slower one shadowing it, and for the bell sounds that flood the second movement. Play the last ten minutes first if you want the shortest route in. A well-drilled Danish orchestra and choir are worth seeking out."],"sallinen-the-red-line":["An opera in two acts, about two hours, in Finnish, set among starving crofters in the far north. The music is bleak, tonal and direct: modal melodic lines, dark orchestral colours, and choral scenes for villagers and for a political meeting. There are no set-piece arias. The vocal writing follows Finnish speech rhythm closely, and the passages that stay with audiences are a long duet for the crofter couple and the scene in which an agitator explains democracy to people who have never voted.","Sallinen wrote his own libretto after Ilmari Kianto's 1909 novel, and the Finnish National Opera gave the premiere in Helsinki in November 1978. The story turns on Finland's first parliamentary election in 1907, the first in Europe with universal suffrage including women, in which voters who could not write marked their ballots with a red line. The opera arrived while Finnish opera was being deliberately rebuilt as a national art form, and it travelled abroad quickly.","The red line of the title is drawn twice. It is the mark on the ballot paper, and at the end it is the wound across the crofter's throat where a bear has killed him: the vote and the death are the same image, and the orchestra makes the connection with a returning figure rather than a word. Listen for how little the music changes register between the political scenes and the domestic ones.","It was a national success and was staged abroad within a few years, part of a wave that briefly made Finnish opera an export. Critics praised its directness and its refusal of both modernist abstraction and folkloric prettiness; others found the idiom too plain to sustain two hours. It remains, with Sallinen's later operas, the standing argument that grand opera can still work on political material.","Read a synopsis first, since the plot is simple but the names are not. Listen to the election meeting scene and the final scene back to back. Then try one of Sallinen's symphonies for the same voice without words. A Finnish cast is essential, because the opera is built on the sound of the language."],"vasks-distant-light":["A violin concerto in one continuous span of about thirty minutes, for soloist and string orchestra. It begins with the violin alone, very high and very quiet, on a rising line that keeps reaching further, and the strings enter beneath it with slow, consonant harmony. Three cadenzas punctuate the piece and grow steadily more agitated; between them come waltz-like episodes and passages of harsh, scraping collective sound. It ends as it began, with the soloist climbing out of hearing.","Gidon Kremer commissioned it for his Kremerata Baltica and gave the premiere in 1997. Vasks, a Latvian pastor's son and a professional double bass player, had spent the Soviet decades writing music that could not say plainly what it meant; by 1997 Latvia had been independent for six years. He has described the title as the light of childhood memory and of stars, both of which reach you long after they were sent.","The waltz episodes are not decoration. Vasks has said they stand for a vanished world, and they are always slightly distorted, the harmony too sweet and the rhythm just off, so nostalgia is offered and undercut in the same breath. Listen also to the string writing at the climaxes, where the players are asked for deliberately ugly sounds, birdsong and wind and something like screaming, inside otherwise consonant music.","It has become one of the most performed violin concertos written since 1990, largely through Kremer's advocacy, and it divides critics along a familiar line between those who find its consonance honest and those who find it easy. Comparison with Pärt is unavoidable but misleading, since Vasks keeps a great deal of violence inside the frame. Latvian audiences hear a political work where Western ones usually hear a spiritual one.","Listen to the opening two minutes alone in a quiet room, one violin climbing, and then to the closing two, and hear that they are the same music. In between, use the three cadenzas as markers. Kremer's own recording is the obvious starting point; younger violinists have since made the piece warmer and less raw, which is worth comparing."],"esenvalds-stars":["Five minutes for unaccompanied mixed choir with a set of glasses tuned by filling them with water and played by rubbing the rims. The glasses sound first, a soft ringing chord that continues under the whole piece, and the choir enters beneath them on Sara Teasdale's short poem about standing alone on a dark hill and looking up. The harmony is slow, warm and mostly consonant, and a solo soprano rises above the texture near the end.","Ešenvalds wrote it in 2011, in his mid-thirties, when he was already the most performed of the younger Latvian choral composers. Latvia's choral tradition is unusually deep, and its Song and Dance Festival still gathers tens of thousands of singers. Ešenvalds came out of that world, studied with Vasks, and then found an international market among American and British university choirs hungry for new repertoire that sounds good and can be learned quickly.","The glasses are not an effect added to a finished piece. They are tuned to a fixed chord that never changes, so the choir's harmony has to move around a drone it cannot alter, which is why the music feels suspended rather than progressing. The number of glasses and the water levels are specified in the score, and in performance the singers usually play them, which means part of the choir stops singing in order to make the sky.","It has become one of the most sung new choral pieces in the English-speaking world, and the objection is predictable: that the effect is easy and the harmony undemanding. Choir directors reply that it is genuinely difficult to tune, that the glasses expose any weakness in blend, and that a five-minute piece which non-specialist audiences ask to hear again is not a small achievement.","Hear it live if you can, for the sight of singers holding water glasses. Otherwise use headphones and listen for the glasses continuing underneath everything, including the silences. Read Teasdale's poem first. Then compare Ešenvalds with Tormis, a generation earlier in the same Baltic choral world, to see how far the tradition has travelled."],"soler-fandango":["One unbroken movement of about a quarter of an hour, built on the fandango's rocking two-chord swing. Over that pattern Soler spins scores of short variations that never stop the pulse: hand-crossings, drummed repeated notes, snapped ornaments imitating a guitar strum, sudden bars of silence where a dancer's heel would land. There is almost no departure from the home harmony, so heat accumulates instead of argument. The ending is abrupt, the way a real fandango stops when the dancers do.","Antonio Soler entered the Hieronymite monastery of El Escorial in 1752 and stayed for life as organist and choirmaster, teaching the young Infante Gabriel and writing more than a hundred keyboard sonatas for him. He almost certainly knew Domenico Scarlatti at the Madrid court. The fandango he takes up here was a street and theatre dance that churchmen periodically condemned as lewd, which makes a monk's quarter-hour meditation on it a peculiar object.","The whole piece sits on the two chords of the Andalusian cadence, the same grip flamenco guitarists still use, so the ear hears change where there is only rhythm and register. Its authorship is not secure: the work survives in a single eighteenth-century manuscript copy and some scholars doubt Soler's hand. Listen for the stretches of near-noise, repeated notes hammered until pitch almost disappears.","The Fandango was unknown until the twentieth century, when harpsichordists rediscovered it and made it a recital showpiece. Debate runs in two directions: whether Soler wrote it at all, and how long it should be, since players cut or extend the variation chain freely. Performances range from eight minutes to twenty. Neither question is settled, and recordings differ wildly in tempo and instrument.","Follow the bass, which barely moves, and let the right hand's inventions wash past. Then compare it with Boccherini's fandango, where a real guitar and castanets do the same job with a smile. A gutsy harpsichord with a rattling lower register suits it better than a polite one; piano performances gain colour and lose bite."],"sor-variations-magic-flute":["Nine minutes for one guitar in four stages: a slow, harmonically searching introduction; Mozart's tune stated plainly; five variations; a brisk coda. The variations move outward from decoration into real difficulty, with running thirds, a minor-key variation that darkens everything, and a final one where a rapid figure must be kept going while the melody sings above it. Sor writes in genuine parts, so at times you hear three independent voices coming from six strings.","Fernando Sor left Spain after the Napoleonic occupation, having taken a post under the French administration, and settled in London in 1815. He played at Philharmonic Society concerts, published songs and guitar music, and wrote ballets. London was Mozart-mad, and variations on a familiar opera tune were the surest way to sell guitar music to amateurs who also wanted to hear a virtuoso play it.","The theme is not an aria but the chorus that breaks out after Papageno's magic bells in the Act I finale, a moment about an instrument enchanting its listeners, which is a sly choice for a guitarist. The searching introduction hides the tune's shape inside its wandering harmony before revealing it, so the theme arrives as a recognition rather than as a beginning.","Contemporaries called Sor the Beethoven of the guitar, meaning he wrote counterpoint where others wrote strumming. The piece survived the nineteenth century as a teaching staple and returned to the concert hall through Andres Segovia, whose edition trims and rewrites; players now often go back to Sor's own text. Arguments continue over gut versus nylon strings and whether the coda needs to be as fast as Segovia took it.","Hear the Mozart first, then this. The pleasure is watching one instrument pretend to be an orchestra and almost succeed. Notice the minor variation, where the piece briefly stops being charming. Recordings on a period guitar strung with gut sound smaller, drier and closer to what Sor heard; modern nylon gives more shine and less grain."],"arriaga-string-quartet-3":["Four movements, about twenty-five minutes. The first is a compact sonata movement with a nervous, off-balance main idea. The Pastorale that follows spins a slow tune over a drone and keeps cutting away to bare two-part texture. The minuet is fast and rough-edged rather than courtly, and the finale drives hard in the minor with the first violin pushed to its limits. The writing is lean, and the viola and cello are given real independence.","Juan Crisostomo de Arriaga was born in Bilbao in 1806 and sent to the Paris Conservatoire at fifteen, where he studied counterpoint with Francois-Joseph Fetis and violin with Pierre Baillot, and was soon made a teaching assistant. The three quartets were published in Paris in 1824, when he was eighteen. Two years later he was dead, ten days short of his twentieth birthday, probably of tuberculosis and overwork.","Arriaga was born exactly fifty years after Mozart and shared his baptismal name, which is why nineteenth-century Bilbao called him the Spanish Mozart. In the Pastorale the drone and the modal turn of the tune point to Basque dance music rather than to Vienna; it is the one place in the quartet where his birthplace is audible. The finale's key change late on is the boldest stroke in the set.","The quartets sold modestly and then vanished. Fetis kept the memory alive in print, and Bilbao's musicians revived them late in the century, naming a theatre after the composer. Modern opinion has stopped treating them as promising juvenilia. The question now is how much is Haydn absorbed at speed and how much is a personal voice; the finale is usually cited as evidence for the second.","Start with the Pastorale, the movement that shows he was more than a fluent student, then take the finale for its drive. Compare with Haydn's Op. 76 and with early Beethoven, the company Arriaga chose for himself. Groups play these on modern and on gut strings; the gut versions bring out the transparent, slightly astringent scoring."],"sarasate-zigeunerweisen":["Nine minutes in four linked sections: a stern orchestral summons, then the violin entering with wide declamatory phrases; a slow, heavily ornamented song played mostly on the lower strings; a short stretch of harmonics and left-hand plucking; and a headlong final dance that accelerates to the end. Everything is designed to display one player, with double stops, artificial harmonics, plucking with the left hand while the bow keeps going, and bouncing bowstrokes at impossible speed.","Pablo de Sarasate was the most admired violinist in Europe in the 1870s, praised for a pure, sweet tone and effortless technique rather than for force. He wrote his own vehicles, mostly on Spanish material, but here turned to the csardas repertoire of Hungarian cafe bands, hugely fashionable across Europe after Liszt's rhapsodies. He published the piece in 1878 and played it everywhere; the title simply means gypsy airs.","The lamenting slow tune is not folk music. It is a song by Elemer Szentirmay, a living Hungarian composer, and its use without credit brought a copyright complaint that Sarasate settled. Note too how the final dance is built to sound improvised while being written out exactly: the pauses that seem to be the player's whim are printed in the score.","Critics have always split between admiration for the writing's efficiency and irritation at its purpose, which is applause. It also carries the period's casual borrowing of Roma music as exotic colour, something modern programme notes tend to flag. Sarasate recorded it himself in 1904, and those discs, thin as they sound, remain the strongest evidence about how such music was actually played: faster, freer and far less sentimental than the later tradition.","Listen for the change of gear into the final dance and count how long the violinist can keep accelerating. Then find the composer's own 1904 recording, whose portamento and offhand speed reset expectations. Compare it with Ravel's Tzigane, which does the same trick with more irony fifty years later."],"tarrega-recuerdos-de-la-alhambra":["Four minutes of continuous tremolo. The thumb plays a slow melody and its accompaniment on the low strings while three fingers repeat a single high note so fast that it sounds sustained, like a bowed line. The piece falls into two halves, the first in the minor, the second turning to the major before the opening returns. Nothing else happens. The whole effect depends on an illusion of singing tone that a plucked instrument cannot really produce.","Francisco Tarrega was the guitar's great teacher and arranger of the late nineteenth century, transcribing Chopin, Beethoven and Albeniz for an instrument then thought unfit for the concert hall. He wrote this after time in Granada and titled it a memory of the Alhambra, the Nasrid palace whose water channels and honeycombed vaults had become a symbol of Spain's Moorish past for tourists and Romantic writers alike.","Tremolo was a known trick, but Tarrega fixed it to a regular pattern of one thumb stroke and three fingers, which is why the note-stream sounds even rather than lumpy; unevenness here is instantly audible, and that makes the piece a merciless test. The dedication is to Alfred Cottin, a Paris friend and guitarist. The Nokia ringtone, often attributed to this piece, comes from a different Tarrega work.","Guitarists disagree about tempo and rubato: taken too fast, the illusion of a bowed line collapses; too slow, and the repeated note becomes audible as repetition. Some concert players resent its ubiquity as a beginner's ambition and a shop-window piece. Others point out that no other work in the repertoire depends so completely on a single physical skill, which is why it never becomes easy.","Listen for the moment the harmony brightens into the major and the melody rises; that is the entire emotional event. Try to hear the individual repeated notes, then stop trying and let them fuse. Compare a modern nylon-string recital recording with an older gut-strung one, and with Albeniz's Granada, which imagines the same city on a piano."],"albeniz-iberia-triana":["The third piece of Book II, five minutes long, named for the gypsy quarter of Seville on the far bank of the Guadalquivir. A clipped, strutting figure in the manner of a paso doble alternates with a swaying sung phrase; the two are eventually forced together, hands crossing and colliding, until the music jams into repeated chords and cuts off. The texture is deliberately overloaded, with three ideas at once and contradictory dynamic markings on the same beat.","Albeniz wrote the twelve pieces of Iberia between 1905 and 1908 in Paris and Nice, in poor health, at the end of a career that had begun with child-prodigy tours and salon dances. He was living among French musicians, and the writing shows it. Blanche Selva, who gave most of the first performances, told him the music was unplayable; Albeniz half agreed and considered destroying part of it.","The strutting theme is a march, but Albeniz keeps displacing its accents so the foot never lands where the ear expects: the swagger is written as a limp. Underneath, the harmony repeatedly slips to the flat second degree, the Andalusian cadence smuggled into a Parisian piano texture. The notation is fanatical, stacking staves and giving one hand three dynamic marks at once, trying to fix the balance of an imagined guitar-and-voice ensemble.","Selva's premieres were respectful and baffled; the pieces spread slowly, kept alive by a few specialists before recordings by Alicia de Larrocha made them standard. Messiaen called Iberia the wonder of the piano. Pianists still argue about whether to thin the impossible chords, and editions differ: modern critical editions have corrected many long-accepted misreadings, which changed how some passages sound.","Start here rather than at the beginning of Iberia, because Triana is the most immediately gripping of the twelve. Listen for the two characters, the strut and the sung phrase, then for the moment they are played simultaneously. Alicia de Larrocha's recordings are the reference; hear one beside a guitar transcription to understand what the piano is imitating."],"albeniz-iberia-el-albaicin":["The first piece of Book III, about seven minutes, named for the old Moorish hill quarter of Granada. It opens with dry snapped chords low on the keyboard, a guitar struck rather than strummed, and then a long vocal line unwinds above, halting and doubling back in the manner of deep song. The two never settle into a comfortable partnership; the guitar keeps interrupting, and the piece fades out on the plucked figure alone.","Book III was finished in 1907, two years before Albeniz died at forty-eight in the French Pyrenees. He had not lived in Spain for years, having passed from childhood touring to London and then to the Paris circle around Vincent d'Indy's Schola Cantorum, so the Granada here is remembered rather than observed. The Alhambra and the Albaicin facing it were by then a standard destination for foreign Romantics.","Debussy reviewed Book III and singled out this piece, admiring the way it conveyed a night full of scent and heat without describing anything, and he then wrote Spanish music of his own that leans on it. The vocal line never resolves where the harmony wants it to, which is exactly the friction between voice and guitar in flamenco, transcribed onto one keyboard.","Iberia was received as a difficult curiosity and only later understood as the summit of Spanish piano music. Debate centres on whether the fiendish notation is essential or obsessive: some pianists thin the textures, arguing that Albeniz overwrote what he could not play himself, while others hold that the crowdedness is the sound. Guitarists have reclaimed several pieces by transcription, which raises the same question backwards.","Listen to the two layers separately, first only the low plucked chords, then only the sung line, and notice how rarely they agree. Follow it with Debussy's Spanish evocations and with a flamenco recording of a solea to hear the source. Alicia de Larrocha and Esteban Sanchez are the classic accounts, very different from each other."],"granados-goyescas":["Six long piano pieces in two books, roughly fifty-five minutes together, subtitled the majos in love. They are not portraits of particular paintings but of the world inside them: flirtation at a window grille, a lantern-lit fandango, a woman's complaint answered by a nightingale, a scene of love and death, and finally a serenade played by a ghost who breaks off mid-phrase. The writing is thickly ornamented, with the melody often buried in the middle of the texture.","Enrique Granados spent years studying Goya in the Prado before writing a note. He had trained in Barcelona and Paris, founded a piano school at home, and made his living teaching; Goyescas was his bid for something larger. He then turned it into an opera, which the Metropolitan Opera premiered in January 1916. Sailing home that March, his ship was torpedoed in the English Channel and he drowned trying to save his wife.","Themes migrate between the pieces: the tune of the fourth, the maiden and the nightingale, returns transformed in the love-and-death scene, so the cycle keeps a memory of itself. The bird cadenza that ends that fourth piece was reportedly a late addition made at his wife's suggestion. The final serenade quotes earlier pieces in fragments and simply stops without a cadence, as the ghost leaves.","The Barcelona premiere in 1911 succeeded and Paris followed. The opera version has fared worse: assembled from the piano pieces with a thin libretto, it is revived rarely, and most listeners meet only its Intermezzo. Pianists argue about the ornamentation, which can sound like guitar filigree or like clutter, and about performing the pieces singly, which the thematic links argue against.","Begin with the fourth piece, a slow song followed by a nightingale cadenza; if it holds you, the rest will. Listen for melody hiding under decoration rather than sitting on top of it. Alicia de Larrocha is the reference here too, and Albeniz's Iberia, written at the same moment and pulling the opposite way towards hardness, is the natural comparison."],"falla-el-amor-brujo":["A twenty-five-minute ballet with a singer, built as a chain of short numbers. Candelas, a young Andalusian Roma widow, is haunted by the ghost of her jealous dead lover and frees herself only by a trick. The scoring is hard and percussive, with a piano inside the orchestra and the trumpet used like a shout; three flamenco-inflected songs interrupt the dancing, meant for a raw chest voice rather than an operatic one. The Ritual Fire Dance is the exorcism at the centre.","Falla came home from seven years in Paris when war broke out in 1914 and wrote this for Pastora Imperio, a celebrated flamenco dancer and singer, whose family supplied Andalusian songs and stories. The first version, a gitaneria for a band of about fifteen players, was given in Madrid in April 1915 and puzzled its audience. He recast it as a full ballet for Paris in the 1920s, and that is the version usually heard.","The vocal writing asks for a cantaora's technique, with nasal placement, bent pitches and cries on the vowel, all of which classically trained mezzos tend to smooth away; the score insists the singer should not sound trained. The ghost is written as an absence, danced but never sung, and the orchestra gives him the same jagged dance music that later drives the exorcism, so possession and cure share a tune.","The 1915 premiere was judged a failure, too raw for a theatre audience and too concert-like for a flamenco one. The 1925 ballet succeeded, and the Ritual Fire Dance became a warhorse in piano transcription, which detached it from the story. Recent performances have returned to the chamber original, arguing that a full orchestra domesticates the sound. The choice of singer, flamenco or classical, remains the loudest argument.","Hear a version with a real cantaora, Ginesa Ortega for instance, beside a conventional mezzo, and the piece changes character completely. Listen for the midnight bells and the flute at dawn that close it. If you know only the Fire Dance, the surprise is how much of the score is quiet and how much of it is silence around the beat."],"falla-el-sombrero-de-tres-picos":["A two-act comic ballet of about forty minutes. A miller and his wife outwit the local magistrate, who covets her and ends the evening soaked in the millstream and wearing the wrong clothes. Falla writes a sequence of hard-edged Spanish dances: a fandango, the miller's farruca with its stamping repeated chords, the wife's seguidillas, and a huge closing jota in which the village tosses the magistrate in a blanket. A mezzo sings brief warnings from offstage.","The piece grew out of a 1917 pantomime, El corregidor y la molinera, which Diaghilev saw and asked Falla to expand for the Ballets Russes. The premiere was at the Alhambra Theatre in London in July 1919, choreographed by Leonide Massine, with curtain, sets and costumes by Picasso and Ernest Ansermet conducting. Massine had travelled through Spain with Falla to learn flamenco steps for the farruca.","The opening fanfare and shouted ole are a bullring gesture, not a theatre one, and the cuckoo call that follows announces the plot in a single word. Falla quotes the four-note opening of Beethoven's Fifth when the magistrate's men come knocking, fate arriving as a joke. The offstage songs warn the wife about her husband's jealousy, so the score narrates as well as dances.","London took it as a hit and Paris confirmed the verdict; Picasso's designs did as much for its fame as the music. The two orchestral suites drawn from it are played far more often than the ballet, which loses both the singer and the plot. Some critics argue that Falla's Spain here is already stylised for export, closer to Stravinsky's Russia in Petrushka than to the Andalusia of El amor brujo.","Go for the complete ballet rather than a suite, so the songs and the pantomime survive. Listen for the farruca's slow build to its stamped ending, then for the jota finale, the loudest happy noise in Spanish music. Recordings with a Spanish mezzo and a conductor unafraid of dance rhythm serve it best; compare it with Petrushka for how a ballet score carries comedy."],"falla-el-retablo-de-maese-pedro":["A twenty-seven-minute chamber opera for puppets, taken from one chapter of Don Quixote. A travelling showman sets up a puppet play about a knight rescuing his wife from the Moors; a boy narrates it in a street-crier's bawl; Don Quixote, watching from the audience, mistakes the show for reality and destroys it with his sword. Falla scores it for about twenty players including harpsichord, and writes deliberately stiff, archaic music for the puppet world inside the frame.","The Princesse Edmond de Polignac commissioned the piece for the puppet theatre in her Paris house. Falla, by then settled in Granada, gave a concert performance in Seville in March 1923 and the staged premiere in the Princesse's salon that June, with Wanda Landowska at the harpsichord; meeting her led directly to the concerto he wrote for her three years later. Spain was at that moment rediscovering and publishing its Renaissance repertoire.","The boy narrator's part is written to be sung flat-toned and mechanically, imitating the pregoneros who cried news in Spanish streets, so a beautiful performance of it is a wrong one. Falla built the archaic sound out of real sources, sixteenth-century keyboard and vihuela music among them, and the trumpet fanfares behind the puppets are stiff on purpose, since puppets cannot phrase.","A salon premiere with large consequences: Stravinsky's neoclassicism and Falla's converge here. Staging is the difficulty, with puppets for the inner play, singers for the outer one, and Don Quixote belonging to both. Companies solve it differently and rarely convincingly, which is why the work is more admired than performed. Recordings sidestep the problem entirely and have kept it alive.","Follow a synopsis, since the frame-within-a-frame carries the joke. Listen for the harpsichord's dry rattle against the winds, and for Don Quixote's closing speech, which turns suddenly warm and sane. Then hear Falla's Harpsichord Concerto, written straight afterwards from the same materials and even more severe."],"turina-la-oracion-del-torero":["Nine minutes for four players in a single movement, with a clear scene behind it. A bullfighter kneels in the chapel beside the ring while the crowd roars outside. Turina alternates two kinds of music: a bright, clattering pasodoble figure that keeps breaking in, and a long quiet melody over held chords for the prayer. The two circle each other without resolving, and the ending pulls back inside the chapel.","Joaquin Turina wrote it for the Cuarteto Aguilar, a family ensemble that played laudes, Spanish lutes of four different sizes, and the piece was first heard on those instruments in Madrid in 1925. He made the string quartet version soon after and a string orchestra version later. Turina had trained in Paris with Vincent d'Indy and, on Albeniz's advice, gone home to write Spanish music instead of French music.","The programme is specific: the idea came to Turina at a bullfight, watching the contrast between the chapel's silence and the noise on the other side of the door. The prayer melody is not a hymn but a saeta-like line, the kind of solo religious song called out during Andalusian Holy Week processions, so the piece sets two Spanish rituals against each other, the sacred and the lethal.","It has stayed Turina's most played work, which annoys admirers of his larger scores. Sceptics call it picture-postcard programme music; defenders point out that neither strand ever wins the argument. The lute-quartet original is almost never heard, and audiences take the string version as definitive, which changes the grain of the sound entirely, since the laudes gave the fanfares a plucked and brittle edge.","Listen for the two worlds and for how thin the wall between them is, since the crowd is audible right through the prayer. Nine minutes is short enough to hear twice in a row, and the second time the joins become obvious. Then try the string orchestra version, which makes the chapel bigger and the prayer more public."],"mompou-musica-callada":["Twenty-eight short piano pieces in four books, most under three minutes, about seventy minutes in all. There is no development and almost no counterpoint: a bell-like chord, a bare melodic fragment, a silence, then the same chord slightly altered. Dynamics stay low and tempos slow. Some pieces dispense with key signatures and let the pulse float, and many end unresolved. Played complete, the effect is closer to a rosary than to a recital programme.","Federico Mompou spent the 1920s and 1930s in Paris and returned to Barcelona in 1941. He wrote very little and very slowly, and called his method recomencar, beginning again, stripping music back rather than building it up. The title comes from Saint John of the Cross, whose Spiritual Canticle speaks of silent music and sounding solitude. The four books appeared between 1959 and 1967, when he was in his sixties and seventies.","Several pieces are built from the same handful of notes, a personal signature Mompou traced to the overtones of a bell; his mother's family owned a bell foundry in Barcelona and he grew up around the sound. Listen for the way a piece often opens on a dissonance and treats it as consonant, never resolving it, so that the silence after each piece has to do the resolving instead.","For decades Mompou was filed as a miniaturist and a French epigone. The complete cycle changed that: heard whole it reads as a serious late work, and comparisons are now made with Satie and with Feldman's late quiet pieces. The composer's own recordings, slow and undemonstrative, are the reference, though some pianists argue they are too withdrawn to project in a concert hall.","Do not use it as background. Play three or four pieces and stop. Listen for the silences, which are written and timed rather than left to taste. Mompou's own recording is unhurried to the point of stillness; Arcadi Volodos and Javier Perianes give warmer, more projected readings that suit a larger room."],"rodrigo-cuatro-madrigales-amatorios":["Four short songs, eight minutes in all, on Spanish love lyrics of the sixteenth century. Rodrigo takes the melodies from Renaissance songbooks and rebuilds everything underneath them: a slow lament about washing with tears, a stark address to a lover who has killed her, a suspicious question to someone coming home late, and a bright spinning final song about returning from the poplar grove where the wind stirs the leaves. The piano imitates plucked strings throughout.","Rodrigo, blind since an epidemic at the age of three, had made his name with the Concierto de Aranjuez in 1940 and settled in Madrid as the leading composer of Franco's Spain, a position of privilege and of compromise. These songs date from 1948 and belong to a wider national project of recovering the vihuela and cancionero repertoire, then being edited and published for the first time. He orchestrated them later.","The tunes are largely by Juan Vasquez, a mid-sixteenth-century composer from Badajoz whose songbooks were being rediscovered; Rodrigo keeps the melodic lines close to the originals and changes everything around them. The last song's rippling accompaniment is a poplar grove in the wind, but it is also a vihuela figure, so the piano is imitating an obsolete instrument imitating a tree.","The set has become a Spanish recital staple, and singers outside Spain often programme it as a lighter alternative to German lieder, which underrates the second song badly. Purists dislike the modern harmonisation of Renaissance material; performers reply that Rodrigo never claimed to be restoring anything. The orchestral version is fuller but blunts the plucked-string effect that gives the piano version its point.","Start with the last song, over in ninety seconds and impossible to dislike, then go back to the first, which is the emotional centre. Compare with a period-instrument recording of Vasquez to hear exactly what Rodrigo kept and what he replaced. Victoria de los Angeles and Teresa Berganza set the standard for the singing."],"gerhard-symphony-no-4-new-york":["One continuous movement of about half an hour for a large orchestra with a big percussion section. There are no themes in the usual sense; the music is built from blocks of colour and rhythm that recur and are re-cut, with long still passages of high sustained string sound broken by percussion attacks. The brass writing is hard and blaring. The piece keeps starting up traffic-like activity that never quite arrives anywhere, then abandons it.","Roberto Gerhard, born in Valls in Catalonia, studied with Felipe Pedrell and then with Schoenberg in Vienna and Berlin, the only Spaniard to do so. The Civil War drove him into exile in 1939 and he spent the rest of his life in Cambridge, writing radio and theatre scores and, late on, a sequence of symphonies. The New York Philharmonic commissioned this one for its 125th anniversary; he died three years after it was played.","Gerhard organised long stretches by proportion rather than by theme, planning durations and densities in advance, and let the serial method govern rhythm as much as pitch. He had worked for years with tape in the BBC's studios, and the orchestral writing borrows the electronic habit of treating a sound as a shaped object with an attack, a body and a decay, which is why so many gestures are single events rather than phrases.","New York was baffled, and British critics were slow too; Gerhard's reputation grew only after his death, helped by recordings of the four symphonies in the 1990s. He is still routinely described as a Catalan composer of Spanish music, which this score contradicts on every page. The open question is whether his late orchestral works belong with the European avant-garde or stand outside every school, which is roughly where he lived.","Do not listen for tunes. Listen for the percussion, which carries the argument, and for the way the piece breathes: dense, then suddenly empty. A few minutes of Varese beforehand makes the language legible. Recordings by the BBC Symphony Orchestra under Matthias Bamert are the usual entry point and the clearest."],"montsalvatge-cinco-canciones-negras":["Five songs, thirteen minutes, on Spanish and Caribbean poems, with the habanera rhythm running through most of them. A nostalgic evocation of Cuba remembered from a Spanish drawing room; a portrait of a Creole girl; a violent little piece about a knifeman; the famous lullaby for a black baby, rocking and tender; and a final song built from nonsense syllables that turns the voice into a percussion instrument. The piano stays dry and rhythmic rather than lush.","Xavier Montsalvatge wrote these in Barcelona in 1945, in a Spain isolated after the Civil War and shut out of the postwar settlement. Catalan composers of his generation looked to the Antilles: Cuba had been Spanish until 1898 and the habanera had crossed back into Catalan popular song. Choosing poems by Nicolas Guillen and Rafael Alberti, both associated with the losing side, was not a neutral act in 1945.","The first song quotes an old habanera by Eduardo Sanchez de Fuentes, so the nostalgia is literal: a real Cuban tune heard through a Spanish piano. The lullaby's text promises the child freedom from the whip, which most audiences hear as sweetness because the music is so gentle. The last song is made from Guillen's invented African-sounding syllables, which mean nothing and suggest everything.","The lullaby escaped the cycle almost immediately and is now sung everywhere, often by singers who never programme the other four. Recent criticism has looked harder at the exoticism, a white Catalan composer writing black songs about Caribbean people, and at Guillen's own use of the same material as anti-colonial protest. Most performers now sing all five, which restores the political edge the lullaby loses alone.","Hear the cycle whole, because the lullaby means something different when the knife song comes before it. Listen for the habanera pattern under almost every number, the same rhythm Bizet used to signify Spain and Montsalvatge uses to signify Cuba. Victoria de los Angeles is the reference; Montserrat Caballe's orchestral version is grander and slower."],"villa-lobos-bachianas-brasileiras-no-1":["Three movements, about twenty minutes, for a choir of cellos, eight at minimum and often more. The first is a fast chattering piece over a walking bass; the second a long slow song, its melody carried by one cello high in its register while the others hold a dark chord beneath; the third a real fugue whose subject sounds like a Rio street tune. Every movement carries two titles, one Bachian and one Brazilian.","Villa-Lobos returned from Paris in 1930 convinced that Bach's counterpoint and Brazilian popular music were versions of the same folk instinct, and began the nine Bachianas Brasileiras. He was simultaneously being made responsible for music education across the whole country under Getulio Vargas, teaching mass singing to stadiums of schoolchildren. He had played cello in Rio cinema and cafe orchestras, which is where the sound of a group of cellos came from.","The double titles are the argument in miniature: the Preludio is also a Modinha, the Brazilian sentimental song, and the Fuga is also a Conversa, a conversation, since Villa-Lobos claimed a fugue subject and a group of street musicians trading phrases were the same thing. The fugue treats its countersubjects loosely by Bach's rules, and deliberately so; the strict form keeps being pulled towards improvisation.","The Bachianas made Villa-Lobos internationally famous and are still the works most often held against him, since critics from the start called the Bach connection a marketing label rather than a technique. Defenders note that this fugue is a genuine one, written by someone who could do it. The cello-choir sound, once a novelty, is now standard, and cello sections programme the piece as a showcase for themselves.","Start with the middle movement for the sound of eight cellos holding one dark chord under a single singing line. Then take the fugue and try to follow the subject as it enters, which is easier here than in most fugues because the subject sounds like a person talking. Compare with the Aria of Bachianas No. 5, the same cello choir with a soprano added."],"villa-lobos-choros-no-10":["Thirteen minutes for chorus and large orchestra in two halves. The first is purely instrumental: birdcalls, the ringing cry of the araponga, and layers of percussion building a forest that grows denser and louder. Then the chorus enters, first on spoken and chanted syllables imitating drums and indigenous chant, and finally with a full-throated Brazilian waltz tune sung over the top. The two worlds run simultaneously to the end without either giving way.","The fourteen Choros were Villa-Lobos's attempt to invent a Brazilian form, named after the street serenading bands of Rio he had played in. He wrote No. 10 in 1926, between his two long stays in Paris, funded by patrons who wanted Brazil represented at the international modernist table. Rio heard it that year and Paris the next, where he was treated as an exotic sensation, a role that suited him and that he encouraged.","The waltz is not his. The melody is by Anacleto de Medeiros, with words by Catulo da Paixao Cearense, a popular song every Brazilian in the hall would have known by heart. Villa-Lobos sets it against choral syllables derived from indigenous chant, so European salon melody and Amerindian ritual sound at once, a picture of the country's mixture that is also, deliberately, a collision.","Paris found it barbaric in exactly the way Paris wanted to be found barbaric, and the label stuck to Villa-Lobos for decades. Modern criticism reads it more carefully: the indigenous material is second-hand, filtered through transcriptions, and the work says more about 1920s Brazilian nationalism than about the peoples it invokes. As music it remains among his boldest scores, and among the hardest to rehearse.","Wait for the choral entry roughly halfway, then for the moment the waltz surfaces through the noise; the effect is a radio finding a station through static. The percussion detail rewards headphones. Compare with the Rite of Spring, which Villa-Lobos knew well, and with the far tamer Little Train of the Caipira."],"ginastera-estancia":["A one-act ballet of about thirty-five minutes set on a cattle ranch across a single day and night. A city boy tries to prove himself to a ranch girl by doing the gauchos' work. The score is bright, hard-edged and rhythmically fierce: dawn and wheat-threshing music, a cattle-driving section, a night scene with a baritone singing verses from the gaucho epic Martin Fierro, and a closing malambo, a competitive men's stamping dance that accelerates until the orchestra can barely hold it.","Lincoln Kirstein's American Ballet Caravan commissioned it during the wartime good-neighbour push that also produced Copland's Latin American pieces. The company folded before staging it, so Ginastera extracted a four-movement suite, first heard in Buenos Aires in 1943; the complete ballet waited until 1952 at the Teatro Colon. He was twenty-five, and Argentina was busy inventing a national art music with state encouragement.","Behind almost every movement lies the guitar: Ginastera's signature is the instrument's six open strings sounded as a chord, and it appears here at structural corners, a national tuning fork he went on using for the rest of his life. The Martin Fierro settings are placed at night, the traditional hour for the payador's singing, so the one lyrical stretch is also the most literary.","The Malambo escaped the ballet and became a concert encore and then a stadium piece, more famous now than anything else Ginastera wrote, which has flattened his reputation towards the picturesque. Critics divide his output into objective nationalism, subjective nationalism and neo-expressionism, with Estancia in the first bracket. Whether the scheme means much is debated; the later operas sound less distant from this than it suggests.","Take the suite first, four movements and about twelve minutes, ending with the Malambo. Then hear the complete ballet for the night scene, which the suite omits and which holds the best music. Compare with Copland's Rodeo, written a year later on the other American continent with much the same idea in mind."],"piazzolla-maria-de-buenos-aires":["An operita in two parts, about ninety-five minutes, for three voices and a small band led by the bandoneon. Maria is born in a slum on a day the bandoneon is in mourning, comes to the city, falls, dies at the end of Part One, and spends Part Two as a shadow wandering Buenos Aires until she gives birth to herself. A narrator, the duende, speaks most of the plot. The music is tango throughout, stretched by fugues, jazz solos and long instrumental interludes.","Piazzolla had spent a decade being denounced by tango traditionalists for adding fugue, dissonance and electric guitar to a sacred form. He met the Uruguayan poet Horacio Ferrer in 1967, and this was their first collaboration: it opened in a small Buenos Aires theatre in May 1968 with Amelita Baltar in the title role, Ferrer himself narrating, and Piazzolla in the band. The libretto's surrealist Spanish is deliberately untranslatable, thick with lunfardo slang.","The bandoneon is a character rather than an accompaniment: it is present at Maria's birth and at her death, and Piazzolla writes for its wheeze and for its ability to stop a chord dead. The number called Fuga y misterio is an actual fugue, exposition and all, his standing provocation to purists who said tango could not carry counterpoint. Listen too for the whistles and spoken interjections that keep the story inside a dream.","The 1968 run was small and the piece nearly disappeared; its real life began in the 1990s, when European and American companies took it up during the general Piazzolla revival. Staging is contentious, since it is neither opera nor musical nor concert tango, and productions swing between magic realism and dance show. Purists on both sides still object: too classical for tango, too tango for opera. That is roughly what it was built to be.","Listen to Yo soy Maria and Fuga y misterio first; if they hold you, the whole thing will. Follow a translation of the narration, since the plot is otherwise unrecoverable. Then compare a recording with an Argentine cast and a real bandoneon against a chamber-orchestra version, to hear how much of the piece lives in the instrument's breathing."],"chavez-sinfonia-india":["A single movement of about eleven minutes that behaves like a compressed four-movement symphony: fast, slow, faster, then a driving finale. Three melodies from indigenous peoples, Huichol, Yaqui and Seri, carry the argument, and Chavez keeps changing the metre from bar to bar so the tunes never fall into a European square. The percussion is the point, with clay rattles, a water gourd, deer-hoof rattles and butterfly-cocoon shakers sitting beside the standard orchestra.","Chavez wrote it in New York in 1935 and 1936 while running the Orquesta Sinfonica de Mexico back home, and conducted the premiere on a radio broadcast in January 1936. Post-revolutionary Mexico was funding a state art of murals, folk collection and public concerts; Chavez was its chief musical administrator as well as its leading composer, and a close friend of Copland, who was watching all of it with interest.","Chavez supplies substitutes for the indigenous instruments when they cannot be obtained, and most orchestras use them, so the piece is usually heard in the version he expected to be a compromise. The melodies are transcriptions rather than inventions and he does almost nothing to them harmonically; the modernism lies in the metre, which is irregular by compositional design rather than by folk practice.","American critics received it warmly as authentic Mexico, and it became the most played Mexican orchestral work after Huapango. Later scholarship is more uneasy about an urban composer with European training using indigenous melodies to represent a nation that was simultaneously suppressing indigenous languages. Chavez's own later music moved away from this manner entirely, which some read as his answer to the criticism.","Eleven minutes, no repeats and no filler, so listen twice. Follow the percussion rather than the tunes and the originality comes forward at once. Then hear Copland's El Salon Mexico, written at the same moment about the same country by a visitor, and Revueltas's Sensemaya for the harsher alternative."],"revueltas-sensemaya":["Seven minutes for large orchestra, built on a limping seven-beat pattern that starts almost inaudibly in the low percussion and never stops. Layer is piled on layer, bass clarinet, tuba, muted trumpets, piano, until the whole orchestra is hammering the same rhythm in conflicting keys at once. There is no melody in the ordinary sense and no relief; the piece simply thickens to a shattering climax and stops dead on a single unison chord.","Silvestre Revueltas took the title and the shape from Nicolas Guillen's poem Sensemaya, subtitled a chant for killing a snake and built out of Afro-Cuban ritual syllables. He made a chamber version with voice in 1937 and this orchestral one in 1938, the year he went to Spain in support of the Republic. He drank himself to death two years later at forty, having written nearly all his best music inside a single decade.","The seven-beat unit is the poem's chanted refrain turned into rhythm, and once you hear the words inside the pattern you cannot unhear them. Revueltas keeps the layers in conflicting keys so the accumulated sound is never a chord you can name, achieving the effect of ritual noise by strict means. The final blow is a bare unison: the snake killed exactly on the beat.","Sensemaya is the most performed Mexican orchestral work abroad and is regularly compared with the Rite of Spring, though the ostinato technique is closer to Varese. Revueltas's reputation suffered from the cult of the doomed alcoholic and from Chavez's institutional shadow. Recent decades have restored him, and the film scores in particular are now taken seriously rather than treated as hackwork.","Count the seven-beat unit at the start, while the texture is still thin, then give up as it thickens. Play it loud. Afterwards find the 1937 chamber version with the poem sung, which lays out the whole design in half the forces. Compare with Varese's Ameriques rather than with Stravinsky and the piece makes more sense."],"ponce-concierto-del-sur":["Three movements, about twenty-three minutes, for guitar and a small orchestra kept deliberately light so the soloist can be heard at all. The first movement is restless and Spanish in accent rather than Mexican; the slow movement is a long quiet song over sustained strings with the guitar mostly alone; the finale is a dance with a strummed refrain. Ponce writes for the instrument's weaknesses as much as its strengths, thinning the orchestra whenever the guitar plays.","Manuel Ponce and Andres Segovia met in Mexico in 1923 and worked together for the rest of Ponce's life; Segovia had been asking for a concerto for years. It was finished in 1941 and the two of them gave the premiere in Montevideo that October, with Ponce conducting. The south of the title is Andalusia, not Mexico: a Mexican composer writing Spanish music for a Spanish player, in the decade when Rodrigo was doing something similar at home.","Ponce had already fooled the musical world once on Segovia's behalf, writing a suite in the style of the baroque lutenist Silvius Leopold Weiss that circulated and was performed as a genuine Weiss discovery. The same taste for stylistic disguise is at work here: nothing in the concerto is quoted, but the Andalusian gestures are impersonated with a forger's accuracy and a forger's confidence.","The concerto has never escaped the shadow of the Concierto de Aranjuez, written two years earlier over similar ground and far more memorably. Guitarists defend it as the better-written piece for the instrument, since Ponce understood the balance problem that Rodrigo mostly solved by luck. It is played steadily rather than constantly, and almost always by specialists rather than by touring orchestras.","The slow movement is the reason to listen: more inward than Rodrigo's famous Adagio and asking less of your attention. Hear Segovia's own recording for the sound Ponce was writing for, small and dry and close, then a modern amplified one to hear what has changed. Compare it directly with Aranjuez, movement for movement."],"moncayo-huapango":["Nine minutes for orchestra, based on three sones from the Veracruz coast. The huapango's characteristic feel, a fast triple pulse that keeps flipping into duple, drives the whole piece, and Moncayo scores it for maximum glare: trumpets in thirds, harp doing the work of a strummed village guitar, violins climbing into the register a huapango singer reaches in falsetto. A slower lyrical son forms the middle before the opening dance returns and accelerates away.","Jose Pablo Moncayo was one of the Grupo de los Cuatro, young Mexican composers formed around Chavez, and played percussion and piano in Chavez's orchestra. He travelled to Alvarado in Veracruz with Blas Galindo to collect songs, and built the piece from three of them. Chavez conducted the premiere at the Palacio de Bellas Artes in August 1941. It became, in effect, Mexico's second national anthem.","The three sones are kept whole rather than fragmented, so the composition lies in the joins and the orchestration rather than in any development. Listen for the violins taking the vocal line up to the pitch a falsetto singer would reach, and for the harp, which is standing in for the small guitars that would carry the rhythm in a village band and cannot be scaled up to an orchestra.","Ubiquity has cost it. Played at every official occasion in Mexico, it is easy to hear as national cliche rather than as a piece of music. Moncayo came to resent being the composer of one work, and little else of his is programmed. Critics who look again usually note the sophistication of the scoring and the fact that the rhythmic ambiguity is genuinely difficult to conduct cleanly.","Listen for the moment the pulse slips between three and two, which happens constantly and is the whole flavour of the dance. Then find a recording by a real huapango group from Veracruz, with jarana, requinto and a falsetto singer, and the orchestral version turns into a translation rather than an original. Nine minutes and no preparation needed."],"guarnieri-danca-brasileira":["Three minutes for piano, and Guarnieri's most played music by a wide margin. A syncopated dance tune in the right hand rides over a stamping bass that lands off the beat; a quieter, more lyrical strain forms the middle before the dance returns harder than before. The rhythm derives from Brazilian urban dance and is notated exactly rather than left to the player's instinct. He later orchestrated it, adding colour and losing some of the piano version's snap.","Camargo Guarnieri was twenty-one and working in Sao Paulo when the writer Mario de Andrade took him up. Andrade's essay on Brazilian music laid down a programme for a national art built from popular urban and rural forms, and Guarnieri became its most disciplined follower, to the point of publishing decades later an open letter attacking Brazilian serialists as anti-national. The Sao Paulo modernists were conducting all these arguments in public.","The left hand is doing the work of a percussion section: the pattern is a hand-drum rhythm transferred to the keyboard, which is why the piece resists rubato and collapses when it is played expressively. Guarnieri asks for almost no pedal, keeping the texture dry and struck. The middle section quotes nothing but leans on the modinha, the sentimental song that keeps surfacing throughout Brazilian art music.","The piece's popularity has done Guarnieri no favours: he wrote symphonies, concertos and hundreds of songs and is remembered for three minutes of dance. His 1950 manifesto against twelve-tone music made him enemies among younger Brazilians and coloured his reputation for decades afterwards. Reassessment is under way, though recordings of the larger works remain scarce outside Brazil.","Three minutes: play it, then play the orchestral version, then decide which rhythm bites harder. Listen to where the bass note falls against the tune, which is almost never together. Follow it with Villa-Lobos for the contrast between a disciplined nationalist and a gloriously undisciplined one working the same territory."],"gomes-il-guarany":["An opera-ballo in four acts, about two and a half hours, sung in Italian. Peri, chief of the Guarani, loves Cecilia, daughter of a Portuguese nobleman in sixteenth-century Brazil; adventurers and a hostile tribe threaten them both, and the opera ends with her father blowing up his own house. The music is Italian through and through, with big ensembles, a heroic tenor and a soprano prayer, and the overture's striding theme is the part everybody knows.","Carlos Gomes was born in Campinas, the son of a bandmaster, and sent to Milan on a scholarship from the Emperor Dom Pedro II. He took Jose de Alencar's novel O Guarani, the founding Brazilian romance about the union of indigenous and Portuguese blood, and had it turned into an Italian libretto. La Scala staged it in March 1870 and it travelled quickly to other European houses, the first opera by a composer from the Americas to do so.","Nothing in the score is Brazilian. The indigenous colour is the standard operatic exoticism of the period, and Alencar reportedly disliked what had been done to his book. The overture's main theme was later adopted as the signature of Brazil's national radio broadcast and is heard daily across the country, so most Brazilians know the tune without knowing that the opera exists.","Verdi is said to have praised Gomes as a real talent, and Milan treated the young composer as a prodigy from the tropics, an exoticism he traded on and then could not escape. Brazil later claimed him as a national founder, which sits awkwardly with an Italian opera about Brazil written for La Scala. Revivals are occasional; the 1990s production and recording with Placido Domingo is the best known.","Start with the overture, four minutes containing all the tunes at once. Then the Act I duet for Peri and Cecilia. Expect Verdi's world rather than Brazil's, because judged as an Italian opera of 1870 it holds up well. The Domingo recording is the practical way in, since there is very little competition on disc."],"lecuona-malaguena":["Six minutes for piano, the last number of the Suite Andalucia. It opens with a descending four-note figure hammered in the bass, a guitar's falling cadence turned into an ostinato, over which a free declamatory melody enters like a singer taking the stage. A slow songlike middle section in the major provides the contrast, then the opening returns and builds to a chordal finish. Almost everything is repetition; the piece works by insistence rather than by development.","Ernesto Lecuona was Cuba's most successful musician of the century, a virtuoso pianist who toured the Americas and Europe and wrote zarzuelas, songs and film music as fluently as he wrote for the piano. The Suite Andalucia of 1933 is Cuban music about Spain, from a composer whose family had made the crossing in the other direction, the same transatlantic double vision that produced the habanera. Hollywood picked the piece up almost immediately.","The descending bass is the Andalusian cadence again, the same figure that runs from Soler through Albeniz to flamenco guitar, and Lecuona simply refuses to leave it. Words were added later by other hands, turning the piece into a song, and the tune has since been recorded by big bands, rock guitarists and brass ensembles, so the piano original is now the least familiar version of it.","Serious criticism has largely ignored Lecuona, filing him with light music because he was popular, wrote for films and played in cabarets. Cuban musicology takes him more seriously, as did the pianists of Jorge Bolet's generation, who kept his music in recital programmes. The Malaguena's ubiquity in arrangement has made it hard to hear the original as composition rather than as raw material.","Hear the piano original before any arrangement; it is faster, harder and far less sentimental than the versions you know. Listen for the four falling notes in the bass and count how many times they come round. Then compare it with Albeniz's Granada, the same Andalusian nostalgia written by someone who had actually been there."],"roldan-ritmicas-5-6":["Two short movements, about five minutes together, for eleven players and nothing but percussion. The instruments are Afro-Cuban: claves, maracas, bongo, guiro, and the quijada, a donkey's jawbone whose loose teeth rattle when it is struck, alongside drums and cymbals from the orchestra. Everything is written out and nothing is improvised. Rhythms are layered three and four deep against each other, and the interest lies entirely in timbre and pattern, since there is no melody at all.","Amadeo Roldan was born in Paris to Cuban parents, trained as a violinist in Madrid, and settled in Havana in 1921, where he became concertmaster and then conductor of the Orquesta Filarmonica. He and Alejandro Garcia Caturla were the centre of afrocubanismo, the movement insisting that Cuba's African inheritance was the country's real music at a time when polite Havana preferred not to notice it. The first four Ritmicas are for wind quintet and piano; these two abandon pitch.","These are among the earliest concert works anywhere written for percussion alone, predating Varese's Ionisation, which Roldan knew and championed in Havana. The quijada is not a novelty instrument: it comes straight from Afro-Cuban carnival practice, and putting it on a concert platform in 1930 was a statement about what counted as Cuban culture. The notation is strict precisely in order to insist that this is composition and not folklore.","Roldan died at thirty-eight and his music went quiet for decades; the Ritmicas survived mainly through percussion ensembles hunting for repertoire older than the 1940s. Historians of percussion music now place them at the origin of the genre alongside Varese, though the claim to priority is complicated by how differently the two composers were thinking. Cuban cultural policy after the revolution reclaimed him as a national figure.","Five minutes, and the fastest way to hear why percussion music became a genre at all. Listen for the quijada's rattle, unmistakable once you have identified it. Play them next to Varese's Ionisation and then next to a recording of a Cuban rumba group; Roldan's position between the two becomes obvious immediately."],"brouwer-elogio-de-la-danza":["Two movements, six minutes, for one guitar. The first, Lento, is slow and sounds unmeasured: a few notes at a time, wide silences, dissonant intervals left hanging. The second, Obstinato, is a driving dance over a repeated pattern whose accents shift constantly, ending in hard strummed chords. Brouwer treats the guitar's percussive resources, struck attacks and rasgueado, as structural material rather than as decorative effect, which was new for the instrument.","Leo Brouwer was in his mid-twenties, five years after the Cuban revolution, running the music department of the new film institute in Havana and absorbing European avant-garde scores that were reaching Cuba for the first time. He was also a formidable guitarist until a hand injury ended his playing career. The title means in praise of dance, the piece was written in connection with choreography, and both movements are conceived as movement rather than as abstract music.","The first movement's silences are timed rather than free: Brouwer notates the pauses, so what sounds hesitant is measured to the beat. In the second, the ostinato keeps its length while the accents travel across it, an African rhythmic principle rather than a European metrical one, and the same principle Brouwer was hearing in Cuban drumming and in the European scores on his desk at the same moment.","The piece arrived when guitarists were desperate for repertoire beyond the Spanish tradition and became a standard almost at once. Brouwer's later turn away from the avant-garde towards a tonal, popular-facing style disappointed listeners who valued the 1960s works most; he has argued the change was political as much as aesthetic. Elogio remains the piece by which his early manner is judged.","Six minutes and a clear pair: stillness, then drive. In the first movement listen for how long the silences are held, since most players shorten them. Then hear his later Cuban Landscape pieces for the other Brouwer. Compare with Villa-Lobos's Etudes to hear what a guitar composer sounded like a generation earlier."],"golijov-la-pasion-segun-san-marcos":["A ninety-five-minute Passion in Spanish, with the chorus singing, dancing, clapping and playing percussion throughout. The arrest and death of Christ are told in Afro-Cuban and Brazilian idioms: rumba, samba, capoeira with the twanging bow of the berimbau, a Cuban drum ensemble. The role of Jesus is shared among several voices rather than given to one singer. Judas's betrayal is a dance number, the crucifixion is set as a lament, and there is no resurrection.","Helmuth Rilling's Bach academy in Stuttgart commissioned four Passions from four composers for the 250th anniversary of Bach's death in 2000. Osvaldo Golijov, an Argentine of Eastern European Jewish descent living in the United States, answered with a Latin American one, written for the Schola Cantorum de Caracas and a band of Cuban and Brazilian musicians. The premiere in Stuttgart in September 2000 produced a standing ovation and a certain amount of scandal.","Golijov is Jewish, and the choice matters: the Passion narratives have a long history of anti-Jewish use, and he sets Mark's text without the crowd's traditional self-condemnation, stopping before the resurrection so that the death is left unresolved. The berimbau, a capoeira instrument brought to Brazil by enslaved Africans, carries Christ's music, so a slave's instrument speaks for the condemned man.","The premiere divided listeners immediately, between those who heard the most alive sacred music in decades and those who heard a crossover product wearing a Passion's clothes. A later controversy over uncredited collaboration in a different Golijov work sharpened questions about how this score's popular material was assembled and by whom. It is still performed, still recorded and still argued about, which is a fair description of its ambition.","Watch it if you can, because the choreography is part of the score rather than decoration. Otherwise listen straight through, since the cumulative sweep is the point and individual numbers give a misleading impression. The Schola Cantorum de Caracas recording is the reference. Hear Bach's St Matthew Passion nearby to feel the argument being conducted with it."],"elgar-enigma-variations":["A quiet, hesitant theme followed by fourteen variations, each headed with the initials or nickname of someone in the composer's circle. The theme alternates a drooping four-note sigh with a rising answer, and both shapes turn up in disguise everywhere afterwards. The portraits range from a stammering amateur pianist to a bulldog tumbling into the River Wye, barking as he scrambles out. At the centre stands the slow ninth variation, Nimrod; the finale is a broad self-portrait that gathers earlier material into a march.","Elgar was forty-one, teaching violin around Malvern and known mainly as a supplier of pieces to provincial choral festivals. One evening in October 1898, tired after a day of lessons, he improvised a tune at the piano and began twisting it into likenesses of friends while his wife Alice listened and guessed. Among them was August Jaeger, the Novello editor who had recently talked him out of abandoning composition altogether. Hans Richter conducted the London premiere in June 1899.","The thirteenth variation is headed only with three asterisks. Over a timpani roll played with side-drum sticks, imitating a ship's engines, the clarinet quotes Mendelssohn's overture Calm Sea and Prosperous Voyage, a farewell to someone crossing the sea. Nimrod's opening deliberately shadows the slow movement of Beethoven's Pathetique sonata, recalling a late-night argument the two men had about Beethoven. Elgar also insisted a larger, unplayed theme runs through the whole set, and never said what it was.","The premiere made Elgar's national reputation in a single evening, and Jaeger promptly badgered him into extending the finale by roughly a hundred bars. The unheard theme has drawn candidates from Auld Lang Syne to Rule, Britannia to the plainchant Dies irae, none of them convincing; many scholars now suspect the puzzle was partly a publicity instinct. Elgar's own electrical recording of 1926 remains the primary evidence for his flexible, constantly nudged tempos.","Nimrod is usually met alone at memorials; hear it where it belongs, arriving after the skittish eighth variation and dissolving into the tenth. Follow one shape only, the falling four notes of the opening, and count how often it returns wearing a different costume. Compare Elgar's own brisk 1926 recording with a slower modern one to hear how much the piece has been sentimentalised."],"elgar-dream-of-gerontius":["Ninety-five minutes in two parts for tenor, mezzo-soprano, bass, large chorus and orchestra. Part One is a dying man's bedside: Gerontius alternates fear and prayer while friends recite the litany around him. Part Two takes place after death, the soul led by an Angel past a chorus of jeering demons to a single overwhelming orchestral crash as it glimpses God, then to the Angel's farewell over softly rocking strings. There are no arias in the old sense; the music runs continuously on recurring motifs.","Elgar set Cardinal Newman's long poem for the Birmingham Triennial Festival, the great shop window of English choral music, and he set it as a Catholic in an emphatically Protestant institution. The commission came at the height of his new fame after the Enigma Variations. He worked fast through 1900 and delivered late; the chorus master, Charles Swinnerton Heap, died during preparations, and his replacement had neither the time nor the sympathy to drill an unfamiliar and chromatic score.","Newman's poem was cut heavily, and the excisions are as revealing as the setting. Elgar removed most of the explicitly Catholic doctrinal machinery from Part Two, leaving the Purgatory that so alarmed Anglican festival committees implied rather than argued. The moment of seeing God is not sung at all: it is a single orchestral chord for full forces, marked to be played with the utmost possible force, and everything before it has been quietly preparing that one instant.","The Birmingham premiere in October 1900 under Hans Richter went badly, the chorus underprepared and audibly lost. Germany rescued it: Julius Buths conducted it at Dusseldorf, where Richard Strauss publicly toasted Elgar as the first English progressive composer, and the work returned home vindicated. Anglican cathedrals initially demanded textual changes before performing it. It is now the pillar of the English oratorio repertory, and the tenor part one of the hardest in it.","Begin with the Prelude, which lays out nearly every idea you will hear later, then jump to the Angel's Farewell at the very end and let the whole span sit between those two poles. Listen for the orchestra doing the theology while the voices do the words. Seek a recording with a tenor who sings Gerontius as a frightened man rather than a heroic one."],"elgar-cello-concerto":["Four movements played in two linked pairs, about half an hour. It opens with the cello alone, four heavy chords like someone clearing their throat before speaking, then a swaying tune for the violas. A flickering, muted scherzo follows; the slow movement is a single short song of some fifty bars; the finale swaggers, then collapses back into the slow movement and the opening chords. The orchestra is large but is kept transparent, often reduced to a handful of lines.","Elgar wrote it at Brinkwells, a cottage in the Sussex woods, in the summer of 1919, recovering from a tonsil operation and from the war. He was sixty-two. The world that had commissioned his marches and symphonies had gone, his wife Alice was a year from death, and he wrote almost nothing of substance afterwards. The premiere in October 1919 was ruined by rehearsal time swallowed by the other conductor on the programme.","The opening recitative is not a mere introduction. Those four chords return to end the finale, and between them the concerto keeps circling the same falling shapes, so the piece behaves less like a display vehicle than like a single long-breathed monologue. The slow movement stays in one narrow register almost throughout, as though the soloist has stopped projecting. Elgar marked the finale's collapse with an instruction that the earlier music be recalled, not merely restated.","Reviews of the botched 1919 premiere blamed the performance rather than the piece, but it stayed a specialist item for forty years. Beatrice Harrison recorded it with Elgar conducting; John Barbirolli championed it; and Jacqueline du Pre's 1965 recording with Barbirolli made it, for a generation, the emblem of English elegy. Some now argue that reading is too grief-soaked, and that the concerto's wit and its dry scherzo get flattened by it.","Listen to the first minute twice: the unaccompanied chords, then the violas' answer. That is the whole argument in miniature. The slow movement lasts barely five minutes, so resist waiting for it to develop; it simply says one thing and stops. Compare du Pre's burning account with a leaner one to hear how differently the same notes can be pointed."],"elgar-symphony-no-1":["Fifty minutes in four movements, framed by a slow, stepping processional tune given out quietly by strings and woodwind over a soft drum tread. That motto is then abandoned, and the first movement proper is fast, restless and in a distant key, as though the opening had been forgotten. The scherzo, marching and skittering by turns, slows without a break into the slow movement, which is the same music turned into a hymn. The motto returns in full at the close.","England had produced no symphony that held the international stage since the eighteenth century, and Elgar was under open pressure to supply one. He had been sketching symphonic material for years, at one point around a projected work on General Gordon. He wrote the piece in 1907 and 1908, by then a national figure with a knighthood, Professor at Birmingham, and privately convinced that the country did not really want serious music from him.","The two adjoining middle movements share their material: the scherzo's scurrying quick figure, slowed right down and given to the strings, becomes the great melody of the slow movement. Elgar never announces the transformation, and it slips past most first hearings. The motto theme is also carefully placed just outside the symphony's home key for most of its appearances, so its final arrival in A-flat sounds like a return the music has been postponing for three-quarters of an hour.","Hans Richter conducted the Manchester premiere in December 1908 and told the orchestra it was the greatest symphony of modern times by the greatest modern composer. It received roughly a hundred performances in its first year, in Britain, Europe and America, an unheard-of figure. Enthusiasm cooled sharply between the wars, when its confidence sounded imperial and dated. Recordings by Boult, Barbirolli and Solti restored it, and its ambivalence is now what critics stress.","Fix the opening processional in your memory in the first ninety seconds, because the symphony spends its length keeping it away from you. Then listen for the joins: the scherzo does not stop before the slow movement, it decelerates into it. Boult's recordings carry the composer's own practice; Barbirolli's are warmer and slower. Try the first movement alone before attempting the whole."],"elgar-sea-pictures":["Five songs for a low female voice and orchestra, about twenty-two minutes. The cycle opens with a lulling rocking figure and closes with a swimmer's defiant plunge into a storm, so the sea moves from cradle to grave across the set. In between come a becalmed Sunday morning at sea, a tiny domestic sketch written by the composer's wife, and a slow, hypnotic song about coral on the seabed. The orchestra is used for atmosphere rather than commentary, often very quietly.","Written for the Norwich Festival of 1899, months after the Enigma Variations had made Elgar's name, and tailored to Clara Butt, a contralto of famous power and physical presence who sang the premiere reportedly dressed as a mermaid. The poems are by five different hands, including Elgar's wife Alice for the third song, and the marine subject suited both the festival's coastal setting and a Victorian public that read sea verse constantly.","The five songs are not merely grouped: fragments of the first song's rocking accompaniment surface in the last, quietly binding a set assembled from unrelated poems. Elgar also composed the fourth song, about coral lying unseen far below, so that the vocal line stays almost motionless while the orchestra shifts underneath it, a picture of stillness at depth. The cycle sits deliberately low, exploiting the bottom of a contralto's range where most composers avoid writing tunes.","It succeeded instantly and then suffered from that success, dismissed for decades as Victorian drawing-room verse in expensive orchestral clothing. Butt's own theatrical manner did not help its later reputation. Janet Baker's recordings with Barbirolli in the 1960s reframed the cycle as serious song, and it is now standard for mezzo-sopranos as well as true contraltos. Debate continues over whether the weaker poems are redeemed by the music or merely carried by it.","Start with Where Corals Lie, the shortest and strangest, then hear the whole set in order to feel the arc from lullaby to drowning. Notice how rarely the orchestra doubles the singer. Baker with Barbirolli is the classic account; try a genuine contralto afterwards to hear the weight Elgar actually wrote for."],"vaughan-williams-symphony-no-5":["Four movements, about forty minutes, and unusually quiet for a wartime symphony. Horns call over held string chords that sit in a key other than the one the horns imply, so the opening hangs unresolved. A brief, scurrying, muted scherzo follows. The third movement, the heart of the work, is a long slow arch for strings, cor anglais and solo woodwind. The finale is a set of variations over a repeating bass, and it ends by returning to the opening horn call, now settled.","Vaughan Williams was seventy and had been working for years on an opera drawn from Bunyan's Pilgrim's Progress that he doubted he would ever finish. He lifted material from its sketches into this symphony and headed movements with Bunyan's stage directions in the manuscript. He conducted the premiere himself at a Proms concert in the Royal Albert Hall in June 1943, with the war's outcome still undecided and London still being bombed.","The score carries a dedication to Sibelius, offered, as Vaughan Williams put it, without permission, and the debt shows in the way long-held string chords let brass calls float free above them. Listeners who know the opera hear more: the slow movement grows from music associated with the House Beautiful, and the finale's serene close is the Pilgrim laying down his burden. The symphony never says this aloud, and Vaughan Williams removed the Bunyan headings before publication.","The 1943 premiere was received as a message of consolation, and some heard it as a valediction from an old man. He then lived fifteen more years and wrote four more symphonies, including the ferocious Sixth, which forced a rethink of the Fifth as a chosen tone rather than a final one. Later criticism has argued about whether its calm is escapist; the more common view now is that it is deliberately, effortfully achieved.","Give the third movement your first attention, ideally with nothing after it. Then hear the whole symphony and notice that the horn call which opens the piece is the same one that closes it, arriving in a key it could not reach at the start. Compare Vaughan Williams's own 1952 recording with a modern one; the differences in tempo are considerable."],"vaughan-williams-symphony-no-4":["Thirty-two minutes of grinding dissonance from a composer known for folk song. It begins with a violent clash of adjacent notes in the full orchestra, and the whole symphony is built from two tiny cells: a rising and falling semitone pair, and a four-note stepwise climb. The slow movement is bleak rather than consoling, ending with a long unaccompanied flute line. The scherzo runs straight into a finale that turns into a hard-driven fugue and slams back to the opening clash.","Composed between 1931 and 1934, and premiered by the BBC Symphony Orchestra under Adrian Boult in April 1935. Vaughan Williams was in his early sixties, and Europe was rearming; commentators immediately read the violence as a response to fascism, which he refused to confirm, saying only that he did not know whether he liked it but that it was what he meant. He was privately amused to be told he had suddenly become modern.","Almost every theme in the symphony can be traced back to the two cells announced in the opening bars, so the aggression is structural rather than decorative. The finale is followed by an epilogue marked as a fugue on the first movement's material, which brings the whole argument back to its source. Listen for the flute at the end of the slow movement: it plays the four-note climb alone, exposed, and it is the same figure that has been battering the orchestra.","The premiere astonished audiences and split critics, some hearing a betrayal of the pastoral manner, others relief that he had one. Vaughan Williams recorded it himself in 1937, a rare document of a composer conducting his own most violent score, and the recording is notably fierce. The habit of reading it as prophecy of war has faded; the current emphasis is on its compression and on how tightly the material is worked.","Learn the first ten seconds. The colliding chord and the four notes that climb out of it are the seeds of everything, and once you can hear them the symphony becomes legible rather than merely loud. Then follow the flute at the close of the slow movement. Vaughan Williams's own 1937 recording is the sharpest; Boult's is broader and lets more detail through."],"vaughan-williams-serenade-to-music":["Thirteen unbroken minutes on the moonlit garden scene from the last act of The Merchant of Venice. A solo violin plays a long, wandering line over quiet strings before any voice enters, and it keeps returning between the vocal paragraphs. The sixteen singers are used as a shifting ensemble rather than a choir: sometimes all together, more often in small overlapping groups or single lines, with individual phrases handed from one voice to the next as the text moves from starlight to music's power.","Written for Henry Wood's golden jubilee as a conductor and first performed at the Royal Albert Hall in October 1938, weeks after the Munich agreement. Vaughan Williams composed each of the sixteen solo parts for a specific named British singer, marking their initials in the score, so the piece is a group portrait of a generation of performers. Rachmaninoff, who played in the same concert, is said to have been reduced to tears by it.","Because the vocal lines were tailored to particular voices, some singers get a single exposed phrase placed exactly where their strength lay, and the initials remain printed in most editions. The solo violin never sings the text yet carries the argument, entering before the words and having the last extended say. Vaughan Williams later made versions for four soloists with chorus and for orchestra alone, but the sixteen-soloist original is the only one that preserves the portrait.","It was an immediate favourite and has been slightly patronised since as an occasional piece, a compliment to a conductor rather than a serious work. Performances usually use the choral arrangement because sixteen soloists of comparable calibre are expensive; purists argue this destroys the whole conception, since the writing depends on individual timbres rather than blend. Recordings of the original version, including one conducted by Wood himself, have shifted opinion back.","Follow the solo violin, not the words, on a first hearing; it will show you the shape. Then read Shakespeare's speech and listen again for the moment the texture thins to a single voice. Seek the version with sixteen separate soloists, and if possible the 1938 recording made with the original cast, for the sound of voices the music was actually measured against."],"vaughan-williams-on-wenlock-edge":["Six settings of Housman for tenor with the unusual accompaniment of piano and string quartet, about twenty-two minutes. The first song puts a gale through the trees as tremolo strings and hammered piano. The third is a dialogue between a dead man and his living friend, the questions and answers separated by chilly bare chords. The fifth, Bredon Hill, is the longest, with church bells built from repeated overlapping notes in the strings, and the last song fades to almost nothing.","Vaughan Williams was thirty-six and had just spent three months in Paris taking lessons from Ravel, who was younger than him, specifically to escape what he called his own heavy contrapuntal habits. He came back with a new interest in colour and in unresolved chords, and wrote this cycle almost at once. Housman's A Shropshire Lad, published in 1896, had become the most set body of English verse of the period, its short poems about dead young men everywhere.","The string quartet is muted for long stretches and often plays chords that go nowhere, a device straight from the Paris lessons; the folk-song collector had learned impressionist harmony and used it for weather. In Bredon Hill the bells are made by sustaining several pitches at slightly different speeds so they never quite line up, which is how real peals sound. Housman himself disliked having his poems set, and objected to the repetition of his words.","Early listeners found the piano and string combination awkward, and the composer later made an orchestral version that is more comfortable and less strange. Housman is reported to have complained about omitted stanzas. The cycle's reputation has risen steadily; it is now heard as the moment English song absorbed French technique without losing its own accent, and the original chamber version has displaced the orchestration in performance.","Start with Is My Team Ploughing, where you can hear the two speakers separated by register and colour, then go back to the first song for the storm. Read the six poems first; they are very short. Choose the original piano and string quartet version, and a tenor who keeps the words plain rather than operatic."],"holst-planets":["Seven movements, about fifty minutes, for an orchestra of unusual size including bass oboe, tenor tuba, organ and, at the end, an offstage women's chorus. Mars grinds forward in a limping five-beat pattern played with the wood of the bow. Venus answers with solo horn and high strings. Mercury flickers, Jupiter strides and then broadens into a hymn tune, Saturn advances on slow alternating chords, Uranus stamps, and Neptune dissolves into wordless voices that fade as a door closes on them.","Holst was teaching at St Paul's Girls' School and Morley College, composing at weekends, and had no expectation of a large public. A friend introduced him to astrology around 1913 and he became a keen, half-embarrassed caster of horoscopes; the movements follow astrological character, not astronomy, which is why there is no Earth. He worked through the first years of the war, unfit for service, finishing in 1917. A private performance for invited guests followed in September 1918.","The order is not the order of the solar system, and the subtitles matter: Saturn is the Bringer of Old Age, which Holst called his favourite movement, and its trudging chords are the sound of time rather than of a planet. Mars was largely finished before the war began, so its brutality is not a war picture. The Neptune chorus stands offstage behind a closed door, and the score instructs that the door be shut slowly during the final bar.","Holst disliked the success it brought him, feeling it eclipsed everything else he wrote, and he was right that it did. Adrian Boult conducted the first performances and the composer's own recordings survive. Later hands added a Pluto movement after the 1930 discovery, which Holst never sanctioned and which the planet's demotion has made doubly awkward. The score's influence on film music, especially science fiction, is direct and openly acknowledged.","Do not start with Mars or Jupiter, which are worn smooth by use. Begin with Saturn and let its two alternating chords tick past, then go to Neptune and listen for the exact moment you can no longer tell whether the voices are still there. Boult's recordings carry the composer's practice; a modern one with a large hall will give you the organ pedal in Mars."],"holst-hymn-of-jesus":["Twenty-two minutes for two full choruses placed apart, a distant semi-chorus and orchestra. It opens with plainchant intoned by trombones and solo voices over almost nothing, then breaks into a wildly rhythmic central section in which the two choirs hurl short phrases at each other from opposite sides, with sudden silences and passages in unusual metres. The text has Christ inviting the disciples to dance in a ring; the music treats that literally, and ends in bare, wide-spaced chords.","Holst learned enough Greek, with help, to make his own translation from the apocryphal Acts of St John, a second-century text outside the biblical canon and unfamiliar to his audience. He composed it in 1917 while also finishing The Planets, and dedicated it to Vaughan Williams. The first performance came in March 1920 at the Queen's Hall with Holst conducting, into a London still absorbing the war and unusually receptive to unorthodox spirituality.","The two chants that open the work are Pange lingua and Vexilla regis, both Passion hymns, so the ecstatic dance that follows is framed by music of the crucifixion. Holst separates the choirs physically and writes for the gap between them, tossing single words across the hall in a technique borrowed from Venetian church music three centuries earlier. The frequent bare fifths and shifting metres come from his study of Sanskrit texts and Hindu rhythm.","It was the greatest critical success of Holst's life, admired by Vaughan Williams above anything else he wrote, and it made the case that the composer of The Planets was more than a colourist. It then fell out of the repertory because it needs two well-drilled choruses and a hall that can separate them. Recordings tend to compress the spatial effect. Its rhythmic freedom is now heard as a genuine English parallel to Stravinsky.","Listen for the antiphony above all: work out which side each phrase is coming from during the dance. The opening trombone chant is slow and easy to miss, but hearing it makes the ending land. Choose a recording that keeps the two choirs distinct in the stereo image, and if you ever get the chance, hear it live in a large resonant building."],"walton-symphony-no-1":["Forty-five minutes, four movements, and famously hard to play. It opens with a quiet drum roll and a held horn note over which oscillating woodwind figures accumulate for several minutes before any tune arrives, a slow filling-up of the orchestra with energy. The scherzo is marked to be played with malice. The slow movement is a long, aching melody begun by the flute. The finale swings into a broad ceremonial march and two fugues, ending in blazing B-flat major.","Walton was in his early thirties, already famous for Facade and Belshazzar's Feast, and stuck. He wrote three movements between 1932 and 1934 and could not finish. His personal life ran alongside the crisis: a long attachment to Imma von Doernberg ended, and Alice Wimborne entered his life. The conductor Hamilton Harty eventually persuaded him to let the three completed movements be performed in December 1934, with the finale added a year later.","The unfinished performance is audible in the finished piece. The first three movements share a taut, obsessive harmonic language that keeps circling without resolving, while the finale, written after the crisis passed, is more public and more diatonic, and some hear the join as a fracture rather than a design. The opening pedal note is held for over fifty bars beneath shifting figures, so the symphony's tension is generated by refusing to move before it moves at all.","The three-movement premiere caused a sensation and the complete performance in November 1935 confirmed Walton as Elgar's successor in public estimation. Critics have argued ever since about the finale: whether its triumph is earned or bolted on, and whether the two fugues are a solution or a display of technique. Its Sibelius debt, obvious in the long-held pedal openings, was noted at once. Orchestras still regard it as a stamina test.","Set aside the first five minutes and simply track how many instruments have joined by the end of them; the excitement is entirely cumulative. Then jump to the slow movement's opening flute line. Compare a recording of the three-movement version, if you find one, with the complete symphony, and decide the finale question for yourself."],"walton-facade":["A sequence of short numbers, each a poem spoken in strict rhythm against music for six players: flute doubling piccolo, clarinet doubling bass clarinet, saxophone, trumpet, cello and percussion. The voice does not sing; it recites at a fixed speed, fitted to the beat like an instrument, and the words are chosen for sound more than sense. The music parodies popular dances of the day, polka, tango, foxtrot, waltz, tarantella, each compressed into a minute or two of deadpan pastiche.","Walton was barely twenty and living in the Chelsea house of the Sitwell family, who had effectively adopted him after Oxford. Edith Sitwell wrote the poems as experiments in rhythm and vowel colour. At the first private performances in 1922 the reciter spoke through a megaphone poked through the mouth of a face painted on a curtain, so the audience saw no performer at all. The public premiere at the Aeolian Hall in June 1923 caused an uproar.","The megaphone, later a purpose-built Sengerphone, was not a joke about volume but about anonymity: Sitwell's point was that the poems should arrive as pure sound with no personality attached. Walton's parodies carry precise musical jokes, including a Rossini quotation and a tango built on a popular song of the day, and several numbers are in awkward metres that make the reciter's job far harder than it sounds. The number of items varied for decades.","Newspapers treated the 1923 premiere as an outrage and Sitwell was mocked for years. Walton kept revising, withdrawing and reinstating numbers, and the piece exists in several versions plus two orchestral suites that removed the words and made it respectable. Critical opinion split between those who see a brilliant piece of English dada and those who find the joke thin at full length. The suites are now played more often than the original.","Hear four or five numbers rather than the whole set at once, and read the poems while listening; the pleasure is in how exactly the syllables land on the beat. Polka and Tango-Pasodoble are the best way in. Look for a recording with Edith Sitwell herself reciting, and then one with a modern actor, to hear how much the delivery changes the piece."],"britten-peter-grimes":["Three acts and a prologue, about two and a half hours. A Suffolk fishing village holds an inquest into the death of a fisherman's apprentice; a second boy dies; the village turns on Grimes and he takes his boat out to sink it. Six orchestral interludes carry the sea and the weather between scenes. The chorus is the village itself, a single hostile organism, and its manhunt in the last act is built from the syllables of Grimes's name shouted over a repeating bass.","Britten, a pacifist, had left for America in 1939 and read an essay by E. M. Forster about the Suffolk poet George Crabbe in a Californian bookshop, which made him homesick enough to return in 1942 across the wartime Atlantic. Serge Koussevitzky's foundation put up the commission. The opera opened at Sadler's Wells in June 1945, a month after victory in Europe, with Peter Pears as Grimes and a company deeply divided about the whole project.","Grimes's own music is set apart by a rising interval that nobody else in the opera sings, and in the mad scene of Act Three his fragments are answered only by an offstage foghorn on a single note and the distant chorus. The Passacaglia interlude repeats one bass figure thirty-nine times while the viola plays the apprentice's theme above it, so the boy who never sings gets a whole movement to himself. The final scene ends with the village's ordinary morning, unchanged.","The premiere was recognised at once as the reopening of English opera after two centuries in which almost nothing had held the stage. Some of the Sadler's Wells company resented the piece and the casting; the public did not. Debate has centred on Grimes himself: a brute, a victim of a conformist community, or a coded study of the outsider by a gay pacifist composer. Britten's own recording with Pears remains the reference.","Hear the Four Sea Interludes first as pure orchestral music, then meet them inside the drama where the storm belongs to a specific argument. In the opera, wait for the moment in Act One when the whole village sings in the pub during the storm and the door keeps blowing open. Britten conducting Pears is the primary document; Vickers offers a fiercer, more dangerous Grimes."],"britten-turn-of-the-screw":["A chamber opera in a prologue and two acts, six singers, thirteen players, about a hundred minutes. A governess arrives at a country house to look after two children and becomes convinced they are being visited by the ghosts of two dead servants. Sixteen short scenes are separated by orchestral variations on a single theme, and the whole opera tightens continuously, like the screw of the title. The children sing nursery rhymes and Latin declensions that curdle as the work proceeds.","Written for the English Opera Group, a small touring company Britten founded partly because full-scale opera houses were unaffordable, and premiered at the Teatro La Fenice in Venice in September 1954 with Britten conducting. Myfanwy Piper adapted Henry James's 1898 novella, and made the crucial decision to let the ghosts sing, which James never does. Britten was then living at Aldeburgh with Peter Pears, who sang the ghost Peter Quint.","The theme announced after the prologue uses all twelve notes, arranged in rising fourths, and the fifteen variations move up one step each time, so the opera is literally screwed a semitone tighter as it goes. Quint's summoning of the boy is sung on melismas over a celesta, an instrument usually reserved for childlike innocence. The boy's Latin lesson, Malo, is a genuine schoolroom mnemonic that Britten turns into the opera's most exposed tune.","Venice received it well and it has never left the repertory, being both cheap to stage and unnervingly effective. The central argument mirrors the one about James's novella: are the ghosts real or the governess's projection? Britten's decision to make them sing pushes towards the first reading, though many productions undercut it. Later criticism has connected the opera's subject, an adult's corrupting fascination with a boy, to Britten's own biography.","Listen for the interludes between scenes: they are variations, and if you catch the rising theme once you will hear the whole opera climbing. Start with Quint's first offstage call to Miles, which is the moment the piece turns from drama into horror. A chamber-scale recording under Britten himself is the place to begin; then see it staged, where the ambiguity is decided in front of you."],"britten-war-requiem":["Eighty-five minutes on three separate musical planes. A full chorus, soprano and large orchestra sing the Latin Requiem; a boys' choir with organ sings from a distance; and a tenor and baritone with a twelve-player chamber orchestra sing nine poems by Wilfred Owen, interrupting the liturgy from close up. The three groups rarely combine. The Latin is public and ceremonial, the Owen settings intimate and bitter, and the work ends with the two soldiers singing themselves to sleep together.","Commissioned for the consecration of the rebuilt Coventry Cathedral in May 1962, beside the ruins of the medieval building destroyed by bombing in 1940. Britten, a conscientious objector in the war, chose soloists to represent the combatant nations: a Russian soprano, an English tenor and a German baritone. Galina Vishnevskaya was refused a Soviet exit visa for the premiere and Heather Harper learned the part in ten days; Vishnevskaya sang on the recording.","An interval of three whole tones, the tritone that medieval theorists called the devil in music, sounds through the entire work, in the bells that punctuate the opening and in the phrase the boys sing. It is only resolved in the final bars. At the end of the Offertorium the Latin text promises the seed of Abraham, while Owen's version of the same story has Abraham kill his son and half the seed of Europe with him; Britten sets both simultaneously.","The premiere was received as a public event as much as a piece, and the recording sold in numbers unheard of for contemporary music. A reaction followed: critics including Stravinsky found it manipulative and its pacifism too easy, and the sheer scale invited charges of sentimentality. That reaction has itself receded. The structural argument, that the liturgy is repeatedly punctured by an individual voice, is now the usual ground of admiration.","Follow one thread: the two male soloists. Everything they sing is Owen, and everything around them is Latin, so the collisions become audible. Start with the Offertorium, where the Abraham story is told twice at once. Britten's own 1963 recording with Pears, Fischer-Dieskau and Vishnevskaya is definitive; hear it once with the poems in front of you."],"tippett-child-of-our-time":["An oratorio in three parts for four soloists, chorus and orchestra, about sixty-six minutes, laid out like a Handel oratorio but with no named characters. Part One states a general condition of oppression, Part Two narrates a boy's shooting of an official and the pogrom that follows, Part Three turns inward towards reconciliation. Five African-American spirituals stand where Bach would have placed chorales, sung by the chorus with a soloist rising out of it, and they are the emotional pillars of the piece.","In November 1938 a seventeen-year-old Polish Jew, Herschel Grynszpan, shot a German diplomat in Paris, and the Nazi regime used the killing as the pretext for Kristallnacht. Tippett, a socialist and pacifist who would later be imprisoned for refusing war duties, began the oratorio within weeks. He asked T. S. Eliot to write the text; Eliot read the draft scenario and told him to write it himself, advice Tippett always said was the making of the work.","Tippett needed something with the communal force of a Lutheran chorale but available to a modern secular audience, and found it in the spirituals after hearing one on the radio. He arranged them in five parts himself. The libretto also runs on Jungian lines learned during his own analysis: the third part is not about the boy at all but about accepting a shadow within, which is why the work ends in acceptance rather than accusation.","The 1944 premiere in London, conducted by Walter Goehr with Peter Pears among the soloists, was a success at a moment when its subject was raw. Objections have been persistent: that the text is amateur, that its Jungian conclusion evades politics, and more recently that the use of spirituals by a white English composer is appropriation. Defenders answer that the spirituals are the point, a borrowed voice for the voiceless.","Go straight to the final spiritual, Deep River, then start at the beginning and hear the four others arrive at the ends of sections like anchors. Read the opening line, that the world turns on its dark side, before listening. Recordings conducted by Tippett himself are blunter and faster than the smoother modern ones; both make a case."],"parry-jerusalem":["Three minutes, sixteen lines of Blake, one tune sung in unison. The melody climbs in long, unhurried steps and is stated twice, with a short instrumental introduction that has become as familiar as the tune itself. There is no counterpoint and no vocal division: the whole force sings one line, and the harmony lives entirely in the accompaniment, which broadens on the second verse. Elgar's later orchestration adds brass and a swelling final phrase, and is the version usually heard.","Parry was seventy, Director of the Royal College of Music, and had taught nearly every British composer of the next generation. The Poet Laureate Robert Bridges asked him for a setting of Blake's preface to Milton to be sung at a Fight for Right rally in March 1916, in the third year of a war whose conduct Parry increasingly disliked. He withdrew his support from the campaign within a year and gave the copyright to the women's suffrage movement instead.","Blake's lines are a series of questions, not a claim: did those feet walk here, and the answer is left open. The tune, sung as patriotic assertion, actually sets a text about refusing to rest until the mills are gone. Parry's own dynamic markings ask for the first verse to be quiet and only the second to open out, an instruction almost universally ignored at large gatherings, where both verses arrive at full volume.","It became the anthem of the suffrage campaign, then of the Women's Institute, then of English sporting crowds and party conferences, and it is regularly proposed as a national anthem for England. Church use has been contested on the grounds that it asserts nothing doctrinal and asks unanswerable questions. Musicians point out that the tune is more subtle than its uses suggest, particularly in the way its phrases keep overshooting their expected landing points.","Hear it once with a small choir and organ before hearing it with a stadium, so that the first verse can be quiet as written. Follow the tune's shape: each phrase reaches a little higher than you expect. Then compare the plain hymn-book version with Elgar's 1922 orchestration, which turns a hymn into a ceremony."],"stanford-symphony-no-3-irish":["Four movements in the German manner, about forty-two minutes, with Irish traditional melody worked into the material rather than pasted on. The first movement is stormy and tightly argued. The scherzo is a jig, fast and light, with the harp used as a colouring instrument. The slow movement takes a lament for a seventeenth-century Irish chieftain and builds a long elegy from it. The finale gathers the earlier themes and drives to a bright major-key ending.","Stanford was thirty-four, Irish-born, Cambridge-trained and German-finished, teaching at the newly founded Royal College of Music where he would go on to shape Vaughan Williams, Holst, Coleridge-Taylor, Ireland and Bliss. He wanted for Ireland what Dvorak was achieving for Bohemia: national material given the full weight of symphonic argument. The premiere came in London in 1887 under Hans Richter, and Stanford's professional network carried it abroad quickly.","The Irish tunes are not quoted as souvenirs; the lament in the slow movement supplies intervals that reappear, disguised, in the finale's main theme, so the nationalism is structural. Stanford's teaching left a deeper mark than his music: he drilled his students in Brahmsian technique while insisting they collect their own country's songs, which is why the English folk-song revival came out of a conservatory rather than around it.","It was Stanford's international breakthrough. Hans von Bulow took it up in Berlin, and Mahler conducted it in New York in 1911, an unusual distinction for a British symphony of the period. Reputation then collapsed: to the next generation Stanford was the academic they had to escape, and Vaughan Williams and others said so publicly. Recordings since the 1980s have made the case that the craft is real and the invention stronger than the caricature allowed.","Begin with the slow movement, where the lament is stated plainly before Stanford starts working on it, then hear the finale and listen for the same intervals returning at speed. The scherzo is the easiest entry point if you want charm first. Any of the complete recorded symphony cycles will do; they are all recent, and none has a rival tradition to fight."],"delius-on-hearing-first-cuckoo-in-spring":["Six minutes for a small orchestra, one continuous slow paragraph with no real theme in the ordinary sense. Muted strings supply a warm cushion of slowly sliding chords, above which the clarinet gives out the cuckoo's two falling notes, three times, at long intervals. A folk tune arrives quietly in the middle and is decorated rather than developed. Nothing is repeated exactly, nothing is stated twice at the same pitch, and the piece dissolves rather than ends.","Delius was a Yorkshire wool merchant's son who had grown oranges in Florida, studied in Leipzig and settled at Grez-sur-Loing outside Paris, where he lived from 1897 and wrote almost everything of consequence. This is one of Two Pieces for Small Orchestra of 1911 and 1912. His music was better known in Germany than in Britain at the time, and Arthur Nikisch conducted the first performance in Leipzig in October 1913.","The middle tune is not English. It is a Norwegian folk melody, In Ola Valley, which Delius knew from Grieg's piano arrangement, so a piece taken as the essence of the English spring is built on Scandinavian material by a composer living in France. The cuckoo call itself is given only to the clarinet and never harmonised the same way twice, and the strings are muted throughout to keep the whole thing at one dynamic level.","Thomas Beecham made Delius's reputation in Britain almost single-handedly, conducting and recording him for fifty years, and this piece became the emblem. That advocacy created a backlash: Delius was dismissed as formless, an amateur of harmony with no capacity for argument. The counter-case is that argument was never the aim and that the slow chromatic drift is precisely controlled. The 1934 wireless broadcast from his deathbed made the legend permanent.","Six minutes, so play it twice. The first time, follow only the clarinet and count the cuckoo calls. The second, ignore the clarinet and listen to how the string chords keep shifting underneath without ever settling. Beecham's recordings remain the standard; a modern one will give you more inner detail and less atmosphere."],"bax-tintagel":["A single-movement tone poem of about fifteen minutes for a large orchestra with harp. It opens with a broad, striding brass theme over rocking strings, the castle on its headland; a slower central section becomes turbulent, with heaving swells in the lower strings and a long chromatic climb; the opening theme then returns in full brass over the whole orchestra. The scoring is thick and glittering, with divided strings and prominent harp, and the sea is rarely off the page.","Bax wrote it between 1917 and 1919 after staying in Cornwall with the pianist Harriet Cohen, with whom he was having an affair that lasted much of his life. He was in his mid-thirties, obsessed with Ireland and Celtic legend to the point of publishing stories under an Irish pseudonym, and had absorbed Strauss, Debussy and the Russians. The ruins at Tintagel carry the Arthurian and Tristan legends, which is the real subject.","Bax quotes Wagner. A phrase from Tristan und Isolde surfaces in the middle section, placed there because Tintagel is King Mark's castle in the legend and because the composer was writing about his own entangled love affair. The quotation is brief and easy to miss under the swell. The opening theme is also constructed to keep sounding like a fanfare without ever settling into a regular fanfare rhythm, so the headland never quite stands still.","Tintagel became and remains the one Bax piece in general circulation, which is a fair summary of his fate: seven symphonies, a Master of the King's Music appointment and near-total neglect. Critics have called the orchestration overloaded and the rhetoric second-hand. The defence, strengthened by complete recorded symphony cycles since the 1980s, is that the sensuous surface is the substance, and that few English composers heard colour as sharply.","Listen for the sea underneath rather than the tune on top; the strings are doing something different from the brass almost throughout. In the middle section, wait for the Wagner phrase in the woodwind and brass. Then try one of the Bax symphonies, the Third for preference, to hear what the neglected part of his output sounds like."],"bridge-the-sea":["An orchestral suite in four movements, about twenty-three minutes. Seascape opens with a broad swelling theme in the strings under a high shimmer. Sea-foam is a fast, light scherzo of flicked woodwind figures. Moonlight is slow and almost static, built from muted strings and a solo flute over held chords. Storm brings the whole orchestra to bear with the first movement's theme returning in the middle of the gale, and the suite ends in calm.","Bridge was in his early thirties, a working viola player in London string quartets and a busy conductor who took over performances at short notice, and he wrote the suite for the Proms, conducting the premiere himself in September 1911. Debussy's La mer was six years old and known in London; English audiences expected sea music to be picturesque, and got something closer to a study in orchestral colour with a picture attached.","A ten-year-old boy in the Norwich audience in 1924 was, in his own words, knocked sideways by it, and asked to be introduced to the composer. Bridge became Benjamin Britten's only private teacher and the one who drilled him in absolute clarity of intention. The sea music that opens Peter Grimes has this suite behind it. Bridge's own later style turned harsh and near-atonal, so this warm early suite is unrepresentative of everything he wrote after 1920.","Warmly received in 1911 and quickly popular, it then suffered when Bridge's post-war music alienated the same audience, and his whole output slipped out of view for half a century. Britten's advocacy, including the Variations on a Theme of Frank Bridge, began the recovery, and recordings from the 1970s onwards have restored both the early suites and the astringent later works. Critics now treat the two Bridges as one continuous development rather than a betrayal.","Take Moonlight first, the shortest and most beautiful, then hear the suite in order for the arc from calm to storm to calm. Listen for the first movement's theme reappearing inside Storm. Afterwards play the Four Sea Interludes from Peter Grimes and hear how much of the teacher survives in the pupil."],"ireland-piano-concerto":["Twenty-five minutes in three movements run together without a break. The first is bright and rhythmically springy, with a piano part full of glinting parallel chords and quick figuration rather than heroic octaves. The central slow movement is the emotional core, a long lyrical melody over a rocking accompaniment that becomes gradually more chromatic. A short cadenza links it to a finale in a jazz-inflected dance rhythm, and the slow movement's tune returns to close the work quietly.","Ireland was fifty, a London organist and teacher who had studied with Stanford and would later teach Britten, and he wrote the concerto in 1930 for Helen Perkin, a young pianist at the Royal College with whom he was deeply and unhappily preoccupied. She gave the premiere at the Proms under Henry Wood in October 1930. London was then full of French music and American dance bands, and both leave marks on the score.","Ireland later removed the dedication to Perkin after they fell out, a small act preserved in the printed scores. The concerto's second subject is derived from a shape used elsewhere in his piano music associated with places and private memory; Ireland attached secret personal and pagan associations to much of his work, including standing stones and Sussex landscapes, and rarely explained them. The finale's syncopations come from the dance music he heard in London, not from any folk source.","It was a genuine hit for two decades, played by every major British pianist, and was for a time the most performed English piano concerto. It then vanished with the rest of Ireland's output when the post-war generation looked elsewhere. Critics divide over whether its lightness is elegance or evasion. Recent recordings have argued for it as the most successful attempt to write a concerto that is neither Rachmaninoff nor Stravinsky.","Go to the slow movement first and stay with the long tune; everything else in the concerto is measured against it. Then hear all three movements in one sitting to feel the joins, which are seamless. Compare a mid-century British recording with a modern one; the older ones are drier and faster, and suit the finale's dance rhythms better."]});